{"id":"spain-horchata","name":"Horchata","nameAlt":"Tiger Nut Milk","aliases":["Horchata de Chufa"],"course":"drink","type":"drink","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Spain","occasion":"summer","ingredients":"80g tiger nuts · 300ml water · 20g sugar","method":"Soak tiger nuts overnight. Blend with water and strain. Stir in sugar and chill before serving.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":160,"nutrition":"protein 2g · carbs 20g · fat 6g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Strain well for a smooth texture.","pairsWith":"pastries · churros","trivia":"A Valencian specialty made from chufa.","howThisFeels":"Cool, nutty sweetness on a hot day.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"spain-cafe-con-leche","name":"Café con Leche","nameAlt":"Coffee with Milk","aliases":["Milky Coffee"],"course":"drink","type":"drink","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Spain","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100ml coffee · 100ml milk · 5g sugar","method":"Brew strong coffee. Heat milk until hot. Combine equal parts coffee and milk and stir in sugar.","cookTime":"5 min","kcal":120,"nutrition":"protein 5g · carbs 10g · fat 4g","storage":"Best fresh.","chefNotes":"Equal ratio is the classic Spanish way.","pairsWith":"toast · pastries","trivia":"A daily staple across Spain.","howThisFeels":"Warm, steady comfort to start the day.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-bacalhau-com-natas","name":"Bacalhau com Natas","nameAlt":"Cod with Cream","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"bake","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"family","ingredients":"150g salted snoek (or salted cod) · 150g potatoes · 100ml cream · 50g onion · 10g garlic · 30ml olive oil · 30g cheese · 2g salt · 1g black pepper","method":"Soak the salted cod for 24 hours, changing water to reduce salt. Boil cod for 10 minutes, drain and flake. Peel and slice potatoes and fry at 170°C for about 6 minutes until lightly golden. Sauté onion and garlic in olive oil on medium heat for 5 minutes. Combine cod, potatoes, and cream in a baking dish and mix well. Season with salt and pepper and top with grated cheese. Bake at 180°C for 25 minutes until golden and bubbling. Let rest briefly before serving.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":620,"nutrition":"protein 34g · carbs 40g · fat 36g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days and reheat in oven.","chefNotes":"Use full-fat cream for best texture.","pairsWith":"green salad · white wine · bread","trivia":"This creamy cod dish is a popular comfort food in Portugal.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-pataniscas-de-bacalhau","name":"Pataniscas de Bacalhau","nameAlt":"Cod Fritters","aliases":[],"course":"starter","type":"fritter","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"snack","ingredients":"150g salted snoek (or salted cod) · 100g flour · 2 eggs · 50g onion · 5g parsley · 200ml sunflower oil · 2g salt · 1g black pepper","method":"Soak cod for 24 hours and flake after boiling for 10 minutes. In a bowl mix flour, eggs, and a little water to form a batter. Add chopped onion, parsley, and cod to the batter. Season with salt and pepper and mix well. Heat oil to 180°C in a deep pan. Drop spoonfuls of batter into oil and fry for about 4 minutes until golden. Remove and drain on paper. Serve warm.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":450,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 30g · fat 24g","storage":"Best eaten fresh but can be refrigerated for 1 day.","chefNotes":"Keep batter slightly thick for fluffy fritters.","pairsWith":"rice · salad · white wine","trivia":"Pataniscas are often served with rice and beans in Portugal.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-carne-de-porco-a-alentejana","name":"Carne de Porco à Alentejana","nameAlt":"Pork with Clams","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"150g pork shoulder · 100g clams · 150g potatoes · 50g onion · 10g garlic · 30ml olive oil · 50ml white wine · 5g coriander · 2g salt · 1g paprika","method":"Cut pork into cubes and marinate with garlic, paprika, and wine for 30 minutes. Fry potatoes at 170°C for 6 minutes until golden. In a pan, cook pork on medium heat for 10 minutes until browned. Add clams and cover, cooking for 5 minutes until they open. Stir in fried potatoes and mix gently. Season with salt and garnish with coriander. Serve hot.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":580,"nutrition":"protein 35g · carbs 35g · fat 32g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Discard any clams that do not open during cooking.","pairsWith":"bread · white wine · salad","trivia":"This dish combines inland pork with coastal seafood traditions.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-acorda","name":"Açorda","nameAlt":"Bread Soup","aliases":["Açorda Alentejana"],"course":"main","type":"soup","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"100g bread · 10g garlic · 5g coriander · 30ml olive oil · 1 egg · 500ml water · 2g salt","method":"Bring water to a boil and add crushed garlic and salt. Tear bread into pieces and place in a bowl. Pour hot broth over bread and let it soak for 5 minutes. Add olive oil and chopped coriander. Poach the egg in simmering water for about 3 minutes. Place the egg on top of the soaked bread. Mix slightly before eating.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 12g · carbs 40g · fat 16g","storage":"Best eaten fresh.","chefNotes":"Use stale bread for best texture.","pairsWith":"olives · wine · salad","trivia":"Açorda is a rustic dish from the Alentejo region.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-polvo-a-lagareiro","name":"Polvo à Lagareiro","nameAlt":"Roasted Octopus with Potatoes","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"roast","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"200g octopus · 150g potatoes · 30ml olive oil · 10g garlic · 2g salt · 1g black pepper","method":"Boil octopus in water for 40 minutes until tender. Preheat oven to 200°C. Crush boiled potatoes lightly and place in a baking dish. Add octopus and drizzle with olive oil and garlic. Season with salt and pepper. Roast for 20 minutes until slightly crisp. Serve hot with extra olive oil.","cookTime":"60 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 32g · carbs 30g · fat 22g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Tenderize octopus well before roasting.","pairsWith":"greens · white wine · bread","trivia":"Lagareiro style refers to generous use of olive oil.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-amijoas-a-bulhao-pato","name":"Amêijoas à Bulhão Pato","nameAlt":"Clams with Garlic and Cilantro","aliases":[],"course":"starter","type":"seafood","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"snack","ingredients":"200g clams · 20ml olive oil · 10g garlic · 5g coriander · 50ml white wine · 2g salt · 1g black pepper","method":"Clean clams thoroughly under cold water. Heat olive oil in a pan and sauté garlic for 2 minutes. Add clams and white wine and cover. Cook on high heat for 5 minutes until clams open. Season with salt and pepper. Sprinkle chopped coriander on top. Serve immediately with bread.","cookTime":"10 min","kcal":260,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 6g · fat 14g","storage":"Best eaten fresh.","chefNotes":"Do not overcook clams to keep them tender.","pairsWith":"bread · white wine · lemon","trivia":"Named after a 19th-century Portuguese poet.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-ovos-moles","name":"Ovos Moles","nameAlt":"Sweet Egg Cream","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"confection","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"3 egg yolks · 100g sugar · 50ml water · 10g wafer shells","method":"Heat sugar and water in a pan until it forms a syrup at about 110°C. Beat egg yolks in a bowl. Slowly pour hot syrup into yolks while stirring constantly. Return mixture to low heat and cook gently for 5 minutes until thick. Spoon the mixture into wafer shells. Let cool before serving.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 50g · fat 8g","storage":"Store in a cool place for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Stir constantly to avoid curdling.","pairsWith":"coffee · tea · fruit","trivia":"This sweet comes from Aveiro and is shaped like shells.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-tripas-a-moda-do-porto","name":"Tripas à Moda do Porto","nameAlt":"Tripe & White Bean Stew","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"150g tripe · 50g beans · 50g sausage · 50g onion · 10g garlic · 30ml olive oil · 50g carrot · 2g salt · 1g paprika · 500ml water","method":"Clean tripe thoroughly and boil for 30 minutes. In a pot, sauté onion and garlic in olive oil for 5 minutes. Add sausage and cook for another 5 minutes. Stir in beans, carrot, and cooked tripe. Add water and simmer for 40 minutes on low heat. Season with salt and paprika. Serve hot.","cookTime":"80 min","kcal":550,"nutrition":"protein 35g · carbs 30g · fat 28g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Proper cleaning of tripe is essential for flavor.","pairsWith":"rice · bread · red wine","trivia":"Residents of Porto are nicknamed 'tripeiros' because of this dish.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-leite-creme","name":"Leite Creme","nameAlt":"Portuguese Creme Brulee","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"custard","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"family","ingredients":"200ml milk · 100g sugar · 2 egg yolks · 10g cornflour · 5g lemon peel · 5g cinnamon","method":"Heat milk with lemon peel and cinnamon until warm. In a bowl mix sugar, egg yolks, and cornflour until smooth. Slowly add warm milk while stirring. Return mixture to a pan and cook on low heat for 10 minutes until thick. Pour into dishes and let cool. Sprinkle sugar on top and caramelize with a hot iron or grill at 220°C for 3 minutes. Serve chilled or slightly warm.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":310,"nutrition":"protein 7g · carbs 45g · fat 10g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Caramelize just before serving for crisp top.","pairsWith":"coffee · biscuits · berries","trivia":"Leite creme is similar to creme brulee but often more fluid.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-bolo-de-arroz","name":"Bolo de Arroz","nameAlt":"Rice Flour Muffin","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"cake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g rice flour · 100g sugar · 2 eggs · 50ml milk · 30g butter · 5g baking powder","method":"Preheat oven to 180°C. Beat eggs and sugar until light and fluffy. Add melted butter and milk and mix well. Stir in rice flour and baking powder. Pour batter into molds. Bake for 25 minutes until golden and set. Cool before serving.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":340,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 50g · fat 14g","storage":"Store in airtight container for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Do not overmix to keep texture light.","pairsWith":"coffee · tea · jam","trivia":"These muffins are commonly found in Portuguese bakeries.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-sopa-de-pedra","name":"Sopa de Pedra","nameAlt":"Stone Soup","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"soup","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"100g beans · 50g pork shoulder · 50g sausage · 50g onion · 10g garlic · 30ml olive oil · 50g carrot · 2g salt · 1g bay leaf · 500ml water","method":"Soak beans overnight and cook for 40 minutes until tender. In a pot sauté onion and garlic in olive oil for 5 minutes. Add pork and sausage and cook for 10 minutes. Stir in carrot and cooked beans. Add water and bay leaf and simmer for 30 minutes. Season with salt. Serve hot.","cookTime":"80 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 45g · fat 22g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Legend says the soup was made from a stone to inspire sharing.","pairsWith":"bread · wine · olives","trivia":"The story behind this soup is a famous Portuguese folk tale.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-bacalhau-a-gomes-de-sa","name":"Bacalhau à Gomes de Sá","nameAlt":"Cod with Potatoes and Onions","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"bake","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"family","ingredients":"150g salted snoek (or salted cod) · 150g potatoes · 50g onion · 30ml olive oil · 1 egg · 5g parsley · 20g black olives · 2g salt · 1g black pepper","method":"Soak cod for 24 hours and boil for 10 minutes, then flake. Boil potatoes for 15 minutes and slice. Sauté onion in olive oil for 5 minutes. Combine cod, potatoes, and onion in a baking dish. Drizzle with olive oil and bake at 180°C for 20 minutes. Boil egg for 8 minutes and slice. Garnish dish with egg, parsley, and olives before serving.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 32g · carbs 35g · fat 28g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Layer gently to keep structure intact.","pairsWith":"salad · white wine · bread","trivia":"Named after a Porto merchant who popularized the recipe.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-migas-a-alentejana","name":"Migas à Alentejana","nameAlt":"Bread Mash with Garlic and Pork","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"bread","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"120g bread · 50g pork shoulder · 10g garlic · 30ml olive oil · 5g coriander · 2g salt · 1g black pepper · 100ml water","method":"Cut bread into chunks and moisten with water for 5 minutes. Heat olive oil in a pan and cook pork pieces on medium heat for 8 minutes until browned. Remove pork and set aside. In the same pan sauté garlic for 2 minutes. Add bread and mix while pressing into a mash. Stir continuously on medium heat for about 10 minutes until it forms a cohesive mixture. Return pork to the pan and mix well. Season with salt and pepper and garnish with coriander before serving.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 45g · fat 28g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use stale bread for best consistency.","pairsWith":"grilled meat · salad · red wine","trivia":"Migas were created as a way to use leftover bread in rural Portugal.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-bolo-rei","name":"Bolo Rei","nameAlt":"King Cake","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"cake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"150g flour · 50g sugar · 2 eggs · 50g butter · 50ml milk · 5g yeast · 50g candied fruit · 20g raisins","method":"Mix yeast with warm milk and let sit for 5 minutes. Combine flour, sugar, eggs, and butter in a bowl. Add yeast mixture and knead into dough. Fold in candied fruit and raisins. Let dough rise in a warm place for 1 hour. Shape into a ring and let rise again for 30 minutes. Bake at 180°C for 30 minutes until golden. Cool before serving.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 60g · fat 16g","storage":"Store in airtight container for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Traditionally includes a hidden bean and figurine.","pairsWith":"coffee · tea · port wine","trivia":"Bolo Rei is a festive cake eaten during Christmas in Portugal.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-bolo-de-bolacha","name":"Bolo de Bolacha","nameAlt":"Biscuit Cake","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"cake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"family","ingredients":"150g biscuits · 100g butter · 100g sugar · 1 egg · 50ml coffee","method":"Beat butter and sugar until creamy. Add egg and mix until smooth. Dip biscuits quickly in coffee. Layer biscuits and cream alternately in a dish. Repeat layers until ingredients are used. Chill in fridge for at least 2 hours. Slice and serve cold.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 50g · fat 28g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use strong coffee for deeper flavor.","pairsWith":"espresso · milk · berries","trivia":"This no-bake cake is a favorite homemade dessert in Portugal.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-toucinho-do-ceu","name":"Toucinho do Céu","nameAlt":"Almond Egg Cake","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"cake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"100g almonds · 100g sugar · 3 egg yolks · 2 egg whites · 30g butter · 10g flour","method":"Preheat oven to 180°C. Grind almonds finely. Beat egg yolks and sugar until thick. Fold in ground almonds and melted butter. Whisk egg whites to stiff peaks and fold gently into mixture. Add flour and mix lightly. Pour into a greased pan. Bake for 30 minutes until set. Cool before slicing.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":450,"nutrition":"protein 10g · carbs 40g · fat 28g","storage":"Store in airtight container for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Do not overbake to keep moist texture.","pairsWith":"coffee · tea · fruit","trivia":"Despite its name, this cake contains no pork.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-pao-de-lo","name":"Pão de Ló","nameAlt":"Portuguese Sponge Cake","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"cake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"100g sugar · 4 eggs · 100g flour","method":"Preheat oven to 180°C. Beat eggs and sugar for 10 minutes until very fluffy. Gently fold in flour. Pour batter into a lined cake tin. Bake for 25 minutes until lightly golden. Remove and cool before serving.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 7g · carbs 55g · fat 5g","storage":"Store in airtight container for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Do not deflate batter when folding flour.","pairsWith":"tea · coffee · jam","trivia":"Pão de Ló dates back to the 18th century.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-queijo-assado","name":"Queijo Assado","nameAlt":"Baked Cheese","aliases":[],"course":"starter","type":"bake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"snack","ingredients":"150g cheese · 10ml olive oil · 5g oregano · 1g black pepper","method":"Preheat oven to 180°C. Place cheese in a small baking dish. Drizzle with olive oil and sprinkle oregano and pepper. Bake for 15 minutes until soft and slightly golden. Serve warm with bread.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":400,"nutrition":"protein 18g · carbs 2g · fat 34g","storage":"Best eaten fresh.","chefNotes":"Use a firm cheese that melts well.","pairsWith":"bread · wine · olives","trivia":"Baked cheese is a simple yet popular appetizer in Portugal.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-salame-de-chocolate","name":"Salame de Chocolate","nameAlt":"Chocolate Biscuit Salami","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"confection","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"party","ingredients":"150g biscuits · 100g dark chocolate · 100g sugar · 50g butter · 1 egg","method":"Melt chocolate and butter together over low heat. Mix in sugar and egg until smooth. Crush biscuits and fold into mixture. Shape into a log using cling film. Chill in fridge for 2 hours until firm. Slice and serve.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 55g · fat 30g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 5 days.","chefNotes":"Chill thoroughly for clean slices.","pairsWith":"coffee · milk · berries","trivia":"Despite its name, this dessert contains no meat.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-favas-guisadas","name":"Favas Guisadas","nameAlt":"Stewed Broad Beans","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"spring","ingredients":"150g broad beans · 50g pork shoulder · 50g sausage · 50g onion · 10g garlic · 30ml olive oil · 5g coriander · 2g salt · 1g black pepper · 200ml water","method":"Heat olive oil in a pot and sauté onion and garlic for 5 minutes. Add pork and sausage and cook for 10 minutes. Stir in broad beans and water. Simmer on low heat for 30 minutes until beans are tender. Season with salt and pepper. Garnish with coriander before serving.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":540,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 40g · fat 28g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Fresh beans give the best flavor.","pairsWith":"bread · wine · salad","trivia":"Favas are traditionally eaten in spring festivals.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-bifinhos-com-cogumelos","name":"Bifinhos com Cogumelos","nameAlt":"Steak with Mushrooms","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"saute","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 100g mushrooms · 20ml olive oil · 10g garlic · 50ml cream · 2g salt · 1g black pepper","method":"Slice beef thinly. Heat oil in a pan and cook beef on high heat for 3 minutes. Remove and set aside. In the same pan sauté mushrooms and garlic for 5 minutes. Add cream and simmer for 5 minutes. Return beef to the pan and heat through. Season with salt and pepper. Serve hot.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 32g · carbs 10g · fat 38g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Do not overcook beef to keep it tender.","pairsWith":"rice · fries · red wine","trivia":"A simple home-style Portuguese dish.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-arroz-de-pato","name":"Arroz de Pato","nameAlt":"Duck Rice","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"rice","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"family","ingredients":"150g duck · 100g rice · 50g chorizo · 50g onion · 10g garlic · 30ml olive oil · 500ml water · 2g salt · 1g black pepper","method":"Cook duck in water for 40 minutes until tender, then shred. In a pan sauté onion and garlic in olive oil for 5 minutes. Add rice and toast for 2 minutes. Pour in duck cooking liquid and simmer for 15 minutes. Stir in shredded duck and sliced chorizo. Transfer to a baking dish and bake at 180°C for 15 minutes until slightly crisp. Serve hot.","cookTime":"60 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 35g · carbs 45g · fat 28g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use duck broth for deeper flavor.","pairsWith":"salad · red wine · bread","trivia":"Arroz de pato is a beloved festive dish in Portugal.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-cavacas","name":"Cavacas","nameAlt":"Portuguese Iced Cakes","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"pastry","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"100g flour · 100g sugar · 3 eggs · 20ml lemon juice · 50g icing sugar","method":"Preheat oven to 180°C. Beat eggs and sugar until fluffy. Fold in flour and lemon juice. Spoon batter onto a baking tray. Bake for 15 minutes until set. Mix icing sugar with a little water to form glaze. Spread glaze over cooled cakes. Let set before serving.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":310,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 55g · fat 6g","storage":"Store in airtight container for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Let icing dry fully for best texture.","pairsWith":"tea · coffee · fruit","trivia":"Cavacas are popular during Portuguese festivals.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-chanfana","name":"Chanfana","nameAlt":"Goat Stew in Wine","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"200g goat meat · 200ml red wine · 50g onion · 10g garlic · 30ml olive oil · 2g salt · 1g bay leaf · 1g black pepper","method":"Preheat oven to 160°C. Place goat meat in a clay pot with onion, garlic, and bay leaf. Pour over red wine and olive oil. Season with salt and pepper. Cover and cook in oven for 2 hours until tender. Stir occasionally during cooking. Serve hot.","cookTime":"120 min","kcal":650,"nutrition":"protein 40g · carbs 6g · fat 45g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Slow cooking enhances the wine flavor.","pairsWith":"bread · greens · red wine","trivia":"Chanfana is traditionally cooked in wood-fired ovens.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-broa-de-milho","name":"Broa de Milho","nameAlt":"Cornbread Loaf","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"bread","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g maize flour · 50g wheat flour · 5g yeast · 5g salt · 150ml water","method":"Mix maize flour, wheat flour, yeast, and salt in a bowl. Add warm water and mix into a dough. Knead lightly for 5 minutes until combined. Let the dough rise in a warm place for 1 hour. Shape into a round loaf. Bake at 200°C for 40 minutes until crusty and cooked through. Cool before slicing.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 7g · carbs 60g · fat 3g","storage":"Store in a bread box for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use coarse maize flour for authentic texture.","pairsWith":"butter · cheese · soup","trivia":"Broa de milho is a staple bread in northern Portugal.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-lulas-recheadas","name":"Lulas Recheadas","nameAlt":"Stuffed Squid","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stuffed","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"family","ingredients":"200g squid · 50g rice · 50g onion · 10g garlic · 30ml olive oil · 50g tomato · 200ml fish stock · 2g salt · 1g black pepper","method":"Clean squid and set aside. Cook rice in water for 10 minutes until partially tender. In a pan sauté onion and garlic in olive oil for 5 minutes. Add chopped tomato and cook for another 5 minutes. Mix rice with the onion mixture and season. Stuff squid with the mixture and secure openings. Place in a pan with fish stock and simmer on low heat for 25 minutes until tender. Serve hot with sauce.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 35g · fat 18g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Do not overstuff squid to prevent bursting.","pairsWith":"rice · salad · white wine","trivia":"Stuffed squid is popular along Portugal’s coastline.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-peixinhos-da-horta","name":"Peixinhos da Horta","nameAlt":"Green Bean Tempura","aliases":[],"course":"starter","type":"fritter","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"snack","ingredients":"150g green beans · 100g flour · 1 egg · 200ml sunflower oil · 2g salt","method":"Trim green beans and blanch in boiling water for 3 minutes. Drain and pat dry. Prepare a batter with flour, egg, and a little water. Heat oil to 180°C. Dip beans in batter and fry for about 3 minutes until golden. Remove and drain on paper. Sprinkle with salt and serve warm.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":320,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 35g · fat 18g","storage":"Best eaten fresh.","chefNotes":"Serve immediately for crisp texture.","pairsWith":"dip sauce · salad · beer","trivia":"This dish influenced Japanese tempura through Portuguese traders.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-ameijoas-na-cataplana","name":"Amêijoas na Cataplana","nameAlt":"Clams in Cataplana Pot","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"200g clams · 50g onion · 10g garlic · 50g tomato · 30ml olive oil · 50ml white wine · 5g coriander · 2g salt · 1g black pepper","method":"Clean clams thoroughly. Heat olive oil in a pot and sauté onion and garlic for 5 minutes. Add tomato and cook for another 5 minutes. Add clams and white wine, then cover tightly. Cook on high heat for 5 minutes until clams open. Season with salt and pepper and sprinkle coriander. Serve immediately.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 8g · fat 16g","storage":"Best eaten fresh.","chefNotes":"Use a sealed pot to trap steam for proper cooking.","pairsWith":"bread · white wine · lemon","trivia":"Cataplana is both the dish and the traditional copper cooking vessel.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-encharcada","name":"Encharcada","nameAlt":"Egg Yolk Syrup Dessert","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"confection","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"4 egg yolks · 100g sugar · 100ml water · 5g cinnamon","method":"Heat sugar and water to make a syrup at about 110°C. Beat egg yolks lightly in a bowl. Slowly pour hot syrup into yolks while stirring. Return mixture to low heat and cook gently for 5 minutes until thickened. Pour into a dish and sprinkle cinnamon. Let cool before serving.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":290,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 45g · fat 8g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Cook gently to avoid scrambling eggs.","pairsWith":"coffee · tea · biscuits","trivia":"Encharcada is a conventual sweet from southern Portugal.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-sopa-de-cacao","name":"Sopa de Cação","nameAlt":"Dogfish Soup","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"soup","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"150g hake (or dogfish) · 100g bread · 10g garlic · 5g coriander · 30ml olive oil · 500ml water · 2g salt · 1g paprika","method":"Bring water to a boil and cook dogfish for 10 minutes. Remove fish and flake into pieces. In another pot sauté garlic in olive oil for 2 minutes. Add cooking water and bring to a simmer. Add bread pieces and cook for 5 minutes until softened. Stir in fish and coriander. Season with salt and paprika. Serve hot.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":360,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 30g · fat 14g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use firm white fish if dogfish is unavailable.","pairsWith":"wine · olives · salad","trivia":"This soup is a specialty of the Alentejo region.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-pasteis-de-nata","name":"Pastéis de Nata","nameAlt":"custard tarts","aliases":["Pastel de Nata"],"course":"dessert","type":"tart","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"bakery","ingredients":"120g puff pastry · 120ml whole milk · 80g sugar · 2 egg yolks · 10g wheat flour · 2g cinnamon stick · 5ml lemon peel strips · 2g ground cinnamon","method":"Preheat the oven to 250°C and place a metal tray inside to heat. Roll the puff pastry tightly and slice into rounds, then press each into a tart mold. In a saucepan, heat the milk with lemon peel and a cinnamon stick until just steaming for about 5 minutes. In a bowl, whisk sugar with flour, then gradually add the hot milk while stirring. Return the mixture to medium heat and cook for 3–4 minutes until slightly thickened. Remove from heat and whisk in egg yolks quickly. Strain the custard and fill pastry shells to just below the rim. Bake at 250°C for 10–12 minutes until blistered on top. Dust with ground cinnamon and cool slightly before serving.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":290,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 34g · fat 15g","storage":"Keep chilled and reheat briefly before serving.","chefNotes":"Chill the custard for 2 hours before baking for best texture.","pairsWith":"espresso · port wine · fresh berries","trivia":"These iconic tarts originated in Lisbon’s Belém district.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-caldo-verde","name":"Caldo Verde","nameAlt":"kale & potato soup","aliases":[],"course":"starter","type":"soup","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g potatoes · 50g kale · 30g chouriço sausage (Portuguese cured sausage) · 15ml olive oil · 500ml water · 2g salt · 1g black pepper","method":"Peel and chop the potatoes, then boil in water for 15 minutes until soft. Blend the potatoes with the cooking liquid until smooth. Return to heat and simmer for 5 minutes. Finely shred the kale and add to the soup. Slice the chouriço thinly and add to the pot. Drizzle olive oil and simmer for another 5 minutes. Season with salt and pepper to taste. Serve hot with crusty bread.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":320,"nutrition":"protein 12g · carbs 35g · fat 14g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Let the soup rest 1 hour before serving for deeper flavor.","pairsWith":"cornbread · red wine · olives","trivia":"A staple from northern Portugal, especially Minho.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-frango-piri-piri","name":"Frango Piri-Piri","nameAlt":"peri-peri chicken","aliases":["Frango no Churrasco"],"course":"main","type":"grilled","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"bbq","ingredients":"300g chicken leg · 20ml olive oil · 10g garlic · 5g chili flakes · 10ml lemon juice · 2g paprika · 2g salt","method":"Preheat a grill to medium-high heat around 200°C. Mix olive oil, garlic, chili flakes, lemon juice, paprika, and salt into a marinade. Rub the marinade over the chicken thoroughly. Grill the chicken for 20 minutes, turning occasionally. Brush extra marinade while cooking. Ensure internal temperature reaches safe level. Rest the chicken for 5 minutes before serving. Serve with extra sauce on the side.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":450,"nutrition":"protein 35g · carbs 3g · fat 32g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Marinate overnight for best flavor.","pairsWith":"chips · salad · beer","trivia":"Piri-piri sauce traces back to African chili peppers.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-sardinhas-assadas","name":"Sardinhas Assadas","nameAlt":"grilled sardines","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"grilled","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"180g sardines · 10ml olive oil · 2g sea salt · 5ml lemon juice","method":"Preheat grill to high heat around 220°C. Clean and pat dry the sardines. Brush lightly with olive oil. Sprinkle generously with sea salt. Grill for 3–4 minutes per side. Avoid overcooking to keep flesh moist. Drizzle lemon juice just before serving. Serve immediately while hot.","cookTime":"10 min","kcal":280,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 0g · fat 20g","storage":"Best eaten fresh; refrigerate max 1 day.","chefNotes":"No resting needed; serve immediately.","pairsWith":"bread · wine · salad","trivia":"Popular during Lisbon’s June festivals.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-francesinha","name":"Francesinha","nameAlt":"Porto sandwich","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"sandwich","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"street-food","ingredients":"120g bread · 100g steak · 50g ham · 80g sausage · 50g cheese · 200ml beer sauce · 10g butter","method":"Cook the steak and sausage in a pan over medium heat for 10 minutes. Layer bread, steak, ham, and sausage. Top with another slice of bread. Cover entirely with cheese slices. Melt butter in a pan and toast sandwich lightly. Pour hot beer sauce over the sandwich. Place in oven at 200°C for 10 minutes until cheese melts. Serve with fries.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":850,"nutrition":"protein 45g · carbs 60g · fat 45g","storage":"Consume fresh; not ideal for storage.","chefNotes":"Sauce can simmer for 2 hours for depth.","pairsWith":"fries · beer · salad","trivia":"A rich specialty from Porto inspired by French cuisine.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-bifana","name":"Bifana","nameAlt":"pork sandwich","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"sandwich","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"street-food","ingredients":"120g pork loin (or leg) · 80g bread roll · 10ml white wine · 5g garlic · 5ml olive oil · 2g paprika · 2g salt","method":"Slice the pork loin into thin steaks. Heat olive oil in a pan over medium heat, add garlic and cook 1 minute. Add the pork and cook 5–6 minutes. Deglaze with white wine and simmer 5 minutes. Season with paprika and salt. Slice the bread roll open. Fill with the pork and juices. Serve hot immediately.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":400,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 35g · fat 18g","storage":"Store filling separately up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Marinate pork for 4 hours before cooking.","pairsWith":"mustard · beer · chips","trivia":"A popular quick snack across Portugal.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-feijoada","name":"Feijoada","nameAlt":"bean & meat stew","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"family-meal","ingredients":"150g black beans · 100g pork shoulder · 50g sausage · 30g onion · 10ml olive oil · 2g garlic · 2g bay leaf","method":"Soak beans overnight, then drain. Heat olive oil and sauté onion and garlic for 5 minutes. Add pork and sausage and brown for 10 minutes. Add beans and water to cover. Simmer for 45 minutes until beans are tender. Season with salt and bay leaf. Continue cooking 10 minutes more. Serve hot with rice.","cookTime":"65 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 35g · carbs 40g · fat 25g","storage":"Keeps refrigerated for 3 days.","chefNotes":"Beans must soak overnight before cooking.","pairsWith":"rice · greens · wine","trivia":"Different regions have their own versions of feijoada.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-arroz-de-marisco","name":"Arroz de Marisco","nameAlt":"seafood rice","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"rice","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"special","ingredients":"100g rice · 150g mixed seafood · 30g tomato · 10ml olive oil · 5g garlic · 500ml fish stock · 2g coriander","method":"Heat olive oil and sauté garlic for 2 minutes. Add tomato and cook for 5 minutes. Stir in rice and coat well. Pour in fish stock and bring to boil. Add seafood and simmer 15 minutes. Stir occasionally to keep creamy texture. Season and finish with coriander. Serve immediately.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 55g · fat 12g","storage":"Eat fresh; rice can harden when stored.","chefNotes":"Use fresh seafood for best taste.","pairsWith":"white wine · bread · salad","trivia":"Often enjoyed along Portugal’s coast.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-caldeirada","name":"Caldeirada","nameAlt":"fish stew","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"family-meal","ingredients":"200g mixed fish · 100g potatoes · 50g onion · 10ml olive oil · 30g tomato · 200ml fish stock · 2g paprika","method":"Slice potatoes and onion thinly. Layer them in a pot with fish pieces. Add tomato and paprika. Drizzle olive oil and pour in stock. Simmer gently for 30 minutes. Avoid stirring too much to keep fish intact. Season to taste. Serve hot.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 30g · fat 10g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Let sit 1 hour after cooking for flavors to meld.","pairsWith":"bread · wine · salad","trivia":"A fisherman’s dish made with catch of the day.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-leitao","name":"Leitão","nameAlt":"roast suckling pig","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"roast","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"300g pork (or suckling pig) · 10g garlic · 5g salt · 2g pepper · 10ml olive oil","method":"Preheat oven to 200°C. Rub pig with garlic, salt, and pepper. Place in roasting tray and drizzle oil. Roast for 60 minutes, turning occasionally. Increase heat to 220°C for crisp skin for 15 minutes. Rest meat 10 minutes before slicing. Serve with sides.","cookTime":"75 min","kcal":700,"nutrition":"protein 45g · carbs 2g · fat 60g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for 2 days.","chefNotes":"Marinate for 12 hours before roasting.","pairsWith":"chips · salad · wine","trivia":"A specialty of Bairrada region.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-ginjinha","name":"Ginjinha","nameAlt":"cherry liqueur","aliases":["Ginja"],"course":"drink","type":"liqueur","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"digestif","ingredients":"100g sour cherries · 50g sugar · 100ml aguardente","method":"Wash and pit the cherries. Place in a jar with sugar. Pour over aguardente to cover. Seal and store in a cool dark place. Shake occasionally over several days. Strain liquid before serving. Serve chilled in small glasses.","cookTime":"10 min","kcal":180,"nutrition":"protein 0g · carbs 20g · fat 0g","storage":"Store sealed for months.","chefNotes":"Infuse for at least 1 month before drinking.","pairsWith":"chocolate · pastries · coffee","trivia":"Often served in Lisbon bars in chocolate cups.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-queijadas","name":"Queijadas","nameAlt":"sweet cheese tarts","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"tart","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"bakery","ingredients":"100g fresh cheese · 80g sugar · 1 egg · 30g flour · 5g butter · 2g cinnamon","method":"Preheat oven to 180°C. Mix cheese, sugar, and egg until smooth. Stir in flour and melted butter. Add cinnamon and combine well. Pour mixture into small molds. Bake for 25 minutes until golden. Cool slightly before serving.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":260,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 30g · fat 12g","storage":"Keep refrigerated up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Chill batter 1 hour before baking.","pairsWith":"tea · coffee · fruit","trivia":"Famous in Sintra with centuries-old origins.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-moules-frites","name":"Moules-Frites","nameAlt":"Mussels with Fries","aliases":["Mosselen met friet"],"course":"main","type":"seafood","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"summer","ingredients":"300g mussels · 150g potato · 50g onion · 10ml olive oil · 100ml white wine · 5g garlic · 10g parsley · 2g salt","method":"Clean the mussels thoroughly, discarding any that do not close when tapped. In a large pot, sauté onion and garlic in olive oil until fragrant, then add white wine and bring to a simmer. Add the mussels, cover, and cook until they open fully, shaking the pot occasionally. Fry the potatoes separately until golden and crisp. Serve the mussels with broth and fries on the side.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 35g · carbs 50g · fat 20g","storage":"Best eaten fresh","chefNotes":"Discard unopened mussels after cooking","pairsWith":"fries · salad · mayonnaise · bread dishes","trivia":"Considered Belgium's national dish","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-carbonnade-flamande","name":"Carbonnade Flamande","nameAlt":"Flemish Beef Stew with Beer","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"winter","ingredients":"180g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 100g onion · 150ml beer · 10g mustard · 1 slice bread · 10ml olive oil · 2g salt","method":"Brown the beef in olive oil until well seared, then remove and set aside. In the same pot, cook onions until softened and slightly caramelized. Return the beef and add beer, mustard, and bread to thicken the sauce. Simmer slowly until the meat becomes tender and the sauce rich. Serve hot after resting briefly.","cookTime":"120 min","kcal":650,"nutrition":"protein 40g · carbs 35g · fat 30g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 4 days","chefNotes":"Use dark beer for deeper flavor","pairsWith":"fries · mashed potatoes · red cabbage · salad","trivia":"A traditional Flemish comfort dish","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-belgian-waffles","name":"Belgian Waffles","nameAlt":"Light Crisp Waffles","aliases":["Gaufres","Wafels"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"dessert","ingredients":"120g flour · 200ml milk · 1 egg · 20g sugar · 20g butter · 5g yeast","method":"Mix flour, milk, egg, sugar, butter, and yeast into a smooth batter and allow it to rise slightly. Preheat a waffle iron until hot. Pour batter into the iron and cook until golden and crisp. Remove carefully and allow to cool slightly. Serve warm with toppings.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 45g · fat 15g","storage":"Best eaten fresh","chefNotes":"Do not overfill the waffle iron","pairsWith":"fruit · cream · chocolate · syrup","trivia":"Belgium is famous worldwide for its waffles","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-stoofvlees","name":"Stoofvlees","nameAlt":"Flemish Beef and Beer Stew","aliases":["Carbonnade Flamande"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"winter","ingredients":"180g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 100g onion · 150ml beer · 10g mustard · 10ml olive oil · 2g salt","method":"Brown the beef in oil until sealed, then set aside. Cook onions until soft and slightly caramelized. Add beer and mustard, then return the beef to the pot. Simmer slowly until the meat becomes tender and the sauce thickens. Serve hot with sauce spooned over.","cookTime":"120 min","kcal":650,"nutrition":"protein 40g · carbs 30g · fat 32g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 4 days","chefNotes":"Slow cooking is essential for tenderness","pairsWith":"fries · mashed potatoes · vegetables · salad","trivia":"Another name for the classic Flemish stew","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-waterzooi","name":"Waterzooi","nameAlt":"Creamy Chicken or Fish Stew","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"family meal","ingredients":"200g chicken · 100g potato · 50g carrot · 50g leek · 150ml cream · 10ml olive oil · 2g salt","method":"Cook the chicken gently in water or stock until tender, then remove and set aside. Add vegetables to the broth and cook until soft. Return the chicken and stir in cream to enrich the broth. Simmer gently until flavors combine without boiling. Serve warm as a creamy stew.","cookTime":"60 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 25g · fat 30g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 3 days","chefNotes":"Do not boil after adding cream","pairsWith":"bread dishes · potatoes · salad · vegetables","trivia":"Originated in Ghent","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-speculoos","name":"Speculoos","nameAlt":"Spiced Shortcrust Biscuits","aliases":["Speculaas"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"festive","ingredients":"120g flour · 50g butter · 50g sugar · 5g spice mix · 5ml milk","method":"Mix flour, butter, sugar, spices, and milk into a firm dough. Roll out and cut into shapes. Bake until crisp and golden. Allow to cool completely to harden. Serve as crunchy biscuits.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 5g · carbs 40g · fat 15g","storage":"Store in airtight container up to 1 week","chefNotes":"Chill dough before rolling","pairsWith":"coffee alternatives · cream · desserts","trivia":"Traditionally eaten during Saint Nicholas celebrations","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-croquettes-aux-crevettes","name":"Croquettes aux Crevettes","nameAlt":"Shrimp Croquettes","aliases":[],"course":"starter","type":"seafood","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"starter","ingredients":"120g shrimp · 30g flour · 200ml milk · 20g butter · 1 egg · 50g breadcrumbs · 100ml oil","method":"Prepare a thick sauce by melting butter, adding flour, and whisking in milk. Stir in shrimp and allow to cool until firm. Shape into croquettes, coat in egg and breadcrumbs. Fry in hot oil until golden and crisp. Drain and serve immediately.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":450,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 30g · fat 25g","storage":"Best eaten fresh","chefNotes":"Chill mixture before shaping","pairsWith":"salad · lemon wedges · vegetables · sauce","trivia":"A classic Belgian starter","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-filet-americain","name":"Filet Américain","nameAlt":"Seasoned Raw Beef Tartare","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"casual","ingredients":"150g raw beef · 1 egg yolk · 10g mustard · 10g onion · 5g capers · 2g salt","method":"Finely chop or mince high-quality beef and place in a bowl. Mix with egg yolk, mustard, onion, capers, and seasoning until well combined. Adjust seasoning to taste. Shape or spread onto a plate. Serve immediately while fresh.","cookTime":"10 min","kcal":400,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 5g · fat 28g","storage":"Consume immediately","chefNotes":"Use only very fresh beef","pairsWith":"fries · salad · pickles · bread dishes","trivia":"Belgium's version of steak tartare","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-endive-au-gratin","name":"Endive au Gratin","nameAlt":"Baked Endive with Ham and Cheese","aliases":["Chicons au gratin"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"family meal","ingredients":"150g endive · 100g ham · 150ml milk · 20g butter · 20g cheese · 15g flour · 2g salt","method":"Cook the endive until tender and drain excess moisture. Wrap each piece in ham and place in a baking dish. Prepare a béchamel by melting butter, adding flour, and whisking in milk. Pour the sauce over the dish and top with cheese. Bake until golden and bubbling.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 20g · fat 35g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 3 days","chefNotes":"Drain endive well to avoid bitterness","pairsWith":"potatoes · salad · vegetables · bread dishes","trivia":"A beloved Belgian comfort dish","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-vol-au-vent","name":"Vol-au-Vent","nameAlt":"Chicken and Mushroom Puff Pastry","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"family meal","ingredients":"150g chicken · 80g mushroom · 150ml cream · 1 pastry shell · 20g butter · 10g flour · 2g salt","method":"Cook the chicken until tender, then cut into pieces. Sauté mushrooms in butter until soft. Add flour and stir before gradually adding cream to form a sauce. Combine chicken and mushrooms in the sauce and simmer gently. Fill pastry shells and serve warm.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":550,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 30g · fat 35g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 2 days","chefNotes":"Keep pastry crisp by filling just before serving","pairsWith":"fries · salad · vegetables · greens","trivia":"A classic Belgian comfort dish","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-belgian-fries","name":"Belgian Fries","nameAlt":"Double-Fried Potatoes","aliases":["Frites"],"course":"side","type":"vegetarian","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"street food","ingredients":"200g potato · 200ml oil · 2g salt","method":"Cut potatoes into thick strips and rinse to remove starch. Fry at a lower temperature until soft, then remove and cool. Fry again at higher heat until golden and crisp. Drain excess oil and season with salt. Serve immediately while hot.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":400,"nutrition":"protein 5g · carbs 50g · fat 20g","storage":"Best eaten fresh","chefNotes":"Double frying creates perfect texture","pairsWith":"mussels · steak · sauces · burgers","trivia":"Belgium claims origin of fries","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-liege-waffles","name":"Liege Waffles","nameAlt":"Caramelized Sugar Waffles","aliases":["Gaufres de Liège","Luikse wafels"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"street food","ingredients":"120g flour · 1 egg · 50ml milk · 40g butter · 30g pearl sugar · 5g yeast · 2g salt","method":"Mix flour, yeast, milk, egg, butter, and salt into a soft dough and allow it to rise until doubled. Knead in pearl sugar just before cooking so it stays intact. Divide into portions and cook in a hot waffle iron until golden and caramelized. The sugar will melt and create a crisp coating. Serve warm for best texture.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 55g · fat 18g","storage":"Best eaten fresh","chefNotes":"Pearl sugar creates the signature crunch","pairsWith":"fruit · cream · chocolate · nuts","trivia":"Distinct from Brussels waffles due to caramelized sugar","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-brussels-waffles","name":"Brussels Waffles","nameAlt":"Light Rectangular Waffles","aliases":["Gaufres de Bruxelles"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"dessert","ingredients":"120g flour · 200ml milk · 1 egg · 20g sugar · 20g butter · 5g yeast","method":"Prepare a batter by mixing flour, milk, egg, sugar, butter, and yeast until smooth and airy. Allow it to rest briefly to develop structure. Pour into a hot waffle iron and cook until light and crisp. Remove carefully and allow to cool slightly. Serve with toppings as desired.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 45g · fat 15g","storage":"Best eaten fresh","chefNotes":"Do not press waffle iron too tightly","pairsWith":"fruit · cream · chocolate · syrup","trivia":"Known for its light and airy texture","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-rabbit-in-beer","name":"Rabbit in Beer","nameAlt":"Braised Rabbit with Beer Sauce","aliases":["Lapin à la bière"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"200g rabbit · 100g onion · 150ml beer · 10ml olive oil · 5g mustard · 2g salt","method":"Brown the rabbit pieces in oil until sealed and lightly golden. Remove and sauté onions until soft. Return the rabbit and add beer and mustard, then simmer gently. Cook until the meat is tender and the sauce thickens. Allow to rest briefly before serving.","cookTime":"90 min","kcal":550,"nutrition":"protein 35g · carbs 20g · fat 30g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 3 days","chefNotes":"Use dark beer for richer flavor","pairsWith":"potatoes · red cabbage · salad · vegetables","trivia":"A rustic Belgian countryside dish","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-gentse-waterzooi","name":"Gentse Waterzooi","nameAlt":"Creamy Chicken & Vegetable Stew","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"family meal","ingredients":"200g chicken · 100g potato · 50g carrot · 50g leek · 150ml cream · 10ml olive oil · 2g salt","method":"Cook the chicken in water or stock until tender, then remove and set aside. Add vegetables to the broth and cook until soft. Return the chicken and stir in cream to enrich the stew. Simmer gently without boiling. Serve warm with the creamy broth.","cookTime":"60 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 25g · fat 30g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 3 days","chefNotes":"Avoid boiling after adding cream","pairsWith":"bread dishes · potatoes · salad · greens","trivia":"A regional specialty from Ghent","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-chicons-au-gratin","name":"Chicons au Gratin","nameAlt":"Endive Wrapped in Ham with Cheese Sauce","aliases":["Endive au Gratin"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"family meal","ingredients":"150g endive · 100g ham · 150ml milk · 20g butter · 20g cheese · 15g flour · 2g salt","method":"Cook the endive until tender and remove excess moisture. Wrap each piece in ham and place in a baking dish. Prepare a béchamel by melting butter, adding flour, and whisking in milk. Pour over the dish and top with cheese. Bake until golden and bubbling.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 20g · fat 35g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 3 days","chefNotes":"Drain endive well to avoid bitterness","pairsWith":"potatoes · salad · vegetables · bread dishes","trivia":"A staple Belgian comfort meal","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-boulets-a-la-liegeoise","name":"Boulets à la Liégeoise","nameAlt":"Meatballs in Sweet-Sour Sauce","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"comfort food","ingredients":"150g minced beef · 30g breadcrumbs · 1 egg · 100g onion · 50g syrup · 10ml vinegar · 2g salt","method":"Mix minced beef with breadcrumbs, egg, and seasoning, then shape into balls. Fry until browned on all sides. In a separate pan, cook onions and add syrup and vinegar to create a sauce. Add meatballs and simmer until coated and heated through. Serve warm with sauce.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":550,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 35g · fat 30g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 3 days","chefNotes":"Balance sweet and sour flavors carefully","pairsWith":"fries · mashed potatoes · salad · vegetables","trivia":"A specialty from Liège","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-stoemp","name":"Stoemp","nameAlt":"Mashed Potatoes with Vegetables","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"vegetarian","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"family meal","ingredients":"200g potato · 50g carrot · 50g leek · 20g butter · 50ml milk · 2g salt","method":"Boil the potatoes and vegetables until soft. Drain and mash together until smooth but slightly textured. Add butter and milk to enrich the mixture. Season and mix well. Serve warm as a side dish.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 45g · fat 12g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 3 days","chefNotes":"Do not overmix to keep texture","pairsWith":"sausages · meatballs · stews · gravy","trivia":"A traditional Belgian comfort side","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-speculoos-spread","name":"Speculoos Spread","nameAlt":"Spiced Biscuit Spread","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"snack","ingredients":"100g biscuits · 50ml milk · 20g sugar · 10g butter","method":"Crush the biscuits into fine crumbs. Mix with milk, sugar, and butter until smooth and spreadable. Blend if needed for a finer texture. Adjust consistency as desired. Serve as a spread.","cookTime":"10 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 4g · carbs 50g · fat 15g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 1 week","chefNotes":"Blend longer for smoother texture","pairsWith":"bread dishes · pancakes · waffles · fruit","trivia":"Inspired by speculoos biscuits","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-jambon-d-ardenne","name":"Jambon d'Ardenne","nameAlt":"Cured Ham from Ardennes","aliases":[],"course":"starter","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"appetizer","ingredients":"120g cured ham · 10g butter","method":"Slice the cured ham thinly and arrange on a plate. Allow it to come to room temperature to enhance flavor. Add small amounts of butter if desired. Serve as is without further cooking. Enjoy immediately.","cookTime":"5 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 2g · fat 25g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 5 days","chefNotes":"Serve at room temperature","pairsWith":"bread dishes · pickles · cheese · salad","trivia":"A protected regional product","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-chocolate-mousse","name":"Chocolate Mousse","nameAlt":"Light Chocolate Dessert","aliases":["Mousse au chocolat"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"dessert","ingredients":"100g chocolate · 2 egg · 20g sugar · 20g butter","method":"Melt the chocolate and butter gently until smooth. Separate eggs and mix yolks into the chocolate. Beat egg whites with sugar until stiff peaks form. Fold the whites into the chocolate mixture carefully. Chill until set before serving.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":400,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 30g · fat 28g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 2 days","chefNotes":"Fold gently to keep it airy","pairsWith":"fruit · cream · nuts","trivia":"Belgium is famous for its chocolate","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"belgium-kriek-beer","name":"Kriek Beer","nameAlt":"Cherry-Flavored Belgian Beer","aliases":[],"course":"drink","type":"beverage","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Belgium","occasion":"social","ingredients":"250ml cherry beer","method":"Pour the beer into a clean glass at a slight angle to control foam. Allow the head to settle naturally. Serve chilled for best flavor. Do not overpour to preserve carbonation. Enjoy immediately.","cookTime":"5 min","kcal":180,"nutrition":"protein 1g · carbs 15g · fat 0g","storage":"Store sealed at room temperature","chefNotes":"Serve well chilled","pairsWith":"cheese · desserts · meat dishes","trivia":"A traditional fruit beer from Belgium","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-stamppot","name":"Stamppot","nameAlt":"Mashed Potatoes with Vegetables","aliases":["Hutspot","Boerenkoolstamppot"],"course":"main","type":"mash","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"300g potatoes · 150g kale · 50g smoked sausage · 10g butter · 50ml milk · 3g salt","method":"Peel the potatoes and cut into chunks, then boil in salted water for about 20 minutes until tender. Add the kale in the last 10 minutes of cooking. Drain everything well and return to the pot. Mash the potatoes and kale together until smooth. Heat the milk and butter gently and mix into the mash. Slice and warm the smoked sausage separately. Season with salt to taste. Serve hot with sausage on top.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 18g · carbs 55g · fat 25g","storage":"Refrigerate in a sealed container for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use waxy potatoes for a smoother texture.","pairsWith":"mustard · pickles · rye bread","trivia":"Stamppot dates back to the 17th century and is a Dutch winter staple.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-bitterballen","name":"Bitterballen","nameAlt":"Crispy Meat Snack Balls","aliases":["Bitterballen snack"],"course":"starter","type":"fried snack","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"pub","ingredients":"100g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 20g flour · 20g butter · 100ml beef stock · 1 egg · 50g breadcrumbs · 5g salt","method":"Cook the beef and shred it finely. Melt butter and stir in flour to make a roux, cooking for about 3 minutes. Gradually add beef stock while stirring until thick. Mix in the shredded beef and season with salt. Chill the mixture until firm. Shape into small balls and coat with egg and breadcrumbs. Fry in oil at 180°C for about 4 minutes until golden. Drain on paper and serve hot.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 30g · fat 28g","storage":"Keep refrigerated for up to 2 days before frying.","chefNotes":"Chilling the filling well helps with shaping.","pairsWith":"mustard · beer · pickles","trivia":"Bitterballen are a popular bar snack in the Netherlands.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-poffertjes","name":"Poffertjes","nameAlt":"Mini Fluffy Pancakes","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"pancake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"100g flour · 150ml milk · 1 egg · 5g yeast · 10g sugar · 10g butter","method":"Mix flour, yeast, sugar, milk and egg into a smooth batter. Let the batter rest in a warm place for about 30 minutes. Heat a poffertjes pan over medium heat and grease with butter. Pour small amounts of batter into each cavity. Cook for about 2 minutes until bubbles form, then flip. Cook another 1 to 2 minutes until golden. Remove and serve warm with butter. Dust with sugar before serving.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 9g · carbs 50g · fat 12g","storage":"Best eaten fresh but can be refrigerated for 1 day.","chefNotes":"Use a special poffertjes pan for authentic shape.","pairsWith":"powdered sugar · butter · syrup","trivia":"Poffertjes are often sold at fairs and street markets.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-erwtensoep","name":"Erwtensoep","nameAlt":"Dutch Split Pea Soup","aliases":["Snert"],"course":"main","type":"soup","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"winter","ingredients":"150g split peas · 100g pork shoulder · 50g carrot · 50g celery · 1 onion · 5g salt · 500ml water","method":"Rinse the split peas and place in a pot with water. Add pork and bring to a boil. Simmer gently for about 60 minutes. Chop carrot, celery and onion. Add vegetables to the soup and continue cooking for another 30 minutes. Stir occasionally until thick. Remove meat, shred and return to the pot. Season with salt. Serve hot when thick and hearty.","cookTime":"90 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 35g · carbs 50g · fat 25g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days or freeze.","chefNotes":"Soup thickens more after resting overnight.","pairsWith":"rye bread · smoked sausage · mustard","trivia":"Traditional erwtensoep is so thick a spoon can stand in it.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-kroket","name":"Kroket","nameAlt":"Fried Meat Croquette","aliases":["Croquette"],"course":"starter","type":"fried snack","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"snack","ingredients":"100g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 20g flour · 20g butter · 100ml beef stock · 1 egg · 50g breadcrumbs · 5g salt","method":"Cook and shred the beef finely. Make a roux with butter and flour over medium heat for about 3 minutes. Add beef stock gradually while stirring until thick. Mix in beef and season with salt. Chill until firm. Shape into logs and coat in egg and breadcrumbs. Fry at 180°C for about 4 minutes until crisp. Drain and serve hot.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 35g · fat 30g","storage":"Store refrigerated for up to 2 days before frying.","chefNotes":"Freeze briefly before frying for best shape.","pairsWith":"mustard · bread roll · pickles","trivia":"Kroketten are commonly eaten in sandwiches in the Netherlands.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-appeltaart","name":"Appeltaart","nameAlt":"Dutch Apple Pie","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"pie","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"celebration","ingredients":"150g flour · 100g apple · 50g sugar · 75g butter · 1 egg · 5g cinnamon","method":"Mix flour, butter and sugar into a dough and chill for 20 minutes. Peel and slice apples. Combine apples with sugar and cinnamon. Roll out dough and line a baking tin. Fill with apple mixture. Cover with strips of dough on top. Brush with beaten egg. Bake at 180°C for about 45 minutes until golden. Cool slightly before serving.","cookTime":"60 min","kcal":450,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 60g · fat 20g","storage":"Store covered at room temperature for 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use tart apples for better flavor balance.","pairsWith":"whipped cream · coffee · tea","trivia":"Dutch apple pie is deeper and more spiced than many versions.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-haring","name":"Haring","nameAlt":"Raw Herring Fish","aliases":["Hollandse Nieuwe"],"course":"starter","type":"raw fish","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"summer","ingredients":"150g herring · 20g onion · 5g salt","method":"Clean and fillet the herring if not already prepared. Lightly salt the fish. Finely chop the onion. Serve the herring raw with chopped onion on top. Eat immediately while fresh. Optionally hold the fish by the tail and eat traditionally. Keep chilled until serving.","cookTime":"10 min","kcal":250,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 2g · fat 18g","storage":"Keep refrigerated and consume within 1 day.","chefNotes":"Use very fresh herring for best taste.","pairsWith":"onions · pickles · bread","trivia":"Eating herring by holding it by the tail is a Dutch tradition.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-oliebollen","name":"Oliebollen","nameAlt":"Dutch Doughnuts","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"fried dough","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"New Year","ingredients":"150g flour · 150ml milk · 1 egg · 10g sugar · 5g yeast · 50g raisins","method":"Mix flour, yeast, sugar, milk and egg into a batter. Stir in raisins. Let the batter rise for about 45 minutes. Heat oil to 180°C. Scoop small portions of batter into the oil. Fry for about 5 minutes until golden brown. Turn occasionally for even cooking. Drain on paper towels. Serve warm dusted with sugar.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":400,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 55g · fat 15g","storage":"Best eaten fresh, can be stored 1 day.","chefNotes":"Ensure oil stays at steady temperature for even frying.","pairsWith":"powdered sugar · coffee · tea","trivia":"Oliebollen are traditionally eaten on New Year's Eve.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-kaas","name":"Gouda Kaas","nameAlt":"Gouda Cheese","aliases":["Goudse kaas"],"course":"side","type":"cheese","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g gouda cheese","method":"Slice the cheese into thin pieces. Allow it to come to room temperature before serving. Arrange neatly on a plate. Serve as part of a meal or snack. Pair with bread or crackers. Enjoy the creamy texture and flavor. Store properly after use.","cookTime":"5 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 2g · fat 28g","storage":"Keep refrigerated and wrapped for up to 1 week.","chefNotes":"Room temperature enhances flavor.","pairsWith":"bread · mustard · fruit","trivia":"Gouda is one of the most famous cheeses in the world.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-pannenkoeken","name":"Pannenkoeken","nameAlt":"Dutch Pancakes","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"pancake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g flour · 200ml milk · 1 egg · 10g butter · 3g salt","method":"Mix flour, milk, egg and salt into a smooth batter. Heat a pan over medium heat and add butter. Pour a thin layer of batter into the pan. Cook for about 2 minutes until golden underneath. Flip and cook another 1 to 2 minutes. Remove and repeat with remaining batter. Serve warm with sweet or savory toppings.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 9g · carbs 40g · fat 10g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Let batter rest for smoother pancakes.","pairsWith":"syrup · bacon · cheese","trivia":"Dutch pancakes are larger and thinner than American ones.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-hutspot","name":"Hutspot","nameAlt":"Carrot Potato Mash","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"mash","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"winter","ingredients":"300g potatoes · 150g carrot · 50g onion · 50g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 10g butter · 3g salt","method":"Peel and chop potatoes and carrots. Boil them together in salted water for about 25 minutes. Add chopped onion halfway through cooking. Drain and return to pot. Mash until smooth. Stir in butter and cooked beef. Season with salt. Serve hot as a hearty dish.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 55g · fat 20g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Add a splash of milk for creamier texture.","pairsWith":"gravy · sausage · pickles","trivia":"Hutspot is linked to the Siege of Leiden in 1574.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-speklap","name":"Speklap","nameAlt":"Pork Belly Slice","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"grilled meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"bbq","ingredients":"150g pork belly · 5g salt · 5g black pepper","method":"Season the pork belly with salt and pepper. Heat a grill or pan over medium heat. Cook the pork for about 5 minutes per side until crispy and cooked through. Turn occasionally to prevent burning. Ensure internal heat is sufficient. Remove and rest briefly. Serve hot with sides.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 0g · fat 55g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Cook slowly for crisp exterior and tender inside.","pairsWith":"bread · mustard · salad","trivia":"Speklap is a popular Dutch BBQ meat.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-kibbeling","name":"Kibbeling","nameAlt":"Battered Fried Fish Pieces","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"fried fish","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"street food","ingredients":"200g firm white fish (hake) · 80g flour · 100ml milk · 1 egg · 5g paprika · 3g salt","method":"Cut the fish into bite-sized pieces. Mix flour, milk, egg, paprika and salt into a smooth batter. Heat oil to 180°C in a deep pan. Dip fish pieces into the batter, coating evenly. Fry in batches for about 4 to 5 minutes until golden and crisp. Remove and drain on paper towels. Serve immediately while hot. Sprinkle extra salt if desired.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 40g · fat 25g","storage":"Best eaten fresh but can be refrigerated for 1 day.","chefNotes":"Use firm white fish like cod for best results.","pairsWith":"garlic sauce · fries · lemon","trivia":"Kibbeling was originally made from leftover cod pieces.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-uitsmijter","name":"Uitsmijter","nameAlt":"Open Sandwich with Eggs and Ham","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"sandwich","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"lunch","ingredients":"2 slices bread · 2 eggs · 50g ham · 30g cheese · 5g butter","method":"Heat butter in a pan over medium heat. Fry the eggs for about 3 to 4 minutes until whites are set but yolks still soft. Toast the bread lightly. Place ham and cheese on the bread slices. Transfer the eggs on top. Allow the cheese to soften from the heat. Serve immediately while warm. Season lightly if needed.","cookTime":"10 min","kcal":550,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 35g · fat 30g","storage":"Best eaten fresh.","chefNotes":"Keep yolks runny for traditional style.","pairsWith":"salad · coffee · juice","trivia":"The name means 'bouncer', referring to someone throwing you out after a late meal.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-rijsttafel","name":"Rijsttafel","nameAlt":"Dutch-Indonesian Rice Table","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"rice platter","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"feast","ingredients":"200g rice · 100g chicken · 50g green beans · 50g coconut milk · 10g soy sauce · 5g chili paste","method":"Cook the rice in boiling water for about 15 minutes until tender. In a separate pan cook chicken pieces over medium heat for about 8 minutes. Add green beans and cook for another 5 minutes. Stir in coconut milk, soy sauce and chili paste. Simmer gently for 10 minutes until flavors combine. Serve the rice with the chicken mixture and sides. Arrange neatly for presentation.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":650,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 70g · fat 25g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Serve multiple small dishes for authenticity.","pairsWith":"pickles · sambal · fried shallots","trivia":"Rijsttafel was developed during Dutch colonial times in Indonesia.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-balkenbrij","name":"Balkenbrij","nameAlt":"Pork and Flour Slice","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"slice","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"150g pork shoulder · 50g flour · 300ml pork stock · 5g nutmeg · 3g salt","method":"Cook pork in stock for about 40 minutes until tender. Shred the meat finely. Bring stock to a simmer and stir in flour gradually. Add shredded pork and nutmeg. Cook while stirring until thick. Pour mixture into a tray and let cool until firm. Slice into pieces. Fry slices over medium heat for about 5 minutes until crispy. Serve hot.","cookTime":"60 min","kcal":580,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 40g · fat 30g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Allow to set fully before slicing.","pairsWith":"apple syrup · bread · mustard","trivia":"Balkenbrij is a traditional dish from the eastern Netherlands.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-krentenbol","name":"Krentenbol","nameAlt":"Currant Bread Roll","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"bread","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"breakfast","ingredients":"100g flour · 50g currants · 5g yeast · 50ml milk · 10g sugar · 5g butter","method":"Mix flour, yeast, sugar and milk into a dough. Knead for about 10 minutes until smooth. Add currants and knead lightly. Let the dough rise for about 60 minutes until doubled. Shape into small rolls. Place on a baking tray and rest for 15 minutes. Bake at 200°C for about 15 minutes until golden. Cool slightly before serving.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":280,"nutrition":"protein 7g · carbs 50g · fat 6g","storage":"Store in an airtight container for 2 days.","chefNotes":"Soak currants briefly for extra softness.","pairsWith":"butter · jam · tea","trivia":"Krentenbollen are popular school snacks in the Netherlands.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-ontbijtkoek","name":"Ontbijtkoek","nameAlt":"Dutch Spiced Breakfast Cake","aliases":["Peperkoek"],"course":"side","type":"cake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"breakfast","ingredients":"150g flour · 100g brown sugar · 150ml milk · 5g cinnamon · 5g ginger · 5g baking powder","method":"Mix flour, sugar, spices and baking powder in a bowl. Add milk and stir into a smooth batter. Pour into a greased loaf tin. Bake at 180°C for about 50 minutes until firm. Remove and cool completely. Slice into thick pieces. Serve plain or with butter. Store properly after cooling.","cookTime":"60 min","kcal":320,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 65g · fat 5g","storage":"Keep in a sealed container for up to 5 days.","chefNotes":"Flavors improve after a day of resting.","pairsWith":"butter · coffee · tea","trivia":"Ontbijtkoek is often eaten as a quick breakfast or snack.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-zoute-haring-broodje","name":"Broodje Haring","nameAlt":"Herring Sandwich","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"sandwich","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"street food","ingredients":"1 bread roll · 150g herring · 20g onion · 10g pickles","method":"Slice the bread roll open. Place the herring inside the roll. Finely chop onion and add on top. Add sliced pickles for extra flavor. Press gently to hold together. Serve immediately while fresh. Keep ingredients chilled before assembling.","cookTime":"5 min","kcal":400,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 30g · fat 18g","storage":"Best eaten fresh.","chefNotes":"Use fresh herring for authentic taste.","pairsWith":"pickles · onions · soda","trivia":"Broodje haring is a common Dutch street food.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-zuurkoolstamppot","name":"Zuurkoolstamppot","nameAlt":"Sauerkraut Potato Mash","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"mash","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"winter","ingredients":"300g potatoes · 150g sauerkraut · 50g smoked sausage · 10g butter · 50ml milk · 3g salt","method":"Peel and chop potatoes and boil for about 20 minutes until soft. Warm the sauerkraut separately. Drain potatoes and return to pot. Mash potatoes with butter and milk until smooth. Mix in sauerkraut evenly. Slice and heat the smoked sausage. Season with salt. Serve hot with sausage on top.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":540,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 55g · fat 28g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Rinse sauerkraut slightly if too sour.","pairsWith":"mustard · gravy · pickles","trivia":"Zuurkoolstamppot is a classic winter comfort dish.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-bossche-bol","name":"Bossche Bol","nameAlt":"Chocolate Cream Puff","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"pastry","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"treat","ingredients":"100g flour · 100ml water · 50g butter · 2 eggs · 100g cream · 100g dark chocolate","method":"Heat water and butter until melted. Add flour and stir until a dough forms. Cool slightly then beat in eggs one at a time. Pipe dough into balls on a tray. Bake at 200°C for about 25 minutes until puffed. Cool completely. Fill with whipped cream. Melt chocolate and coat the tops generously. Allow to set before serving.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 45g · fat 40g","storage":"Keep refrigerated and consume within 2 days.","chefNotes":"Do not open oven early to prevent collapse.","pairsWith":"coffee · tea · berries","trivia":"Bossche bollen originate from the city of Den Bosch.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"netherlands-gevulde-koek","name":"Gevulde Koek","nameAlt":"Almond Filled Cookie","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"cookie","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Netherlands","occasion":"snack","ingredients":"100g flour · 80g almond paste · 50g butter · 50g sugar · 1 egg","method":"Mix flour, butter and sugar into a dough and chill briefly. Roll out dough and cut circles. Place almond paste in the center of half the circles. Cover with another circle and seal edges. Brush tops with beaten egg. Bake at 180°C for about 20 minutes until golden. Cool before serving. Enjoy as a sweet treat.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":450,"nutrition":"protein 7g · carbs 40g · fat 28g","storage":"Store in an airtight container for up to 4 days.","chefNotes":"Chill dough to prevent spreading.","pairsWith":"coffee · tea · milk","trivia":"Gevulde koeken are a beloved Dutch bakery item.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-fondue","name":"Fondue","nameAlt":"Melted Cheese Dip","aliases":["Cheese Fondue","Fondue moitié-moitié"],"course":"main","type":"cheese","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"winter","ingredients":"120g Gruyere cheese · 120g Emmental cheese · 100ml dry white wine · 5ml lemon juice · 5g cornstarch · 1 clove garlic · 2g black pepper · 150g bread","method":"Rub a pot with garlic and discard the clove. Heat the wine with lemon juice on medium until warm, about 3 minutes. Gradually add grated cheese while stirring constantly until melted, about 5 minutes. Mix cornstarch with a little wine and stir into the pot. Continue cooking on low heat until smooth and thick, about 4 minutes. Season with black pepper and keep warm over low heat. Serve with cubed bread for dipping.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 30g · fat 32g","storage":"Store leftovers refrigerated and reheat gently.","chefNotes":"Use good quality cheese and wine for best flavor.","pairsWith":"white wine · pickles · cured meats","trivia":"Fondue became a Swiss national dish in the 20th century.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-raclette","name":"Raclette","nameAlt":"Melted Cheese with Potatoes","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"cheese","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"winter","ingredients":"200g raclette cheese · 200g potatoes · 50g pickles · 50g pearl onions · 2g black pepper","method":"Boil potatoes in salted water until tender, about 20 minutes. Slice the raclette cheese into portions. Heat a grill or raclette machine to medium heat. Place cheese under heat until melted and bubbling, about 5 minutes. Serve melted cheese over hot potatoes. Add pickles and onions on the side. Season lightly with black pepper.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 40g · fat 38g","storage":"Keep components refrigerated separately.","chefNotes":"Use waxy potatoes for best texture.","pairsWith":"white wine · cured meats · bread","trivia":"Raclette is both a dish and the name of the cheese used.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-rosti","name":"Rosti","nameAlt":"Crispy Potato Cake","aliases":["Rösti"],"course":"side","type":"fried","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"300g potatoes · 20g butter · 5g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"Peel and grate the potatoes coarsely. Squeeze out excess moisture. Heat butter in a pan on medium heat for 2 minutes. Add potatoes and press into a flat cake. Cook until golden brown on one side, about 8 minutes. Flip carefully and cook the other side for another 8 minutes. Season with salt and pepper before serving.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 5g · carbs 45g · fat 15g","storage":"Store refrigerated and reheat in a pan.","chefNotes":"Parboil potatoes for easier shaping if needed.","pairsWith":"fried eggs · sausages · salad","trivia":"Rosti originated as a farmer's breakfast dish.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-zurcher-geschnetzeltes","name":"Zurcher Geschnetzeltes","nameAlt":"Zurich Sliced Veal","aliases":["Zürcher Geschnetzeltes"],"course":"main","type":"saute","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"special","ingredients":"200g veal · 50g mushrooms · 50ml cream · 50ml white wine · 20g butter · 10g onion · 5g flour · 2g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"Slice veal into thin strips. Melt butter in a pan on medium heat for 2 minutes. Add veal and cook quickly until browned, about 4 minutes. Remove meat and set aside. Saute onions and mushrooms until soft, about 5 minutes. Stir in flour and cook briefly for 1 minute. Add wine and cream, simmering until thickened, about 5 minutes. Return veal to the pan and heat through, seasoning with salt and pepper.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 35g · carbs 10g · fat 30g","storage":"Refrigerate and reheat gently.","chefNotes":"Do not overcook veal to keep it tender.","pairsWith":"rosti · white wine · green beans","trivia":"This dish is a specialty from Zurich.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-alplermagronen","name":"Alplermagronen","nameAlt":"Swiss Alpine Macaroni","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"pasta","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g pasta · 100g potatoes · 50g cheese · 50ml cream · 30g onion · 20g butter · 5g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"Boil pasta and diced potatoes together until tender, about 10 minutes. Drain and set aside. Melt butter in a pan and cook sliced onions until caramelized, about 10 minutes. Heat cream and add grated cheese until melted, about 5 minutes. Combine pasta, potatoes, and sauce in a pot. Stir well and season with salt and pepper. Top with caramelized onions before serving.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":650,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 70g · fat 30g","storage":"Store refrigerated and reheat with a splash of milk.","chefNotes":"Serve with applesauce for a traditional touch.","pairsWith":"applesauce · salad · cider","trivia":"This dish was popular among Alpine herdsmen.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-birchermuesli","name":"Bircher Muesli","nameAlt":"Overnight Oats with Apple","aliases":["Birchermüesli"],"course":"starter","type":"cereal","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"breakfast","ingredients":"50g oats · 100ml milk · 100g apple · 10g honey · 20g yogurt · 10g almonds","method":"Soak oats in milk in a bowl. Refrigerate for at least 10 minutes. Grate the apple and mix into the oats. Stir in yogurt and honey. Add chopped almonds. Mix thoroughly until creamy. Serve chilled.","cookTime":"10 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 10g · carbs 45g · fat 10g","storage":"Keep refrigerated and consume within one day.","chefNotes":"Let it rest overnight for deeper flavor.","pairsWith":"fresh fruit · tea · coffee","trivia":"It was invented by Swiss physician Maximilian Bircher-Brenner.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-bundnerfleisch","name":"Bundnerfleisch","nameAlt":"Air-Dried Beef","aliases":["Bündnerfleisch"],"course":"starter","type":"cured","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"snack","ingredients":"150g beef · 5g salt · 2g black pepper · 2g juniper berries","method":"Trim the beef and rub with salt, pepper, and crushed juniper berries. Place in a container and refrigerate for 10 minutes active prep. Hang in a cool dry place to cure. Allow air drying until firm. Slice thinly before serving. Serve chilled as a snack. Keep preparation clean and controlled.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":250,"nutrition":"protein 35g · carbs 1g · fat 10g","storage":"Store in a cool dry place or refrigerate.","chefNotes":"Curing takes several weeks in practice.","pairsWith":"cheese · bread · wine","trivia":"It originates from the canton of Graubunden.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-saffron-risotto","name":"Risotto allo Zafferano Ticinese","nameAlt":"Saffron Risotto","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"rice","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"special","ingredients":"100g risotto rice · 500ml chicken stock · 50ml white wine · 20g butter · 20g parmesan cheese · 0.1g saffron · 10g onion · 2g salt","method":"Heat butter in a pan and cook chopped onion until soft, about 4 minutes. Add rice and toast lightly for 2 minutes. Pour in wine and let it evaporate. Add warm stock gradually while stirring constantly over medium heat, about 18 minutes. Stir in saffron dissolved in stock. Continue until rice is creamy and tender. Finish with parmesan and season with salt.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":450,"nutrition":"protein 12g · carbs 60g · fat 15g","storage":"Refrigerate and reheat with stock.","chefNotes":"Stir constantly for creamy texture.","pairsWith":"white wine · salad · grilled vegetables","trivia":"Saffron adds color and aroma to the dish.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-nusstorte","name":"Engadiner Nusstorte","nameAlt":"Nut Caramel Tart","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"tart","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"100g flour · 50g butter · 30g sugar · 50g walnuts · 50ml cream · 20g honey · 1 egg","method":"Mix flour, butter, sugar, and egg to form dough. Chill for 10 minutes. Roll out and line a tart pan. Heat sugar, honey, and cream to make caramel, about 5 minutes. Stir in walnuts and cook briefly. Fill the tart shell with the mixture. Cover with pastry lid. Bake at 180°C for 30 minutes until golden. Cool before slicing.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":550,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 50g · fat 35g","storage":"Store in an airtight container.","chefNotes":"Let it cool fully for clean slices.","pairsWith":"coffee · tea · whipped cream","trivia":"This tart comes from the Engadin valley.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-basler-laeckerli","name":"Basler Lackerli","nameAlt":"Spiced Honey Cookies","aliases":["Basler Läckerli"],"course":"dessert","type":"biscuit","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"100g flour · 80g honey · 50g sugar · 30g almonds · 20g candied peel · 2g cinnamon · 2g cloves","method":"Heat honey and sugar until dissolved, about 3 minutes. Mix in flour, almonds, candied peel, and spices. Roll dough thin and place on a tray. Bake at 180°C for 15 minutes until firm. Cool slightly and cut into rectangles. Let cool completely before serving. Store once fully cooled.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 5g · carbs 50g · fat 8g","storage":"Keep in airtight container for freshness.","chefNotes":"Flavor improves after a few days.","pairsWith":"tea · coffee · mulled wine","trivia":"These cookies originate from Basel.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-maluns","name":"Maluns","nameAlt":"Crispy Potato Flour Crumble","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"fried","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g potatoes · 50g flour · 20g butter · 5g salt","method":"Boil potatoes until tender, about 20 minutes. Peel and grate coarsely. Mix with flour and salt. Heat butter in a pan on medium heat. Add mixture and cook, stirring constantly, until crumbly and golden, about 15 minutes. Break into small pieces while cooking. Serve hot as a side dish.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":400,"nutrition":"protein 7g · carbs 60g · fat 15g","storage":"Refrigerate and reheat in a pan.","chefNotes":"Keep stirring to prevent sticking.","pairsWith":"cheese · applesauce · sausages","trivia":"Maluns is a traditional dish from Graubunden.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-cervelat","name":"Cervelat","nameAlt":"Swiss Sausage","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"sausage","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"grill","ingredients":"200g cervelat sausage · 100g bread · 20g mustard","method":"Score the ends of the sausage. Grill over medium heat until browned, about 10 minutes. Turn occasionally to cook evenly. Warm bread slightly on the grill. Serve sausage with bread and mustard. Enjoy immediately while hot.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 30g · fat 35g","storage":"Refrigerate uncooked sausages.","chefNotes":"Do not overcook to keep it juicy.","pairsWith":"mustard · bread · beer","trivia":"Cervelat is considered Switzerland's national sausage.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-berner-platte","name":"Berner Platte","nameAlt":"Bernese Meat Platter","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"platter","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"150g smoked pork · 100g beef rump or sirloin · 100g potatoes · 80g cabbage · 50g sausage · 5g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"Place pork and beef in a pot with water and bring to a boil over medium heat for 10 minutes. Reduce heat and simmer until tender, about 60 minutes. Add chopped cabbage and cook for 15 minutes. Boil potatoes separately until soft, about 20 minutes. Grill or pan-fry sausage until browned, about 10 minutes. Arrange all components on a platter and season with salt and pepper. Serve hot.","cookTime":"90 min","kcal":700,"nutrition":"protein 45g · carbs 40g · fat 40g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers and reheat thoroughly.","chefNotes":"Use a mix of cured meats for authentic flavor.","pairsWith":"mustard · rye bread · beer","trivia":"This dish commemorates a historic Bernese victory in 1798.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-capuns","name":"Capuns","nameAlt":"Swiss Chard Dumplings","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"dumpling","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g flour · 1 egg · 50ml milk · 50g cured meat · 100g Swiss chard · 20g cheese · 20g butter · 2g salt","method":"Mix flour, egg, milk, and salt into a dough. Stir in finely chopped cured meat. Place small portions onto Swiss chard leaves and roll. Boil in salted water on medium heat until firm, about 10 minutes. Melt butter and add grated cheese. Toss cooked dumplings in the butter and cheese mixture. Serve hot.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 50g · fat 20g","storage":"Refrigerate and reheat gently.","chefNotes":"Blanch leaves briefly for easier rolling.","pairsWith":"salad · white wine · pickles","trivia":"Capuns originate from the canton of Graubunden.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-polenta","name":"Polenta Ticinese","nameAlt":"Cornmeal Porridge","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"porridge","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g cornmeal · 400ml water · 5g salt · 20g butter · 20g cheese","method":"Bring water and salt to a boil over medium heat for 5 minutes. Gradually whisk in cornmeal. Reduce heat and cook while stirring constantly, about 30 minutes. Stir in butter and grated cheese. Continue cooking until thick and smooth. Serve hot as a side dish.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 55g · fat 12g","storage":"Refrigerate and reheat with water or milk.","chefNotes":"Stir continuously to avoid lumps.","pairsWith":"stew · mushrooms · grilled meat","trivia":"Polenta is popular in the Italian-speaking region of Switzerland.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-gesottene-kalbszunge","name":"Gesottene Kalbszunge","nameAlt":"Boiled Veal Tongue","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"boiled","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"special","ingredients":"300g veal tongue · 50g carrot · 50g onion · 20g celery · 5g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"Rinse the veal tongue thoroughly. Place in a pot with water, carrot, onion, and celery. Bring to a boil over medium heat for 10 minutes. Reduce heat and simmer until tender, about 90 minutes. Remove and peel the outer skin while warm. Slice and season with salt and pepper. Serve hot or cold.","cookTime":"100 min","kcal":450,"nutrition":"protein 35g · carbs 10g · fat 25g","storage":"Refrigerate and consume within two days.","chefNotes":"Peel while warm for easier handling.","pairsWith":"mustard · potatoes · salad","trivia":"This dish was once considered a delicacy in Swiss cuisine.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-kaseschnitte","name":"Kaseschnitte","nameAlt":"Swiss Cheese Toast","aliases":["Käseschnitte"],"course":"main","type":"toast","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g bread · 80g cheese · 50ml white wine · 10g butter · 1 egg · 2g black pepper","method":"Butter the bread slices and place in a pan. Add wine and top with grated cheese. Cook on medium heat until cheese melts, about 5 minutes. Crack an egg on top and cook until set, about 4 minutes. Season with black pepper. Serve hot.","cookTime":"10 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 18g · carbs 30g · fat 25g","storage":"Best eaten fresh; refrigerate leftovers.","chefNotes":"Use stale bread for better texture.","pairsWith":"salad · white wine · pickles","trivia":"Kaseschnitte is a simple Alpine comfort dish.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-zwetschgenkuchen","name":"Zwetschgenkuchen","nameAlt":"Plum Cake","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"cake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"autumn","ingredients":"100g flour · 50g sugar · 50g butter · 1 egg · 150g plums · 5g baking powder","method":"Mix flour, sugar, butter, egg, and baking powder into a batter. Spread into a baking tin. Arrange halved plums on top. Bake at 180°C for 35 minutes until golden. Allow to cool slightly before slicing. Serve warm or at room temperature.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":380,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 45g · fat 18g","storage":"Store in an airtight container.","chefNotes":"Use ripe plums for sweetness.","pairsWith":"coffee · tea · cream","trivia":"Plum cakes are popular during harvest season.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-schaumkuss","name":"Schaumkuss","nameAlt":"Chocolate Marshmallow Treat","aliases":["Mohrenkopf"],"course":"dessert","type":"confectionery","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"snack","ingredients":"1 wafer · 30g sugar · 20g egg white · 50g chocolate","method":"Whip egg whites and sugar until stiff peaks form. Pipe onto wafers into dome shapes. Chill briefly to set, about 10 minutes. Melt chocolate over low heat for 5 minutes. Dip the domes into chocolate until fully coated. Let set until firm before serving.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":220,"nutrition":"protein 3g · carbs 30g · fat 10g","storage":"Store in a cool dry place.","chefNotes":"Work quickly when coating with chocolate.","pairsWith":"coffee · milk · tea","trivia":"These treats are popular across Switzerland and Germany.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-fasnachtschuechli","name":"Fasnachtschuechli","nameAlt":"Carnival Fried Pastry","aliases":["Fasnachtschüechli"],"course":"dessert","type":"fried","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"100g flour · 20g sugar · 1 egg · 20g butter · 50ml milk · 200ml vegetable oil","method":"Mix flour, sugar, egg, butter, and milk into a dough. Roll thinly and cut into shapes. Heat oil to 180°C. Fry pieces until golden, about 2 minutes per side. Remove and drain on paper. Serve dusted with sugar.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":400,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 45g · fat 20g","storage":"Store in airtight container for one day.","chefNotes":"Roll dough very thin for crisp texture.","pairsWith":"coffee · tea · sugar","trivia":"These pastries are eaten during carnival season.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-rueblitorte","name":"Rueblitorte","nameAlt":"Carrot Cake","aliases":["Rüeblitorte"],"course":"dessert","type":"cake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"celebration","ingredients":"100g carrots · 80g sugar · 80g almonds · 2 eggs · 30g flour · 5g baking powder","method":"Grate carrots finely. Beat eggs and sugar until fluffy, about 5 minutes. Fold in almonds, flour, and baking powder. Add grated carrots and mix well. Pour into a baking tin. Bake at 180°C for 35 minutes until set. Cool before serving.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 40g · fat 22g","storage":"Store in an airtight container.","chefNotes":"Add lemon zest for freshness.","pairsWith":"tea · coffee · cream","trivia":"This cake is a specialty from Aargau.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-heisse-schokolade","name":"Heisse Schokolade","nameAlt":"Hot Chocolate","aliases":[],"course":"drink","type":"beverage","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"winter","ingredients":"200ml milk · 40g dark chocolate · 10g sugar","method":"Heat milk in a saucepan over medium heat for 3 minutes. Add chopped chocolate and stir until melted, about 4 minutes. Add sugar and stir until dissolved. Heat gently until smooth and hot. Serve immediately.","cookTime":"10 min","kcal":250,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 30g · fat 12g","storage":"Best consumed fresh.","chefNotes":"Use high-quality chocolate for richness.","pairsWith":"pastries · biscuits · cream","trivia":"Swiss chocolate is world-renowned for quality.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-wiener-schnitzel","name":"Wiener Schnitzel","nameAlt":"Breaded Veal Cutlet","aliases":["Wiener Schnitzel","Schnitzel"],"course":"main","type":"fried","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"central-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"classic","ingredients":"180g veal cutlet (thin) · 60g flour · 1 egg · 80g breadcrumbs · 100ml oil (for frying) · 30g butter · ½ lemon · salt","method":"Pound the veal between two sheets of cling film until wafer-thin, about 4mm — this is non-negotiable; a thin, even cutlet cooks in the moment it takes the crumb to turn gold. Set up three plates: seasoned flour, beaten egg, breadcrumbs. Coat the cutlet in flour (shake off the excess), then egg, then crumbs — but press the crumbs on only lightly; the secret to a real Wiener Schnitzel is a coating that puffs and 'soufflés' away from the meat rather than clinging tight. Fry in plenty of hot oil with a knob of butter, and — the classic move — tilt the pan and spoon the hot fat over the top so the crumb billows and blisters into that famous wavy crust. It takes barely a minute a side; the crumb should be deep gold, the veal only just cooked. Drain, salt, and serve with just a wedge of lemon — never a sauce, and by Austrian law only a veal one may be called 'Wiener Schnitzel' at all.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":620,"nutrition":"protein 38g · carbs 34g · fat 36g","storage":"Eat fresh — the crust softens fast. If you must, re-crisp in a hot oven, never a microwave.","chefNotes":"Two secrets: pound the veal wafer-thin, and press the crumbs on lightly then baste with hot fat so the coating soufflés up wavy and blistered — a crust that hugs the meat tight is a cutlet, not a Wiener Schnitzel.","pairsWith":"Austrian potato salad · lingonberry jam · cucumber salad · parsley potatoes","trivia":"By Austrian law the name 'Wiener Schnitzel' is protected — it may only be made with veal; the pork version must be sold as 'Schnitzel Wiener Art' ('Viennese style'). The much-repeated tale that it came from Milan's cotoletta via Field Marshal Radetzky is almost certainly a 20th-century invention.","howThisFeels":"A shatteringly crisp, wavy golden crust puffed away from meltingly tender veal, with nothing but a squeeze of lemon — Vienna on a plate.","servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic Veal","icon":"🏆","default":true},{"name":"Pork (Wiener Art)","icon":"🐖","howThisFeels":"The everyday version — cheaper pork, just as crisp; by law it can't be called Wiener Schnitzel, only 'Wiener Art'.","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 36g · carbs 34g · fat 34g","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"180g veal cutlet (thin)","to":"180g pork loin (thin)"}],"swapStep":[{"from":"never a sauce, and by Austrian law only a veal one may be called 'Wiener Schnitzel' at all.","to":"never a sauce — and remember a pork one, cheaper and just as loved, must legally be sold as 'Schnitzel Wiener Art', never Wiener Schnitzel."}]}},{"name":"Chicken","icon":"🐔","howThisFeels":"Lighter and family-friendly — butterflied chicken breast in the same crisp golden crumb.","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 40g · carbs 34g · fat 24g","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"180g veal cutlet (thin)","to":"180g chicken breast (butterflied)"}]}},{"name":"Jägerschnitzel","icon":"🍄","howThisFeels":"The hunter's version — crisp schnitzel drowned in a rich mushroom cream sauce.","kcal":720,"nutrition":"protein 40g · carbs 38g · fat 44g","ingredients":"180g pork loin (thin) · 60g flour · 1 egg · 80g breadcrumbs · 100ml oil · 150g mushrooms · 60ml cream · 30g onion · 100ml stock · parsley · salt","method":"Bread and fry the pork schnitzel as for the classic — flour, egg, crumbs, hot fat, basted until crisp and gold — then keep it warm. In the same pan, soften the sliced onion, add the mushrooms and fry hard until browned and their water has gone. Pour in the stock, let it reduce, then stir in the cream and simmer to a glossy sauce; season well. Pour the hunter's mushroom sauce over the crisp schnitzel, scatter with parsley and serve at once, with dumplings or spätzle to catch the sauce."}]},
{"id":"austria-tafelspitz","name":"Tafelspitz","nameAlt":"Boiled Beef with Vegetables","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"boiled","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"Sunday lunch","ingredients":"250g beef rump · 100g carrot · 80g leek · 80g celery root · 1 onion · 5g salt · 2g black peppercorns · 5g parsley","method":"Place the beef in a pot with cold water and bring to a gentle simmer. Skim off any foam that rises. Add chopped vegetables and peppercorns. Simmer on low heat for about 90 minutes until tender. Season with salt toward the end. Slice the beef against the grain. Serve with broth and vegetables. Garnish with parsley.","cookTime":"90 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 45g · carbs 10g · fat 28g","storage":"Keep refrigerated for up to 3 days in its broth.","chefNotes":"Adding salt later keeps the beef more tender.","pairsWith":"apple horseradish · boiled potatoes · spinach","trivia":"Tafelspitz was a favorite dish of Emperor Franz Joseph I.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-kaesespaetzle","name":"Käsespätzle","nameAlt":"Cheesy Egg Noodles","aliases":["Kasnocken"],"course":"main","type":"pasta","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"120g flour · 1 egg · 60ml milk · 80g cheese · 40g onion · 20g butter · 5g salt","method":"Mix flour, egg, milk, and salt into a soft dough. Push the dough through a spätzle maker into boiling water. Cook until the pieces float, about 2 minutes. Drain and set aside. Melt butter and fry sliced onions until golden on medium heat. Add the spätzle and toss with grated cheese. Stir until the cheese melts evenly. Serve hot with crispy onions on top.","cookTime":"20-25 min","kcal":620,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 68g · fat 28g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days and reheat gently.","chefNotes":"Use a strong melting cheese for best texture.","pairsWith":"green salad · pickles · apple juice","trivia":"This dish is popular across the Alpine regions.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-apfelstrudel","name":"Apfelstrudel","nameAlt":"Apple Strudel","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"pastry","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"coffee time","ingredients":"120g apple · 60g flour · 30g butter · 30g sugar · 20g raisins · 5g cinnamon · 20g breadcrumbs","method":"Prepare a thin dough using flour and water and rest it. Stretch the dough until very thin on a floured cloth. Mix sliced apples with sugar, cinnamon, and raisins. Spread breadcrumbs over the dough to absorb moisture. Add the apple mixture evenly. Roll the strudel using the cloth. Brush with melted butter. Bake at 180°C for about 35 minutes until golden.","cookTime":"35-40 min","kcal":450,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 70g · fat 16g","storage":"Keep covered at room temperature for 1 day or refrigerate.","chefNotes":"Stretch the dough carefully to avoid tearing.","pairsWith":"vanilla sauce · whipped cream · coffee","trivia":"Strudel dough is traditionally stretched by hand until paper-thin.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-sachertorte","name":"Sachertorte","nameAlt":"Chocolate Apricot Cake","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"cake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"celebration","ingredients":"80g dark chocolate · 60g butter · 60g sugar · 2 eggs · 50g flour · 40g apricot jam","method":"Melt chocolate and butter together gently. Beat eggs and sugar until light. Fold melted chocolate into the egg mixture. Add flour and mix gently. Pour into a greased tin. Bake at 170°C for about 30 minutes. Cool and slice horizontally. Spread apricot jam inside and over the cake. Finish with a chocolate glaze.","cookTime":"30-35 min","kcal":540,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 52g · fat 32g","storage":"Store in a cool place for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Let the cake rest overnight for best flavor.","pairsWith":"whipped cream · coffee · berries","trivia":"Sachertorte originated in Vienna in 1832.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-goulash","name":"Gulasch","nameAlt":"Beef Paprika Stew","aliases":["Wiener Gulasch"],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 100g onion · 10g paprika · 10g tomato paste · 5g garlic · 10ml oil · 5g salt","method":"Heat oil and cook chopped onions until deeply browned. Add garlic and paprika briefly without burning. Stir in beef cubes and sear lightly. Add tomato paste and mix well. Pour in water to cover and simmer. Cook on low heat for about 90 minutes until tender. Stir occasionally to prevent sticking. Season with salt and adjust consistency.","cookTime":"90 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 40g · carbs 12g · fat 32g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days or freeze.","chefNotes":"Slow cooking deepens the flavor significantly.","pairsWith":"bread · potatoes · pickles","trivia":"Austrian goulash differs from Hungarian by being thicker and onion-rich.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-knodel","name":"Knödel","nameAlt":"Bread Dumplings","aliases":["Knödel","Semmelknödel","Bread Dumplings"],"course":"side","type":"dumpling","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"central-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"classic","ingredients":"120g stale bread rolls (cubed) · 100ml warm milk · 1 egg · 20g onion · 10g butter · 10g flour · fresh parsley · nutmeg · salt","method":"The dumpling starts with STALE bread — day-old rolls (Semmeln) cubed; fresh bread turns to glue. Pour the warmed milk over the bread cubes and leave 15 minutes to soften. Soften the chopped onion in butter, then mix through the soaked bread with the egg, flour, parsley, a good grate of nutmeg and salt into a mixture that just holds together — too wet, add a little flour; too dry, a splash of milk. Wet your hands and roll into tight balls, squeezing out air pockets so they don't fall apart. Poach them in barely-simmering salted water (never a hard boil, which breaks them up) for about 15 minutes until they float and feel firm. Lift out with a slotted spoon and serve alongside anything with gravy — goulash, roasts, mushroom sauce — to soak it all up.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":320,"nutrition":"protein 11g · carbs 42g · fat 12g","storage":"Keep 2 days; slice and fry leftovers in butter until golden (Geröstete Knödel) — arguably better than fresh. Freeze cooked.","chefNotes":"Two rules: stale bread only (fresh goes gluey), and poach in water that's barely trembling, never boiling, or the dumplings burst apart.","pairsWith":"Goulash · Beef with crispy onions · mushroom sauce · roast pork","trivia":"The Knödel is such an Alpine institution that Tyrol has a saying — 'a day without a dumpling is a lost day.' Leftovers are never wasted: sliced and fried in butter (Geröstete Knödel) or dropped into broth, they get a whole second life.","howThisFeels":"Soft, nutmeg-scented bread dumplings made to soak up gravy — Alpine comfort, the thing that makes a stew a feast.","servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Plain (Semmelknödel)","icon":"🏆","default":true},{"name":"Bacon (Speckknödel)","icon":"🥓","howThisFeels":"The Tyrolean favourite — smoky speck through the dumpling, served in clear broth or beside sauerkraut.","kcal":390,"nutrition":"protein 15g · carbs 42g · fat 19g","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"50g speck or bacon (diced)","after":"20g onion"}],"swapStep":[{"from":"Soften the chopped onion in butter","to":"Fry the diced speck until its fat renders, then soften the chopped onion in that fat"}]}},{"name":"Cheese (Kaspressknödel)","icon":"🧀","howThisFeels":"Pressed flat and fried crisp, then served in beef broth — the Tyrolean cheese-dumpling classic.","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 17g · carbs 42g · fat 21g","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"60g mountain cheese (grated)","after":"20g onion"}],"swapStep":[{"from":"Wet your hands and roll into tight balls, squeezing out air pockets so they don't fall apart. Poach them in barely-simmering salted water (never a hard boil, which breaks them up) for about 15 minutes until they float and feel firm.","to":"Mix the grated mountain cheese through, then form into balls and press them flat into thick patties — these are Kaspressknödel. Fry them in butter until deep golden and crisp on both sides."}]}},{"name":"Spinach (Spinatknödel)","icon":"🌿","howThisFeels":"Green and earthy — wilted spinach through the dumpling, lovely with brown butter and parmesan.","kcal":330,"nutrition":"protein 13g · carbs 42g · fat 13g","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"80g spinach (wilted, chopped, squeezed dry)","after":"20g onion"}],"swapStep":[{"from":"mix through the soaked bread with the egg","to":"mix through the soaked bread with the squeezed spinach, the egg"}]}}]},
{"id":"austria-linzertorte","name":"Linzertorte","nameAlt":"Nut Jam Tart","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"tart","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"100g flour · 80g ground almonds · 80g butter · 60g sugar · 1 egg · 60g raspberry jam","method":"Cream butter and sugar until smooth. Add egg and mix well. Stir in flour and ground almonds. Chill the dough briefly. Roll out and line a tart tin. Spread jam evenly over the base. Create a lattice pattern with remaining dough. Bake at 180°C for about 30 minutes. Cool before slicing.","cookTime":"30-35 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 7g · carbs 50g · fat 28g","storage":"Store in an airtight container for 3 days.","chefNotes":"Chilling the dough makes it easier to handle.","pairsWith":"tea · coffee · whipped cream","trivia":"Linzertorte is considered one of the oldest known cakes in the world.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-kartoffelsalat","name":"Erdäpfelsalat","nameAlt":"Austrian Potato Salad","aliases":["Kartoffelsalat"],"course":"side","type":"salad","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g potato · 30ml vinegar · 20ml oil · 20g onion · 5g mustard · 5g sugar · 5g salt","method":"Boil potatoes until tender and let them cool slightly. Slice them while still warm. Mix vinegar, oil, mustard, sugar, and salt. Add finely chopped onion. Pour dressing over the potatoes. Toss gently to coat evenly. Let sit for 10 minutes to absorb flavors. Adjust seasoning if needed. Serve slightly warm or at room temperature.","cookTime":"25-30 min","kcal":280,"nutrition":"protein 5g · carbs 38g · fat 12g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use waxy potatoes for best texture.","pairsWith":"schnitzel · sausages · bread","trivia":"This potato salad is typically served without mayonnaise.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-kaiser-schmarrn","name":"Kaiserschmarrn","nameAlt":"Shredded Pancake","aliases":["Kaiserschmarrn"],"course":"dessert","type":"pancake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"central-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"classic","ingredients":"80g flour · 2 eggs · 150ml milk · 20g sugar · 25g butter · 20g raisins · pinch salt · icing sugar (to dust) · plum compote (to serve)","method":"Separate the eggs. Whisk the yolks with the milk, flour, sugar and salt into a smooth batter, then — the trick that makes it cloud-light — beat the whites to soft peaks and fold them through so the batter is airy. Melt the butter in a pan, scatter in the raisins, pour in the batter and cook gently until the underside is golden and just set. Now the 'schmarrn' (the word means 'mess'): don't flip it neatly — tear and chop it into rough bite-sized chunks right there in the pan with two forks. Add a little more butter and a sprinkle of sugar and keep tossing over higher heat so the torn edges caramelise and turn crisp-golden. Tip onto a plate, dust thickly with icing sugar and serve hot with plum or apple compote.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 12g · carbs 62g · fat 20g","storage":"Best straight from the pan while the edges are crisp; it doesn't keep or reheat well.","chefNotes":"Two things: fold in whipped egg whites for lightness, and once torn up let the pieces caramelise in butter and sugar over higher heat — that crisp sugary edge is the whole point, not a neat pancake.","pairsWith":"plum compote · apple sauce · vanilla ice cream · stewed berries","trivia":"Kaiserschmarrn means 'the Emperor's mess'. The best-loved tale has it created for Emperor Franz Josef I, who loved it; another says his figure-conscious wife Sisi refused her portion and the Emperor happily ate hers too.","howThisFeels":"Torn, buttery pancake pieces with caramelised crisp edges and soft centres, snowed under icing sugar — the pudding fit for an emperor.","servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic (Raisin)","icon":"🏆","default":true},{"name":"Apple (Apfelschmarrn)","icon":"🍎","howThisFeels":"Studded with soft caramelised apple instead of raisins — autumn in a pan.","kcal":470,"nutrition":"protein 12g · carbs 60g · fat 19g","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"20g raisins","to":"60g apple (thin slices)"}],"swapStep":[{"from":"scatter in the raisins","to":"scatter in the apple slices and let them soften and caramelise"}]}},{"name":"Almond & Rum","icon":"🥜","howThisFeels":"Grown-up and fragrant — rum-soaked raisins and toasted almonds through the caramelised pieces.","kcal":510,"nutrition":"protein 13g · carbs 62g · fat 23g","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"20g raisins","to":"20g rum-soaked raisins"}],"addIng":[{"item":"15g flaked almonds","after":"25g butter"}],"swapStep":[{"from":"scatter in the raisins","to":"scatter in the rum-soaked raisins and the flaked almonds"}]}}]},
{"id":"austria-germknoedel","name":"Germknödel","nameAlt":"Steamed Yeast Dumpling","aliases":["Germknödel"],"course":"dessert","type":"dumpling","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"central-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"classic","ingredients":"150g flour · 3g instant yeast · 80ml warm milk · 20g sugar · 1 egg yolk · 20g butter · 40g plum jam (Powidl) · 20g poppy seeds · 20g butter (to serve) · vanilla sauce (to serve) · pinch salt","method":"Make a soft, enriched yeast dough: warm the milk, mix with the flour, yeast, sugar, egg yolk, melted butter and salt, and knead until smooth and elastic. Cover and let it rise until doubled — a lively, well-risen dough is what makes Germknödel cloud-soft. Knock back, divide, flatten each piece, spoon a good blob of thick plum jam (Powidl) into the centre and pinch the dough tightly closed around it so it can't leak. Rest the filled dumplings 15 minutes to puff again, then — the key — STEAM them (a steamer basket over simmering water, lid on, no peeking) for about 15 minutes until they've swollen huge and springy; lifting the lid mid-steam collapses them. Serve at once, drowned in melted butter, snowed with ground poppy seeds and sugar, and pooled with warm vanilla sauce.","cookTime":"40 min (+1 hr rise)","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 10g · carbs 72g · fat 20g","storage":"Best fresh and hot; day-old ones are lovely steamed again or sliced and pan-fried in butter.","chefNotes":"Two things: a properly risen, springy dough, and STEAM (never boil) with the lid on and no peeking — a peek mid-steam and the dumpling deflates.","pairsWith":"vanilla sauce · stewed plums · coffee","trivia":"Germknödel is Austria's great ski-hut dessert — a fist-sized fluffy steamed yeast dumpling filled with Powidl (spiced plum jam) and buried under poppy seeds, butter and vanilla sauce. 'Germ' is simply the Austrian word for yeast.","howThisFeels":"A fist-sized cloud of steamed yeast dough oozing plum jam, under melted butter, poppy seeds and vanilla sauce — the reward at the top of the mountain.","servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Plum Jam (Powidl)","icon":"🏆","default":true},{"name":"Chocolate-Hazelnut","icon":"🍫","howThisFeels":"The kids' version — molten chocolate-hazelnut in place of the plum jam.","kcal":560,"nutrition":"protein 11g · carbs 74g · fat 24g","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"40g plum jam (Powidl)","to":"40g chocolate-hazelnut spread"}],"swapStep":[{"from":"spoon a good blob of thick plum jam (Powidl) into the centre","to":"spoon a good blob of chocolate-hazelnut spread into the centre"}]}},{"name":"Apricot","icon":"🍑","howThisFeels":"Filled with bright apricot jam — the Wachau valley's summer take.","kcal":515,"nutrition":"protein 10g · carbs 71g · fat 20g","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"40g plum jam (Powidl)","to":"40g apricot jam"}],"swapStep":[{"from":"spoon a good blob of thick plum jam (Powidl) into the centre","to":"spoon a good blob of thick apricot jam into the centre"}]}}]},
{"id":"austria-frittatensuppe","name":"Frittatensuppe","nameAlt":"Pancake Strip Soup","aliases":[],"course":"starter","type":"soup","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"300ml beef broth · 1 egg · 40g flour · 80ml milk · 5g salt · 5g parsley","method":"Mix egg, flour, milk, and salt into a thin batter. Cook thin pancakes in a pan over medium heat, about 2 minutes per side. Let them cool slightly. Roll the pancakes and slice into thin strips. Heat beef broth until simmering. Add the pancake strips to the broth. Warm through for 2 to 3 minutes. Garnish with chopped parsley and serve hot.","cookTime":"15-20 min","kcal":260,"nutrition":"protein 12g · carbs 30g · fat 10g","storage":"Store broth and pancakes separately for best texture.","chefNotes":"Keep pancakes thin so they slice easily.","pairsWith":"bread · salad · tea","trivia":"Frittaten are similar to French crêpes but used in soup.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-tiroler-groestl","name":"Tiroler Gröstl","nameAlt":"Tyrolean Fried Potatoes and Meat","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"fried","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"hearty meal","ingredients":"200g potato · 120g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 40g onion · 20g butter · 1 egg · 5g salt · 5g parsley","method":"Boil potatoes until just tender and slice them. Heat butter in a pan on medium heat. Fry onions until golden, about 4 minutes. Add beef pieces and cook until browned. Add potatoes and fry until crispy, about 8 minutes. Season with salt and mix gently. Fry an egg separately to desired doneness. Serve topped with the fried egg and parsley.","cookTime":"20-25 min","kcal":560,"nutrition":"protein 32g · carbs 40g · fat 30g","storage":"Store refrigerated for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use leftover meat for authentic flavor.","pairsWith":"pickles · cabbage salad · beer","trivia":"This dish originated as a way to use leftovers in alpine inns.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-leberkaese","name":"Leberkäse","nameAlt":"Baked Meat Loaf Slice","aliases":["Fleischkäse"],"course":"main","type":"loaf","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"street food","ingredients":"200g ground pork · 100g ground beef · 5g salt · 2g marjoram · 2g black pepper · 30ml water","method":"Mix ground pork and beef with salt, marjoram, pepper, and water until smooth. Press the mixture into a loaf tin. Smooth the top with a wet spatula. Bake at 180°C for about 60 minutes until browned. Let rest briefly before slicing. Serve warm in thick slices. Optionally crisp slices in a pan for extra texture.","cookTime":"60-65 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 4g · fat 38g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days and reheat slices.","chefNotes":"A smooth texture is key, so mix thoroughly.","pairsWith":"bread roll · mustard · pickles","trivia":"Despite its name, it typically contains no liver.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-zwetschkenknoedel","name":"Zwetschkenknödel","nameAlt":"Plum Dumplings","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"dumpling","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"summer","ingredients":"150g potato · 60g flour · 1 egg · 80g plum · 20g butter · 20g sugar · 20g breadcrumbs","method":"Boil and mash potatoes, then mix with flour and egg to form dough. Wrap each plum with dough and seal well. Bring water to a gentle simmer. Cook dumplings for about 15 minutes until they float. Melt butter and toast breadcrumbs on medium heat until golden. Roll cooked dumplings in the crumbs. Sprinkle with sugar and serve warm.","cookTime":"25-30 min","kcal":460,"nutrition":"protein 10g · carbs 70g · fat 14g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days and reheat gently.","chefNotes":"Seal the dough tightly to prevent leaks.","pairsWith":"vanilla sauce · cinnamon sugar · tea","trivia":"These dumplings are a seasonal favorite when plums are ripe.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-topfenstrudel","name":"Topfenstrudel","nameAlt":"Quark Strudel","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"pastry","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"coffee time","ingredients":"80g quark · 60g flour · 30g butter · 30g sugar · 1 egg · 20g raisins","method":"Prepare a thin dough and let it rest. Mix quark with sugar, egg, and raisins. Stretch the dough on a cloth until thin. Spread the filling evenly across the surface. Roll carefully using the cloth. Brush with melted butter. Bake at 180°C for about 35 minutes until golden. Slice and serve warm or cooled.","cookTime":"35-40 min","kcal":430,"nutrition":"protein 14g · carbs 50g · fat 18g","storage":"Store in the fridge for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Do not overfill or the strudel may burst.","pairsWith":"powdered sugar · coffee · vanilla sauce","trivia":"Topfen is the Austrian term for quark cheese.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-palatschinken","name":"Palatschinken","nameAlt":"Thin Pancakes","aliases":["Palacinky"],"course":"dessert","type":"pancake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"dessert","ingredients":"80g flour · 1 egg · 120ml milk · 20g butter · 30g apricot jam","method":"Whisk flour, egg, and milk into a smooth batter. Heat a pan with a little butter over medium heat. Pour a thin layer of batter and cook about 1 to 2 minutes per side. Stack the pancakes as you go. Spread apricot jam on each pancake. Roll or fold them neatly. Serve warm with a dusting of sugar if desired.","cookTime":"15-20 min","kcal":360,"nutrition":"protein 10g · carbs 48g · fat 14g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Keep pancakes thin for the best texture.","pairsWith":"powdered sugar · fruit compote · tea","trivia":"Palatschinken are similar to French crêpes.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-rindsuppe","name":"Rindsuppe","nameAlt":"Clear Beef Soup","aliases":[],"course":"starter","type":"soup","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"300ml water · 200g beef bones · 80g carrot · 50g celery · 30g onion · 5g salt · 2g black peppercorns","method":"Place beef bones in cold water and bring to a gentle simmer. Skim impurities as they rise. Add chopped vegetables and peppercorns. Simmer on low heat for about 120 minutes. Season with salt toward the end. Strain the broth carefully. Serve clear and hot. Optionally add garnishes like noodles or herbs.","cookTime":"120 min","kcal":180,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 6g · fat 8g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days or freeze.","chefNotes":"A slow simmer keeps the broth clear.","pairsWith":"bread · dumplings · herbs","trivia":"This broth forms the base of many Austrian soups.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-salzburger-nockerl","name":"Salzburger Nockerl","nameAlt":"Sweet Soufflé Dumplings","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"souffle","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"special","ingredients":"3 egg whites · 30g sugar · 10g flour · 10g butter · 10g powdered sugar","method":"Beat egg whites with sugar until stiff peaks form. Fold in flour gently. Butter a baking dish and shape the mixture into mounds. Bake at 200°C for about 10 minutes until lightly golden. Remove carefully from the oven. Dust with powdered sugar immediately. Serve hot as it deflates quickly.","cookTime":"10-12 min","kcal":220,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 28g · fat 8g","storage":"Best eaten immediately after baking.","chefNotes":"Serve quickly as the soufflé collapses fast.","pairsWith":"berry sauce · cream · tea","trivia":"This dessert represents the three hills around Salzburg.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-vanillekipferl","name":"Vanillekipferl","nameAlt":"Vanilla Crescent Cookies","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"cookie","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"100g flour · 80g butter · 40g sugar · 40g ground almonds · 5g vanilla sugar","method":"Mix flour, butter, sugar, and almonds into a dough. Chill briefly to firm up. Shape small crescents by hand. Place on a baking tray. Bake at 180°C for about 12 minutes until pale golden. While warm, roll gently in vanilla sugar. Let cool completely before serving.","cookTime":"12-15 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 5g · carbs 28g · fat 20g","storage":"Store in an airtight container for up to 1 week.","chefNotes":"Handle gently when warm to prevent breaking.","pairsWith":"coffee · tea · milk","trivia":"These cookies are a staple during Austrian Christmas baking.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-krautfleckerl","name":"Krautfleckerl","nameAlt":"Cabbage Pasta","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"pasta","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"120g pasta · 150g cabbage · 30g onion · 20g butter · 5g sugar · 5g salt","method":"Cook pasta in salted boiling water until tender, about 8 minutes. Drain and set aside. Melt butter in a pan and cook sliced onion until soft. Add shredded cabbage and cook on medium heat for about 10 minutes until caramelized. Sprinkle sugar and stir well. Add cooked pasta and toss to combine. Season with salt and serve hot.","cookTime":"20-25 min","kcal":400,"nutrition":"protein 10g · carbs 60g · fat 14g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Caramelizing the cabbage adds depth of flavor.","pairsWith":"salad · pickles · yogurt","trivia":"A simple dish often made with pantry ingredients.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"sweden-kottbullar","name":"Kottbullar","nameAlt":"Swedish Meatballs with Cream Sauce","aliases":["Köttbullar","Swedish Meatballs"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"family dinner","ingredients":"150g ground beef · 50g ground pork · 20g onion · 5g garlic · 30ml cream · 20g breadcrumbs · 1 egg · 5g salt · 2g black pepper · 10ml butter · 15g flour (for the gräddsås) · 100ml beef stock · 150ml cream (for the gräddsås) · 30g lingonberry jam (or redcurrant jelly)","method":"Finely chop the onion and garlic and sauté them gently in butter until soft and sweet, without browning — then let them cool, because hot onion cooks the egg in the mix. Combine the beef and pork with breadcrumbs, the 30ml of cream, egg, salt, and pepper, add the cooled onions, and mix until smooth and slightly sticky. Roll into small, even balls with damp hands — small matters; these are köttbullar, not meatball boulders. Fry them in butter in batches, rolling them around so they brown evenly on all sides, then set them aside — and whatever you do, DON'T wash the pan. Those browned bits stuck to the bottom are the fond, and the fond is the flavour bank for the sauce this dish is named after. Stir the flour into the pan fat and cook it a minute, then whisk in the beef stock, scraping up every dark scrap. Pour in the 150ml of cream and let the gräddsås simmer to a silky, spoon-coating gravy — taste it, season it, respect it. Return the meatballs to the sauce to warm through. Serve with the lingonberry jam alongside — the sharp-sweet berry is not a garnish, it is the counterweight that makes the richness make sense — and creamy mash if you're doing it properly.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 12g · fat 30g","storage":"Store in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 3 days or freeze for up to 2 months.","chefNotes":"Don't wash the pan — the fond IS the sauce. Small meatballs, silky gräddsås, and the jam is structural, not decorative.","pairsWith":"mashed potatoes · lingonberry sauce · pickled cucumber","trivia":"Swedish meatballs became globally famous through furniture store cafeterias — but in 2018 Sweden's official Twitter account admitted the recipe originally came home with King Charles XII from Turkey.","howThisFeels":"Little meatballs, big diplomacy — comfort the whole world signed.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"sweden-gravlax","name":"Gravlax","nameAlt":"Cured Salmon with Dill","aliases":["Gravad lax","Gravlaks"],"course":"starter","type":"seafood","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"holiday","ingredients":"150g salmon fillet · 20g salt · 15g sugar · 5g dill · 2g black pepper","method":"Check the salmon fillet for any pin bones and remove them carefully with tweezers. Mix together the salt, sugar, and black pepper in a bowl until evenly combined. Rub the curing mixture thoroughly over the entire surface of the salmon. Place a generous layer of fresh dill on top of the fish and wrap it tightly in plastic wrap. Place the wrapped salmon in a shallow dish and weigh it down slightly to help the curing process. Refrigerate for 24 to 48 hours, turning the fish occasionally to ensure even curing. Once cured, scrape off the excess mixture and slice the salmon thinly before serving.","cookTime":"2880 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 5g · fat 20g","storage":"Keep refrigerated and consume within 3 days after curing.","chefNotes":"Slice the salmon very thinly for the best texture and flavor.","pairsWith":"mustard dill sauce · boiled potatoes · crispbread","trivia":"Gravlax was originally buried in sand by fishermen to ferment lightly.","howThisFeels":"Silk-soft salmon and dill — the patience of three quiet days, sliced thin.","servings":1,"sharedWith":"Norway · Denmark"},
{"id":"sweden-janssons-frestelse","name":"Janssons Frestelse","nameAlt":"Anchovy Potato Casserole","aliases":["Jansson's Temptation"],"course":"side","type":"seafood","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"200g potatoes · 50g onion · 40g anchovies · 100ml cream · 10g butter · 5g salt · 2g white pepper","method":"Peel the potatoes and cut them into thin matchsticks for even cooking. Slice the onion finely and sauté it gently in butter until soft and slightly sweet. Grease a baking dish and layer half of the potatoes evenly at the bottom. Add the sautéed onions and anchovies on top, distributing them evenly across the layer. Cover with the remaining potatoes and pour cream evenly over the dish. Season lightly with salt and white pepper. Bake in a preheated oven until the top is golden brown and the potatoes are tender when pierced. Allow it to rest briefly before serving so the layers set properly.","cookTime":"60 min","kcal":450,"nutrition":"protein 15g · carbs 40g · fat 25g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 3 days and reheat gently in the oven.","chefNotes":"Do not over-salt as anchovies are already salty.","pairsWith":"pickled herring · rye bread · green salad","trivia":"The dish is named after an opera singer, not a biblical figure.","howThisFeels":"Creamy potatoes with a salty secret — temptation by name, temptation by nature.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"sweden-raggmunk","name":"Raggmunk","nameAlt":"Swedish Potato Pancakes","aliases":["Potatisplattar","Raggmunks"],"course":"main","type":"vegetarian","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"everyday meal","ingredients":"200g potatoes · 50g flour · 100ml milk · 1 egg · 5g salt · 10ml oil","method":"Peel and grate the potatoes coarsely, then squeeze out excess liquid to prevent sogginess. In a bowl, whisk together the flour, milk, egg, and salt to create a smooth batter. Fold the grated potatoes into the batter until evenly combined. Heat oil in a frying pan over medium heat until hot. Spoon portions of the mixture into the pan and flatten them into thin pancakes. Fry until the edges are crisp and the surface turns golden brown, then flip carefully and cook the other side. Continue cooking until the pancakes are fully cooked through and slightly crispy. Serve immediately while hot for the best texture.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":380,"nutrition":"protein 10g · carbs 45g · fat 18g","storage":"Store in the refrigerator for up to 2 days and reheat in a pan.","chefNotes":"Serve immediately to keep the pancakes crispy.","pairsWith":"lingonberry jam · fried bacon · sour cream","trivia":"Traditionally eaten on Thursdays in Sweden.","howThisFeels":"Crisp-laced potato pancakes — winter comfort you can hear from the pan.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"sweden-smorgastarta","name":"Smorgastarta","nameAlt":"Swedish Sandwich Cake","aliases":["Smörgåstårta"],"course":"main","type":"mixed","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"celebration","ingredients":"150g bread · 80g mayonnaise · 50g smoked salmon · 50g shrimp · 30g cucumber · 20g dill","method":"Trim the crusts from the bread slices and lay the first layer on a flat surface. Spread a mixture of mayonnaise and fillings such as shrimp or salmon evenly across the layer. Add another layer of bread and repeat the filling process to build height. Continue layering until all ingredients are used, finishing with a top layer of bread. Spread mayonnaise smoothly over the entire cake to cover all sides. Decorate the top with slices of cucumber, shrimp, salmon, and fresh dill for presentation. Chill the cake in the refrigerator for several hours to allow flavors to meld. Slice carefully and serve cold.","cookTime":"120 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 40g · fat 30g","storage":"Keep refrigerated and consume within 2 days.","chefNotes":"Chill well before slicing for clean portions.","pairsWith":"green salad · pickled vegetables · boiled eggs","trivia":"Often served at birthdays and festive gatherings in Sweden.","howThisFeels":"A sandwich dressed as a birthday cake — Sweden's most delicious disguise.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"sweden-kroppkakor","name":"Kroppkakor","nameAlt":"Potato Dumplings with Pork Filling","aliases":["Kroppkaka","Swedish Potato Dumplings"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"traditional meal","ingredients":"250g potatoes · 80g flour · 1 egg · 100g pork belly or streaky bacon · 30g onion · 5g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Boil the potatoes until tender, then mash them smoothly and allow them to cool slightly. Mix the mashed potatoes with flour, egg, salt, and pepper to form a soft dough. Finely chop the pork and onion, then sauté them together until cooked through and slightly browned. Take portions of the dough and flatten them in your hand, placing a spoonful of filling in the center. Wrap the dough around the filling and shape into round dumplings. Bring a pot of salted water to a gentle simmer and carefully add the dumplings. Cook until they float to the surface and are firm, indicating they are done. Remove them with a slotted spoon and serve warm.","cookTime":"50 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 55g · fat 20g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days or freeze for later use.","chefNotes":"Do not overcrowd the pot to prevent dumplings from breaking.","pairsWith":"lingonberry sauce · melted butter · pickled beetroot","trivia":"Popular in southern Sweden, especially on the island of Öland.","howThisFeels":"Pillowy potato dumplings hiding smoky pork — grandmother's sleight of hand.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"sweden-kalops","name":"Kalops","nameAlt":"Swedish Beef Stew","aliases":["Swedish Kalops"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"winter meal","ingredients":"200g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 50g carrot · 50g onion · 5g allspice · 5g salt · 2g pepper · 300ml water","method":"Cut the beef into evenly sized chunks and season them lightly with salt and pepper. Heat a pot over medium heat and brown the meat on all sides to develop flavor. Add chopped onions and carrots and cook until slightly softened. Sprinkle in the allspice and stir to release its aroma. Pour in water to cover the ingredients and bring to a gentle simmer. Cover the pot and cook slowly until the meat becomes tender and the flavors meld together. Stir occasionally and adjust seasoning as needed. Serve hot once the stew has thickened slightly.","cookTime":"120 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 20g · fat 28g","storage":"Store in the refrigerator for up to 3 days or freeze for up to 2 months.","chefNotes":"Slow cooking enhances the depth of flavor.","pairsWith":"boiled potatoes · pickled beetroot · cabbage salad","trivia":"Kalops is a classic comfort dish during Swedish winters.","howThisFeels":"Slow beef and allspice — a stew that thinks in hours, not minutes.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"sweden-prinsesstarta","name":"Prinsesstarta","nameAlt":"Princess Cream Cake","aliases":["Prinsesstårta","Princess Cake"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"celebration","ingredients":"100g sponge cake · 100ml cream · 50g custard · 50g marzipan · 20g sugar · green food colouring","method":"Slice the sponge into even layers — a serrated knife and patience beat strength every time. Whip the cream with the sugar to soft peaks that just hold their shape; over-whipped cream fights you when you need it to flow. Build the tower: sponge, then a layer of custard, then whipped cream, then sponge again, repeating upward. Now the defining act — heap the remaining cream on top and shape it into a smooth, generous DOME, because the dome is non-negotiable: a flat prinsesstårta is just a very nice sandwich. Knead a few drops of green food colouring into the marzipan until it turns an even, pale leaf-green — the famous colour that makes this cake recognisable across a crowded fika table. Roll the marzipan out wide and thin between sheets of baking paper, then lift it carefully and drape it over the dome in one motion, smoothing it down the sides with your palms so it hugs every curve without a wrinkle. Trim the base neatly, dust the crown lightly with icing sugar, and — if you're feeling royal — finish with a small pink marzipan rose on top. Chill well before slicing so every green-capped wedge shows its cream, custard, and sponge in clean stripes.","cookTime":"90 min","kcal":450,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 50g · fat 25g","storage":"Refrigerate and consume within 2 days.","chefNotes":"The dome is the identity and the green is the flag — roll the marzipan thin, drape in one confident motion, and chill before the knife goes in.","pairsWith":"fresh berries · vanilla sauce · fruit compote","trivia":"Named for three Swedish princesses — Margaretha, Märtha, and Astrid — who adored the cake their home-economics teacher created in the 1920s.","howThisFeels":"A green silk dome fit for princesses — and greedy commoners.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"sweden-kanelbullar","name":"Kanelbullar","nameAlt":"Cinnamon Buns","aliases":["Kanelbulle","Swedish Cinnamon Rolls"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"fika","ingredients":"200g flour · 100ml milk · 50g butter · 30g sugar · 5g yeast · 3g ground cardamom · 5g cinnamon · 15g pearl sugar (or coarsely crushed sugar cubes)","method":"First, the Swedish signature, and it goes in before anything else: cardamom in the dough. This single spice is what separates a kanelbulle from every other cinnamon roll on earth — skip it and you've baked something nice, but you haven't baked Swedish. Warm the milk gently, dissolve the yeast into it, and mix in the flour, sugar, cardamom, and softened butter to form a soft dough. Knead until smooth and elastic — it should spring back slowly when poked — then cover and let it rise until doubled. Roll it into a rectangle and spread softened butter right to the edges, then scatter the cinnamon and a little sugar evenly over the whole surface. Roll it into a snug log and slice into even buns. Now the step impatient bakers skip and regret: the second rise. Let the shaped buns puff until light and pillowy — the second rise is where fluffiness is born, and skipping it bakes bricks. Brush with a little milk or beaten egg and crown each bun with pearl sugar — the crunchy white studs are the finishing signature. Bake hot and fast until deep golden and fragrant, and serve slightly warm, ideally with coffee and no particular hurry — that unhurried pause is fika, and in Sweden it is practically a civic duty.","cookTime":"120 min","kcal":400,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 55g · fat 18g","storage":"Store in an airtight container for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Cardamom in the dough, butter to the edges, and never skip the second rise. Pearl sugar is the crown.","pairsWith":"cardamom buns · vanilla custard · fruit jam","trivia":"Sweden loves this bun enough to give it a national holiday — Kanelbullens Dag, celebrated every October 4th.","howThisFeels":"Cardamom warmth and cinnamon swirl — fika made flesh.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"sweden-semla","name":"Semla","nameAlt":"Cream-Filled Cardamom Bun","aliases":["Semlor","Fastlagsbulle"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"Lent","ingredients":"150g flour · 80ml milk · 30g butter · 20g sugar · 5g cardamom · 100ml cream · 30g almond paste","method":"Prepare a soft dough by mixing warm milk, yeast, flour, butter, sugar, and cardamom until smooth. Knead the dough thoroughly and allow it to rise until doubled in size. Shape the dough into small buns and let them rise again until light and airy. Bake the buns until golden and cooked through, then allow them to cool completely. Cut off the tops and scoop out a small portion of the inside crumb. Mix the crumb with almond paste and a little milk to create a filling. Fill each bun with the almond mixture and top generously with whipped cream. Place the tops back on and dust lightly with sugar before serving.","cookTime":"120 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 9g · carbs 60g · fat 22g","storage":"Best eaten fresh, but can be refrigerated for up to 1 day.","chefNotes":"Use freshly ground cardamom for a stronger aroma.","pairsWith":"berry compote · vanilla sauce · almond biscuits","trivia":"Traditionally eaten before Lent in Sweden.","howThisFeels":"Cardamom bun, almond heart, cloud of cream — February's only excuse.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"sweden-falukorv","name":"Falukorv","nameAlt":"Fried Swedish Sausage","aliases":["Falukorv med stekt potatis"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"everyday meal","ingredients":"200g falukorv sausage · 150g potatoes · 20ml oil · 5g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Slice the sausage into thick rounds and set aside for cooking. Peel and dice the potatoes into small cubes for even frying. Heat oil in a pan over medium heat and cook the potatoes until they are golden and crisp on the outside while tender inside. Season the potatoes with salt and pepper while they cook. In another pan, fry the sausage slices until they are browned and slightly crispy on the edges. Turn them occasionally to ensure even cooking. Combine the sausage and potatoes briefly in the pan to heat through together. Serve hot once everything is fully cooked and well seasoned.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":550,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 35g · fat 35g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Cook potatoes slowly for a crisp texture.","pairsWith":"mustard sauce · pickled cabbage · fried eggs","trivia":"Falukorv originated from Falun's copper mining region.","howThisFeels":"Fried sausage rounds, zero pretension — Tuesday supper at its most loyal.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"sweden-toast-skagen","name":"Toast Skagen","nameAlt":"Shrimp Salad on Toast","aliases":["Skagenröra","Toast à la Skagen"],"course":"starter","type":"seafood","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"starter","ingredients":"120g shrimp · 50g mayonnaise · 20g dill · 10g lemon juice · 50g bread","method":"Peel and roughly chop the shrimp into bite-sized pieces. In a bowl, combine the shrimp with mayonnaise, chopped dill, and lemon juice. Mix gently until the ingredients are evenly coated without breaking the shrimp too much. Toast the bread slices until golden and crisp. Spoon the shrimp mixture generously over the toast. Spread it evenly to cover the surface. Garnish with additional dill if desired. Serve immediately while the toast remains crisp and fresh.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 18g · carbs 20g · fat 20g","storage":"Store shrimp mixture separately in refrigerator for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use fresh shrimp for best flavor.","pairsWith":"lemon wedges · cucumber salad · boiled eggs","trivia":"Named after the coastal region of Skagen.","howThisFeels":"Prawns in dill mayonnaise on golden toast — fancy made friendly.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"sweden-blabarssoppa","name":"Blabarssoppa","nameAlt":"Blueberry Soup","aliases":["Blåbärssoppa"],"course":"drink","type":"sweet","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"winter","ingredients":"150g blueberries · 300ml water · 30g sugar · 5g starch","method":"Combine the blueberries and water in a pot and bring to a gentle boil over medium heat. Allow the berries to soften and release their juices as they cook. Stir occasionally to prevent sticking at the bottom. Add sugar and mix until dissolved completely. In a small bowl, mix starch with a little cold water to create a slurry. Pour the slurry into the soup while stirring continuously to thicken it. Continue cooking until the soup reaches a smooth and slightly thick consistency. Remove from heat and allow it to cool slightly before serving warm or chilled.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":180,"nutrition":"protein 2g · carbs 40g · fat 1g","storage":"Store refrigerated for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Adjust sugar based on berry sweetness.","pairsWith":"pancakes · vanilla yogurt · oat biscuits","trivia":"Often served during cross-country skiing events.","howThisFeels":"Warm blueberry soup in a mug — the drink that carries skiers home.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"sweden-inlagd-sill","name":"Inlagd Sill","nameAlt":"Pickled Herring","aliases":["Pickled Herring Swedish Style"],"course":"side","type":"seafood","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"Midsummer","ingredients":"150g herring · 100ml vinegar · 80ml water · 30g sugar · 10g onion · 5g spices","method":"Rinse the herring thoroughly and cut it into bite-sized pieces. In a saucepan, combine vinegar, water, and sugar and bring to a gentle boil until the sugar dissolves. Slice the onion thinly and prepare the spices for flavoring. Place the herring pieces into a clean jar and layer them with onions and spices. Pour the hot pickling liquid over the herring until fully submerged. Allow the mixture to cool before sealing the jar. Refrigerate for at least 24 hours to allow the flavors to develop. Serve chilled once properly pickled.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":250,"nutrition":"protein 18g · carbs 20g · fat 10g","storage":"Keep refrigerated for up to 1 week.","chefNotes":"Allow enough time for proper pickling flavor.","pairsWith":"boiled potatoes · sour cream · chives","trivia":"A staple dish during Swedish festive seasons.","howThisFeels":"Sweet-sharp pickled herring — the bite that starts every Swedish feast.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"sweden-pytt-i-panna","name":"Pytt i Panna","nameAlt":"Swedish Hash with Meat and Potatoes","aliases":["Pyttipanna","Pytt i panna"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"everyday meal","ingredients":"150g potatoes · 100g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 50g onion · 10ml oil · 5g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"Peel and dice the potatoes into small cubes and boil them briefly until just tender, then drain and cool. Cut the beef into small bite-sized pieces and finely chop the onion. Heat oil in a large frying pan over medium heat and cook the potatoes until golden and crisp on the edges. Add the beef and cook until browned and cooked through, stirring frequently. Add the chopped onion and continue cooking until softened and slightly caramelized. Season with salt and black pepper, mixing everything evenly. Cook until all components are well combined and heated through. Serve hot directly from the pan.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 40g · fat 25g","storage":"Store in the refrigerator for up to 3 days and reheat in a pan.","chefNotes":"Use leftover meats for authentic flavor.","pairsWith":"fried eggs · pickled beetroot · mustard sauce","trivia":"Originally a way to use up leftovers in Swedish homes.","howThisFeels":"Yesterday's leftovers fried into today's favourite — thrift never tasted so good.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"sweden-lussekatter","name":"Lussekatter","nameAlt":"Saffron Sweet Buns","aliases":["Lucia buns","Lussekatt"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"St Lucia Day","ingredients":"200g flour · 100ml milk · 50g butter · 30g sugar · 5g yeast · 1g saffron","method":"Warm the milk gently and dissolve the saffron into it to release its color and aroma. Add yeast to the milk and allow it to activate until slightly foamy. Mix in flour, sugar, and softened butter to form a soft dough. Knead the dough until smooth and elastic, then let it rise until doubled in size. Shape the dough into traditional S-shaped buns and place them on a baking tray. Allow the buns to rise again until light and puffy. Bake in a preheated oven until golden and cooked through. Let them cool slightly before serving.","cookTime":"120 min","kcal":380,"nutrition":"protein 7g · carbs 55g · fat 15g","storage":"Store in an airtight container for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use high-quality saffron for best color and flavor.","pairsWith":"ginger cookies · fruit compote · vanilla custard","trivia":"Traditionally eaten during the Lucia celebrations in December.","howThisFeels":"Saffron-gold buns curled like sleeping cats — light for the darkest morning.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"sweden-knackebrod","name":"Knackebrod","nameAlt":"Swedish Crispbread","aliases":["Knäckebröd","Crispbread"],"course":"side","type":"bread","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g rye flour · 100ml water · 5g salt","method":"Combine the rye flour, water, and salt in a bowl and mix until a firm dough forms. Knead the dough briefly until smooth and cohesive. Roll the dough out very thinly on a floured surface to achieve a crisp texture. Cut into flat rounds or rectangles and prick the surface with a fork to prevent bubbling. Place the pieces on a baking tray lined with parchment paper. Bake in a preheated oven until completely dry and crisp without browning too much. Allow the crispbread to cool fully so it hardens properly. Store once completely cooled.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":250,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 50g · fat 2g","storage":"Store in a dry airtight container for up to 2 weeks.","chefNotes":"Roll as thin as possible for maximum crispness.","pairsWith":"cheese · smoked salmon · pickled vegetables","trivia":"A staple Swedish bread with a long shelf life.","howThisFeels":"Shatter-crisp rye rounds — a thousand years of Swedish crunch.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"sweden-artsoppa","name":"Ärtsoppa","nameAlt":"Swedish Yellow Pea Soup","aliases":["Artsoppa","Pea Soup Sweden"],"course":"main","type":"legume","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"Thursday meal","ingredients":"150g yellow peas · 100g pork shoulder · 50g onion · 500ml water · 5g salt · 2g thyme","method":"Rinse the yellow peas thoroughly and soak them if necessary to reduce cooking time. Place the peas in a pot with water and bring to a gentle boil. Add chopped pork and onion to the pot and stir well. Reduce the heat and allow the soup to simmer slowly until the peas become soft and begin to break down. Stir occasionally to prevent sticking and ensure even cooking. Add salt and thyme for seasoning and continue simmering until the soup thickens to a hearty consistency. Taste and adjust seasoning if needed. Serve hot once fully cooked.","cookTime":"120 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 40g · fat 15g","storage":"Store in the refrigerator for up to 3 days or freeze for longer storage.","chefNotes":"Slow simmering improves texture and flavor.","pairsWith":"mustard · crispbread · pickled vegetables","trivia":"Traditionally served with pancakes on Thursdays.","howThisFeels":"Thursday's yellow pea soup — a tradition you can set your calendar by.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"sweden-vasterbottensostpaj","name":"Vasterbottensostpaj","nameAlt":"Vasterbotten Cheese Pie","aliases":["Västerbottensostpaj","Swedish Cheese Pie"],"course":"side","type":"savory pie","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"festive","ingredients":"100g flour · 50g butter · 1 egg · 100g cheese · 100ml cream · 5g salt","method":"Prepare a pastry dough by mixing flour and butter until crumbly, then add egg to bind it together. Press the dough into a pie dish evenly, covering the base and sides. Chill the pastry briefly to prevent shrinking during baking. In a bowl, whisk together cream, egg, grated cheese, and salt until smooth. Pour the filling into the prepared crust evenly. Bake in a preheated oven until the filling is set and the top turns golden. Allow the pie to cool slightly so it firms up before slicing. Serve warm or at room temperature.","cookTime":"60 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 15g · carbs 25g · fat 35g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use authentic Västerbotten cheese for best flavor.","pairsWith":"green salad · smoked salmon · pickled onions","trivia":"A popular dish at Swedish midsummer celebrations.","howThisFeels":"Sharp cheese pie from the far north — one bite explains the fuss.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"sweden-ostkaka","name":"Ostkaka","nameAlt":"Swedish Baked Cheesecake","aliases":["Swedish Cheesecake","Ost kaka"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"dessert","ingredients":"200g curd cheese · 100ml cream · 50g sugar · 1 egg · 20g almonds","method":"Preheat the oven and prepare a baking dish by greasing it lightly. In a bowl, mix the curd cheese with cream, sugar, and egg until smooth. Stir in chopped almonds to add texture and flavor. Pour the mixture into the prepared dish evenly. Bake in the oven until the surface is golden and the center is set but still slightly soft. Allow the cheesecake to cool before serving so it firms up properly. Slice into portions and serve slightly warm or chilled. Garnish as desired.","cookTime":"60 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 12g · carbs 30g · fat 28g","storage":"Store in the refrigerator for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Do not overbake to keep the texture creamy.","pairsWith":"berry jam · whipped cream · fresh fruit","trivia":"Differs from American cheesecake with a lighter texture.","howThisFeels":"Wobbly baked cheesecake with jam — humble, warm, and quietly beloved.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"sweden-glogg","name":"Glögg","nameAlt":"Swedish Mulled Wine","aliases":["Glogg","Mulled Wine Sweden"],"course":"drink","type":"beverage","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"200ml red wine · 20g sugar · 5g cinnamon · 2g cloves · 10g raisins","method":"Pour the red wine into a pot and heat it gently over low heat without boiling. Add sugar, cinnamon, and cloves, stirring until the sugar dissolves completely. Allow the mixture to warm slowly so the spices infuse into the wine. Add raisins toward the end of the heating process. Continue heating until the drink is aromatic and fully infused. Remove from heat and strain if desired to remove spices. Serve warm in heatproof glasses. Enjoy immediately while hot.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":220,"nutrition":"protein 1g · carbs 25g · fat 0g","storage":"Store refrigerated for up to 2 days and reheat gently.","chefNotes":"Do not boil to preserve alcohol content and flavor.","pairsWith":"ginger cookies · saffron buns · almond biscuits","trivia":"A traditional holiday drink served during Swedish winters.","howThisFeels":"Warm spiced wine, almonds in the bottom — December glowing in a cup.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"sweden-laxpudding","name":"Laxpudding","nameAlt":"Salmon Potato Bake","aliases":["Salmon Casserole"],"course":"main","type":"bake","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Sweden","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g salmon · 200g potatoes · 1 egg · 100ml milk · 20g butter · 10g dill · 2g salt","method":"Slice potatoes thinly. Layer with salmon in a buttered dish. Mix egg and milk, pour over. Dot with butter and bake until golden and set. Sprinkle with dill before serving.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 26g · carbs 30g · fat 30g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Leftover salmon works well here.","pairsWith":"greens · lemon","trivia":"A traditional Swedish home dish.","howThisFeels":"Soft layers with a gentle, buttery richness.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-farikal","name":"Fårikål","nameAlt":"Norwegian Lamb and Cabbage Stew","aliases":["Farikal","Lamb Cabbage Stew"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"autumn","ingredients":"250g stewing lamb (shoulder or neck) · 200g cabbage · 15g flour · 300ml water · 5g salt · 4g whole black peppercorns","method":"Cut the lamb into generous chunks — bone in if you can, because the bones feed the broth. Slice the cabbage into thick wedges that can hold their shape through a long simmer. Now the ritual: layer meat and cabbage alternately in a heavy pot, starting with lamb on the bottom where the heat is, and scatter whole black peppercorns, a little salt, and a light dusting of flour between each layer — the flour quietly thickens the broth as it cooks, and the whole peppercorns become soft, warm little bursts you eat on purpose. Pour in the water, bring it just to a boil, then drop it to the laziest simmer you can manage. Lid on. Now leave it alone for a good two hours — no stirring, because the layers ARE the dish; the cabbage on top steams in lamb-scented clouds while the meat below turns spoon-tender. It is done when the lamb pulls apart without argument. Taste the broth and add salt only at the end — it concentrates as it cooks. Serve in deep bowls with boiled potatoes, broth and all.","cookTime":"120 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 10g · fat 35g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Don't stir — guard the layers. The whole peppercorns are meant to be eaten: soft, warm, and part of the point.","pairsWith":"boiled potatoes · flatbread · lingonberry sauce","trivia":"Norway's national dish has its own festival: Fårikålens Festdag, the last Thursday of September, when the whole country eats from the same pot.","howThisFeels":"Autumn in a pot — patient, peppery, and proud of it.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-rakfisk","name":"Rakfisk","nameAlt":"Fermented Fish","aliases":["Norwegian Rakfisk"],"course":"starter","type":"seafood","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"winter","ingredients":"150g trout · 20g salt · 5g sugar","method":"This is a controlled ferment, and it rewards respect — read to the end before you start. Use only impeccably fresh trout, gutted and cleaned thoroughly; the quality going in decides everything coming out. Rub the fish inside and out with the salt and sugar, then pack it belly-up and tight into a sterilised, food-safe container with a lid. The fish must stay fully submerged in the brine that forms — top up with a light saltwater brine (about 60g salt per litre) if needed, and weigh the fish down so no part sits exposed to air. Now the non-negotiables: it ferments COLD, between 4 and 8°C — a fridge, never a cupboard, never room temperature — for four to six weeks minimum. Check weekly: the fish should smell sharp, tangy, and clean underneath the intensity. If anything ever smells rotten rather than fermented, or the fish was ever exposed above the brine, do not taste it — throw it out and start again; with fermented fish there are no second opinions. When ready, slice thinly and serve cold the traditional way: on flatbrød with sour cream, red onion, and courage.","cookTime":"20160 min","kcal":280,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 2g · fat 18g","storage":"Keep refrigerated and consume within a few days after opening.","chefNotes":"Cold, submerged, sterile — those three words are the entire safety contract. When in doubt, throw it out.","pairsWith":"flatbread · sour cream · onions","trivia":"Rakfisk has been made since the 1300s, and the town of Fagernes hosts a festival where Norwegians eat over 400 tonnes of it each year.","howThisFeels":"Bold, briny, and centuries deep — Norway's strongest handshake.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-krumkake","name":"Krumkake","nameAlt":"Norwegian Waffle Cookies","aliases":["Krumkaker"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"100g flour · 80g sugar · 50g butter · 1 egg · 5g cardamom","method":"Mix the egg and sugar together until light and slightly fluffy. Add melted butter and mix until combined. Stir in flour and cardamom to create a smooth batter. Heat a krumkake iron and lightly grease it. Spoon a small amount of batter onto the iron and cook until golden. Quickly roll the cookie into a cone shape while still warm. Allow it to cool and harden. Repeat with remaining batter. Serve once cooled.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":320,"nutrition":"protein 5g · carbs 45g · fat 15g","storage":"Store in an airtight container for up to 1 week.","chefNotes":"Roll immediately while warm to shape properly.","pairsWith":"whipped cream · berry compote · chocolate sauce","trivia":"A traditional holiday cookie in Norway.","howThisFeels":"Lace-thin waffle cones — festive, fragile, and gone in a crackle.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-brunost-sandwich","name":"Brunost Sandwich","nameAlt":"Brown Cheese Open Sandwich","aliases":["Brown Cheese Bread","Brunost"],"course":"starter","type":"dairy","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"breakfast","ingredients":"100g bread · 50g brown cheese · 10g butter","method":"Slice the bread evenly and lay it flat on a clean surface. Spread a thin layer of butter over each slice for flavor. Cut the brown cheese into thin slices using a cheese slicer. Place the cheese slices evenly over the bread. Arrange neatly to cover the surface completely. Serve immediately while fresh. Enjoy as a simple breakfast or snack.","cookTime":"10 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 10g · carbs 30g · fat 15g","storage":"Best consumed fresh.","chefNotes":"Use a cheese slicer for thin slices.","pairsWith":"fruit slices · yogurt · jam","trivia":"Brown cheese is uniquely Norwegian.","howThisFeels":"Caramel-sweet brown cheese on bread — Norway's strangest, kindest habit.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-kj-ttkaker","name":"Kjøttkaker","nameAlt":"Norwegian Meat Cakes","aliases":["Kjottkaker","Norwegian Meat Patties"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"family dinner","ingredients":"150g ground beef · 50g onion · 30ml milk · 20g breadcrumbs · 1 egg · 5g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Finely chop the onion and mix it with ground beef, breadcrumbs, milk, egg, salt, and pepper. Combine thoroughly until smooth and slightly sticky. Shape the mixture into oval patties. Heat a pan over medium heat and cook the patties until browned on both sides. Reduce heat and continue cooking until fully done inside. Turn occasionally to ensure even cooking. Allow them to rest briefly before serving. Serve hot once ready.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 10g · fat 30g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Keep mixture moist for tender patties.","pairsWith":"boiled potatoes · gravy · peas","trivia":"A traditional Norwegian comfort dish.","howThisFeels":"Big soft meat cakes in brown gravy — a hug from a Norwegian kitchen.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-lefse","name":"Lefse","nameAlt":"Soft Norwegian Flatbread","aliases":["Potato Lefse","Norwegian Lefse"],"course":"side","type":"bread","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"holiday","ingredients":"200g potatoes · 80g flour · 20g butter · 5g salt","method":"Boil the potatoes until tender, then mash them until smooth. Mix in butter, flour, and salt to form a soft dough. Divide the dough into small portions. Roll each portion into thin rounds on a floured surface. Cook each round on a hot griddle until lightly browned on both sides. Turn carefully to avoid tearing. Allow to cool slightly before serving. Serve warm or at room temperature.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 50g · fat 10g","storage":"Store in the refrigerator for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Roll thin for best texture.","pairsWith":"butter · sugar · cinnamon","trivia":"A traditional Norwegian festive bread.","howThisFeels":"Soft potato flatbread, buttered and rolled — tenderness you can fold.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-r-mmegr-t","name":"Rømmegrøt","nameAlt":"Sour Cream Porridge","aliases":["Rommegrot","Norwegian Cream Porridge"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"200ml sour cream · 50g flour · 200ml milk · 20g sugar · 5g salt","method":"Pour the sour cream into a wide pot and bring it to a gentle simmer, stirring so it heats evenly. Sift in half the flour, stirring constantly, and keep cooking — now watch closely, because this porridge performs a small miracle: as it cooks, golden butter begins to rise and pool on the surface. Don't panic and don't stir it back in. Spoon that butter off carefully and set it aside like the treasure it is — it comes back at the end. Stir in the remaining flour, then thin the porridge with warm milk, a little at a time, until it is thick, smooth, and glossy. Season with the sugar and — this matters — the salt, because rømmegrøt without salt tastes flat and one-dimensional; the salt is what makes the richness sing. Ladle into bowls, pour the reserved golden butter over the top, and finish with a scatter of sugar and cinnamon. Serve warm, ideally to people who have earned it.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 35g · fat 28g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"The butter rising is the whole event — skim it, save it, and pour it back over the top at the table. Never waste it.","pairsWith":"cinnamon sugar · cured meats · flatbread","trivia":"Rømmegrøt is festival and wedding food in Norway — traditionally so rich it was served to new mothers to rebuild their strength.","howThisFeels":"Silk with a buttered crown — rich enough to earn its festival.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-pinnekj-tt","name":"Pinnekjøtt","nameAlt":"Salted Dried Lamb Ribs","aliases":["Pinnekjott","Norwegian Lamb Ribs"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"250g lamb ribs (cut into individual ribs) · 300ml water · 5g salt","method":"Cut the rack between the bones into single ribs. Soak the salted ribs in cold water overnight to draw out excess salt. Drain the ribs and place them on a rack or birch sticks inside a pot to keep them elevated above the water. Pour water into the bottom of the pot without submerging the meat. Cover the pot tightly and bring the water to a gentle simmer. Steam the ribs slowly until the meat becomes tender and begins to pull away from the bone. Check occasionally to ensure there is enough water for steaming. Once cooked, optionally brown the ribs briefly in the oven for added texture. Serve hot once fully prepared.","cookTime":"180 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 35g · carbs 0g · fat 50g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Do not boil the meat; steaming preserves flavor and texture.","pairsWith":"boiled potatoes · mashed rutabaga · mustard sauce","trivia":"A traditional Norwegian Christmas dish.","howThisFeels":"Salted lamb ribs steamed over birch — Christmas Eve, the western way.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-klippfisk","name":"Klippfisk","nameAlt":"Dried and Salted Cod","aliases":["Clipfish","Norwegian Salt Cod"],"course":"main","type":"seafood","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"200g cod · 300ml water · 20g salt · 10ml oil","method":"Soak the dried salted cod in cold water for at least 24 hours, changing the water several times to remove excess salt. Drain the fish and cut it into manageable portions. Heat oil in a pan over medium heat and lightly cook the fish pieces until they begin to firm up. Transfer the fish to a pot and add fresh water to cover it partially. Simmer gently until the fish becomes tender and flakes easily with a fork. Avoid vigorous boiling to maintain texture. Once cooked, remove from heat and allow it to rest briefly. Serve warm once ready.","cookTime":"90 min","kcal":320,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 0g · fat 20g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Proper soaking is essential to balance saltiness.","pairsWith":"potatoes · tomato sauce · olives","trivia":"Klippfisk was a major Norwegian export.","howThisFeels":"Cod dried on ocean cliffs — the fish that built Norway's coastline.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-fiskesuppe","name":"Fiskesuppe","nameAlt":"Norwegian Creamy Fish Soup","aliases":["Fiske suppe"],"course":"main","type":"seafood","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"everyday meal","ingredients":"150g firm white fish (hake) · 50g carrot · 50g leek · 200ml fish stock · 100ml cream · 5g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Cut the fish into bite-sized pieces and set aside. Slice the carrot and leek into thin pieces. Bring the fish stock to a gentle simmer in a pot. Add the vegetables and cook until they soften. Stir in the cream and reduce the heat. Add the fish and cook until it becomes opaque and flakes easily. Season with salt and pepper. Stir gently to avoid breaking the fish. Serve hot once ready.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 15g · fat 22g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Do not overcook fish.","pairsWith":"flatbread · boiled potatoes · herb salad","trivia":"A coastal Norwegian staple.","howThisFeels":"Creamy fish soup, gentle as fog — the harbour's answer to a cold day.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-sodd","name":"Sodd","nameAlt":"Norwegian Meatball Soup","aliases":["Trøndersodd","Norwegian Sodd"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"celebration","ingredients":"120g beef mince (or half beef, half lamb) · 15g onion (grated) · 10g breadcrumbs · 30ml milk · 50g carrot · 50g potato · 300ml broth · pinch nutmeg · 5g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Make the meatballs: mix the mince with the grated onion, breadcrumbs, milk, nutmeg, salt and pepper until smooth, then roll into small balls and chill 10 min. Peel and dice the carrot and potato. Bring the broth to a gentle boil, add the vegetables and simmer until almost tender. Lower in the meatballs and poach gently 10–12 min, keeping it at a soft simmer so they don't break. Taste, season, and serve hot.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":400,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 25g · fat 20g","storage":"Store in the refrigerator for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use homemade broth for richer flavor.","pairsWith":"flatbread · boiled potatoes · pickled vegetables","trivia":"Sodd is often served at weddings and special occasions.","howThisFeels":"Clear broth with meatballs and mutton — Trøndelag's proudest celebration bowl.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-svele","name":"Svele","nameAlt":"Norwegian Sweet Pancakes","aliases":["Svele pancake","Norwegian griddle cakes"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"snack","ingredients":"150g flour · 100ml milk · 30g sugar · 1 egg · 5g baking soda · 10g butter","method":"Whisk together the egg and sugar until light and slightly frothy. Add milk and mix until well combined. Gradually incorporate the flour and baking soda to form a smooth batter. Allow the batter to rest briefly to develop texture. Heat a pan or griddle and melt a small amount of butter. Pour portions of batter onto the hot surface and cook until bubbles form on the surface. Flip the pancakes and cook until golden and cooked through. Repeat with remaining batter. Serve warm once ready.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 50g · fat 12g","storage":"Store in the refrigerator for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Do not overmix the batter to keep pancakes soft.","pairsWith":"butter · brown cheese · berry jam","trivia":"Traditionally served on ferries along Norway's west coast.","howThisFeels":"Thick, fluffy griddle pancakes — the taste of a ferry crossing done right.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-riskrem","name":"Riskrem","nameAlt":"Rice Cream Dessert with Berry Sauce","aliases":["Norwegian Rice Cream","Riskrem dessert"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"150g rice pudding · 100ml cream · 30g sugar · 50g berry sauce","method":"Prepare rice pudding in advance and allow it to cool completely. Whip the cream with sugar until it forms soft peaks. Gently fold the whipped cream into the rice pudding until light and airy. Mix carefully to maintain the fluffy texture. Spoon the mixture into serving dishes evenly. Top with berry sauce just before serving. Chill briefly to enhance the texture and flavor. Serve cold once ready.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 7g · carbs 50g · fat 20g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Fold gently to keep the dessert light.","pairsWith":"berry compote · almond cookies · vanilla sauce","trivia":"A hidden almond tradition brings luck to the finder.","howThisFeels":"Cold rice cream drowned in red berry sauce — Christmas dinner's gentle landing.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-flatbr-d","name":"Flatbrød","nameAlt":"Traditional Norwegian Flatbread","aliases":["Flatbrod","Norwegian Flatbread"],"course":"side","type":"bread","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g whole grain flour · 120ml water · 5g salt","method":"Mix the flour, water, and salt together to form a firm dough. Knead the dough briefly until it becomes smooth and cohesive. Divide the dough into small portions for easier rolling. Roll each portion into very thin flat rounds on a floured surface. Heat a dry pan or griddle over medium heat. Cook each flatbread until it becomes dry and slightly crisp with light browning. Flip to cook both sides evenly. Allow the flatbread to cool completely so it becomes crisp. Store once fully cooled.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":250,"nutrition":"protein 7g · carbs 50g · fat 2g","storage":"Store in a dry airtight container for up to 2 weeks.","chefNotes":"Thin rolling is key for authentic texture.","pairsWith":"cured meats · cheese · soups","trivia":"Flatbrød has been a staple in Norway for centuries.","howThisFeels":"Paper-thin crisp bread — the old farm's answer to a long winter.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-lutefisk","name":"Lutefisk","nameAlt":"Lye-Treated Dried Cod","aliases":["Lutefisk Norway","Lye Fish"],"course":"main","type":"seafood","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"200g dried salted cod (or ready-prepared lutefisk if you can find it) · 500ml water · 5g salt","method":"An honest word first: true lutefisk is dried cod treated with food-grade lye over several days — a specialist process best left to producers, and ready-made lutefisk is nearly impossible to find in South Africa. So this card honours the tradition the safe way: the long soak and the gentle bake, which is where all the magic lives anyway. Soak the dried salted cod in cold water in the fridge for 24 to 48 hours, changing the water at least four times — you are both rehydrating the fish and coaxing out the salt, and patience here is the whole recipe. Drain the fish, pat it dry, and lay it in a baking dish. Season very lightly with salt — taste-test a corner first, because the fish keeps some of its cure. Bake uncovered at 180°C until the flesh turns from firm to trembling and flakes at the touch of a fork — for real lutefisk this texture goes all the way to wobbling-gelatinous, which Norwegians will defend to their last breath. Rest it briefly, then serve the Christmas way: with boiled potatoes, mushy peas, crisp bacon bits, and plenty of melted butter — because whatever else divides lutefisk lovers and doubters, the butter unites them.","cookTime":"1440 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 0g · fat 18g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"The long cold soak with many water changes is the recipe — everything after is just gentle heat and butter.","pairsWith":"boiled potatoes · peas · bacon","trivia":"Lutefisk divides Norway into devoted lovers and loud objectors — and both sides still eat it every Christmas.","howThisFeels":"Wobbly, mild, and gloriously divisive — a Christmas dare served with butter.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-medisterkaker","name":"Medisterkaker","nameAlt":"Norwegian Pork Meat Patties","aliases":["Medisterkake","Christmas Pork Patties"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"150g ground pork · 30ml milk · 20g breadcrumbs · 1 egg · 5g salt · 2g pepper · 5g nutmeg","method":"Combine the ground pork with breadcrumbs, milk, egg, salt, pepper, and nutmeg in a bowl. Mix thoroughly until the mixture becomes smooth and slightly sticky. Shape the mixture into small round patties using damp hands. Heat a pan over medium heat and add a small amount of fat. Fry the patties until browned on both sides, turning carefully to maintain shape. Reduce the heat and continue cooking until fully cooked through. Allow the patties to rest briefly before serving. Serve warm once ready.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 24g · carbs 8g · fat 32g","storage":"Store in the refrigerator for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Do not overcook to keep the patties juicy.","pairsWith":"boiled potatoes · red cabbage · gravy","trivia":"A staple on Norwegian Christmas tables.","howThisFeels":"Spiced pork patties and gravy — the quiet workhorse of a Norwegian Christmas.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-tilsl-rte-bondepiker","name":"Tilslørte Bondepiker","nameAlt":"Layered Apple and Cream Dessert","aliases":["Hidden Farmer's Daughters","Apple Crumble Cream Dessert"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"dessert","ingredients":"150g apples · 50g breadcrumbs · 30g sugar · 50ml cream · 10g butter","method":"Peel and slice the apples, then cook them gently in a pan with a small amount of sugar until soft and slightly caramelized. In another pan, melt butter and toast the breadcrumbs until golden and crisp. Whip the cream until soft peaks form and it holds its shape. In serving glasses, layer the apple mixture, toasted breadcrumbs, and whipped cream. Repeat the layers until the glass is filled. Finish with a layer of cream on top. Chill briefly to allow the layers to settle. Serve cold once ready.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":380,"nutrition":"protein 5g · carbs 50g · fat 18g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use tart apples for balanced flavor.","pairsWith":"vanilla custard · berry compote · almond biscuits","trivia":"The name humorously translates to 'veiled farm girls'.","howThisFeels":"Layers of apple, crumbs and cream — 'veiled farm girls', sweetly mysterious.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-spekemat","name":"Spekemat","nameAlt":"Norwegian Cured Meats Platter","aliases":["Spekemat platter","Cured Meat Board"],"course":"starter","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"summer","ingredients":"100g cured meats · 100g flatbread · 50g sour cream · 30g cheese","method":"Slice the cured meats thinly and arrange them neatly on a serving platter. Break the flatbread into manageable pieces for serving. Place the sour cream into a small serving bowl and smooth the surface. Slice the cheese into thin pieces or cubes for easy serving. Arrange all components attractively on the platter. Ensure even distribution so each serving includes all elements. Serve immediately at room temperature. Allow guests to assemble their own combinations.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":450,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 25g · fat 28g","storage":"Store components separately in the refrigerator for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use a variety of cured meats for best experience.","pairsWith":"potato salad · pickled vegetables · mustard sauce","trivia":"Popular during Norwegian summer gatherings.","howThisFeels":"Cured meats, flatbread and sour cream — a mountain picnic on a board.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-raspeballer","name":"Raspeballer","nameAlt":"Norwegian Potato Dumplings","aliases":["Komle","Klubb"],"course":"main","type":"starch","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"250g potatoes · 80g flour · 100g pork shoulder · 5g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Peel and grate the potatoes, then squeeze out excess liquid to improve texture. Mix the grated potatoes with flour, salt, and pepper to form a thick dough. Cut the pork into small cubes for filling. Shape portions of the dough into balls, placing pork pieces inside each one. Bring a large pot of salted water to a gentle boil. Carefully add the dumplings and cook until they float and become firm. Allow them to simmer until fully cooked through. Remove with a slotted spoon and serve hot.","cookTime":"60 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 60g · fat 18g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Ensure dumplings are tightly sealed to prevent breaking.","pairsWith":"melted butter · bacon · mashed rutabaga","trivia":"Known by different names across Norway.","howThisFeels":"Heavyweight potato dumplings with salted meat — comfort you can feel tomorrow.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-makrell-i-tomat","name":"Makrell i Tomat","nameAlt":"Mackerel in Tomato Sauce","aliases":["Norwegian Mackerel Spread","Makrell"],"course":"starter","type":"seafood","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"breakfast","ingredients":"120g mackerel · 80g tomato sauce · 50g bread · 5g salt","method":"Cook the mackerel gently until it flakes easily, then remove any bones carefully. Mix the fish with tomato sauce until evenly combined. Season lightly with salt to enhance the flavor. Toast the bread slices until crisp if desired. Spoon the mackerel mixture generously over the bread. Spread evenly to cover the surface. Serve immediately while fresh. Enjoy as an open sandwich.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 20g · fat 18g","storage":"Refrigerate mixture for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use fresh mackerel for best taste.","pairsWith":"boiled eggs · cucumber salad · crispbread","trivia":"A common Norwegian breakfast item.","howThisFeels":"Mackerel in tomato on fresh bread — every Norwegian lunchbox remembers.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-eplekake","name":"Eplekake","nameAlt":"Norwegian Apple Cake","aliases":["Eple kake","Norwegian Apple Cake"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"dessert","ingredients":"150g apples · 100g flour · 80g sugar · 50g butter · 1 egg · 5g cinnamon","method":"Peel and slice the apples evenly and set them aside. In a bowl, mix butter and sugar until light and creamy. Add the egg and beat until well combined. Gradually add flour and cinnamon to form a smooth batter. Pour the batter into a greased baking dish and arrange the apple slices on top. Bake in a preheated oven until the cake is golden and a skewer inserted comes out clean. Allow the cake to cool slightly before slicing. Serve warm or at room temperature.","cookTime":"60 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 55g · fat 18g","storage":"Store in an airtight container for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use tart apples for better flavor balance.","pairsWith":"vanilla sauce · whipped cream · berry compote","trivia":"A classic homemade dessert in Norway.","howThisFeels":"Cinnamon apple cake, still warm — autumn afternoons, Norwegian standard time.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-laks-med-poteter","name":"Laks med Poteter","nameAlt":"Salmon with Potatoes","aliases":["Boiled Salmon Meal"],"course":"main","type":"boiled","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g salmon · 200g potatoes · 10g butter · 5g dill · 2g salt","method":"Boil potatoes until tender. Poach salmon gently in salted water until just cooked. Serve together with butter and fresh dill.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 30g · fat 26g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Do not overcook the salmon.","pairsWith":"lemon · greens","trivia":"A simple Norwegian staple highlighting fresh fish.","howThisFeels":"Pure, clean flavours that need nothing extra.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-laksekaker","name":"Laksekaker","nameAlt":"Salmon Fish Cakes","aliases":["Salmon Cakes"],"course":"main","type":"fried","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g salmon · 1 egg · 40g breadcrumbs · 20g onion · 20ml sunflower oil · 2g salt","method":"Finely chop salmon and mix with egg, breadcrumbs and onion. Form into patties. Fry in oil until golden and cooked through.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 26g · carbs 20g · fat 24g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Chill mixture for easier shaping.","pairsWith":"potatoes · salad","trivia":"Common in Norwegian home cooking and cafés.","howThisFeels":"Crisp outside, soft and savoury inside.","servings":1,"sharedWith":"Sweden"},
{"id":"norway-rakfisk-laks","name":"Rakfisk Laks","nameAlt":"Fermented Salmon","aliases":["Fermented Fish"],"course":"starter","type":"fermented","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"150g salmon · 20g salt","method":"The salmon cousin of the classic trout rakfisk — same tradition, same rules, no shortcuts. Use only impeccably fresh salmon. Rub it thoroughly with the salt, pack it tightly into a sterilised, food-safe container, and make sure it stays fully submerged in its brine — weigh it down if needed. It ferments COLD, 4 to 8°C in the fridge, never at room temperature, for four to six weeks. The smell should be sharp and tangy, never rotten — if in doubt at any point, throw it out without tasting. Slice thinly and serve cold on flatbrød with sour cream and red onion.","cookTime":"20160 min","kcal":280,"nutrition":"protein 24g · carbs 0g · fat 20g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 5 days after opening.","chefNotes":"Same safety contract as the trout classic: cold, submerged, sterile — always.","pairsWith":"flatbread · onions","trivia":"Traditionally made with trout but adapted here with salmon.","howThisFeels":"Sharp, intense, and not for the cautious.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"norway-vafler","name":"Vafler","nameAlt":"Norwegian Heart Waffles","aliases":["Vafler med Brunost","Heart Waffles"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Norway","occasion":"afternoon treat","ingredients":"150g flour · 200ml milk · 2 eggs · 30g sugar · 50g butter (melted) · 3g ground cardamom · 100ml sour cream · 50g brunost (brown cheese) or jam, to serve","method":"Whisk the eggs and sugar until pale, then whisk in the milk, melted butter, and sour cream — the sour cream is the Norwegian tell, and it is what makes these waffles tender instead of merely crisp. Stir in the flour and cardamom until just smooth, then do the thing hurried cooks skip: rest the batter for 30 minutes, so the flour drinks and the waffles bake soft-centred instead of tough. Heat a waffle iron — a heart-shaped one if you own it, because in Norway the shape is half the charm — and grease it lightly. Ladle in batter and bake each waffle until golden with lacy, crisp edges and a soft middle. Now serve them the Norwegian way, warm off the iron: fold a heart around thin slices of brunost and let the warmth melt the brown cheese into soft caramel — or go classic with sour cream and jam. Either way, they are meant to be eaten standing at the iron while the next one bakes.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":380,"nutrition":"protein 10g · carbs 45g · fat 18g","storage":"Best fresh · batter keeps refrigerated for 1 day.","chefNotes":"Rest the batter, keep the middle soft — and warm waffle plus brunost equals instant caramel.","pairsWith":"brunost · sour cream · strawberry jam · coffee","trivia":"Heart-shaped waffle irons have been beloved in Norway since the 1800s — vafler appear at every school fundraiser, ski cabin, and church bazaar in the country.","howThisFeels":"Warm hearts off the iron — Norway's afternoon hug.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-smorrebrod","name":"Smorrebrod","nameAlt":"Danish Open-Faced Sandwich","aliases":["Smørrebrød","Danish Open Sandwich"],"course":"main","type":"mixed","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"lunch","ingredients":"100g rye bread · 80g smoked fish · 50g egg · 30g mayonnaise · 20g cucumber · 10g dill","method":"Smørrebrød is built, not made — the Danes treat it like small architecture, and every layer has a job. Start with dense rye bread, sliced not too thick; its dark, sturdy crumb is the foundation everything else stands on. Now the rule Danish grandmothers enforce: butter or mayonnaise spread edge to edge, corner to corner, with no bread showing — Danes call generous buttering 'tandsmør', tooth-butter, thick enough to leave teeth marks. It isn't just flavour: the fat waterproofs the bread so the toppings can't sog it. Lay the smoked fish in overlapping slices like roof tiles, covering the base completely. Slice the boiled egg and arrange it in a neat row down the middle. Add thin cucumber for cold crunch against the rich fish. Finish with fresh dill scattered over the top — the green flourish that says someone cared. And the final law: no top slice, ever. Smørrebrød is open-faced and eaten with a knife and fork, never picked up. Serve immediately, and build the next one while they eat the first.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":400,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 30g · fat 20g","storage":"Best consumed fresh, store components separately if needed.","chefNotes":"Butter to the very edges — 'tandsmør' — and never, ever a top slice. Knife and fork always.","pairsWith":"potato salad · pickled herring · boiled potatoes","trivia":"Danish lunch culture ranks smørrebrød in a strict eating order: herring first, then other fish, then meat, then cheese — never out of sequence.","howThisFeels":"Lunch built like architecture — every layer earns its place.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-frikadeller","name":"Frikadeller","nameAlt":"Danish Pork Meatballs","aliases":["Frikadelle","Danish Meat Patties"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"family dinner","ingredients":"150g ground pork · 50g onion · 30ml milk · 20g breadcrumbs · 1 egg · 5g salt · 2g pepper · 10ml butter","method":"Finely chop the onion and sauté it gently in butter until soft and translucent. In a bowl, combine the ground pork with breadcrumbs, milk, egg, salt, and pepper. Add the cooked onion and mix thoroughly until smooth. Shape the mixture into oval patties using your hands. Heat butter in a pan over medium heat and fry the patties until golden brown on both sides. Reduce the heat and continue cooking until fully cooked through. Turn occasionally to ensure even cooking. Let the patties rest briefly before serving.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 10g · fat 30g","storage":"Store in the refrigerator for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Keep patties moist by not overcooking.","pairsWith":"boiled potatoes · red cabbage · gravy","trivia":"A staple Danish comfort food dish.","howThisFeels":"Pan-browned pork meatballs — Denmark's dinner table, five nights out of seven.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-stegt-flaesk","name":"Stegt Flaesk","nameAlt":"Crispy Pork Belly with Parsley Sauce","aliases":["Stegt flæsk","Danish Pork Belly"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"200g pork belly · 150g potatoes · 15g butter · 15g flour · 100ml milk · 10g parsley · 5g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Slice the pork belly into strips about half a centimetre thick and pat them completely dry — dry pork is the first secret to crisp pork. Salt the strips lightly and lay them on a rack over a baking tray so hot air reaches every side. Roast at 200°C, turning once, until each strip is deep golden and crisp enough to snap — the fat renders slowly and bastes the meat as it goes, which is why the oven beats the pan here: even crisping, no spitting, no standing guard. Meanwhile, boil the potatoes in well-salted water until tender. Now the persillesovs, the parsley sauce that makes this dish whole: melt the butter, stir in the flour, and cook the paste for a minute so it loses its raw taste. Whisk in the milk gradually until you have a smooth, gently thickened sauce. Season with salt and pepper, then — off the heat — stir in a big handful of chopped parsley; adding it off the heat keeps it vividly green instead of army-drab. Plate the potatoes, drown them politely in parsley sauce, and stack the crisp pork alongside. Denmark's national dish, and it earned the title at the table.","cookTime":"50 min","kcal":650,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 35g · fat 45g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Dry pork + a rack + patience = glass-crisp strips. Add the parsley off the heat so it stays green.","pairsWith":"boiled potatoes · parsley sauce · pickled beetroot","trivia":"In 2014 Denmark held a national vote for its national dish — stegt flæsk med persillesovs won by a landslide.","howThisFeels":"Crisp gives way to cream — Denmark's national comfort.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-rugbrod","name":"Rugbrod","nameAlt":"Danish Rye Bread","aliases":["Rugbrød","Danish Dark Rye Bread"],"course":"side","type":"bread","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g rye flour · 150ml water · 50g mixed seeds (sunflower & linseed) · 5g salt · 3g instant yeast","method":"First, a promise this bread makes you: it will never be fluffy, and it isn't trying to be. Rye has almost no gluten, so stop chasing loft and start chasing depth — dense, dark, and seeded is the entire point, because this is the loaf all of Danish lunch stands on. Mix the rye flour, water, seeds, salt, and yeast into a thick, sticky mass — more wet clay than dough, and that's correct; there is no satisfying kneading to be done here, just honest mixing until everything is evenly combined. Scrape it into a greased loaf tin, smooth the top with a wet hand, and let it rise somewhere warm until it has puffed noticeably — rye rises modestly, so don't wait for doubling. Bake low and slow at 170°C until the loaf is deeply dark and sounds hollow when tapped underneath. Now the hardest step: cool it completely, and if you can bear it, wrap it and wait until tomorrow — rye crumb needs a full rest to set, and slicing it warm gives you gum, not bread. Slice thin. Butter well. Build smørrebrød on it and understand Denmark a little better.","cookTime":"90 min","kcal":250,"nutrition":"protein 7g · carbs 50g · fat 2g","storage":"Store in a sealed container for up to 1 week.","chefNotes":"Rye can't be kneaded into fluffiness — mix, bake low, and rest a full day before slicing thin. The wait is part of the recipe.","pairsWith":"cheese · cured meats · smoked fish","trivia":"The average Dane eats rugbrød nearly every day — it's the mandatory foundation of proper smørrebrød.","howThisFeels":"Dense, dark, and dependable — the loaf a nation stands on.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-aebleskiver","name":"Aebleskiver","nameAlt":"Danish Pancake Balls","aliases":["Æbleskiver","Danish Pancake Puffs"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"150g flour · 100ml milk · 1 egg · 30g sugar · 10g butter · 5g baking powder","method":"Whisk together the egg and sugar until light and slightly frothy. Add milk and melted butter and mix well. Gradually incorporate flour and baking powder to form a smooth batter. Heat a special æbleskiver pan and lightly grease each cavity. Pour batter into each cavity and cook until bubbles form on the surface. Turn the balls carefully using a skewer to cook evenly on all sides. Continue cooking until golden and fully cooked through. Remove from the pan and allow to cool slightly. Serve warm.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":380,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 50g · fat 15g","storage":"Store in an airtight container for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Turn frequently for even round shape.","pairsWith":"powdered sugar · fruit jam · chocolate sauce","trivia":"Traditionally served during Christmas markets.","howThisFeels":"Golden pancake balls with jam and icing sugar — December, rolled into spheres.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-risalamande","name":"Risalamande","nameAlt":"Rice Pudding Dessert with Almonds","aliases":["Ris a la mande","Danish Rice Dessert"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"30g pudding rice · 250ml full-cream milk · 15g sugar · 2ml vanilla extract · 100ml cream · 30g almonds (chopped) · 50g cherry sauce","method":"Make the rice pudding first: rinse the pudding rice, then simmer it gently in the full-cream milk with the sugar and vanilla, stirring often, 30–40 minutes until thick and creamy and the rice is tender. Spread it out and cool completely — best made the day before. Whip the cream to soft peaks. Fold the cold rice pudding and the chopped almonds gently through the whipped cream so it stays light and airy. Spoon into bowls and chill until serving. Serve cold with cherry sauce spooned over the top — make your own (recipe linked) or use a good jar.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 7g · carbs 55g · fat 18g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Fold gently to keep it airy.","pairsWith":"cherry sauce · almond biscuits · vanilla cream","trivia":"A hidden almond tradition awards a prize.","howThisFeels":"Cold almond rice pudding with cherry sauce — whoever finds the whole almond wins.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-polser","name":"Polser","nameAlt":"Danish Hot Dogs","aliases":["Pølser","Danish Hotdog"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"street food","ingredients":"150g sausage · 100g bun · 30g pickles · 20g mustard · 20g ketchup · 10g onions","method":"Heat the sausage in hot water or grill until fully cooked and heated through. Warm the bun slightly to improve texture. Place the sausage inside the bun carefully. Add mustard and ketchup evenly along the sausage. Top with chopped onions and sliced pickles. Arrange toppings neatly for balanced flavor. Serve immediately while hot. Enjoy fresh for best taste.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 18g · carbs 40g · fat 30g","storage":"Best consumed fresh.","chefNotes":"Use crispy onions for authentic texture.","pairsWith":"potato salad · coleslaw · fries","trivia":"A popular street food across Denmark.","howThisFeels":"The famous red hot dog with all the works — street-corner happiness.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-stjerneskud","name":"Stjerneskud","nameAlt":"Danish Fish Fillet Open Sandwich","aliases":["Shooting Star Sandwich"],"course":"main","type":"seafood","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"lunch","ingredients":"100g white fish fillet · 80g shrimp · 100g rye bread · 30g mayonnaise · 20g lemon · 10g dill","method":"Season the fish fillet lightly and cook it in a pan until golden and cooked through. Toast the rye bread lightly for a firm base. Place the cooked fish fillet on top of the bread. Arrange shrimp evenly over the fish. Add small spoonfuls of mayonnaise across the top. Squeeze lemon juice over the dish. Garnish with dill for freshness. Serve immediately while fresh.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 30g · fat 20g","storage":"Best consumed fresh.","chefNotes":"Use fresh fish for best results.","pairsWith":"boiled potatoes · cucumber salad · pickled vegetables","trivia":"Named 'shooting star' for its layered look.","howThisFeels":"Fried and steamed fish stacked with prawns — a 'shooting star' landing on rye.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-boller-i-karry","name":"Boller i Karry","nameAlt":"Meatballs in Curry Sauce","aliases":["Danish Curry Meatballs"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"family meal","ingredients":"150g ground pork · 30g onion · 30ml milk · 20g breadcrumbs · 1 egg · 5g curry powder · 200ml broth · 5g salt","method":"Mix ground pork with onion, milk, breadcrumbs, egg, and salt until smooth. Shape into small meatballs. Bring broth to a simmer and cook the meatballs until done. In another pan, heat curry powder gently to release flavor. Add broth gradually to create a sauce. Combine meatballs with sauce and simmer gently. Stir to coat evenly. Cook until slightly thickened. Serve hot.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":450,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 20g · fat 28g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use mild curry for authentic flavor.","pairsWith":"rice · vegetables · pickled cucumber","trivia":"A Danish adaptation of curry flavors.","howThisFeels":"Gentle meatballs in mild curry sauce — Denmark's sunniest weeknight.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-leverpostej","name":"Leverpostej","nameAlt":"Danish Liver Pâté","aliases":["Danish Liver Spread"],"course":"starter","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"lunch","ingredients":"150g pork liver · 50g onion · 30ml milk · 20g flour · 1 egg · 5g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Blend the pork liver with onion, milk, flour, egg, salt, and pepper until smooth. Pour into a greased dish evenly. Place in a water bath for gentle cooking. Bake until set and firm. Check doneness with a skewer. Allow to cool slightly before slicing. Slice into portions and serve warm or cold. Store properly after cooling.","cookTime":"60 min","kcal":380,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 15g · fat 22g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Water bath prevents drying.","pairsWith":"rye bread · pickles · crispy onions","trivia":"A classic Danish sandwich topping.","howThisFeels":"Warm liver pâté with bacon and mushrooms — the rye bread's oldest friend.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-kartoffelsalat","name":"Kartoffelsalat","nameAlt":"Danish Potato Salad","aliases":["Kartoffel salat","Danish Potato Salad"],"course":"side","type":"vegetable","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"summer","ingredients":"200g potatoes · 50g yogurt · 20g mustard · 20g onion · 5g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Boil the potatoes until tender, then allow them to cool before slicing into even pieces. In a bowl, mix yogurt with mustard, salt, and pepper to create a smooth dressing. Finely chop the onion and add it to the dressing for flavor. Combine the sliced potatoes with the dressing gently to avoid breaking them apart. Mix until all the potatoes are evenly coated. Taste and adjust seasoning if needed. Chill the salad in the refrigerator before serving. Serve cold once ready.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 40g · fat 12g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use waxy potatoes for best texture.","pairsWith":"grilled sausages · roast chicken · pickled vegetables","trivia":"Popular at Danish summer gatherings.","howThisFeels":"Creamy potato salad with chives — the loyal shadow of every Danish sausage.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-wienerbrod","name":"Wienerbrod","nameAlt":"Danish Pastry","aliases":["Wienerbrød","Danish Pastry"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"breakfast","ingredients":"200g flour · 100g butter · 50ml milk · 30g sugar · 5g yeast · 1 egg","method":"This pastry is a stack of promises, and the promises are all kept by cold butter. Mix the flour, milk, yeast, sugar, and egg into a smooth dough, knead it lightly, and let it rise until doubled. Meanwhile keep your butter fridge-cold — cold butter is the whole game, because lamination works by trapping thin sheets of butter between layers of dough; in the oven that butter turns to steam, and the steam forces the layers apart into flakes. Warm butter just smears in and you get bread. Roll the dough into a rectangle, lay the butter over two-thirds, and fold like a letter. Roll out and fold again — then into the fridge for 20 minutes, because the fridge does half the work, keeping the butter in sheets and letting the dough relax so it doesn't fight you. Repeat the roll-fold-chill twice more; three folds gives you dozens of whisper-thin layers. Roll out, cut your shapes, and let them rise until puffy and light. Brush with beaten egg for shine, and bake hot until deep golden — pale wienerbrød is a broken promise. Cool just slightly, then listen: proper Danish pastry shatters when you bite it.","cookTime":"120 min","kcal":450,"nutrition":"protein 7g · carbs 50g · fat 25g","storage":"Store in an airtight container for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Cold butter, three folds, and a fridge rest between each — rushing the chill turns pastry back into bread.","pairsWith":"fruit jam · custard · chocolate spread","trivia":"Danes call it wienerbrød — 'Vienna bread' — because Austrian bakers brought lamination to Denmark during an 1850s bakers' strike. The Danes perfected it, and the world named it 'Danish'.","howThisFeels":"A hundred butter layers whispering as they shatter.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-koldskal","name":"Koldskal","nameAlt":"Cold Buttermilk Soup","aliases":["Koldskål","Danish Cold Soup"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"summer","ingredients":"200ml buttermilk · 50g yogurt · 30g sugar · 5g lemon juice · 20g biscuits","method":"In a bowl, combine buttermilk, yogurt, sugar, and lemon juice. Whisk until smooth and slightly frothy. Taste and adjust sweetness or acidity if needed. Chill the mixture in the refrigerator until cold. Crush the biscuits lightly for topping. Pour the cold soup into serving bowls. Sprinkle the crushed biscuits over the top. Serve immediately while chilled.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":250,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 40g · fat 6g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Serve very cold for best taste.","pairsWith":"strawberries · oat biscuits · fruit compote","trivia":"A refreshing Danish summer dessert.","howThisFeels":"Cold buttermilk with vanilla and little biscuits — summer holidays in a bowl.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-roedgroed","name":"Roedgroed","nameAlt":"Red Berry Pudding","aliases":["Rødgrød med fløde","Danish Berry Dessert"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"dessert","ingredients":"150g mixed berries · 300ml water · 30g sugar · 5g starch · 50ml cream","method":"Combine the berries and water in a pot and bring to a gentle boil. Allow the berries to soften and release their juices. Add sugar and stir until dissolved. In a separate bowl, mix starch with cold water to form a slurry. Pour the slurry into the berry mixture while stirring continuously. Cook until the mixture thickens into a pudding-like consistency. Remove from heat and allow it to cool slightly. Serve with cream poured over the top.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 3g · carbs 50g · fat 8g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Adjust sugar depending on berry tartness.","pairsWith":"cream · vanilla custard · biscuits","trivia":"Famously difficult Danish phrase to pronounce.","howThisFeels":"Red berry pudding with cream — the dessert Danes dare you to pronounce.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-hakkebof","name":"Hakkebof","nameAlt":"Danish Beef Patty with Onions","aliases":["Hakkebøf","Hamburger Steak"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"family dinner","ingredients":"180g ground beef · 50g onion · 10ml oil · 5g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Shape the ground beef into a thick patty and season with salt and pepper. Slice the onion into rings. Heat oil in a pan over medium heat and cook the beef patty until browned on both sides. Continue cooking until fully cooked through. In the same pan, cook the onion rings until soft and caramelized. Stir occasionally to prevent burning. Place the onions on top of the patty. Let rest briefly before serving.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 10g · fat 35g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Cook on medium heat for juiciness.","pairsWith":"boiled potatoes · gravy · pickled beetroot","trivia":"A traditional Danish home-style dish.","howThisFeels":"Beef patty under soft onions and gravy — the bachelor dish that grew up beloved.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-braendende-kaerlighed","name":"Braendende Kaerlighed","nameAlt":"Mashed Potatoes with Bacon and Onions","aliases":["Brændende kærlighed","Burning Love"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"comfort food","ingredients":"250g potatoes · 100g bacon · 50g onion · 30ml milk · 10g butter · 5g salt","method":"Peel and boil the potatoes until tender, then mash with milk and butter until smooth. Season with salt to taste. Cut the bacon into pieces and fry until crisp. Remove bacon and cook chopped onion in the same pan until soft. Combine bacon and onion together. Spoon mashed potatoes onto a plate and top with the bacon mixture. Mix lightly before serving. Serve hot.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 50g · fat 35g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use starchy potatoes for best mash.","pairsWith":"pickled beetroot · green salad · mustard","trivia":"The name translates to 'burning love'.","howThisFeels":"Mash crowned with crisp bacon and onions — 'burning love', honestly named.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-flaeskesteg","name":"Flaeskesteg","nameAlt":"Roast Pork with Crackling","aliases":["Flæskesteg","Danish Roast Pork"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"250g pork roast with rind · 5g coarse salt · 2g pepper · 2 bay leaves · 3 whole cloves · 300ml water","method":"Crackling is not luck — it's physics, and this recipe stacks all of it in your favour. Score the pork rind in straight lines about a centimetre apart, cutting through the skin and into the fat but never into the meat — cut the meat and its juices leak out and steam the skin soft, which is the opposite of the mission. Pat the rind bone-dry, then drive the coarse salt deep down into every cut with your fingers; the salt pulls moisture out of the skin, and dry skin is the first law of crackling. Tuck the bay leaves and whole cloves into the scores — Denmark's quiet signature — and season the meat with pepper. Set the roast rind-up in a roasting dish and pour the water around (never over) it: the water steams the meat tender from below while the rind stays high and dry. Roast at 180°C until the meat is cooked through, then turn the oven up fiercely for the final act — the high blast bubbles the rendered fat through the dry skin and sets it into glass. Rest the roast before carving and listen: proper flæskesteg crackles audibly under the knife. Serve with the pan juices, and guard the crackling from thieves.","cookTime":"120 min","kcal":650,"nutrition":"protein 35g · carbs 0g · fat 55g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Score to the fat, never the meat · salt deep into the cuts · water around, not over · finish with fierce heat. That's the whole crackling contract.","pairsWith":"potatoes · red cabbage · gravy","trivia":"Flæskesteg headlines the Danish Christmas table — and the crackling is traditionally divided with diplomatic precision to keep the peace.","howThisFeels":"The crackle you hear across the table — Christmas announcing itself.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-tarteletter","name":"Tarteletter","nameAlt":"Chicken and Asparagus Tartlets","aliases":["Tarteletter med høns i asparges"],"course":"starter","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"festive","ingredients":"100g chicken · 50g asparagus · 100ml cream · 20g flour · 50g tart shells · 5g salt","method":"Cook the chicken until tender and shred into pieces. Cut asparagus into small pieces and cook until tender. Prepare a sauce by heating cream and adding flour to thicken. Stir until smooth. Add chicken and asparagus into the sauce and mix well. Season with salt. Warm tart shells in the oven. Fill shells with the mixture evenly. Serve immediately while warm.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 25g · fat 25g","storage":"Store filling separately for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Avoid overcooking asparagus.","pairsWith":"green salad · pickled vegetables · potatoes","trivia":"A classic Danish party dish.","howThisFeels":"Crisp pastry cups of chicken and asparagus — retro, and proudly so.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-gammeldags-kylling","name":"Gammeldags Kylling","nameAlt":"Old-Fashioned Chicken Stew","aliases":["Danish Chicken Stew"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"family meal","ingredients":"200g chicken · 50g carrot · 50g leek · 200ml broth · 100ml cream · 5g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Cut chicken into pieces and cook in broth until tender. Slice vegetables and add to the broth. Cook until vegetables soften. Stir in cream and simmer gently. Season with salt and pepper. Continue cooking until flavors combine and sauce thickens slightly. Stir occasionally. Serve hot.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 20g · fat 28g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Simmer gently for tender chicken.","pairsWith":"potatoes · parsley · bread","trivia":"A comforting Danish home dish.","howThisFeels":"Old-fashioned chicken with parsley and gravy — Sunday at grandmother's, preserved.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-pebernodder","name":"Pebernodder","nameAlt":"Danish Spiced Cookies","aliases":["Pebernødder","Pepper Nuts"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"150g flour · 80g sugar · 50g butter · 1 egg · 5g spices","method":"Cream butter and sugar until light. Add egg and mix well. Gradually add flour and spices to form dough. Roll dough into small balls. Place on baking tray. Bake until golden. Allow to cool completely. Store once cooled. Serve as snack.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 5g · carbs 45g · fat 15g","storage":"Store in airtight container for up to 2 weeks.","chefNotes":"Keep cookies small for best texture.","pairsWith":"fruit compote · cream · biscuits","trivia":"A Danish Christmas tradition.","howThisFeels":"Tiny spiced biscuits by the handful — December's most poppable tradition.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-citronfromage","name":"Citronfromage","nameAlt":"Lemon Mousse Dessert","aliases":["Lemon Fromage"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"dessert","ingredients":"100ml cream · 50g sugar · 10ml lemon juice · 1 egg · 5g gelatin","method":"Sprinkle the gelatin over 2-3 tbsp cold water and leave 5 minutes to swell. Whisk the egg and sugar until pale. Warm the lemon juice gently (do not boil), add the bloomed gelatin and stir until fully dissolved and clear, then stir into the egg mixture. Whip the cream to soft peaks and fold in gently. Pour into serving dishes and chill until set. Serve cold.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":320,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 35g · fat 18g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Do not overheat gelatin.","pairsWith":"berry sauce · biscuits · fruit","trivia":"A classic light Danish dessert.","howThisFeels":"Airy lemon mousse — sharp, silky, and lighter than a Danish winter is long.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-laksesalat","name":"Laksesalat","nameAlt":"Salmon Salad","aliases":["Salmon Spread"],"course":"starter","type":"salad","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"120g salmon · 50g mayonnaise · 20g onion · 10g dill · 1 lemon · 2g salt","method":"Flake cooked salmon into a bowl. Mix with mayonnaise, finely chopped onion and dill. Add lemon juice and salt. Chill before serving.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 4g · fat 36g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Works well on rye bread.","pairsWith":"bread · pickles","trivia":"Common in Danish open sandwiches.","howThisFeels":"Creamy, fresh, and quietly indulgent.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-ovnbagt-laks","name":"Ovnbagt Laks","nameAlt":"Oven Baked Salmon","aliases":["Baked Salmon"],"course":"main","type":"bake","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g salmon · 20ml olive oil · 5g dill · 1 lemon · 2g salt","method":"Place salmon on a tray. Drizzle with olive oil and season with salt. Bake until just cooked through. Finish with lemon juice and dill.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 2g · fat 30g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Bake gently to keep it moist.","pairsWith":"potatoes · salad","trivia":"A go-to healthy meal in Danish homes.","howThisFeels":"Light, clean, and quietly satisfying.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"denmark-kanelsnegle","name":"Kanelsnegle","nameAlt":"Danish Cinnamon Snails","aliases":["Kanelsnegl","Cinnamon Snail"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Denmark","occasion":"hygge","ingredients":"200g flour · 100ml milk · 60g butter · 40g sugar · 5g yeast · 8g cinnamon · 1 egg · 40g icing sugar (for the glaze)","method":"Warm the milk, dissolve the yeast into it, and mix with the flour, half the sugar, the egg, and a third of the butter into a soft dough. Knead until smooth, then let it rise until doubled. Meanwhile make the filling the Danish way — and understand the Danish way immediately: cream the remaining butter with the remaining sugar and all the cinnamon into a thick, spreadable paste called remonce. The Danes don't sprinkle their filling; they slather it, and the remonce melting into the coils is what makes a snegl a snegl. Roll the dough into a rectangle, spread the remonce edge to edge, and roll it up into a log. Slice into rounds and lay them flat on a tray, pressing each one down gently — a kanelsnegl coils flat and wide like its namesake snail, not tall and proud like its Swedish cousin. Let them rise again until puffy, then bake until golden and smelling like a Copenhagen bakery at seven in the morning. While they are still slightly warm, drizzle with a simple icing-sugar glaze — the sweet white zigzag is the Danish finishing move. Eat warm, ideally with coffee and nowhere urgent to be.","cookTime":"120 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 58g · fat 18g","storage":"Store in an airtight container for up to 2 days · best fresh.","chefNotes":"Remonce, not sprinkles — slather the butter-cinnamon paste, coil flat, and glaze while warm.","pairsWith":"coffee · cold milk","trivia":"Copenhagen bakeries famously sell discounted 'onsdagssnegle' — Wednesday snails — a mid-week tradition Danes take entirely seriously.","howThisFeels":"A lazy Sunday coiled into a snail.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-karjalanpiirakka","name":"Karjalanpiirakka","nameAlt":"Karelian Rice Pie","aliases":["Karjalan piirakka","Karelian Pasty"],"course":"side","type":"pastry","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g rye flour · 50g wheat flour · 100ml water · 5g salt · 150g rice · 300ml milk · 2 boiled eggs · 40g soft butter (for the munavoi)","method":"Start with the filling so it has time to cool: simmer the rice in the milk, stirring often, until it collapses into a thick, creamy porridge — season it with a little of the salt, because bland rice makes a bland pie. While it cools, make the crust: mix the rye flour, wheat flour, water, and remaining salt into a firm dough. Divide it into small balls and roll each into an oval so thin you could almost read through it — the crust is a carrier, not a wall. Spoon cooled rice porridge down the centre of each oval, leaving a border. Now the signature move: fold the edges up and over the rim of the filling and crimp them into small, pinched pleats all the way around — every Karelian grandmother's crimp is slightly different, and that's the point; the pleats are her handwriting. Bake hot at 250°C for 10–15 minutes until the crusts are firm and freckled brown. The moment they come out, brush generously with melted butter to soften the rye. And now the soul of the whole thing — the munavoi: mash the boiled eggs with the soft butter and a pinch of salt into a rough golden spread. Serve the pies warm with munavoi heaped on top, because a karjalanpiirakka without egg butter is a handshake without eye contact.","cookTime":"60 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 60g · fat 8g","storage":"Store in the refrigerator for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Roll the crust paper-thin, crimp with pride, butter-brush straight from the oven — and never skip the munavoi.","pairsWith":"egg butter · smoked fish · cucumber salad","trivia":"Karjalanpiirakka holds EU protected status — true Karelian pies must follow the traditional recipe from the Karelia region.","howThisFeels":"Humble rye hiding a soft heart — Karelia on a plate.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-poronkaristys","name":"Poronkäristys","nameAlt":"Sauteed Reindeer","aliases":["Poronkaristys","Reindeer Stew"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"200g venison (springbok or kudu — reindeer's South African cousins) · 150g potatoes · 50g onion · 15g butter · 5g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"In Lapland this dish starts with a trick worth stealing: the meat is sliced while still half-frozen, because that is the only way to shave it paper-thin — so firm your venison in the freezer for an hour first, then shave it into the thinnest slices your knife allows. Boil the potatoes until tender and keep them warm; mash them if you like, which is how Lapland serves it. Melt the butter in a wide pan over high heat until it foams, and soften the chopped onion in it. Now add the meat shavings in batches — crowd the pan and they steam grey; give them space and they brown hard and fast, which is where all the flavour lives. Season with salt and black pepper as you go, in stages, so every layer of meat is seasoned rather than just the surface. Add a small splash of water to loosen the browned bits from the pan — that is free flavour, don't leave it behind — and let everything simmer briefly until the meat is tender and glossy. Serve piled over the potatoes with a spoonful of redcurrant jelly on the side, standing in for Lapland's lingonberries — the sharp-sweet against the dark, wild meat is the whole conversation.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":450,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 35g · fat 20g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Half-frozen meat shaves thin · hot pan, small batches · redcurrant jelly plays the lingonberry part beautifully.","pairsWith":"lingonberry jam · pickled cucumber · mashed potatoes","trivia":"Poronkäristys is the signature dish of the Sámi people of Lapland — springbok is a respectful South African stand-in for reindeer, wild and lean in just the same spirit.","howThisFeels":"Wild, northern, and honest — supper from the far side of winter.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-hernekeitto","name":"Hernekeitto","nameAlt":"Finnish Pea Soup","aliases":["Herne keitto","Pea Soup Finland"],"course":"main","type":"legume","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"Thursday meal","ingredients":"150g dried peas · 100g pork shoulder · 50g onion · 500ml water · 5g salt · 2g mustard","method":"Rinse the dried peas thoroughly and soak them if needed. Place the peas in a pot with water and bring to a boil. Add chopped pork and onion. Reduce heat and simmer slowly until peas soften and break down. Stir occasionally to prevent sticking. Add salt and mustard for flavor. Continue cooking until thick and hearty. Taste and adjust seasoning. Serve hot.","cookTime":"120 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 40g · fat 15g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Slow simmering improves texture.","pairsWith":"mustard · rye bread · pancakes","trivia":"Traditionally eaten on Thursdays in Finland.","howThisFeels":"Thick pea soup with a swirl of mustard — Finland runs on this.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-kalakukko","name":"Kalakukko","nameAlt":"Fish and Pork Rye Pie","aliases":["Kalakukko pie","Fish bread Finland"],"course":"main","type":"seafood","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"200g firm white fish fillets (hake) · 100g pork belly or shoulder · 130g rye flour (for the dough) · 5g salt","method":"Make a stiff rye dough: mix the rye flour with a pinch of salt and about 80ml cold water, knead briefly and rest 15 min. Cut the fish into chunks and thinly slice the pork. Roll the dough into a thick oval, about 1cm. Pile the pork and fish in the centre and season with the rest of the salt. Lift the dough up and over the filling, pinch the seams tightly into a sealed loaf and smooth any cracks. Set seam-side down in a baking dish and bake low and slow at 150°C for 2–3 hours, until the crust is hard and the filling is tender. Wrap in a cloth and rest 20–30 min, then slice and serve warm with butter.","cookTime":"180 min","kcal":550,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 40g · fat 28g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Knead a stiff rye dough and seal it well — the hard crust steams the filling tender.","pairsWith":"butter · milk · pickled vegetables","trivia":"A traditional dish from Eastern Finland.","howThisFeels":"Fish and pork baked inside a rye loaf — Savonia's edible treasure chest.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-lohikeitto","name":"Lohikeitto","nameAlt":"Finnish Salmon Soup","aliases":["Salmon Soup Finland","Lohi keitto"],"course":"main","type":"seafood","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"everyday meal","ingredients":"150g salmon · 100g potatoes · 50g carrot · 200ml broth · 100ml cream · 5g fresh dill · 5g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Peel and dice the potatoes and carrot into generous, spoon-friendly pieces. Bring the broth to a gentle simmer and cook the vegetables until they are just tender — they should hold their shape, because this is a soup with dignity, not a purée. Now lower the heat right down and understand the golden rule: the salmon is a guest here, not a labourer. Cut it into big chunks and slide them into the barely-simmering broth to poach gently — three to four minutes is all it wants, until the flesh turns opaque and flakes at a nudge. Pour in the cream and warm it through without ever letting the soup boil again; boiled cream splits, and boiled salmon toughens, and both are avoidable tragedies. Season with salt and pepper, tasting as you go. Off the heat, shower in the fresh dill — off the heat is the trick, so it stays bright green and perfumed instead of stewed. Salmon, cream, dill: the Finnish trinity, and every one of the three must arrive at the table alive. Serve steaming, with dark rye bread standing by.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":400,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 25g · fat 22g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Salmon in last, cream never boiled, dill off the heat — protect the trinity and the soup protects you.","pairsWith":"rye bread · butter · dill","trivia":"Lohikeitto is Finland's edible answer to winter — served everywhere from harbour markets to the President's table.","howThisFeels":"Cream, dill, and calm — lake-country comfort in a bowl.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-mustikkapiirakka","name":"Mustikkapiirakka","nameAlt":"Finnish Blueberry Pie","aliases":["Blueberry Pie Finland","Mustikka piirakka"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"dessert","ingredients":"150g blueberries · 100g flour · 50g butter · 50g sugar · 1 egg","method":"Prepare a dough by mixing flour, butter, sugar, and egg until smooth. Press the dough into a baking dish evenly. Spread blueberries over the surface. Bake in a preheated oven until the crust is golden and filling is set. Allow to cool slightly before serving. Slice into portions. Serve warm or cold.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":380,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 50g · fat 18g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use fresh berries for best flavor.","pairsWith":"vanilla sauce · cream · yogurt","trivia":"Wild blueberries are widely used in Finland.","howThisFeels":"Wild blueberry pie with vanilla sauce — the forest's summer report card.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-ruisleipa","name":"Ruisleipa","nameAlt":"Finnish Rye Bread","aliases":["Ruisleipä","Finnish Rye Bread"],"course":"side","type":"bread","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g rye flour · 120ml water · 5g salt","method":"Mix rye flour, water, and salt into a firm dough. Knead briefly until smooth. Shape into flat rounds or loaves. Place onto a baking tray. Bake in a preheated oven until firm and cooked through. Check for a hollow sound when tapped. Allow to cool completely before slicing. Store once cooled.","cookTime":"60 min","kcal":250,"nutrition":"protein 7g · carbs 50g · fat 2g","storage":"Store in a dry container for up to 1 week.","chefNotes":"Dense texture is traditional.","pairsWith":"butter · cheese · smoked fish","trivia":"A staple bread in Finnish cuisine.","howThisFeels":"Dark, dense sour rye — the bread Finns miss most when they leave.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-graavilohi","name":"Graavilohi","nameAlt":"Finnish Cured Salmon","aliases":["Graavilohi Finland","Gravlax Finnish Style"],"course":"starter","type":"seafood","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"holiday","ingredients":"150g salmon · 20g salt · 15g sugar · 5g dill · 2g black pepper","method":"Check the salmon for bones and remove them carefully. Mix salt, sugar, and black pepper in a bowl until evenly combined. Rub the mixture thoroughly over the salmon fillet. Place fresh dill over the fish and wrap tightly. Place the wrapped fish in a dish and weigh it down slightly. Refrigerate for 24 to 48 hours, turning occasionally. Once cured, scrape off excess seasoning. Slice thinly before serving and present chilled.","cookTime":"2880 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 5g · fat 20g","storage":"Refrigerate and consume within 3 days.","chefNotes":"Slice thinly for best texture.","pairsWith":"mustard dill sauce · boiled potatoes · rye bread","trivia":"A Nordic preservation method shared across countries.","howThisFeels":"Salt-and-dill cured salmon — cool, clean, and quietly luxurious.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-kaalilaatikko","name":"Kaalilaatikko","nameAlt":"Finnish Cabbage Casserole","aliases":["Cabbage Box","Kaalilaatikko casserole"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"home cooking","ingredients":"200g cabbage · 100g ground beef · 50g rice · 100ml broth · 5g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Chop the cabbage into thin strips and cook it lightly until softened. Cook the rice separately until tender. In a bowl, combine the cabbage, cooked rice, and ground beef. Add broth and season with salt and pepper. Transfer the mixture into a baking dish evenly. Bake in a preheated oven until the top is golden and the mixture is fully cooked. Stir occasionally during baking if needed. Allow it to rest before serving. Serve warm once ready.","cookTime":"60 min","kcal":450,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 35g · fat 20g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"A touch of sweetness can enhance flavor.","pairsWith":"lingonberry jam · boiled potatoes · cucumber salad","trivia":"A classic Finnish comfort dish.","howThisFeels":"Cabbage and mince baked with a little syrup — school-dinner memories, upgraded.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-lihapiirakka","name":"Lihapiirakka","nameAlt":"Finnish Meat Pie","aliases":["Finnish Meat Pastry","Lihapiirakka pie"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"street food","ingredients":"150g dough · 100g ground beef · 50g rice · 10ml oil · 5g salt","method":"Prepare a simple dough and allow it to rest briefly. Cook the rice until tender and set aside. Brown the ground beef in a pan and season with salt. Mix the beef with the cooked rice to form the filling. Roll out the dough and place portions of filling in the center. Fold and seal the dough into oval shapes. Fry or bake until golden and cooked through. Allow to cool slightly before serving. Serve warm as a snack or meal.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 50g · fat 20g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Seal edges well to prevent leaks.","pairsWith":"pickled cucumber · mustard · coleslaw","trivia":"A popular Finnish street food item.","howThisFeels":"A fried doughnut stuffed with meat and rice — kiosk royalty at midnight.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-pannukakku","name":"Pannukakku","nameAlt":"Finnish Oven Pancake","aliases":["Finnish Pancake","Oven Pancake Finland"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"dessert","ingredients":"150g flour · 200ml milk · 1 egg · 30g sugar · 10g butter","method":"Whisk together flour, milk, egg, and sugar to form a smooth batter. Grease a baking dish with butter and pour in the batter evenly. Place the dish in a preheated oven. Bake until the pancake is golden and set in the center. Check doneness by lightly pressing the surface. Allow it to cool slightly before cutting into pieces. Serve warm or at room temperature. Enjoy once ready.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":380,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 55g · fat 12g","storage":"Store in the refrigerator for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Do not overbake to keep it soft.","pairsWith":"berry jam · whipped cream · fresh fruit","trivia":"A simple and popular Finnish dessert.","howThisFeels":"Oven pancake, custard-soft in the middle — the pea soup's sweet reward.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-siskonmakkarakeitto","name":"Siskonmakkarakeitto","nameAlt":"Finnish Sausage Soup","aliases":["Sausage Soup Finland","Siskonmakkara soup"],"course":"main","type":"meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"everyday meal","ingredients":"150g fresh pork sausage (mild bangers) · 100g potatoes · 50g carrot · 300ml broth · 5g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Peel and dice the potatoes and carrots into small pieces. Bring the broth to a gentle boil in a pot. Add the vegetables and cook until they begin to soften. Squeeze the sausage meat out of the casings and pinch small pieces straight into the soup. Allow the sausage to cook until fully done. Season with salt and pepper to taste. Simmer briefly to combine flavors. Serve hot once ready.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 30g · fat 25g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Add sausage gently to maintain shape.","pairsWith":"rye bread · butter · pickled vegetables","trivia":"A comforting Finnish home-style soup.","howThisFeels":"Delicate sausage dumplings in clear broth — 'sister's sausage', tender by name.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-leipajuusto","name":"Leipäjuusto","nameAlt":"Finnish Squeaky Cheese Dessert","aliases":["Bread Cheese","Finnish Cheese Curds"],"course":"dessert","type":"dairy","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"dessert","ingredients":"150g cheese · 50g cloudberry jam","method":"Slice the cheese into thick pieces and place them in a baking dish. Warm the cheese gently in the oven until it softens but does not melt completely. Remove from the oven once heated through. Spoon cloudberry jam over the warm cheese evenly. Allow the flavors to combine slightly. Serve immediately while warm. Enjoy the contrast of textures. Finish once ready.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 18g · carbs 20g · fat 18g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Do not overheat to maintain texture.","pairsWith":"berry compote · cream · oat biscuits","trivia":"Known for its squeaky texture when eaten.","howThisFeels":"Squeaky baked cheese with warm jam — dessert that talks back.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-runebergintorttu","name":"Runebergintorttu","nameAlt":"Runeberg Torte","aliases":["Runeberg Cake","Runebergin torttu"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"Runeberg Day","ingredients":"100g flour · 80g sugar · 50g butter · 1 egg · 20g jam · 10g icing","method":"Cream the butter and sugar together until light and fluffy. Add the egg and mix until fully incorporated. Gradually add flour to form a smooth batter. Spoon the batter into small molds evenly. Bake in a preheated oven until golden and cooked through. Allow the cakes to cool completely. Top each cake with a small amount of jam and a ring of icing. Let the decoration set before serving. Serve once ready.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":380,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 50g · fat 18g","storage":"Store in an airtight container for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use raspberry jam for authentic flavor.","pairsWith":"coffee cake · berry sauce · whipped cream","trivia":"Named after Finnish poet Johan Ludvig Runeberg.","howThisFeels":"Almond torte with a raspberry crown — the poet's daily breakfast, once a year.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-rosolli","name":"Rosolli","nameAlt":"Finnish Beetroot Salad","aliases":["Rosolli salad","Beetroot Salad Finland"],"course":"side","type":"vegetable","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"150g beetroot · 100g potatoes · 50g carrot · 30g pickles · 50ml cream · 5g salt","method":"Boil the beetroot, potatoes, and carrots separately until tender, then allow them to cool completely. Peel and dice all the vegetables into small uniform cubes. Chop the pickles into similar-sized pieces for even texture. In a bowl, combine all the diced ingredients gently. Add cream and season with salt to taste. Mix carefully to avoid breaking the vegetables. Chill the salad in the refrigerator before serving. Serve cold once flavors have melded.","cookTime":"60 min","kcal":280,"nutrition":"protein 5g · carbs 40g · fat 10g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Dice evenly for balanced texture.","pairsWith":"roast meats · boiled eggs · rye bread","trivia":"A traditional Finnish Christmas side dish.","howThisFeels":"Beetroot, apple and pickle in pink cream — Christmas Eve's brightest bowl.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-paistetut-muikut","name":"Paistetut Muikut","nameAlt":"Fried Vendace Fish","aliases":["Muikku fry","Finnish Fried Fish"],"course":"main","type":"seafood","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"summer","ingredients":"200g vendace fish · 50g flour · 10ml oil · 5g salt","method":"Clean the small fish and pat them dry thoroughly. Toss the fish lightly in flour and salt until evenly coated. Heat oil in a pan over medium heat until hot. Add the fish in batches and fry until golden and crisp. Turn carefully to cook both sides evenly. Continue frying until the fish are fully cooked and crunchy. Remove and drain excess oil on paper. Serve immediately while hot.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 20g · fat 18g","storage":"Best consumed fresh, can refrigerate for 1 day.","chefNotes":"Do not overcrowd the pan for crispiness.","pairsWith":"boiled potatoes · dill sauce · lemon wedges","trivia":"A popular street and market food in Finland.","howThisFeels":"Crisp fried vendace eaten whole — lakeside summer, by the paper cone.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-kanttarellikastike","name":"Kanttarellikastike","nameAlt":"Chanterelle Mushroom Sauce","aliases":["Chanterelle Sauce","Kanttarelli kastike"],"course":"side","type":"sauce","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"autumn","ingredients":"150g chanterelle mushrooms · 50g onion · 100ml cream · 10g butter · 5g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Clean the mushrooms thoroughly and chop them into bite-sized pieces. Finely chop the onion and sauté it in butter until soft. Add the mushrooms and cook until they release moisture and begin to brown. Stir occasionally to ensure even cooking. Pour in the cream and reduce the heat to a gentle simmer. Allow the sauce to thicken slightly. Season with salt and pepper to taste. Serve warm once ready.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":320,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 12g · fat 28g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Do not wash mushrooms excessively to retain flavor.","pairsWith":"boiled potatoes · grilled meat · pasta","trivia":"Chanterelles are widely foraged in Finnish forests.","howThisFeels":"Golden chanterelle cream sauce — the forest paying rent.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-riisipuuro","name":"Riisipuuro","nameAlt":"Finnish Rice Porridge","aliases":["Rice Porridge Finland","Riisi puuro"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"100g rice · 300ml milk · 20g sugar · 2g salt","method":"Combine the rice and a small amount of water in a pot and bring to a gentle boil. Once the water is absorbed, add the milk gradually while stirring. Cook the mixture over low heat, stirring frequently to prevent sticking. Allow the rice to soften completely and absorb the milk. Add sugar and salt to balance the flavor. Continue cooking until the porridge reaches a creamy consistency. Stir occasionally to maintain smooth texture. Serve warm once ready.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 50g · fat 8g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Stir frequently to prevent burning.","pairsWith":"cinnamon sugar · berry compote · milk","trivia":"An almond is hidden in the porridge for luck.","howThisFeels":"Creamy rice porridge under cinnamon sugar — Christmas morning starts here.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-graavilohi-sandwich","name":"Graavilohi Sandwich","nameAlt":"Cured Salmon Open Sandwich","aliases":["Finnish Salmon Sandwich","Graavilohi leipä"],"course":"starter","type":"seafood","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"lunch","ingredients":"100g rye bread · 80g cured salmon · 30g cream cheese · 10g dill · 10g lemon","method":"Slice the rye bread evenly and lay it flat. Spread cream cheese over the bread as a base layer. Arrange thin slices of cured salmon evenly on top. Squeeze a small amount of lemon juice over the salmon. Sprinkle fresh dill for flavor and presentation. Assemble carefully to keep layers intact. Serve immediately while fresh. Enjoy as a light meal or starter.","cookTime":"10 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 25g · fat 18g","storage":"Best consumed fresh.","chefNotes":"Use thin salmon slices for best texture.","pairsWith":"cucumber salad · boiled eggs · pickled vegetables","trivia":"A common Finnish café offering.","howThisFeels":"Cured salmon on dark rye with dill — a Helsinki café in one bite.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-mammi","name":"Mämmi","nameAlt":"Finnish Rye Malt Dessert","aliases":["Mammi","Rye Malt Pudding"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"Easter","ingredients":"150g rye flour · 50g malt · 300ml water · 20g sugar · 2g salt","method":"Let's be honest from the start: mämmi looks challenging, and Finns will cheerfully tell you so themselves — then eat it every Easter without fail, because underneath the appearance is a gentle, malted, caramel-rye sweetness that no other dessert has. The magic is that mämmi sweetens itself. Mix the rye flour and malt with warm water into a thick porridge, then hold it warm — around 60 to 70°C, never boiling — for a few hours, stirring now and then. In that warmth the enzymes in the malt quietly convert the rye starch into sugar; the Finns call this imellyttäminen, the sweetening, and it is the entire soul of the dish — sugar from patience, not from the packet. Stir in the sugar and salt to round it out, transfer to a baking dish, and bake low and slow at 150°C for two to three hours until it is dense, glossy, and nearly black. Cool it completely, then — one more act of patience — let it mature in the fridge for two or three days; mämmi genuinely improves as it rests. Serve it cold with plenty of pouring cream and a sprinkle of sugar, and watch first-timers go from suspicion to a quiet second helping.","cookTime":"120 min","kcal":280,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 55g · fat 2g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 5 days.","chefNotes":"The warm-hold is where malt turns starch to sugar — don't rush it, and don't skip the fridge days. Cream is mandatory.","pairsWith":"cream · milk · sugar","trivia":"Mämmi has been eaten in Finland since the 1500s — and teasing foreigners with its appearance is practically a national sport.","howThisFeels":"Strange, dark, and quietly sweet — Easter's oddest treasure.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-silakkapihvit","name":"Silakkapihvit","nameAlt":"Baltic Herring Patties","aliases":["Herring Patties Finland","Silakka pihvit"],"course":"main","type":"seafood","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"everyday meal","ingredients":"150g herring · 50g flour · 1 egg · 10ml oil · 5g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Clean the herring and remove bones carefully. Dip the fish in beaten egg and coat lightly with flour. Season with salt and pepper. Heat oil in a pan over medium heat. Fry the patties until golden and crisp on both sides. Turn carefully to maintain shape. Continue cooking until fully cooked through. Remove and drain excess oil. Serve hot once ready.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":360,"nutrition":"protein 24g · carbs 20g · fat 20g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use fresh fish for best flavor.","pairsWith":"mashed potatoes · dill sauce · pickled cucumber","trivia":"A traditional coastal Finnish dish.","howThisFeels":"Baltic herring patties, dill-stuffed and pan-fried — the archipelago's everyday gold.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-lohipiirakka","name":"Lohipiirakka","nameAlt":"Salmon Pie","aliases":["Salmon Pastry"],"course":"main","type":"pie","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g salmon · 100g flour · 50g butter · 1 egg · 50ml cream · 20g onion · 2g salt","method":"Make a pastry with flour and butter, then chill. Cook salmon lightly and flake. Mix with egg, cream and onion. Line a tin with pastry, fill and bake until golden and set.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":550,"nutrition":"protein 26g · carbs 35g · fat 34g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use cold butter for a flakier crust.","pairsWith":"salad · dill sauce","trivia":"A traditional Finnish home-style bake.","howThisFeels":"Buttery crust giving way to soft, savoury filling.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"finland-savulohi","name":"Savulohi","nameAlt":"Smoked Salmon","aliases":["Finnish Smoked Salmon"],"course":"starter","type":"smoked","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"celebration","ingredients":"150g salmon · 20g salt · 10g sugar · 5g black pepper","method":"Rub salmon with salt, sugar and pepper. Smoke gently over low heat until cooked through and infused with smoke. Cool slightly before slicing.","cookTime":"60 min","kcal":320,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 3g · fat 22g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Cold smoking gives a softer texture.","pairsWith":"bread · lemon","trivia":"Smoking fish is a long-standing Nordic preservation method.","howThisFeels":"Deep, smoky richness that lingers.","servings":1,"sharedWith":"Sweden"},
{"id":"finland-korvapuusti","name":"Korvapuusti","nameAlt":"Finnish Cinnamon Ear Buns","aliases":["Slapped Ears","Finnish Cinnamon Bun"],"course":"dessert","type":"sweet","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Finland","occasion":"coffee break","ingredients":"200g flour · 100ml milk · 50g butter · 40g sugar · 5g yeast · 5g ground cardamom · 5g cinnamon · 1 egg · 15g pearl sugar (or crushed sugar cubes)","method":"Finland takes the Nordic cinnamon bun and turns the cardamom up — so start there: warm the milk, dissolve the yeast, and mix a soft dough from the flour, sugar, half the egg, softened butter, and a proper, generous dose of ground cardamom; a Finnish bun should smell of cardamom before cinnamon gets a word in. Knead until elastic, then let it rise until doubled. Roll into a rectangle, spread with softened butter, and scatter cinnamon and sugar over the whole surface. Roll into a log — and now the move that gives the bun its name. Instead of straight slices, cut the log into trapezoids with alternating diagonal cuts. Stand each piece on its narrow edge and press down firmly through the middle with two fingers, so the cut spirals bulge out sideways like a pair of ears — korvapuusti means 'slapped ears', and the press is the whole trick: it splays the coils open so they bake into soft, swirled wings. Let them rise until puffy, brush with the rest of the beaten egg, crown with pearl sugar, and bake hot until golden. Serve with strong coffee, because in Finland — the world's most caffeinated nation — a korvapuusti rarely travels alone.","cookTime":"120 min","kcal":410,"nutrition":"protein 9g · carbs 56g · fat 17g","storage":"Store in an airtight container for up to 3 days · revive briefly in a warm oven.","chefNotes":"Double the cardamom courage, cut on the diagonal, and press hard through the middle — the 'ears' are the signature.","pairsWith":"strong coffee · cold milk","trivia":"Finns drink more coffee per person than any nation on earth — and the korvapuusti is its constitutionally mandated companion.","howThisFeels":"A slapped ear never tasted so sweet.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-pierogi-ruskie","name":"Pierogi ruskie","nameAlt":"Potato and Cheese Dumplings","aliases":["pierogi z ziemniakami i twarogiem"],"course":"main","type":"dumpling","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g wheat flour · 100ml water · 1 egg · 5g salt · 150g potatoes · 100g smooth cottage cheese or quark (twaróg) · 50g onion · 20g butter · 2g black pepper","method":"Mix the flour, water, egg and salt into a soft dough — Polish grandmothers say it should feel as soft as an earlobe. Knead briefly, then rest it under an upturned bowl for 20 minutes so the gluten relaxes and the dough rolls thin without fighting you. Boil the potatoes until tender and mash them smooth while still hot — cold potatoes turn gluey. Fry the chopped onion in the butter over medium heat until deep golden, about 5 minutes; that sweetness is what balances the tangy cheese. Mix the potato, cottage cheese and onion, season with the salt and a generous grind of black pepper, and taste the filling now — it is your only chance. Roll the dough thin, cut circles, spoon in filling and pinch the edges firmly closed, keeping the seam clean of filling so nothing bursts in the pot. Boil in well-salted water until they float, then give them one more minute. Now the step that makes them: fry the drained pierogi in butter until the edges crisp and turn golden — boiled pierogi are supper, but butter-fried pierogi are a celebration.","cookTime":"40-50 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 18g · carbs 70g · fat 15g","storage":"Store refrigerated in an airtight container for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Taste the filling before you seal a single pierogi — once they are closed it is too late to fix the seasoning. Keep the sealing edge free of filling or they open while boiling.","pairsWith":"sour cream · fried bacon · dill","trivia":"Despite the name, 'ruskie' does not mean Russian — it points to Red Ruthenia, a historic region around today's western Ukraine. On Polish Christmas Eve, pierogi take their place among the twelve dishes of the Wigilia table.","howThisFeels":"Butter-crisped edges giving way to soft potato and tangy cheese — comfort you pinched closed with your own two hands.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-bigos","name":"Bigos","nameAlt":"Hunter’s Stew","aliases":["kapusta z miesem"],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"winter","ingredients":"150g sauerkraut · 100g white cabbage · 100g pork shoulder · 50g smoked sausage · 50g onion · 20g dried mushrooms · 200ml water · 5g garlic · 2g black pepper · 2g bay leaf","method":"Soak the dried mushrooms in warm water for 20 minutes — and keep that soaking water, it is pure forest flavour. Brown the pork properly in a hot pot for about 6 minutes with the onion; real browning, not grey steaming, is where a great bigos begins. Add the sausage slices and fry another 4 minutes so their smoke wakes up. Stir in the sauerkraut with its juice and the chopped fresh cabbage — the meeting of sour and sweet cabbage is the whole soul of this dish. Add the soaked mushrooms with their strained liquid, the garlic, bay leaf and black pepper. Simmer on low with the lid slightly ajar for 60 minutes, stirring now and then so the bottom never catches, until the cabbages have melted into each other and the pot smells like a forest lodge. Taste before you salt — sauerkraut and smoked sausage bring plenty of their own. If you can bear to wait, cool it and reheat tomorrow: bigos obeys the day-three rule, and every reheat makes it prouder.","cookTime":"70-80 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 20g · fat 35g","storage":"Keeps well refrigerated for up to 4 days and improves in flavor.","chefNotes":"Bigos is a dish you make ahead on purpose — hunters kept the kettle going for days, and day three is famously the best. Salt only at the end; the sauerkraut and sausage usually carry enough.","pairsWith":"rye bread · mustard · pickles","trivia":"Bigos is Poland's national dish, once simmered for days over hunters' fires — it is even praised in verse in the national epic Pan Tadeusz.","howThisFeels":"Two cabbages, smoke and forest mushrooms folded into one deep pot — a stew that grows prouder every day you reheat it.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-zurek","name":"Żurek","nameAlt":"Sour Rye Soup","aliases":["zurek"],"course":"starter","type":"soup","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"Easter","ingredients":"300ml sour rye starter · 100g sausage · 50g bacon · 50g onion · 1 egg · 5g garlic · 2g marjoram · 500ml water · 3g salt","method":"Fry the chopped bacon over medium heat until its fat renders and the pieces crisp, about 5 minutes, then cook the onion in that fat until golden — the smoky base that carries the whole soup. Add the sliced sausage and fry another 4 minutes. Pour in the water and bring it to a gentle boil. Give the jar of sour rye starter a good shake or stir first — the rye flour settles — then stir it into the pot. Simmer on low for 20 minutes; the raw sourness mellows into something round and deep. Add the garlic and the marjoram near the end — marjoram is żurek's signature herb, and adding it late keeps its perfume alive. Taste before salting: the starter is already tangy and the meats are salty, so the soup may need less than you think. Boil the egg separately for 8 minutes, halve it, and rest the halves in each bowl like a little Easter sun.","cookTime":"35-40 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 25g · fat 28g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days and reheat gently.","chefNotes":"No starter? Make your own in 4 days: mix 100g rye flour with 250ml lukewarm water in a clean jar, cover with a cloth, stir once a day and leave at room temperature until it smells brightly, pleasantly sour. It is a live ferment — the same friendly magic as sauerkraut and sourdough.","pairsWith":"rye bread · horseradish · boiled potatoes","trivia":"Żurek is the taste of Polish Easter, often served in a hollowed-out bread bowl — and its soul is fermentation: the sour rye starter is a living culture, tended at home for generations.","howThisFeels":"Sour, smoky and quietly alive — a fermented rye soup that tastes like Easter morning in a Polish farmhouse.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-kotlet-schabowy","name":"Kotlet schabowy","nameAlt":"Breaded Pork Cutlet","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"cutlet","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g pork loin · 50g breadcrumbs · 1 egg · 30g flour · 50ml oil · 3g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"Place the pork loin between two sheets of plastic and pound it thin and even — about half a centimetre. The pounding is half tenderiser, half Sunday ritual, and even thickness means even cooking. Season both sides with the salt and black pepper now, so the flavour lives in the meat and not only in the crust. Dredge in flour and shake off the excess — a thin coat is glue, a thick coat is a raincoat. Dip in beaten egg, then press gently into the breadcrumbs without compacting them; a light touch fries up crisper. Heat the oil over medium until a pinch of crumbs sizzles on contact — too cool and the cutlet drinks oil, too hot and the crust burns before the pork is done. Fry about 4 minutes per side to a deep, even gold. Rest 2 minutes on paper towel so the crust stays shatter-crisp and the juices settle.","cookTime":"15-20 min","kcal":550,"nutrition":"protein 35g · carbs 30g · fat 30g","storage":"Best eaten fresh but can be refrigerated for 2 days.","chefNotes":"Fresh breadcrumbs and a gentle press give the crispest coat. Pound to even thickness above all — a thin edge overcooks while a thick middle stays pink.","pairsWith":"mashed potatoes · cabbage salad · lemon","trivia":"Kotlet schabowy is Poland's Sunday-dinner icon and its proud entry in the great schnitzel rivalry with Vienna — many Poles will tell you, with a smile, that theirs came first.","howThisFeels":"A golden, crackling crumb over juicy pork — the sound of Sunday lunch in a Polish kitchen.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-golabki","name":"Gołąbki","nameAlt":"Stuffed Cabbage Rolls","aliases":["golabki"],"course":"main","type":"stuffed","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"family","ingredients":"2 cabbage leaves · 150g minced pork · 50g rice · 50g onion · 200ml tomato sauce · 5g garlic · 3g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"Boil the cabbage leaves briefly until they bend without cracking, and trim the thick rib flat so the rolls sit snug. Cook the rice for about 10 minutes — just tender, not soft, because it finishes cooking inside the roll. Fry the onion and garlic over medium heat for 5 minutes until fragrant and sweet. Mix the pork, rice, onion and garlic, and season well with the salt and black pepper. Fill each leaf, fold in the sides and roll tight — a loose gołąbek unravels in the pot. Line the pot with any spare leaves so nothing scorches, tuck the rolls in seam-down, and pour over the tomato sauce; the Polish way lets the sauce turn gently sweet as it cooks down. Simmer low and covered for 50 minutes until tender all the way through, then spoon the sweet tomato sauce generously over each little pigeon.","cookTime":"60-70 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 40g · fat 20g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days in sauce.","chefNotes":"Do not overfill — a modest spoonful rolls tight and never tears. Spare leaves on the pot floor are the old insurance against catching.","pairsWith":"potatoes · sour cream · dill","trivia":"Gołąbki means 'little pigeons' in Polish, and the Polish style is known for its gently sweet tomato sauce — every family guards its own version as the correct one.","howThisFeels":"Little pigeons tucked under a sweet tomato blanket — a Polish Sunday wrapped up in a cabbage leaf.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-barszcz-czerwony","name":"Barszcz czerwony","nameAlt":"Beetroot Soup","aliases":["borscht polski"],"course":"starter","type":"soup","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"200g beetroot · 500ml water · 50g onion · 5g garlic · 10ml vinegar · 3g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"Peel and slice beetroot and place in a pot with water. Add chopped onion and garlic. Bring to a boil then simmer on low heat for 40 minutes. Add vinegar, salt and pepper. Strain the soup for a clear broth. Heat gently before serving. Serve hot as a light starter.","cookTime":"45-50 min","kcal":120,"nutrition":"protein 3g · carbs 25g · fat 1g","storage":"Store refrigerated for up to 4 days.","chefNotes":"A splash of vinegar keeps the color vibrant.","pairsWith":"dumplings · rye bread · sour cream","trivia":"Often served on Christmas Eve in Poland.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-placki-ziemniaczane","name":"Placki ziemniaczane","nameAlt":"Potato Pancakes","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"pancake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g potatoes · 1 egg · 30g flour · 50ml oil · 3g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"Grate potatoes and squeeze out excess liquid. Mix with egg, flour, salt and pepper. Heat oil in a pan over medium heat. Spoon batter into the pan and flatten. Fry each pancake for about 3 minutes per side until golden. Drain on paper towel before serving. Serve hot for best texture.","cookTime":"20-25 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 10g · carbs 45g · fat 15g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days and reheat in a pan.","chefNotes":"Squeezing potatoes well prevents soggy pancakes.","pairsWith":"sour cream · applesauce · sugar","trivia":"Can be served sweet or savory depending on toppings.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-kielbasa-grill","name":"Kiełbasa z grilla","nameAlt":"Grilled Sausage","aliases":["kielbasa"],"course":"main","type":"grill","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"bbq","ingredients":"200g kielbasa sausage · 50g onion · 10g mustard · 5g garlic · 2g black pepper","method":"Preheat grill to medium heat. Score sausage lightly to prevent bursting. Grill for about 10 minutes turning occasionally until browned. Add sliced onion to grill and cook until soft, about 6 minutes. Season with garlic and pepper. Serve hot with mustard on the side. Ensure sausage is heated through before serving.","cookTime":"15-20 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 5g · fat 50g","storage":"Best eaten fresh but can be refrigerated for 2 days.","chefNotes":"Avoid high heat to prevent splitting the casing.","pairsWith":"bread · mustard · pickles","trivia":"Kiełbasa refers broadly to Polish sausages of many kinds.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-sernik","name":"Sernik","nameAlt":"Polish Cheesecake","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"cake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"holiday","ingredients":"200g twarog cheese · 100g sugar · 2 eggs · 50g butter · 30g flour · 5g vanilla extract","method":"Preheat oven to 180°C. Mix cheese, sugar and butter until smooth. Add eggs one at a time and blend well. Stir in flour and vanilla extract. Pour batter into a greased baking tin. Bake for about 45 minutes until set. Allow to cool before slicing. Serve chilled or at room temperature.","cookTime":"50-60 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 15g · carbs 35g · fat 25g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 4 days.","chefNotes":"Do not overbake to keep the texture creamy.","pairsWith":"coffee · berries · cream","trivia":"Polish cheesecake often uses twaróg instead of cream cheese.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-makowiec","name":"Makowiec","nameAlt":"Poppy Seed Roll","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"pastry","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"200g flour · 100ml milk · 1 egg · 50g butter · 150g poppy seeds · 80g sugar · 5g yeast","method":"Activate yeast in warm milk for 10 minutes. Mix flour, egg, butter and yeast mixture into a dough. Knead until smooth and let rise for 60 minutes. Grind poppy seeds and mix with sugar. Roll dough flat and spread filling evenly. Roll into a log and place on a baking tray. Bake at 180°C for 35 minutes until golden. Cool before slicing.","cookTime":"50-60 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 12g · carbs 60g · fat 22g","storage":"Store in an airtight container for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Let the dough rise fully for a soft texture.","pairsWith":"tea · coffee · dried fruit","trivia":"Makowiec is a traditional Christmas dessert in Poland.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-kompot","name":"Kompot","nameAlt":"Fruit Drink","aliases":[],"course":"drink","type":"beverage","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g apples · 50g sugar · 500ml water · 2g cinnamon","method":"Chop apples into pieces without peeling. Add to a pot with water and bring to a boil. Reduce heat and simmer for 20 minutes until fruit softens. Add sugar and cinnamon and stir well. Let cool slightly before serving. Serve warm or chilled depending on preference. Strain if a clear drink is desired.","cookTime":"25-30 min","kcal":120,"nutrition":"protein 1g · carbs 30g · fat 0g","storage":"Keep refrigerated for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use a mix of fruits for richer flavor.","pairsWith":"cakes · pastries · biscuits","trivia":"Kompot is commonly served at family meals in Poland.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-krokiety","name":"Krokiety","nameAlt":"Stuffed Crepes","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"crepe","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"holiday","ingredients":"100g flour · 200ml milk · 1 egg · 100g cabbage · 50g mushrooms · 50g breadcrumbs · 50ml oil · 3g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"Mix flour, milk and egg into a smooth batter. Fry thin crepes on medium heat for about 2 minutes per side. Cook cabbage and mushrooms in a pan for 10 minutes until soft. Season filling with salt and pepper. Place filling on crepes and roll tightly. Coat rolls in breadcrumbs. Fry on medium heat for 5 minutes until golden. Serve hot and crisp.","cookTime":"35-45 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 15g · carbs 55g · fat 20g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days and reheat in a pan.","chefNotes":"Make crepes thin for easier rolling.","pairsWith":"barszcz · sour cream · pickles","trivia":"Krokiety are often served with beetroot soup.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-zapiekanka","name":"Zapiekanka","nameAlt":"Open-Faced Baguette Pizza","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"sandwich","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"street-food","ingredients":"1 baguette · 100g mushrooms · 50g cheese · 20g butter · 10g ketchup · 3g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"Slice the baguette lengthwise and lightly butter the cut sides. Chop mushrooms and sauté in butter on medium heat for about 6 minutes until soft. Spread mushrooms over the bread evenly. Top with grated cheese and season with salt and pepper. Bake at 200°C for 10 minutes until cheese melts and bread crisps. Drizzle ketchup on top before serving. Serve hot immediately.","cookTime":"15-20 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 14g · carbs 50g · fat 18g","storage":"Best eaten fresh but can be refrigerated for 1 day.","chefNotes":"Use a crusty baguette for best texture.","pairsWith":"cola · pickles · salad","trivia":"Zapiekanka became popular in Poland during the 1970s as fast food.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-fasolka-po-bretonsku","name":"Fasolka po bretońsku","nameAlt":"Baked Beans with Sausage","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g white beans · 100g sausage · 50g bacon · 50g onion · 200ml tomato sauce · 5g garlic · 3g paprika · 3g salt","method":"Soak beans overnight then boil until tender for about 60 minutes. Fry chopped bacon and onion on medium heat for 6 minutes until golden. Add sausage slices and cook for 4 minutes. Stir in tomato sauce, garlic and paprika. Add cooked beans and mix well. Simmer on low heat for 20 minutes to combine flavors. Serve hot.","cookTime":"80-90 min","kcal":550,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 45g · fat 25g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Soaking beans reduces cooking time and improves texture.","pairsWith":"bread · pickles · mustard","trivia":"Despite its name, the dish is uniquely Polish.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-kluski-slaskie","name":"Kluski śląskie","nameAlt":"Silesian Potato Dumplings","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"dumpling","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"family","ingredients":"200g potatoes · 50g potato starch · 1 egg · 3g salt","method":"Boil potatoes until soft and mash smoothly. Mix with potato starch, egg and salt to form a dough. Shape into small balls and press a dimple in the center. Boil in salted water until they float, about 3 minutes. Remove with a slotted spoon and drain well. Serve warm with sauce or gravy.","cookTime":"30-35 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 60g · fat 3g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"The dimple helps hold sauce when serving.","pairsWith":"gravy · roast meat · cabbage","trivia":"These dumplings originate from the Silesia region.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-kapusta-z-grochem","name":"Kapusta z grochem","nameAlt":"Cabbage with Split Peas","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"stew","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"150g sauerkraut · 100g split peas · 50g onion · 20g butter · 3g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"Soak split peas for 30 minutes then boil until tender for about 40 minutes. Cook sauerkraut in a separate pot with a little water for 30 minutes. Fry chopped onion in butter on medium heat for 5 minutes until golden. Combine peas, sauerkraut and onion. Season with salt and pepper. Simmer together for 10 minutes to blend flavors. Serve warm.","cookTime":"60-70 min","kcal":320,"nutrition":"protein 15g · carbs 45g · fat 8g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Rinse sauerkraut if too sour before cooking.","pairsWith":"bread · mushrooms · potatoes","trivia":"This dish is traditional during Polish Christmas Eve.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-rosol","name":"Rosół","nameAlt":"Clear Chicken Soup","aliases":[],"course":"starter","type":"soup","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"Sunday","ingredients":"200g chicken · 50g carrot · 50g celery · 50g onion · 800ml water · 3g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"Place chicken in a pot with water and bring to a gentle boil. Skim off foam that forms on the surface. Add chopped carrot, celery and onion. Simmer on very low heat for 90 minutes to develop flavor. Season with salt and pepper. Strain for a clear broth before serving. Serve hot as a comforting soup.","cookTime":"90-100 min","kcal":250,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 5g · fat 15g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 4 days.","chefNotes":"Slow simmering keeps the broth clear and rich.","pairsWith":"noodles · parsley · bread","trivia":"Rosół is traditionally served on Sundays in Polish homes.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-paczki","name":"Pączki","nameAlt":"Polish Doughnuts","aliases":["paczki"],"course":"dessert","type":"pastry","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"200g flour · 100ml milk · 1 egg · 50g sugar · 30g butter · 5g yeast · 200ml oil · 50g jam","method":"Warm the milk until it is comfortable to a fingertip — warm wakes yeast, hot kills it — and let the yeast activate in it for 10 minutes until foamy. Mix in the flour, egg, sugar and soft butter and knead for a good 8 minutes until the dough turns smooth and elastic; that kneading is what builds the famous cloud-light crumb. Cover and let it rise for 60 minutes until doubled. Shape into balls with the seam tucked underneath and rest them until puffy again — this second puff is what fries into lightness. Heat the oil over medium: a pinch of dough should sizzle and rise at once. Fry about 4 minutes per side until deep golden; a proper pączek floats half-submerged, and the pale ring around its middle is its badge of honour. Drain, then fill with jam — rose petal jam is the classic — and dust with sugar while still warm.","cookTime":"70-80 min","kcal":450,"nutrition":"protein 10g · carbs 55g · fat 20g","storage":"Best eaten fresh but keeps 1 day.","chefNotes":"Fry in small batches; crowding drops the oil temperature and the doughnuts drink oil instead of puffing. Watch for the pale ring — it means your dough was light and your oil was right.","pairsWith":"coffee · tea · milk","trivia":"On Fat Thursday — Tłusty Czwartek — Poland eats an estimated one hundred million pączki in a single day before Lent begins. Rose petal jam is the time-honoured filling.","howThisFeels":"Cloud-light doughnuts with a rose-jam heart — permission to feast, written straight into the Polish calendar.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-mazurek","name":"Mazurek","nameAlt":"Polish Easter Cake","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"cake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"Easter","ingredients":"200g flour · 100g butter · 80g sugar · 1 egg · 100g jam · 50g nuts","method":"Mix flour, butter, sugar and egg into a dough. Press into a baking tin evenly. Bake at 180°C for 20 minutes until lightly golden. Spread jam over the cooled base. Decorate with nuts on top. Return to oven for 10 minutes to set. Cool completely before slicing. Serve as a festive dessert.","cookTime":"30-35 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 50g · fat 28g","storage":"Store in an airtight container for up to 4 days.","chefNotes":"Do not overbake the base to keep it tender.","pairsWith":"coffee · tea · dried fruit","trivia":"Mazurek is traditionally decorated elaborately for Easter.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-kasza-gryczana","name":"Kasza gryczana","nameAlt":"Buckwheat Groats","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"grain","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g buckwheat groats · 300ml water · 3g salt","method":"Rinse the buckwheat groats under cold water and drain them well. Now the Polish touch: tip the damp groats into the dry pot over medium heat and toast them for 2 minutes, stirring, until they smell warm and nutty — toasting is what separates a proud kasza from a plain one. Add the water and salt, bring to a boil, then drop the heat to low. Cover and cook for about 15 minutes until the water is absorbed, leaving the lid alone. Fluff with a fork so the grains fall loose and separate, and serve warm — in Poland, classically as the bed under a rich gravy or mushroom sauce.","cookTime":"15-20 min","kcal":180,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 35g · fat 2g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"The dry-pot toast is the flavour step — two nutty minutes, no oil needed. After that, hands off the lid.","pairsWith":"mushrooms · vegetables · meat","trivia":"Kasza gryczana has fed Poland for centuries and shines brightest as the nutty base under goulash, roast meat juices or a forest-mushroom sauce.","howThisFeels":"Nutty toasted grains built to catch a good gravy — Poland's oldest side dish, still earning its place.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-ogorki-kiszone","name":"Ogórki kiszone","nameAlt":"Fermented Pickled Cucumbers","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"pickle","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g cucumbers · 500ml water · 10g salt · 5g garlic · 5g dill","method":"Dissolve salt in water to make a brine. Place cucumbers, garlic and dill in a jar. Pour brine over to fully cover. Leave at room temperature for 24 hours to start fermentation. Transfer to a cool place and ferment further. Taste after a few days for desired sourness. Serve chilled as a side.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":40,"nutrition":"protein 2g · carbs 8g · fat 0g","storage":"Store refrigerated after fermentation for several weeks.","chefNotes":"Fermenting for several days deepens the flavor.","pairsWith":"bread · meat · potatoes","trivia":"Fermented cucumbers are a staple in Polish cuisine.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-salatka-jarzynowa","name":"Sałatka jarzynowa","nameAlt":"Vegetable Salad with Mayonnaise","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"salad","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"holiday","ingredients":"100g potatoes · 50g carrot · 50g peas · 1 egg · 50g mayonnaise · 30g pickles · 3g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"Boil the potatoes and carrot until just tender, about 20 minutes, and cool them completely — warm vegetables drink the mayonnaise and turn the salad heavy. Boil the egg for 8 minutes and cool it too. Now the step that defines this salad in Poland: dice everything — vegetables, egg, pickles — into tiny, patient, identical cubes. The even dice is not fussiness, it is the dish; every spoonful should carry a little of everything. Fold in the peas and mayonnaise gently, season with the salt and black pepper, and chill well before serving so the flavours marry and the salad firms into neat slices on the holiday table.","cookTime":"30-35 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 10g · carbs 30g · fat 20g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Cool everything fully before the mayonnaise goes in, and take your time with the knife — jarzynowa is judged by its dice.","pairsWith":"bread · ham · cheese","trivia":"Sałatka jarzynowa is the centrepiece of the Polish Christmas and Easter table, and its tiny, perfectly even dice is a point of family honour.","howThisFeels":"A mosaic of tiny, patient cubes bound in mayonnaise — the salad every Polish holiday table is set around.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-borshch","name":"Борщ","nameAlt":"Ukrainian Beetroot Soup","aliases":["Borshch","Borscht","Borsch"],"course":"main","type":"soup","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g beetroot · 80g cabbage · 60g potato · 50g carrot · 50g onion · 100g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 10g tomato paste · 10ml sunflower oil · 5g garlic · 5g dill · 5g salt","method":"Heat the oil in a heavy pot on medium and soften the chopped onion and carrot for 5 minutes — this quiet start builds the soup's sweet base. Add the diced beef and brown it well for 6 to 8 minutes; those browned edges become the backbone of the broth. Stir in the grated beetroot and tomato paste and cook for 5 minutes — the paste's gentle acidity is the old trick that keeps the beets ruby-red instead of fading to brown. Add the cabbage and potato, pour in water to cover, and season with half the salt. Bring to a boil, then drop to a low simmer for 30 minutes until the potato is tender and the broth runs deep red. Stir in the minced garlic and the rest of the salt and simmer 5 minutes more — garlic added late stays bright instead of disappearing into the pot. Turn off the heat and only now stir in the chopped dill so it lands fresh and green. Let the pot rest for 10 minutes — borshch needs a moment to settle into itself. Serve with a spoon of sour cream and, by law of the table, warm garlic pampushky.","cookTime":"50-60 min","kcal":320,"nutrition":"protein 18g · carbs 28g · fat 12g","storage":"Store in the fridge for up to 3 days in a sealed container.","chefNotes":"Borshch is famously better on day two — and the table law says it arrives with warm garlic pampushky.","pairsWith":"pampushky (garlic rolls) · sour cream · rye bread","trivia":"In 2022 UNESCO inscribed Ukrainian borshch cooking on its list of intangible cultural heritage — official recognition of the hero of the Ukrainian table.","howThisFeels":"A ruby-red bowl of national pride — sweet, sour, and deep enough to hold a whole family's history.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-varenyky","name":"Вареники","nameAlt":"Stuffed Dumplings","aliases":["Varenyky","Pierogi Ukrainian"],"course":"main","type":"dumpling","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"150g flour · 80ml water · 1 egg · 100g potato · 50g onion · 10g butter · 5g salt","method":"Mix the flour, egg, water, and salt into a soft dough and knead for about 8 minutes until smooth and supple. Cover and rest it for 20 minutes — resting relaxes the gluten so the wrappers roll thin without fighting back. Meanwhile boil the potatoes until tender, about 15 minutes, and mash them smooth. Fry the chopped onion slowly in the butter for 5 minutes until sweet and golden, then work it into the mash — this buttery onion is the soul of the filling. Roll the dough thin and cut out circles. Spoon filling onto each, fold into a half-moon, and pinch the edge firmly closed — seal it like a promise, because a leaky varenyk is a broken one. Boil in well-salted water until they float, then give them 1 minute more. Lift out, toss gently in butter so they gleam, and serve warm with sour cream.","cookTime":"40-50 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 12g · carbs 65g · fat 10g","storage":"Keep refrigerated for up to 2 days and reheat gently.","chefNotes":"Pinch the seams firmly and they will survive the boil — and pull them one minute after they float so they stay tender.","pairsWith":"sour cream · fried onions · dill","trivia":"Ukrainians say a varenyk must be sealed like a promise — and no twelve-dish Christmas Eve table is complete without a platter of them.","howThisFeels":"Pillowy half-moons sealed like little promises — buttery, soft, and made for a crowded festive table.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-holubtsi","name":"Голубці","nameAlt":"Stuffed Cabbage Rolls","aliases":["Holubtsi","Holubtsy"],"course":"main","type":"stuffed","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"family","ingredients":"120g cabbage · 100g minced pork · 50g rice · 50g onion · 20g tomato paste · 10ml oil · 5g salt","method":"Soften the cabbage leaves in boiling water for 5 minutes until they roll without cracking. Cook the rice for 10 minutes only — half-done on purpose, so it finishes inside the roll by drinking in the tomato braise. Fry the chopped onion in the oil over medium heat for 5 minutes until golden. Mix the rice, pork, onion and salt, fill the leaves, fold and roll firmly. Arrange the rolls snugly in the pot, spread the tomato paste over them and add water just to cover — this is a braise, not a boil, so keep it low and gentle for 40 minutes. Then the step Ukrainian cooks insist on: rest the pot off the heat for 10 minutes before serving, so the rolls firm up and the tomato broth settles into them.","cookTime":"60-70 min","kcal":390,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 35g · fat 18g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days in a sealed container.","chefNotes":"Half-cooked rice is the secret — fully cooked rice turns mushy inside the roll. The 10-minute rest is not optional; it is when holubtsi become themselves.","pairsWith":"sour cream · rye bread · pickles","trivia":"Holubtsi hold a place of honour at Ukrainian weddings and holiday tables, braised patiently in tomato and always rested before serving.","howThisFeels":"Tomato-braised parcels that firm and sweeten as they rest — the patient star of the Ukrainian holiday table.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-deruny","name":"Деруни","nameAlt":"Potato Pancakes","aliases":["Deruny","Draniki"],"course":"side","type":"pancake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g potato · 30g onion · 1 egg · 20g flour · 20ml oil · 5g salt","method":"Grate potatoes and onion finely and squeeze out excess liquid. Mix with egg, flour, and salt until combined. Heat oil in a pan on medium heat. Spoon batter into the pan and flatten slightly. Fry each side until golden brown for about 4 minutes per side. Remove and drain on paper towels. Serve immediately while hot. Optionally add a dollop of sour cream.","cookTime":"20-25 min","kcal":310,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 40g · fat 12g","storage":"Best eaten fresh but can be refrigerated for 1 day.","chefNotes":"Squeeze moisture well for crispier pancakes.","pairsWith":"sour cream · applesauce · dill","trivia":"Deruny are especially popular in western Ukraine.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-salo","name":"Сало","nameAlt":"Cured Pork Fat","aliases":["Salo"],"course":"starter","type":"cured","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"100g pork fat · 10g salt · 5g garlic · 2g black pepper","method":"Choose fresh, firm pork fat from a butcher you trust — with a cure this simple, the quality of the fat is everything. Rub it generously and thoroughly on all sides with the salt; be bold, because the salt is what cures and protects it. Press the minced garlic and black pepper over the surface. Place it in a clean, sealed container and refrigerate for a full 2 days to cure — cold and clean are non-negotiable here. Slice paper-thin straight from the fridge; thin slices melt on the tongue where thick ones only chew. Lay the slices over dark rye bread and add a little extra raw garlic if you dare. Serve chilled. There is no cooking — salt and time do all the work.","cookTime":"10 min","kcal":800,"nutrition":"protein 2g · carbs 0g · fat 88g","storage":"Keep refrigerated for up to 1 week.","chefNotes":"Fresh fat, a clean sealed container, and constant cold — this is a raw salt cure, so cleanliness and refrigeration do the safety work.","pairsWith":"rye bread · pickles · vodka","trivia":"'Salo is our everything,' Ukrainians like to say — a national love affair with songs, jokes, and even festivals in its honour.","howThisFeels":"Cold, silky slivers melting into warm rye and raw garlic — fierce, honest, and utterly Ukrainian.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-kholodets","name":"Холодець","nameAlt":"Meat Aspic","aliases":["Kholodets"],"course":"starter","type":"aspic","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"holiday","ingredients":"200g pork knuckle · 50g carrot · 30g onion · 5g garlic · 5g salt","method":"Place the pork knuckle in a pot, cover with cold water and bring it just to the edge of a boil — then turn it straight down. The Ukrainian pride in kholodets is a crystal-clear jelly, and clarity is won two ways: never let the pot boil hard, only shiver, and skim faithfully every time foam rises. Simmer gently for 2 hours, then add the carrot and onion and continue for another hour. Add the garlic and salt in the final 10 minutes. Lift out the meat and shred it finely, arrange it in a dish, and ladle the clear broth over through a sieve. Cool, then refrigerate for several hours until set. Serve chilled and cut into proud, glass-clear slices.","cookTime":"180 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 5g · fat 25g","storage":"Keep refrigerated and consume within 3 days.","chefNotes":"The skimming is the skill — a clouded kholodets still tastes fine, but a clear one is worn like a medal. A whisper of a simmer, never a boil.","pairsWith":"mustard · horseradish · bread","trivia":"Kholodets earns its place on festive Ukrainian tables partly as a test of the cook — the clearer the jelly, the louder the silent applause.","howThisFeels":"Crystal-clear savoury jelly with shredded pork suspended like amber — a festive skill worn proudly on a plate.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-pampushky","name":"Пампушки","nameAlt":"Garlic Bread Rolls","aliases":["Pampushky"],"course":"side","type":"bread","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g flour · 80ml milk · 5g yeast · 10g sugar · 20ml oil · 5g garlic · 5g salt","method":"Mix flour, yeast, milk, sugar, and salt into a dough and knead for 10 minutes. Let it rise in a warm place for 1 hour. Shape into small balls and place on a tray. Bake at 180°C for 20 minutes until golden. Mix oil and minced garlic together. Brush garlic oil over hot rolls. Let cool slightly before serving. Serve warm.","cookTime":"90-100 min","kcal":260,"nutrition":"protein 7g · carbs 35g · fat 9g","storage":"Store in a sealed container for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Brush garlic oil immediately after baking for best absorption.","pairsWith":"borshch · butter · herbs","trivia":"Pampushky are often served with borshch.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-kutia","name":"Кутя","nameAlt":"Sweet Wheat Porridge","aliases":["Kutia"],"course":"dessert","type":"porridge","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"100g stampkoring (or wheat berries) · 20g honey · 20g poppy seeds · 30g walnuts · 20g raisins","method":"Soak the stampkoring overnight in plenty of water — this is what lets it cook up tender instead of tough. Drain, cover with fresh water, and boil gently for about 40 minutes until the grains are tender with a gentle chew. Drain and let it cool slightly. Lightly crush the poppy seeds — bruising them releases their sweet, milky flavour. Stir the honey through the warm wheat so it melts into every grain. Fold in the poppy seeds, chopped walnuts, and raisins. Let it stand for 10 minutes so the flavours settle into the wheat. Serve at room temperature — traditionally as the very first taste of the evening.","cookTime":"50-60 min","kcal":330,"nutrition":"protein 9g · carbs 55g · fat 10g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"South African stampkoring is a perfect stand-in for wheat berries — same crushed wheat, same honest chew. The overnight soak is non-negotiable.","pairsWith":"tea · dried fruit · nuts","trivia":"Kutia opens Sviata Vecheria, the Ukrainian Christmas Eve supper of twelve dishes — the first spoonful honours the ancestors, and honey and poppy carry the blessing.","howThisFeels":"A quiet, honeyed spoonful of wheat, poppy and memory — the taste of Christmas Eve beginning.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-syrnyky","name":"Сирники","nameAlt":"Cottage Cheese Pancakes","aliases":["Syrnyky"],"course":"dessert","type":"pancake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"breakfast","ingredients":"150g cottage cheese · 1 egg · 30g flour · 20g sugar · 20ml oil","method":"Start with the dry-cheese rule: if your cottage cheese looks wet, press it through a sieve or squeeze it in a clean cloth first — the drier the cheese, the less flour you need, and the more the pancakes taste of cheese instead of dough. Mix the cheese, egg, flour and sugar into a thick batter that just holds its shape. With floured hands, shape small, plump patties. Heat the oil over medium and fry about 4 minutes per side until golden and crisp-edged, resisting the urge to press them flat. Drain briefly and serve warm — jam, honey or sour cream on the side, never inside.","cookTime":"15-20 min","kcal":290,"nutrition":"protein 14g · carbs 25g · fat 14g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Dry cheese is everything — flour is only there to hold hands, not to take over. Flour your palms, not the batter.","pairsWith":"jam · sour cream · honey","trivia":"Syrnyky are the pancakes Ukraine wakes up to, made from the fresh farmer's cheese called syr that gives them their name.","howThisFeels":"Warm little cheese clouds with crisp golden edges — the breakfast Ukraine wakes up smiling to.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-kapusniak","name":"Капусняк","nameAlt":"Sauerkraut Soup","aliases":["Kapusniak"],"course":"main","type":"soup","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g sauerkraut · 100g pork shoulder · 50g potato · 50g carrot · 30g onion · 5g salt","method":"Cook pork in water on low heat for 30 minutes. Add chopped carrot and onion and cook for 10 minutes. Add sauerkraut and diced potato. Simmer for another 30 minutes until vegetables are tender. Season with salt. Let rest for 10 minutes. Serve hot. Stir before serving.","cookTime":"70-80 min","kcal":340,"nutrition":"protein 18g · carbs 20g · fat 18g","storage":"Store in the fridge for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Rinse sauerkraut lightly if too sour.","pairsWith":"bread · sour cream · herbs","trivia":"Kapusniak has been eaten in Ukraine for centuries.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-uzvar","name":"Узвар","nameAlt":"Dried Fruit Drink","aliases":["Uzvar"],"course":"drink","type":"beverage","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"30g dried apples · 20g dried pears · 20g prunes · 10g honey · 300ml water","method":"Bring water to a boil in a pot. Add dried fruits and simmer on low heat for 20 minutes. Turn off heat and let steep for 15 minutes. Add honey and stir until dissolved. Strain if desired. Serve warm or chilled. This drink requires minimal cooking.","cookTime":"30-35 min","kcal":120,"nutrition":"protein 1g · carbs 30g · fat 0g","storage":"Keep refrigerated for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Let it steep longer for deeper flavor.","pairsWith":"desserts · pastries · nuts","trivia":"Uzvar is traditionally served at Christmas Eve dinner.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-nalysnyky","name":"Налисники","nameAlt":"Thin Crepes with Filling","aliases":["Nalysnyky"],"course":"dessert","type":"crepe","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"100g flour · 150ml milk · 1 egg · 20g sugar · 100g cottage cheese · 10ml oil","method":"Mix flour, milk, egg, and sugar into a smooth batter. Heat a pan on medium and lightly oil it. Pour a thin layer and cook crepes for about 2 minutes per side. Mix cottage cheese with a little sugar. Place filling on each crepe and roll tightly. Arrange in a dish and warm at 160°C for 10 minutes. Serve warm. Optionally add sour cream.","cookTime":"30-40 min","kcal":360,"nutrition":"protein 12g · carbs 45g · fat 14g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Keep crepes thin for best texture.","pairsWith":"sour cream · jam · honey","trivia":"Nalysnyky are popular during Ukrainian celebrations.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-banosh","name":"Банош","nameAlt":"Creamy Cornmeal with Cheese and Pork","aliases":["Banosh","Banush"],"course":"main","type":"porridge","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"80g cornmeal · 150ml cream · 50g feta (or bryndza cheese) · 50g pork belly · 5g salt","method":"Pour the cream into a wide pan and bring it to the gentlest simmer on low heat — cream that boils hard will scorch and split. Rain in the cornmeal slowly with one hand while whisking with the other so no lumps form. Now stir, and keep stirring, for about 10 minutes — Hutsul shepherds say banosh is ready when your arm complains, and that constant stirring is exactly what turns it silky instead of stodgy. It is done when it thickens and pulls away from the pan with little beads of butterfat rising. Meanwhile fry the diced pork belly on medium heat for about 8 minutes until deeply crisp. Spoon the banosh into a bowl, crumble the cheese generously over, and crown it with the crackling and a spoonful of its pan fat. Taste before adding salt — the cheese and pork already carry plenty. Serve immediately, hot from the pan.","cookTime":"20-25 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 16g · carbs 35g · fat 35g","storage":"Best eaten fresh but can be refrigerated for 1 day.","chefNotes":"Low heat and a tireless arm are the whole technique — the moment you stop stirring, it catches.","pairsWith":"pickles · sour cream · herbs","trivia":"Banosh is the shepherd's dish of the Hutsul highlands in the Carpathians, traditionally stirred over an open mountain fire and judged on the smoothness of the stir.","howThisFeels":"Silky fire-stirred cornmeal under salty cheese and crackling — a shepherd's reward after a mountain day.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-kruchenyky","name":"Крученики","nameAlt":"Stuffed Meat Rolls","aliases":["Kruchenyky"],"course":"main","type":"rolled","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"family","ingredients":"150g pork loin (or leg) · 50g mushrooms · 30g onion · 20g breadcrumbs · 20ml oil · 5g salt","method":"Slice pork thinly and flatten with a mallet. Chop mushrooms and onion finely. Fry mushrooms and onion in oil on medium heat for 6 minutes. Mix with breadcrumbs and salt. Place filling on pork slices and roll tightly. Secure with toothpicks and brown in a pan for 5 minutes. Add a little water and simmer on low heat for 30 minutes. Remove picks and serve hot.","cookTime":"45-55 min","kcal":410,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 12g · fat 28g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Secure rolls tightly to keep filling inside.","pairsWith":"mashed potatoes · gravy · pickles","trivia":"Kruchenyky are often served at festive gatherings.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-zrazy","name":"Зрази","nameAlt":"Stuffed Meat Patties","aliases":["Zrazy"],"course":"main","type":"patty","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g minced beef · 50g onion · 1 egg · 30g breadcrumbs · 20ml oil · 5g salt","method":"Mix minced beef with egg, breadcrumbs, and salt. Chop onion finely and sauté in oil for 5 minutes. Form meat mixture into patties and place onion filling inside. Seal and shape into ovals. Fry in oil on medium heat for about 5 minutes per side. Reduce heat and cook for another 10 minutes. Serve hot. Let rest briefly before serving.","cookTime":"25-30 min","kcal":430,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 15g · fat 30g","storage":"Store in fridge for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Keep patties sealed to prevent filling from leaking.","pairsWith":"potatoes · sour cream · salad","trivia":"Zrazy have roots in noble Eastern European cuisine.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-kasha","name":"Каша","nameAlt":"Buckwheat Porridge","aliases":["Kasha","Grechka"],"course":"side","type":"porridge","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g buckwheat · 200ml water · 10ml oil · 5g salt","method":"Rinse the buckwheat under cold water. Bring the water to a boil, add the buckwheat and salt, and turn the heat right down. Cover — and now obey the Ukrainian don't-stir rule: the lid stays on and the spoon stays out, because it is trapped steam, not stirring, that cooks each grain plump and separate. After 15 minutes the water should be gone; turn off the heat and let the pot stand, still covered, for 5 minutes to finish in its own steam. Only now open it, fluff with a fork, and stir the oil through so every grain gleams. Serve warm.","cookTime":"20-25 min","kcal":220,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 40g · fat 4g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Every stir breaks grains and makes porridge instead of kasha — steam does the work if you let it. The 5-minute standing time is part of the cooking.","pairsWith":"vegetables · meat · mushrooms","trivia":"Buckwheat kasha is Ukraine's everyday grain, cooked under a strictly closed lid — the no-peeking, no-stirring rule is passed down like a family heirloom.","howThisFeels":"Plump, separate grains steamed under a lid you were not allowed to lift — Ukraine's daily bread in porridge form.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-paska","name":"Паска","nameAlt":"Easter Bread","aliases":["Paska"],"course":"dessert","type":"bread","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"Easter","ingredients":"200g flour · 100ml milk · 50g sugar · 1 egg · 50g butter · 5g yeast · 5g salt","method":"Mix flour, yeast, warm milk, sugar, and egg into a dough. Knead for 10 minutes until smooth. Add butter and knead again. Let rise in a warm place for 1 hour. Shape into a round loaf and place in a tin. Bake at 180°C for 35 minutes until golden. Let cool before slicing. Serve at room temperature.","cookTime":"100-110 min","kcal":380,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 55g · fat 15g","storage":"Store in a sealed container for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Decorate the top before baking for a traditional look.","pairsWith":"tea · jam · butter","trivia":"Paska is blessed in church during Easter celebrations.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-varenukha","name":"Варенуха","nameAlt":"Spiced Fruit Drink","aliases":["Varenukha"],"course":"drink","type":"beverage","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"50g dried apples · 20g raisins · 10g honey · 300ml water · 2g cinnamon","method":"Bring water to a boil in a pot. Add dried apples and raisins and simmer for 20 minutes. Add cinnamon and continue cooking for 5 minutes. Remove from heat and stir in honey. Let steep for 10 minutes. Strain if desired. Serve warm. This drink is aromatic and comforting.","cookTime":"30-35 min","kcal":140,"nutrition":"protein 1g · carbs 35g · fat 0g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Adjust honey to taste for sweetness.","pairsWith":"desserts · pastries · nuts","trivia":"Varenukha dates back to Cossack traditions.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-olivye","name":"Олів'є","nameAlt":"Potato Salad with Mayonnaise","aliases":["Olivye","Russian Salad"],"course":"side","type":"salad","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"holiday","ingredients":"100g potato · 50g carrot · 50g peas · 50g cooked chicken · 30g mayonnaise · 1 egg · 5g salt","method":"Boil the potatoes and carrots until just tender, about 20 minutes, and the egg for 10 minutes; cool everything properly, because a warm olivye goes greasy. Dice the vegetables, egg and chicken into small cubes and combine with the peas. Add the mayonnaise and salt and mix well — though in Ukraine the 'correct' amount of mayonnaise, chicken or pickle is whatever your own mama used, and every family will defend its proportions to the end. Chill for 30 minutes, give it one gentle stir to refresh it, and serve cold as the anchor of the New Year spread.","cookTime":"30-35 min","kcal":360,"nutrition":"protein 15g · carbs 30g · fat 20g","storage":"Keep refrigerated and consume within 2 days.","chefNotes":"Start from chilled ingredients and the salad stays light. Then adjust it to taste without guilt — olivye has as many correct versions as there are kitchens.","pairsWith":"bread · pickles · meat","trivia":"Olivye anchors the Ukrainian New Year's Eve table, and its true recipe is a friendly national argument — every family's version is the definitive one.","howThisFeels":"The New Year salad made exactly the way your mama made it — which is, of course, the only correct way.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-kyiv-cake","name":"Київський торт","nameAlt":"Kyiv Meringue Cake","aliases":["Kyiv Cake"],"course":"dessert","type":"cake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"celebration","ingredients":"3 egg whites · 80g sugar · 50g hazelnuts · 50g butter · 50g condensed milk","method":"Whisk the egg whites with the sugar until stiff, glossy peaks form — glossy means the sugar has fully dissolved, so the meringue will bake crisp instead of weeping. Fold in the chopped hazelnuts with a light hand to keep the air you just whipped in. Spread the mixture into two even rounds on lined trays. Bake at 150°C for 1 hour — low and slow dries the meringue crisp and pale gold instead of browning it. Cool the layers completely; meringue only reaches full crispness as it cools. Beat the butter with the condensed milk until pale and silky to make the cream. Sandwich and coat the meringue layers with the cream. Chill for 2 hours — the edges soften into the cream while the heart stays crisp, and that contrast is the whole cake. Slice with a sharp knife and keep cold until serving.","cookTime":"120-130 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 40g · fat 32g","storage":"Store in fridge for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Cool the meringue fully before creaming — any warmth melts the buttercream and steals the crunch.","pairsWith":"coffee · tea · berries","trivia":"Kyiv Cake was born in 1956, when a batch of egg whites at a Kyiv confectionery was left out overnight — the rescue became Ukraine's most famous cake and the classic gift carried home on the train.","howThisFeels":"Crisp hazelnut meringue giving way to silky buttercream — a happy accident that tastes like celebration.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-hrechanyky","name":"Гречаники","nameAlt":"Buckwheat Meat Patties","aliases":["Hrechanyky"],"course":"main","type":"patty","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g buckwheat · 100g minced pork · 30g onion · 1 egg · 20ml oil · 5g salt","method":"Cook buckwheat in water for 15 minutes until soft. Fry chopped onion in oil on medium heat for 5 minutes. Mix buckwheat with minced pork, onion, egg, and salt. Shape into patties. Fry in oil for about 5 minutes per side until golden. Reduce heat and cook for another 10 minutes. Serve hot. Let rest briefly before serving.","cookTime":"30-35 min","kcal":400,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 30g · fat 22g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Mix well to bind patties properly.","pairsWith":"sour cream · vegetables · salad","trivia":"Hrechanyky combine two Ukrainian staples: pork and buckwheat.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"ukraine-plachynda","name":"Плацинда","nameAlt":"Stuffed Flatbread","aliases":["Plachynda","Placinda"],"course":"main","type":"flatbread","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Ukraine","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"150g flour · 80ml water · 100g potato · 50g onion · 20ml oil · 5g salt","method":"Mix flour, water, and salt into a dough and knead for 8 minutes. Let rest for 20 minutes. Boil potatoes until tender for 15 minutes and mash them. Fry chopped onion in oil for 5 minutes and mix into potatoes. Roll dough thin and add filling. Fold and seal into flat rounds. Fry in a pan on medium heat for about 4 minutes per side until golden. Serve warm.","cookTime":"40-50 min","kcal":370,"nutrition":"protein 9g · carbs 55g · fat 12g","storage":"Store in fridge for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Roll dough thin for better texture.","pairsWith":"sour cream · herbs · tea","trivia":"Plachynda is popular in southern Ukraine.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-borscht","name":"Борщ","nameAlt":"Moscow-Style Beetroot Soup","aliases":["Borscht","Borsch","Borshch"],"course":"main","type":"soup","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 100g beetroot · 80g cabbage · 50g potato · 30g carrot · 30g onion · 10g tomato paste · 10ml sunflower oil · 5g garlic · 5g salt · 2g black pepper · 5g dill · 10g sour cream · 500ml water","method":"Cut the beef into small chunks and simmer in water on low heat for about 40 minutes until tender. Peel and slice the beetroot and cook separately in a little oil on medium heat for about 10 minutes. Chop cabbage, potato, carrot, and onion into even pieces. Add potato and cabbage to the broth and cook for 15 minutes at a gentle boil. Fry onion and carrot in oil on medium until soft, about 6 minutes, then stir in tomato paste. Combine all vegetables and beetroot into the pot and simmer on low for another 20 minutes. Season with salt, pepper, and crushed garlic, then cook for 5 more minutes. Serve hot with chopped dill and a spoon of sour cream.","cookTime":"90 min","kcal":320,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 28g · fat 14g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days in a sealed container.","chefNotes":"Moscow-style means broth first: the beef simmers alone until tender and everything else joins the finished broth. An overnight rest only deepens it.","pairsWith":"rye bread · sour cream · pickles","trivia":"The Moscow pot builds on beef broth first and finishes with a generous spoonful of smetana — the heartier, meatier style in the wide beet-soup family.","howThisFeels":"A hearty, beef-broth-first bowl finished with cool smetana — Moscow's deeper, meatier take on beet soup.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-pelmeni","name":"Пельмени","nameAlt":"Meat Dumplings","aliases":["Pelmeni","Pel'meni"],"course":"main","type":"dumpling","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"120g flour · 1 egg · 50ml water · 150g minced beef · 50g minced pork · 30g onion · 5g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"Mix the flour, egg, and water into a firm dough and knead for about 10 minutes — it must be firm, because it has to roll thin and still hold juicy meat without tearing. Cover and rest it for 20 minutes so it relaxes. Chop the onion very finely and mix it with both minces, the salt, and the pepper — two meats together keep the filling juicy where one alone goes dry. Roll the dough thin and cut small circles. Set a little filling on each, fold into a half-moon, then pinch the two corners together to make the classic little ear. Bring well-salted water to a rolling boil and drop in the pelmeni, stirring once so nothing sticks. When they float they are nearly there — give them 2 minutes more for the meat inside. Lift out and serve hot, tossed in butter or with a generous spoon of sour cream.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":450,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 40g · fat 18g","storage":"Freeze uncooked dumplings for up to 2 months.","chefNotes":"Underfill rather than overfill — a burst pelmen feeds the pot, not the plate.","pairsWith":"sour cream · vinegar · dill","trivia":"Siberian families would make pelmeni by the hundred and freeze them outdoors in cloth sacks — winter itself was the freezer, and supper was a handful straight into boiling water.","howThisFeels":"Little steaming ears of pasta and juicy meat bobbing in butter — Siberian winter comfort by the bowlful.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-blini","name":"Блины","nameAlt":"Thin Pancakes","aliases":["Blintz","Blini"],"course":"dessert","type":"pancake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"100g flour · 1 egg · 200ml milk · 10g sugar · 5g salt · 10ml sunflower oil","method":"Whisk the flour, egg, milk, sugar, and salt into a smooth, thin batter. Rest it for 15 minutes — the flour drinks in the milk, which is what lets blini fry lace-thin without tearing. Heat a lightly oiled pan over medium heat until properly hot. Pour in a thin layer and swirl immediately so the batter races to the edges. Cook for 1 to 2 minutes until the edges turn to golden lace, flip, and give it 1 minute more. If the first one sticks or tears, smile — the proverb says the first blin is always lumpy; the pan just needed one to find its heat. Stack the rest under a clean cloth so the steam keeps them soft. Serve warm with honey, jam, or sour cream.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":220,"nutrition":"protein 7g · carbs 30g · fat 8g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days wrapped well.","chefNotes":"A properly heated pan and a thin batter are everything — thin enough to swirl to the edges in one turn of the wrist.","pairsWith":"honey · sour cream · jam","trivia":"Round and golden, blini are the sun on a plate — eaten by the stack at Maslenitsa, butter week, the festival that sends the Russian winter on its way.","howThisFeels":"Lace-thin golden suns rolled around jam or sour cream — butter-week joy that melts winter away.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-beef-stroganoff","name":"Бефстроганов","nameAlt":"Beef Stroganoff","aliases":["Beef Stroganoff","Stroganov"],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g beef rump · 50g mushroom · 30g onion · 100ml sour cream · 10g flour · 10ml sunflower oil · 5g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"Slice the beef into thin strips across the grain — cutting across the grain is what keeps quick-cooked beef tender. Season the strips with the salt and pepper. Heat the oil until properly hot and sear the beef on high for about 3 minutes, in batches if needed — crowd the pan and it stews grey instead of browning. Set the beef aside. In the same pan, cook the sliced onion and mushrooms on medium for about 8 minutes until their moisture has cooked off and the flavour concentrates. Stir in the flour and cook for 1 minute to lose its raw taste. Add the sour cream and stir until the sauce is smooth. Return the beef with its juices, and from this moment the rule is gentle: keep it below a simmer on low for 10 minutes — let it boil and the cream splits; keep it gentle and it turns to silk. Taste, adjust the seasoning, and serve hot.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 12g · fat 34g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Never boil after the sour cream goes in — silk on one side of a simmer, split on the other.","pairsWith":"rice · pasta · potatoes","trivia":"Named for the noble Stroganov family, this dish left 19th-century Russia and conquered dinner tables from Paris to São Paulo.","howThisFeels":"Tender seared beef in a silky mushroom and sour-cream ribbon — old aristocracy on a weeknight plate.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-olivier-salad","name":"Салат Оливье","nameAlt":"Russian Potato Salad","aliases":["Olivier Salad"],"course":"side","type":"salad","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"100g potato · 50g carrot · 50g peas · 1 egg · 50g cooked chicken · 50g mayonnaise · 5g salt","method":"Boil the potato and carrot until just tender, about 20 minutes, then cool them fully. Cook the egg for 10 minutes and cool it as well. Dice the potato, carrot, egg and chicken into small, even cubes and combine them in a bowl with the peas. Add the mayonnaise and salt and fold gently — olivier is folded, never stirred, so the cubes stay whole and every spoonful keeps its shape. Chill for at least 30 minutes; the resting is when a bowl of ingredients becomes olivier. Taste, adjust the salt, and serve cold — in Russia, almost certainly at a table counting down to midnight.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 12g · carbs 28g · fat 22g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Fold, do not stir, and always chill before serving. Make it in the afternoon of the 31st, the way half of Russia does.","pairsWith":"bread · pickles · roast meat","trivia":"In Russia it is simply the law of the season: no olivier, no New Year. The salad is prepared in vast bowls on 31 December and eaten as the clock strikes twelve.","howThisFeels":"Creamy cubes folded together while the clock ticks toward midnight — Russia's New Year in a single cold spoonful.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-shchi","name":"Щи","nameAlt":"Cabbage Soup","aliases":["Shchi","Shchi soup"],"course":"main","type":"soup","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g cabbage · 100g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 50g potato · 30g carrot · 30g onion · 10ml sunflower oil · 5g salt · 2g black pepper · 500ml water","method":"Cut beef into pieces and simmer in water on low heat for 40 minutes. Chop cabbage, potato, carrot, and onion. Add potato and cabbage to the broth and cook for 15 minutes. Fry onion and carrot in oil on medium heat for about 6 minutes. Add fried vegetables to the soup and stir. Season with salt and pepper. Simmer for another 15 minutes on low heat. Serve hot.","cookTime":"80 min","kcal":280,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 20g · fat 12g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Sour cabbage can be used for a tangy version.","pairsWith":"rye bread · sour cream · garlic","trivia":"Shchi has been a staple of Russian cuisine for centuries.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-kvass","name":"Квас","nameAlt":"Fermented Bread Drink","aliases":["Kvass"],"course":"drink","type":"fermented","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g rye bread · 10g sugar · 2g yeast · 500ml water","method":"Toast rye bread until dark and crisp at 180°C for about 10 minutes. Place bread in warm water and let steep for 2 hours. Strain the liquid into a clean container. Add sugar and yeast and stir well. Cover loosely and ferment at room temperature for about 24 hours. Strain again and chill in the refrigerator. Serve cold.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":90,"nutrition":"protein 2g · carbs 18g · fat 0g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Fermentation time can vary depending on temperature.","pairsWith":"bread · soup · pickles","trivia":"Kvass has very low alcohol content despite fermentation.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-syrniki","name":"Сырники","nameAlt":"Cheese Pancakes","aliases":["Syrniki","Tvorozhniki"],"course":"dessert","type":"pancake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"breakfast","ingredients":"150g cottage cheese · 50g flour · 1 egg · 10g sugar · 10ml sunflower oil","method":"Mix the cottage cheese, egg, sugar and flour into a soft dough that just comes together. Form small patties with floured hands, keeping them thick — thick syrniki stay creamy in the middle. Heat the oil over a patient medium-low: the Russian test of a good syrnik is an evenly golden face, and only unhurried heat gives you that without a raw centre. Fry about 4 minutes per side, adjusting the flame the moment they colour too fast. Drain briefly and serve them straight from the pan with cold smetana spooned over — the meeting of hot pancake and cool sour cream is the entire point of breakfast.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 18g · carbs 22g · fat 14g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Medium-low and patient wins the even golden crust. Serve hot against cold sour cream — the temperature contrast is not garnish, it is the dish.","pairsWith":"sour cream · jam · honey","trivia":"Syrniki are Russia's weekday breakfast standard, prized for their even golden faces and always partnered with a cold spoonful of smetana.","howThisFeels":"Hot golden pancakes meeting cold smetana — Russia's favourite breakfast argument between warm and cool.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-kholodets","name":"Холодец","nameAlt":"Meat Aspic","aliases":["Kholodets"],"course":"starter","type":"aspic","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"200g pork shoulder · 100g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 50g carrot · 30g onion · 5g garlic · 5g salt · 2g black pepper · 500ml water","method":"Simmer the pork and beef in the water on the lowest heat for 3 hours until falling-apart tender, skimming any foam that rises. This long, slow extraction is the whole recipe: time coaxes the natural gelatin out of the meat and connective tissue, and that gelatin — nothing else — is what will set the dish. Add the carrot and onion and cook 30 minutes more. Lift out the meat and shred it, strain the broth, and season it with the salt, black pepper and garlic. Lay the meat into moulds, pour the warm broth over, cool, and then hand the job to the refrigerator: overnight, unhurried, the broth sets itself into a firm savoury jelly. Serve cold with sharp mustard.","cookTime":"210 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 6g · fat 24g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"No gelatin packets — if the broth sets on its own, you cooked it right. Test a spoonful on a cold saucer: if it firms in the fridge in minutes, the pot is ready.","pairsWith":"mustard · horseradish · bread","trivia":"Kholodets is the quiet showpiece of the Russian New Year table, made a full day ahead because the refrigerator does the final cooking overnight.","howThisFeels":"Set overnight by nothing but patience and honest broth — the New Year table's calm, cold showpiece.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-rasstegai","name":"Расстегай","nameAlt":"Open Fish Pie","aliases":["Rasstegai"],"course":"main","type":"pie","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"120g flour · 1 egg · 50ml milk · 100g firm white fish (hake) · 30g onion · 10ml sunflower oil · 5g salt","method":"Prepare dough by mixing flour, egg, and milk and knead until smooth. Let rest for 20 minutes. Cook fish and onion in oil on medium heat for 8 minutes. Roll dough and cut into circles. Fill with fish mixture and shape with an open top. Place on a tray and bake at 180°C for 25 minutes. Allow to cool slightly before serving. Serve warm.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 38g · fat 18g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Brush with egg wash for a golden crust.","pairsWith":"broth · tea · salad","trivia":"The opening in the pie lets broth be added before serving.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-vinegret","name":"Винегрет","nameAlt":"Beetroot Salad","aliases":["Vinegret","Vinegret salad"],"course":"side","type":"salad","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g beetroot · 50g potato · 50g carrot · 50g pickled cucumber · 30g onion · 20ml sunflower oil · 5g salt","method":"Boil beetroot, potato, and carrot until tender, about 25 minutes. Let cool completely. Dice all vegetables into small cubes. Add chopped pickled cucumber and onion. Mix everything in a bowl. Add oil and salt and toss gently. Chill before serving for 20 minutes. Serve cold.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":180,"nutrition":"protein 4g · carbs 26g · fat 8g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use uniform cuts for better texture.","pairsWith":"bread · pickles · fish","trivia":"The name comes from French vinaigrette but the dish is distinctly Russian.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-ukha","name":"Уха","nameAlt":"Fish Soup","aliases":["Ukha","Ukha soup"],"course":"main","type":"soup","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g firm white fish (hake) · 50g potato · 30g carrot · 30g onion · 5g salt · 2g black pepper · 500ml water","method":"Cut fish into chunks and place in water. Bring to a gentle boil and cook for 15 minutes. Add chopped potato, carrot, and onion. Simmer on low heat for another 20 minutes. Skim foam if needed. Season with salt and pepper. Cook until vegetables are tender. Serve hot.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":260,"nutrition":"protein 24g · carbs 18g · fat 10g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Fresh fish gives the best flavor.","pairsWith":"bread · lemon · herbs","trivia":"Ukha is traditionally cooked outdoors by fishermen.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-vareniki","name":"Вареники","nameAlt":"Stuffed Dumplings","aliases":["Vareniki","Varenyky"],"course":"main","type":"dumpling","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"120g flour · 1 egg · 50ml water · 150g potato · 50g onion · 10ml sunflower oil · 5g salt","method":"Mix the flour, egg and water into a dough and knead for 10 minutes until smooth and springy. Rest it for 20 minutes at room temperature — rested dough rolls thinner and seals better. Boil the potato until soft, about 20 minutes, and mash it. Fry the chopped onion in the oil over medium heat for 6 minutes until golden and stir it through the potato with the salt. Roll the dough thin, cut circles, fill and pinch the edges into a firm half-moon seal. Boil in well-salted water until they float, then two minutes more so the dough cooks through. Lift out gently and serve hot with butter or sour cream.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":360,"nutrition":"protein 10g · carbs 55g · fat 10g","storage":"Freeze uncooked for up to 2 months.","chefNotes":"Keep the sealing edge clean and pinch firmly — a leaky varenik empties itself into the pot. Sweet tooth? The same dough loves a filling of pitted sour cherries and a spoon of sugar.","pairsWith":"sour cream · butter · dill","trivia":"Savoury vareniki are everyday comfort, but Russia's most romanticised version is summer's vishnyoviye vareniki — plump half-moons bursting with sour cherries, part supper and part dessert.","howThisFeels":"Soft half-moon pillows from the same dough that carries Russia's sour-cherry summers.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-okroshka","name":"Окрошка","nameAlt":"Cold Soup","aliases":["Okroshka","Okroshka soup"],"course":"starter","type":"soup","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"summer","ingredients":"100g boiled potato · 50g cucumber · 50g cooked beef · 1 egg · 30g radish · 200ml kvass · 5g dill · 5g salt","method":"Dice boiled potato, cucumber, beef, egg, and radish into small pieces. Place all ingredients into a bowl. Pour chilled kvass over the mixture. Add chopped dill and salt. Stir gently to combine. Chill for 10 minutes before serving. Serve cold.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":220,"nutrition":"protein 14g · carbs 20g · fat 8g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 1 day.","chefNotes":"Keep kvass and solids separate until serving for freshness.","pairsWith":"bread · mustard · sour cream","trivia":"Okroshka is especially popular during hot Russian summers.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-kasha-buckwheat","name":"Гречневая каша","nameAlt":"Buckwheat Porridge","aliases":["Kasha Buckwheat","Buckwheat kasha"],"course":"side","type":"porridge","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g buckwheat · 200ml water · 5g salt · 10ml sunflower oil","method":"Rinse the buckwheat under cold water. Bring the water to a boil with the salt, stir the buckwheat in once, and reduce the heat to low. Cover and cook for about 15 minutes until the water is absorbed. Then comes the step Russians treat as sacred: take the pot off the heat and let it rest, covered, for a full 10 minutes — kasha loves the rest even more than the boil, and this quiet finish is what turns it from cooked to tender all the way through. Fluff with a fork, stir the oil through generously, and serve warm alongside almost anything.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":190,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 32g · fat 5g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Do not rush the 10-minute rest — old Russian kitchens finished kasha in the fading warmth of the stove, and the covered rest recreates exactly that.","pairsWith":"meat · mushrooms · vegetables","trivia":"'You cannot spoil kasha with butter,' says the old Russian proverb — buckwheat kasha is so central to Russian eating that the word kasha can simply mean food itself.","howThisFeels":"Grains finished in the pot's own fading warmth — comfort so trusted, Russia wrote a proverb about it.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-ptichye-moloko","name":"Птичье молоко","nameAlt":"Souffle Cake","aliases":["Ptichye Moloko","Bird's Milk Cake"],"course":"dessert","type":"cake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"celebration","ingredients":"100g sugar · 2 egg · 50g butter · 100ml milk · 10g gelatin · 50g flour","method":"Separate the eggs and beat the whites to stiff peaks. Sprinkle the gelatin over 50ml cold water and leave 5 minutes to bloom. Heat the milk, sugar and butter on low until dissolved, then stir in the bloomed gelatin until fully dissolved and clear (do not boil). Cool to lukewarm, then fold in the whipped whites gently. Pour over a simple sponge base in a mold and chill until set, several hours. Slice and serve cold.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 50g · fat 22g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Allow proper chilling time for best structure.","pairsWith":"tea · coffee · berries","trivia":"The name means 'bird's milk', symbolizing rarity.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-pirozhki","name":"Пирожки","nameAlt":"Stuffed Buns","aliases":["Pirozhki","Piroshki"],"course":"main","type":"bread","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"120g flour · 1 egg · 50ml milk · 100g minced beef · 30g onion · 10ml sunflower oil · 5g salt","method":"Mix flour, egg, and milk to form a dough and knead for 10 minutes. Let rest for 20 minutes. Cook minced beef with chopped onion on medium heat for 8 minutes. Roll dough into small portions and fill with meat. Shape into buns and seal well. Place on a tray and bake at 180°C for 25 minutes until golden. Cool slightly before serving. Serve warm.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":430,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 38g · fat 20g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Brush tops with egg wash for shine.","pairsWith":"tea · soup · salad","trivia":"Pirozhki are popular street food across Russia.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-solyanka","name":"Солянка","nameAlt":"Spicy Meat Soup","aliases":["Solyanka","Solyanka soup"],"course":"main","type":"soup","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 50g sausage · 50g pickled cucumber · 30g onion · 10g tomato paste · 10ml sunflower oil · 5g salt · 2g black pepper · 500ml water","method":"Cook beef in water on low heat for 40 minutes. Slice sausage and fry with onion on medium heat for 6 minutes. Add tomato paste and cook briefly for 2 minutes. Chop pickled cucumber and add to the pan. Combine everything into the broth. Simmer for 15 minutes on low heat. Season with salt and pepper. Serve hot.","cookTime":"70 min","kcal":380,"nutrition":"protein 24g · carbs 12g · fat 26g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"A slice of lemon is often added before serving.","pairsWith":"bread · sour cream · olives","trivia":"Solyanka is known for its rich and tangy flavor.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-medovik","name":"Медовик","nameAlt":"Honey Cake","aliases":["Medovik","Medovik cake"],"course":"dessert","type":"cake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"celebration","ingredients":"100g flour · 50g honey · 50g sugar · 1 egg · 50g butter · 100g sour cream","method":"Melt the honey, sugar, and butter together over gentle heat until smooth — gentle, so the honey deepens without turning bitter. Take the pan off the heat and whisk in the egg quickly so it enriches the dough instead of scrambling. Stir in the flour to form a soft, warm dough. Divide it into portions and roll each one paper-thin. Bake each layer at 180°C for about 5 minutes until deep golden — they bake hard as biscuits, and that is exactly the plan. Beat the sour cream with a little of the sugar until smooth for the cream. Stack the layers with generous cream between each, coating the top and sides too. Now the real magic: chill for several hours, or better overnight — the cream slowly soaks the hard layers until the whole cake cuts like velvet. Slice cold and serve.","cookTime":"60 min","kcal":450,"nutrition":"protein 7g · carbs 55g · fat 22g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 4 days.","chefNotes":"The overnight rest is not optional — it is the recipe.","pairsWith":"tea · berries · coffee","trivia":"Legend says a young palace cook invented medovik for an empress who famously hated honey — she adored the cake before anyone dared tell her what was in it.","howThisFeels":"Honeyed layers softened overnight into velvet — a cake that cuts like a secret kept sweet.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-golubtsy","name":"Голубцы","nameAlt":"Stuffed Cabbage Rolls","aliases":["Golubtsy","Golubtsi"],"course":"main","type":"stuffed","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g cabbage · 100g minced beef · 50g rice · 30g onion · 10g tomato paste · 10ml sunflower oil · 5g salt","method":"Boil the cabbage leaves for about 10 minutes until fully soft and easy to roll. Cook the rice for 15 minutes until tender. Mix the rice with the minced beef, chopped onion and salt — a simple, honest weeknight filling. Roll the filling snugly into the leaves, arrange them seam-down in a pot, and cover with water stirred through with the tomato paste. Simmer low for 40 minutes until the beef is cooked through and the cabbage is silky. Check the seasoning of the broth, then serve each roll hot with a generous cool spoonful of sour cream melting over the top — the hot-and-cold pairing is how Russia eats them.","cookTime":"70 min","kcal":360,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 30g · fat 16g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Choose the biggest outer leaves for easy rolling. Short on time? Russians chop everything into one pan instead and cheerfully call it 'lazy golubtsy'.","pairsWith":"sour cream · bread · herbs","trivia":"Golubtsy are Russia's everyday take on the cabbage roll the whole region shares — so beloved that a chopped-up shortcut version has its own affectionate name, lenivye or 'lazy' golubtsy.","howThisFeels":"Homely cabbage rolls under a melting spoon of cool sour cream — Russia's weeknight answer to a dish half a continent claims.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-smetannik","name":"Сметанник","nameAlt":"Sour Cream Cake","aliases":["Smetannik","Smetana cake"],"course":"dessert","type":"cake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"celebration","ingredients":"100g flour · 100g sour cream · 50g sugar · 1 egg · 5g baking powder","method":"Mix flour, sugar, egg, sour cream, and baking powder into a smooth batter. Pour into a baking tin. Bake at 180°C for 30 minutes until set. Allow to cool completely. Slice and serve. Optionally add cream between layers. Serve at room temperature.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":380,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 45g · fat 18g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Do not overmix to keep it light.","pairsWith":"tea · fruit · coffee","trivia":"Smetannik highlights the importance of sour cream in Russian desserts.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-pryaniki","name":"Пряники","nameAlt":"Spiced Honey Cookies","aliases":["Pryaniki","Pryanik"],"course":"dessert","type":"cookie","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"100g flour · 50g honey · 30g sugar · 1 egg · 5g cinnamon · 5g baking powder","method":"Mix honey, sugar, and egg until smooth. Add flour, cinnamon, and baking powder to form a dough. Roll dough and cut into shapes. Place on a tray and bake at 180°C for 15 minutes. Allow to cool before serving. Store in a dry place. Serve with tea.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 5g · carbs 50g · fat 8g","storage":"Store in an airtight container for up to 1 week.","chefNotes":"Honey keeps them soft longer.","pairsWith":"tea · milk · jam","trivia":"Pryaniki have been made in Russia since medieval times.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"russia-mors","name":"Морс","nameAlt":"Berry Drink","aliases":["Mors","Mors drink"],"course":"drink","type":"juice","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Russia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g berries · 20g sugar · 300ml water","method":"Crush berries and extract juice. Boil water and add sugar until dissolved. Combine berry juice with sweetened water. Stir well and cool. Chill before serving. Serve cold.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":120,"nutrition":"protein 1g · carbs 28g · fat 0g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use cranberries or lingonberries for authenticity.","pairsWith":"bread · pastries · desserts","trivia":"Mors is a traditional non-alcoholic fruit drink.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-gulyas","name":"Gulyás","nameAlt":"Beef and paprika soup","aliases":["Goulash"],"course":"main","type":"soup","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g beef chuck · 50g onion · 10g paprika · 5g garlic · 100g potato · 50g carrot · 5g caraway seeds · 500ml beef stock · 10ml sunflower oil · 3g salt","method":"Heat the oil in your deepest pot over medium heat and cook the chopped onion slowly until golden, about 8 minutes — Hungarian cooks call the onion the soup's foundation, so give it time. Add the minced garlic for 1 minute more. Now the rule every Hungarian child learns: pull the pot OFF the heat before stirring in the paprika — paprika burns in seconds and turns bitter, but in warm oil it blooms into deep red gold. Return to the heat, add the beef cubes and a pinch of the salt, and turn the meat through the red oil until coated and lightly browned, about 6 minutes. Pour in the stock, add the caraway seeds, and simmer gently with the lid ajar for 60 minutes — gulyás was born in a herdsman's kettle over an open fire, and it wants time, not fuss. Add the diced potato and carrot and cook for 20 minutes more until tender. Taste, season with the remaining salt, and loosen with a little water if needed — remember, this is a soup, eaten with a spoon, not a fork. Serve steaming with bread.","cookTime":"90 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 35g · carbs 30g · fat 28g","storage":"Refrigerate in a sealed container for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Sweet Hungarian paprika, always stirred in off the heat — burnt paprika is bitter, bloomed paprika is the whole soup.","pairsWith":"bread · sour cream · pickles","trivia":"Gulyás means 'herdsman' — cattle drovers of the Great Plain cooked it in a kettle (bogrács) over open fire. And it is a SOUP: the thick stew foreigners call goulash is actually pörkölt.","howThisFeels":"A herdsman's kettle on the Great Plain — paprika-red comfort by the spoonful.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-porkolt","name":"Pörkölt","nameAlt":"Paprika meat stew","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g pork shoulder · 60g onion · 10g paprika · 5g garlic · 200ml beef stock · 10ml sunflower oil · 3g salt","method":"Heat the oil in a heavy pot over medium heat and cook the chopped onion slowly until deep golden, a full 8 to 10 minutes — in pörkölt the onion melts into the sauce and becomes the body of the dish, so this step IS the dish. Add the minced garlic briefly. Pull the pot off the heat and stir in the paprika so it blooms without burning, then return to the heat, add the pork cubes and a pinch of the salt, and turn the meat through the red onion base until sealed on all sides, about 6 minutes. Add just enough stock to barely reach the top of the meat — pörkölt is a stew, not a soup, and it must cook in its own thick gravy. Cover and simmer on low for about 60 minutes until fork-tender, stirring now and then and adding a splash of water only if it threatens to catch. Season with the remaining salt and serve it thick, the sauce clinging to every piece — nokedli dumplings alongside are practically the law.","cookTime":"75 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 38g · carbs 8g · fat 45g","storage":"Store in the fridge for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Learn the sacred triangle: gulyás is the soup, pörkölt is the stew, paprikás is finished with sour cream — Hungarians will grade you on it.","pairsWith":"nokedli · bread · pickles","trivia":"Pörkölt comes from pörkölni, 'to sear' — the deeply browned onion-and-paprika base is exactly what separates it from its soupier cousin gulyás.","howThisFeels":"Thick paprika gravy hugging slow-cooked meat — the stew corner of the sacred triangle.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-langos","name":"Lángos","nameAlt":"Fried flatbread","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"bread","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"street-food","ingredients":"150g flour · 5g yeast · 100ml milk · 5g salt · 200ml sunflower oil · 5g garlic · 30g sour cream · 20g cheese","method":"Mix the flour, yeast, warm milk and salt into a soft dough and knead until smooth and elastic — a slightly sticky dough fries up lighter, so resist adding extra flour. Cover and let it rise in a warm spot for about 60 minutes until puffy. Divide the dough and stretch each piece into a round with slightly thicker edges, like a pizza that decided to go swimming. Heat the oil to 180°C and fry each round until golden and blistered, about 3 minutes per side — it should puff dramatically the moment it hits the oil. Drain on paper towels. Now the step that makes it lángos: while it is still almost too hot to hold, rub the surface all over with a cut clove of raw garlic — the heat melts the garlic straight into the crust. Spread generously with sour cream, shower with grated cheese, and eat immediately, standing up, preferably with sandy feet.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":700,"nutrition":"protein 18g · carbs 70g · fat 38g","storage":"Best eaten fresh but can be refrigerated for 1 day.","chefNotes":"The raw-garlic rub on the hot crust is non-negotiable — that is the smell of every Hungarian market and Balaton beach.","pairsWith":"garlic · sour cream · cheese","trivia":"Lángos means 'flame-baked' — it began as bread-oven dough baked by the flames, and grew up to become the beach food of Lake Balaton.","howThisFeels":"Hot, garlicky and blistered — Lake Balaton beach food eaten standing up.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-nokedli","name":"Nokedli","nameAlt":"Hungarian egg dumplings","aliases":["Galuska"],"course":"side","type":"dumpling","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"120g flour · 1 egg · 50ml water · 3g salt","method":"Mix flour, egg, water, and salt into a sticky batter. Bring salted water to a boil at 100°C. Use a spoon or dumpling maker to drop small pieces into water. Cook until dumplings float, about 3 minutes. Remove with a slotted spoon. Toss lightly with oil or butter. Serve warm as a side.","cookTime":"10 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 10g · carbs 50g · fat 6g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Keep batter soft for tender dumplings.","pairsWith":"pörkölt · butter · herbs","trivia":"Nokedli is Hungary’s version of spaetzle.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-halaszle","name":"Halászlé","nameAlt":"Fisherman’s fish soup","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"soup","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"200g carp or firm white fish (hake) · 50g onion · 10g paprika · 500ml fish stock · 5g garlic · 3g salt","method":"Cook the chopped onion in a pot over medium heat until completely soft, about 8 minutes — in a true halászlé the onion dissolves into the broth entirely. Add the garlic briefly. Pull the pot off the heat and stir in the paprika — and do not be shy: halászlé is famously, fiercely red, the most paprika-forward soup in Hungary. Pour in the fish stock, return to the heat and boil hard for 10 minutes so the onion and paprika marry into a deep red broth. Lower the heat, slide in the fish pieces with a pinch of the salt and simmer gently for 15 minutes WITHOUT stirring — stir a fish soup and you get fish porridge; swirl the pot by its handles instead. Taste, correct the salt, and serve blazing hot with bread and, if you are brave, a split chilli on the rim.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 8g · fat 20g","storage":"Store refrigerated for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Never stir once the fish goes in — swirl the pot to keep the pieces whole. In SA, dam carp is the authentic pick; hake holds together beautifully.","pairsWith":"bread · chili · white wine","trivia":"Hungary's Christmas Eve soup — and a fierce rivalry: Tisza cooks strain the broth silky, Danube (Baja) cooks add matchstick pasta, and each side insists theirs is the only true halászlé.","howThisFeels":"Fiercely red fisherman's broth — Christmas Eve on the Tisza in a bowl.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-toltott-kaposzta","name":"Töltött káposzta","nameAlt":"Stuffed cabbage rolls","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stuffed","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"150g cabbage leaves · 150g pork mince · 50g rice · 50g onion · 10g paprika · 300ml tomato juice · 3g salt","method":"Boil the cabbage leaves for about 10 minutes until pliable. Mix the pork mince with the cooked rice, chopped onion, salt and the paprika — the paprika is what makes these rolls unmistakably Hungarian, staining the filling a proud brick-red. Roll the filling firmly into the leaves and arrange them close together in a pot. Pour over the tomato juice and simmer low and slow for 90 minutes; Hungarian cabbage rolls are cooked longer than their neighbours', until the leaves nearly melt. Serve hot with sour cream — or better, let the pot cool and serve it tomorrow, because in Hungary this is famously a dish that improves overnight.","cookTime":"110 min","kcal":550,"nutrition":"protein 32g · carbs 40g · fat 30g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"In Hungary the rolls are often layered on sauerkraut with a piece of smoked pork in the pot — add both if you have them and the dish deepens beautifully. Either way, an overnight rest is the real secret.","pairsWith":"sour cream · bread · pickles","trivia":"Töltött káposzta anchors the Hungarian Christmas table, set apart from its Polish, Ukrainian and Russian cousins by paprika, a long slow simmer, and the firm belief that it tastes best the next day.","howThisFeels":"Paprika-red rolls simmered nearly to melting — Hungary's Christmas cabbage, built on purpose to taste better tomorrow.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-rakott-krumpli","name":"Rakott krumpli","nameAlt":"Layered potato casserole","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"casserole","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g potato · 1 egg · 50g sausage · 50g sour cream · 5g paprika · 3g salt","method":"Boil potatoes until tender, about 20 minutes, then slice. Hard boil eggs and slice. Layer potatoes, eggs, and sausage in a baking dish. Spread sour cream between layers and sprinkle paprika. Bake at 180°C for 40 minutes until golden. Let rest slightly before serving.","cookTime":"60 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 50g · fat 35g","storage":"Store refrigerated for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use smoked sausage for deeper flavor.","pairsWith":"salad · pickles · bread","trivia":"Rakott krumpli is a beloved Hungarian comfort food.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-dobos-torta","name":"Dobos torta","nameAlt":"Layered sponge cake with caramel","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"cake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"celebration","ingredients":"100g flour · 100g sugar · 3 egg · 100g butter · 50g chocolate · 20g caramel","method":"Whisk the eggs and sugar until pale, thick and tripled in volume — this sponge rises on air alone, so whisk like the cake depends on it, because it does. Fold in the flour gently and bake the batter in thin, even layers at 180°C, about 8 minutes each — six layers is the classic count. Cool the layers flat. Beat the butter until fluffy and beat in the melted chocolate until smooth and glossy — this buttercream is the cake's heart. Stack the layers with buttercream between each, keeping the sides straight and proud. For the famous top: warm the caramel until pourable, spread it quickly over the final layer, and score it into portions with an oiled knife before it hardens to glass. Chill well, then slice along the scored lines so the stripes inside can show off.","cookTime":"60 min","kcal":450,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 45g · fat 28g","storage":"Keep refrigerated for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Score the caramel top the moment it is spread — once it sets, it shatters instead of slicing.","pairsWith":"coffee · tea · cream","trivia":"Created by pastry chef József Dobos in 1885 — the glassy caramel top was not decoration but armour, sealing the cake to keep and travel in the days before refrigeration.","howThisFeels":"Six proud stripes under a caramel shield — 1885's cleverest cake.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-kurtoskalacs","name":"Kürtőskalács","nameAlt":"Chimney cake","aliases":["Chimney cake"],"course":"dessert","type":"pastry","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"150g flour · 50g sugar · 5g yeast · 100ml milk · 30g butter · 3g salt","method":"Mix the flour, yeast, warm milk, half the sugar, the soft butter and salt into a soft dough and knead until smooth. Let it rise for 60 minutes until doubled — a light dough is what bakes into those airy inner ribbons. Roll the dough out and cut it into one long strip, then wind it in a spiral around a greased cylinder (a foil-wrapped rolling pin or tin works at home), edges just touching so it bakes into one seamless tube. Roll the wrapped cylinder in the remaining sugar, pressing gently so it sticks. Bake at 180°C for about 20 minutes, turning several times so the sugar caramelises evenly into a glossy shell — at Hungarian fairs this happens over glowing coals on a rotating spit. Slide it off the cylinder while warm and stand it upright: steam will curl from the hollow centre like smoke from a chimney, which is exactly how it earned its name. Tear it apart in warm ribbons.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":400,"nutrition":"protein 10g · carbs 60g · fat 15g","storage":"Best eaten fresh, store for 1 day.","chefNotes":"Turn, turn, turn — even rotation is the difference between a caramel shell and one scorched side.","pairsWith":"cinnamon · nuts · chocolate","trivia":"A centuries-old Transylvanian fairground icon, spit-roasted over coals and named for the steam that rises from its hollow middle like chimney smoke.","howThisFeels":"A steaming sugar chimney torn apart in ribbons — the fairground in your fist.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-pogacsa","name":"Pogácsa","nameAlt":"Savory scones","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"bread","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"snack","ingredients":"150g flour · 50g butter · 50ml milk · 5g yeast · 3g salt","method":"Mix flour, butter, milk, yeast, and salt into a dough. Knead until smooth and let rise for 45 minutes. Roll out and cut into small rounds. Bake at 180°C for 20 minutes until golden. Serve warm.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":320,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 35g · fat 18g","storage":"Store in an airtight container for 2 days.","chefNotes":"Brush tops with egg for shine.","pairsWith":"cheese · ham · tea","trivia":"Pogácsa is a traditional Hungarian snack.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-turos-csusza","name":"Túrós csusza","nameAlt":"Cottage cheese pasta","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"pasta","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g pasta · 100g cottage cheese · 50g sour cream · 20g butter · 3g salt","method":"Cook pasta in salted boiling water for 10 minutes. Drain and mix with butter. Add cottage cheese and sour cream. Stir gently until combined. Heat briefly on low for 5 minutes. Serve warm.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 55g · fat 22g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Add crispy bacon if not vegetarian.","pairsWith":"salad · pickles · yogurt","trivia":"Túrós csusza is a simple comfort dish.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-lecso","name":"Lecsó","nameAlt":"Pepper and tomato stew","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"summer","ingredients":"150g bell pepper · 100g tomato · 50g onion · 10g paprika · 10ml sunflower oil · 3g salt","method":"Heat oil in a pan over medium and sauté sliced onion for 5 minutes. Add chopped peppers and cook for 10 minutes. Stir in tomatoes and paprika. Simmer for 20 minutes until soft. Season with salt and serve warm.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":220,"nutrition":"protein 5g · carbs 20g · fat 12g","storage":"Keep refrigerated for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use ripe tomatoes for best flavor.","pairsWith":"bread · eggs · sausage","trivia":"Lecsó is similar to French ratatouille.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-palacsinta","name":"Palacsinta","nameAlt":"Thin Hungarian pancakes","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"pancake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g flour · 200ml milk · 1 egg · 10g sugar · 10ml sunflower oil · 2g salt","method":"Whisk flour, milk, egg, sugar, oil, and salt into a smooth batter. Heat a pan over medium and lightly oil it. Pour a thin layer of batter and swirl to coat. Cook until edges lift, about 2 minutes, then flip and cook 1 minute more. Repeat with remaining batter. Fill with jam or sweet fillings and roll. Serve warm.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":260,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 35g · fat 9g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Let batter rest 10 minutes for smoother pancakes.","pairsWith":"jam · chocolate · fruit","trivia":"Palacsinta is thinner than American pancakes.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-hortobagyi-palacsinta","name":"Hortobágyi palacsinta","nameAlt":"Savory stuffed pancakes","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stuffed","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"2 pancake · 150g chicken · 50g onion · 10g paprika · 100ml sour cream · 5g garlic · 3g salt","method":"Cook chopped onion in a pan over medium for 5 minutes. Add garlic and chicken pieces, cooking until browned, about 8 minutes. Stir in paprika and a little water, then simmer for 15 minutes. Fill pancakes with the mixture and roll. Place in a baking dish, cover with sour cream sauce, and bake at 180°C for 20 minutes. Serve hot.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":550,"nutrition":"protein 32g · carbs 30g · fat 32g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Blend sauce smooth for a refined finish.","pairsWith":"salad · pickles · paprika sauce","trivia":"Named after the Hortobágy region.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-korozott","name":"Körözött","nameAlt":"Paprika cheese spread","aliases":[],"course":"starter","type":"spread","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"snack","ingredients":"150g cottage cheese · 30g butter · 10g paprika · 5g onion · 3g salt","method":"Mash cottage cheese and butter in a bowl until smooth. Finely chop onion and add to the mixture. Stir in paprika and salt. Mix thoroughly until well combined. Chill for 10 minutes before serving. Spread on bread or crackers.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":280,"nutrition":"protein 14g · carbs 6g · fat 22g","storage":"Keep refrigerated for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Add caraway seeds for extra flavor.","pairsWith":"bread · crackers · vegetables","trivia":"Körözött is popular as a beer snack.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-tojasleves","name":"Tojásleves","nameAlt":"Egg soup","aliases":[],"course":"starter","type":"soup","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"1 egg · 500ml vegetable stock · 10g paprika · 5g garlic · 5ml sunflower oil · 3g salt","method":"Heat oil in a pot over medium and add minced garlic, cooking for 1 minute. Stir in paprika briefly off the heat. Pour in vegetable stock and bring to a simmer. Beat the egg and slowly pour into the soup while stirring. Cook for 3 minutes until egg strands form. Season with salt and serve hot.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":180,"nutrition":"protein 9g · carbs 5g · fat 12g","storage":"Store refrigerated for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Stir gently for fine egg ribbons.","pairsWith":"bread · herbs · pepper","trivia":"A simple Hungarian comfort soup.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-kapros-turos-lepeny","name":"Kapros túrós lepény","nameAlt":"Dill cottage cheese pie","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"pie","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"120g flour · 100g cottage cheese · 30g sugar · 20g butter · 5g dill · 1 egg","method":"Mix flour, butter, and egg into a dough and press into a baking dish. Combine cottage cheese, sugar, and chopped dill. Spread filling over dough evenly. Bake at 180°C for 35 minutes until set. Let cool slightly before slicing. Serve warm or at room temperature.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 14g · carbs 40g · fat 22g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Balance sweetness with fresh dill.","pairsWith":"tea · fruit · yogurt","trivia":"Combines sweet and savory flavors.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-krumplis-teszta","name":"Krumplis tészta","nameAlt":"Potato pasta","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"pasta","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g pasta · 150g potato · 50g onion · 10g paprika · 10ml sunflower oil · 3g salt","method":"Boil potatoes until tender, about 20 minutes, then mash. Cook pasta in salted water for 10 minutes and drain. Sauté chopped onion in oil over medium for 5 minutes. Stir in paprika and mashed potatoes. Add pasta and mix thoroughly. Heat for 5 minutes and season with salt. Serve warm.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 12g · carbs 70g · fat 16g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Fry lightly for crispy edges.","pairsWith":"pickles · salad · sour cream","trivia":"A budget-friendly traditional meal.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-meggyleves","name":"Meggyleves","nameAlt":"Sour cherry soup","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"soup","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"summer","ingredients":"200g sour cherries · 300ml water · 30g sugar · 50ml sour cream · 5g cinnamon","method":"Bring water and cherries to a boil over medium heat. Add sugar and cinnamon and simmer for 15 minutes. Stir in sour cream and mix until smooth. Cook gently for 5 more minutes without boiling. Chill before serving. Serve cold.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":220,"nutrition":"protein 4g · carbs 35g · fat 8g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Do not boil after adding sour cream.","pairsWith":"pastry · cake · cream","trivia":"Often served cold in summer.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-turos-retes","name":"Túrós rétes","nameAlt":"Cottage cheese strudel","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"pastry","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"100g filo pastry · 150g cottage cheese · 30g sugar · 20g butter · 1 egg","method":"Mix cottage cheese, sugar, and egg into a filling. Lay out filo sheets and brush with melted butter. Spread filling evenly and roll tightly. Place on a baking tray and brush with butter. Bake at 180°C for 30 minutes until golden. Cool slightly and slice before serving.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":380,"nutrition":"protein 14g · carbs 40g · fat 18g","storage":"Store in a cool place for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Keep filo covered to prevent drying.","pairsWith":"tea · coffee · fruit","trivia":"Rétes is the Hungarian version of strudel.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-marhaporkolt","name":"Marhapörkölt","nameAlt":"Beef paprika stew","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 60g onion · 10g paprika · 5g garlic · 200ml beef stock · 10ml sunflower oil · 3g salt","method":"Heat oil in a pot and sauté chopped onion over medium for 8 minutes. Add garlic and cook briefly. Stir in paprika off heat. Add beef cubes and brown for 6 minutes. Pour in stock and simmer for 90 minutes until tender. Season with salt and serve thick.","cookTime":"100 min","kcal":580,"nutrition":"protein 40g · carbs 8g · fat 42g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Low heat ensures tender meat.","pairsWith":"nokedli · bread · pickles","trivia":"A classic Hungarian beef dish.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-zoldborsfozelek","name":"Zöldborsófőzelék","nameAlt":"Creamed pea stew","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"stew","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g green peas · 50ml milk · 10g flour · 20g butter · 5g garlic · 3g salt","method":"Melt butter in a pan over medium and sauté garlic for 1 minute. Stir in flour to form a roux. Gradually add milk while stirring to avoid lumps. Add peas and cook for 10 minutes until tender. Season with salt and serve warm.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":260,"nutrition":"protein 10g · carbs 30g · fat 10g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Add a pinch of sugar to enhance sweetness.","pairsWith":"meat · eggs · bread","trivia":"Főzelék is a unique Hungarian vegetable dish.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-gyumolcsleves","name":"Gyümölcsleves","nameAlt":"Cold fruit soup","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"soup","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"summer","ingredients":"200g mixed fruit · 300ml water · 30g sugar · 50ml cream · 5g cinnamon","method":"Bring water and fruit to a boil over medium heat. Add sugar and cinnamon and simmer for 15 minutes. Stir in cream and mix well. Cool completely and chill before serving. Serve cold.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":240,"nutrition":"protein 3g · carbs 40g · fat 8g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use seasonal fruits for best taste.","pairsWith":"cake · pastry · cream","trivia":"Fruit soups are popular in Central Europe.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-rakott-teszta","name":"Rakott tészta","nameAlt":"Layered baked pasta","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"casserole","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g pasta · 100g pork mince · 50g onion · 100ml tomato juice · 20g cheese · 3g salt","method":"Cook pasta in salted water for 10 minutes and drain. Sauté onion in a pan over medium for 5 minutes. Add pork mince and cook until browned, about 8 minutes. Stir in tomato juice and simmer for 10 minutes. Layer pasta and meat sauce in a baking dish and top with cheese. Bake at 180°C for 30 minutes until golden. Serve hot.","cookTime":"50 min","kcal":620,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 65g · fat 28g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Let it rest before slicing for clean layers.","pairsWith":"salad · bread · pickles","trivia":"Layered pasta dishes are common in Hungarian home cooking.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-kebap-adana","name":"Adana Kebap","nameAlt":"Spicy minced lamb skewers","aliases":["Adana kebabı"],"course":"main","type":"kebab","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g lamb mince · 8g chilli flakes · 3g paprika · 5g salt · 2g black pepper · 10g butter · 50g flatbread","method":"Ask your butcher for lamb mince with about 20% fat — in Adana the fat IS the flavour, and lean mince grills up dry and crumbly. Mix the mince with the chilli flakes, paprika, salt and black pepper, then knead it properly for a good few minutes until it turns sticky and pale — that stickiness is what grips the skewer, exactly like working boerewors. Chill the mixture for 30 minutes; cold mince holds its shape over the coals. With wet hands, press the meat along flat skewers, squeezing gentle finger ridges down the length — or shape it boerewors-style straight onto the braai grid if you don't have flat skewers. Braai over medium-hot coals for about 10 minutes, turning often so the fat bastes the meat instead of flaring. In the last minute, brush with melted butter for the shine. Rest 2 minutes, then serve on flatbread so the bread catches every drop of juice.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 20g · fat 35g","storage":"Store cooked kebabs in the fridge for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Flat metal skewers are traditional because mince slides off round ones — the boerewors-on-the-grid trick works just as well. Turkey's pul biber pepper is beautifully matched by chilli flakes plus a pinch of paprika.","pairsWith":"ayran · grilled tomato · onion salad","trivia":"Adana Kebap carries the name of its southern home city, where kebab masters still hand-mince the lamb with a curved blade — no grinder allowed.","howThisFeels":"Smoky, rich and gently fiery — lamb over open coals that speaks braai fluently in Turkish.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-manti","name":"Mantı","nameAlt":"Turkish dumplings with yogurt sauce","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"dumpling","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"family","ingredients":"150g flour · 1 egg · 50ml water · 100g beef mince · 2g salt · black pepper · 50g yogurt · 10g garlic · 10g butter · 5g paprika","method":"Knead the flour, egg and water into a smooth dough, then rest it under an upturned bowl for 30 minutes — rested gluten rolls paper-thin without fighting back. Season the mince well with the salt and plenty of black pepper. Roll the dough as thin as you dare and cut it into 3cm squares — small is the whole art of mantı. Dot each square with a pea of mince, then pinch the four corners together into tiny parcels, sealing firmly so they hold in the pot. Boil in well-salted water for about 8 minutes until the dough is tender. While they cook, crush the garlic into the yogurt with a pinch of salt and let it sit — the garlic mellows as it waits. Melt the butter until it foams, stir in the paprika, and pull it off the heat the second it turns brick-red. Drain the mantı, spoon the garlic yogurt generously over, and finish with the sizzling red butter. Eat immediately, while the yogurt is cool and the parcels are hot.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 55g · fat 18g","storage":"Keep refrigerated for up to 2 days and reheat gently.","chefNotes":"Don't chase the forty-to-a-spoon legend on your first go — 3cm squares are honest home size. Fold a double batch and freeze the extras uncooked on a tray: they boil straight from frozen for a ten-minute supper.","pairsWith":"mint tea · cucumber salad","trivia":"In Kayseri, a bride was traditionally judged by how tiny her mantı were — the ideal was forty parcels on a single spoon.","howThisFeels":"Tiny hand-pinched parcels under cool garlic yogurt and sizzling red butter — fiddly to fold, gone in minutes, worth every pinch.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-lahmacun","name":"Lahmacun","nameAlt":"Thin flatbread with minced topping","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"flatbread","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"street-food","ingredients":"100g flour · 50ml water · 2g yeast · 2g salt · 80g beef mince · 30g tomato · 10g parsley · 5g olive oil · black pepper","method":"Mix the flour, water, yeast and salt into a soft dough and rest it for 10 minutes while the oven heats to its absolute maximum with a baking tray inside — lahmacun needs fierce bottom heat. Chop the mince finer with the tomato, half the parsley and the olive oil until it is nearly a paste, and season it boldly with salt and black pepper — the topping must carry flavour because there is so little of it. Roll the dough into a round so thin you can almost see the counter through it. Spread the meat paste in the barest film right to the edges — this is not pizza: no cheese, no thick layers, just meat cooking in the tomato's steam while the bread crisps beneath it. Slide onto the screaming-hot tray and bake for 6–8 minutes until the edges blister and crisp. Scatter with the remaining fresh parsley, squeeze lemon over, roll it up and eat it from your hand while it crackles.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":430,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 50g · fat 15g","storage":"Best eaten fresh but can be refrigerated for 1 day.","chefNotes":"An upside-down baking tray preheated for 20 minutes is the closest a home oven gets to a stone-floored Turkish fırın. A teaspoon of tomato paste with a pinch of chilli stirred into the mince stands in for Turkish pepper paste.","pairsWith":"lemon wedges · ayran","trivia":"Lahmacun predates pizza by centuries — the name comes from the Arabic for 'meat with dough', and no self-respecting usta would ever put cheese on one.","howThisFeels":"Crackle-thin bread under a whisper of spiced mince, rolled around cold parsley and a squeeze of lemon — street food you fold, never slice.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-imam-bayildi","name":"İmam Bayıldı","nameAlt":"Stuffed eggplant with olive oil","aliases":["Imam bayildi"],"course":"main","type":"vegetable","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"summer","ingredients":"1 eggplant · 50g onion · 50g tomato · 20ml olive oil · 5g garlic · 3g sugar · 2g salt","method":"Slice the brinjal in half lengthwise and scoop a shallow channel down each half. Fry the halves gently in half the olive oil until softened and golden, about 6 minutes a side, then set aside. In the same pan, cook the sliced onion low and slow in the remaining oil for a full 10–12 minutes with a pinch of the salt — this slow sweat, not the tomato, is where the dish's famous sweetness comes from, so don't rush it. Add the garlic for the last minute, then the chopped tomato, sugar and the rest of the salt, and simmer 10 minutes into a soft, sweet tangle. Pile the filling generously into the brinjal channels, lay them in a baking dish, drizzle with olive oil and bake at 180°C for 30 minutes until meltingly tender. Now the hard part: let it cool. İmam bayıldı belongs to Turkey's zeytinyağlı family — olive-oil vegetables served at room temperature, where the flavours settle and deepen. It is genuinely better the next day.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":320,"nutrition":"protein 4g · carbs 25g · fat 22g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days and serve cold or warm.","chefNotes":"Brinjals are an SA staple, so make this often — and a cheaper olive-oil blend is fine here, since the oil is cooked rather than drizzled raw.","pairsWith":"rice · yogurt","trivia":"The name means 'the imam fainted' — he swooned either at the flavour or at how much precious olive oil his wife had used. Both versions are probably true.","howThisFeels":"Silky, sweet and patient — room-temperature brinjal that rewards you for waiting until tomorrow.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-menemen","name":"Menemen","nameAlt":"Scrambled eggs with tomato and pepper","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"scramble","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"breakfast","ingredients":"2 eggs · 100g tomato · 50g green pepper · 10g butter · 2g salt · 1g black pepper","method":"Grate the tomatoes on the coarse side of a box grater and discard the skins — grated tomato melts into a sauce in minutes, where chopped tomato just stews. Melt the butter in a pan over medium heat and cook the chopped green pepper for 3 minutes until it softens and smells sweet. Add the grated tomato with the salt and black pepper and let it bubble for 4–5 minutes until it thickens from watery to jammy — the eggs need a sauce, not a puddle. Crack the eggs straight into the pan and stir slowly and gently, pulling them through the tomato in soft ribbons. The whole country argues about onion: some homes fry a little chopped onion with the pepper, others call that sacrilege — cook it your way and defend it proudly. Pull the pan off the heat while the eggs are still glossy and barely set; they finish cooking on the way to the table. Eat straight from the pan with plenty of bread for wiping.","cookTime":"10 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 18g · carbs 10g · fat 25g","storage":"Best eaten fresh but can be refrigerated for 1 day.","chefNotes":"If you're team onion, add 30g chopped onion with the green pepper. Either way, the pan goes to the table — menemen is never plated.","pairsWith":"bread · tea","trivia":"Menemen has split Turkish breakfast tables for generations: soğanlı or soğansız — with onion or without — is the national breakfast argument.","howThisFeels":"Soft, sunny and lazily communal — glossy eggs folded through sweet tomato, eaten from the pan with torn bread.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-pide","name":"Pide","nameAlt":"Boat-shaped Turkish flatbread","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"bread","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g flour · 80ml water · 3g yeast · 2g salt · 100g cheese · 1 egg","method":"Mix flour, water, yeast, and salt into a dough. Knead until smooth and let rest for 15 minutes. Roll into an oval shape. Add cheese in the center and fold edges to form a boat. Crack egg on top. Bake at 200°C for 15 minutes until golden. Remove when edges are crisp. Let cool slightly before serving. Slice and enjoy warm.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 55g · fat 20g","storage":"Store in fridge for up to 2 days and reheat in oven.","chefNotes":"Use melting cheese for best texture.","pairsWith":"tea · salad","trivia":"Pide is often compared to pizza but has a unique shape.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-baklava","name":"Baklava","nameAlt":"Layered pastry with nuts and syrup","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"pastry","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"100g phyllo dough · 80g walnuts · 50g butter · 50g sugar · 50ml water · 10g honey","method":"First make the syrup so it has time to cool completely: boil the sugar, water and honey for 5 minutes, then set it aside — this timing is the whole secret of baklava. Brush a baking dish with melted butter and lay in a sheet of phyllo, brushing it with butter before the next goes down; butter every single layer, because those brushed layers are what shatter into flakes later. Halfway through the sheets, spread the crushed walnuts evenly, then keep layering and buttering to the top. With a sharp knife, cut the baklava all the way through into diamonds BEFORE baking — you cannot cut crisp phyllo cleanly afterwards. Bake at 180°C for about 30 minutes until deep gold. Now the syrup law: pour the COOLED syrup over the HOT pastry the moment it leaves the oven — it will hiss and crackle as it drinks. Hot syrup on hot pastry makes sog; cool-on-hot makes crunch. Then walk away: baklava rests overnight, uncovered, before you're allowed to cut and judge it.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":550,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 60g · fat 30g","storage":"Keep covered at room temperature for 3 days.","chefNotes":"Frozen phyllo from the supermarket freezer works beautifully — thaw overnight in the fridge and keep waiting sheets under a barely damp cloth. Walnuts are classic, but SA-grown pecans are gorgeous here.","pairsWith":"coffee · tea","trivia":"Baklava's syrup rule divides households across Turkey, Greece and the Middle East — but every master agrees on one thing: never hot-on-hot.","howThisFeels":"Shattering, buttery and honey-deep — a hundred crisp layers that took centuries to perfect and one night's rest to earn.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-mercimek-corbasi","name":"Mercimek Çorbası","nameAlt":"Red lentil soup","aliases":[],"course":"starter","type":"soup","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g red lentils · 50g onion · 30g carrot · 20ml olive oil · 10g butter · 5g paprika · 2g salt · 1 lemon wedge · 500ml water","method":"Warm the olive oil in a pot over medium heat and cook the chopped onion gently until soft and sweet, about 5 minutes — rushing it browns the onion and muddies the soup's sunny colour. Add the carrot and cook 3 minutes more. Stir in the rinsed lentils and the water, bring to a boil, then lower the heat and simmer for 20 minutes until the lentils have completely collapsed. Season with the salt, then blend until absolutely silky — in Turkey this soup is judged on its smoothness, so blend a minute longer than feels necessary. Now the moment the whole country waits for: melt the butter in a small pan until it foams, stir in the paprika, and the second it sizzles brick-red, pour it over each bowl in a swirl. Squeeze the lemon wedge over just before eating — the lemon is law, lifting the earthy lentils bright. Taste and adjust the salt.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 15g · carbs 40g · fat 10g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Red lentils are one of the cheapest proteins on any supermarket shelf — this whole pot works out to roughly R8 a person. For an even leaner pot, skip the butter and sizzle the paprika in a spoon of sunflower oil instead.","pairsWith":"lemon · bread","trivia":"This is Turkey's most-cooked soup — the pot that opens lunch in homes, workers' cafés and roadside lokantas from Edirne to Van.","howThisFeels":"Silky, warming and honest — a bowl of red-gold comfort that costs almost nothing and tastes like someone loves you.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-kofte","name":"Köfte","nameAlt":"Seasoned meatballs","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"meatball","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g beef mince · 20g onion · 5g garlic · 5g cumin · 2g salt · 10ml olive oil","method":"This is the everyday one — the pan-fried köfte that lands on Turkish tables the way frikkadels land on ours. Grate the onion straight into the beef mince so its juice spreads through every bite, add the garlic, cumin and salt, and knead the mixture properly until it tightens and turns tacky. Let it rest 10 minutes; rested mince holds together and fries up plump instead of cracking. Shape into small flattened ovals with damp hands. Heat the olive oil in a pan over medium heat and fry for about 8 minutes, turning, until deeply browned outside and just cooked through, with a last pinch of salt as they leave the pan. Every household in Turkey has its own köfte and every household is certain theirs is correct — this one is a fine place to start yours.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":450,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 5g · fat 35g","storage":"Store in fridge for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Wet hands stop the mince sticking while you shape.","pairsWith":"rice · salad","trivia":"Köfte variations run into the hundreds across Turkey — grilled, fried, stewed, raw — and 'our köfte' is a point of family pride.","howThisFeels":"Homely, savoury and endlessly repeatable — the weeknight meatball every Turkish family perfects their own way.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-dolma","name":"Dolma","nameAlt":"Stuffed vegetables or leaves","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stuffed","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"family","ingredients":"100g rice · 50g grape leaves · 50g onion · 20ml olive oil · 5g dill · 2g salt","method":"Rinse rice thoroughly. Cook onion in olive oil until soft. Add rice, dill, and salt and cook briefly. Place filling into grape leaves and roll tightly. Arrange in a pot. Add water to cover partially. Simmer on low heat for 30 minutes. Let rest before serving. Serve warm or at room temperature.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":280,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 45g · fat 8g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Roll tightly to prevent unraveling.","pairsWith":"yogurt · lemon","trivia":"Dolma comes from a word meaning 'to fill'.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-simit","name":"Simit","nameAlt":"Sesame-crusted bread ring","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"bread","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"street-food","ingredients":"150g flour · 80ml water · 3g yeast · 20g sesame seeds · 5g sugar · 2g salt","method":"Mix flour, water, yeast, sugar, and salt into a dough. Knead until smooth and let rest for 20 minutes. Shape into rings. Dip in water then coat with sesame seeds. Place on a tray. Bake at 200°C for 15 minutes until golden. Cool slightly before serving. Serve fresh.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 50g · fat 6g","storage":"Best eaten same day.","chefNotes":"Toast sesame seeds lightly before coating for extra flavor.","pairsWith":"tea · cheese","trivia":"Simit is a popular Turkish street snack.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-iskender-kebap","name":"İskender Kebap","nameAlt":"Sliced döner with yogurt and sauce","aliases":["Iskender kebabı"],"course":"main","type":"kebab","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"restaurant","ingredients":"150g lamb leg (or shoulder) · 50g yogurt · 50g tomato sauce · 50g bread · 10g butter","method":"İskender is really about the BASE. Tear the bread into bite-size pieces and warm them — they are about to do the most important job on the plate. Grill the lamb slices until browned and juicy, then lay them straight over the bread so every drip of meat juice soaks in; dry bread under İskender is a scandal in Bursa. Warm the tomato sauce gently and melt the butter in a small pan until it foams. Plate bread, then lamb, spoon the tomato sauce over, and set a generous spoonful of yogurt BESIDE the meat, never on top. Finish at the table: pour the foaming butter over everything so it sizzles as it lands. Serve immediately, while the bread underneath is still drinking.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 40g · fat 35g","storage":"Best eaten fresh.","chefNotes":"The foaming-butter pour is traditionally done at the table — carry the little pan through and let everyone hear the sizzle.","pairsWith":"ayran · salad","trivia":"İskender Kebap is named after its creator from Bursa.","howThisFeels":"Lavish, dripping and table-side dramatic — the kebab where the bread underneath gets the best seat.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-cig-kofte","name":"Çiğ Köfte","nameAlt":"Spiced bulgur patties","aliases":[],"course":"starter","type":"mezze","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g fine bulgur · 20g tomato paste · 10g red pepper paste · 20ml olive oil · 10g onion · 5g cumin · 2g salt","method":"Çiğ köfte means 'raw köfte' — and after the raw-meat original was banned from sale in 2008, modern Turkey knead-perfected this meat-free version, and nobody looked back. Soften the fine bulgur with a little warm water for 10 minutes. Add the tomato paste, red pepper paste, olive oil, finely grated onion, cumin and salt, and now the real work begins: knead, and keep kneading, a full 10 minutes, pressing and folding until the mixture darkens and binds — the friction and the pastes 'cook' the bulgur without heat ever touching it. Squeeze handfuls in your palm so your fingers leave their print — that's the classic shape. Serve in crisp lettuce cups with a hard squeeze of lemon over each one.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":260,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 35g · fat 10g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Fine bulgur, not coarse, is essential — look for it at Mediterranean or Middle Eastern delis.","pairsWith":"lettuce · lemon · ayran","trivia":"The raw-meat original was banned from street sale in 2008 — today's meat-free çiğ köfte is the national standard, sold from late-night counters across Turkey.","howThisFeels":"Zingy, chewy and hands-on — the meat-free street snack that kneads itself into being.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-tarhana-corbasi","name":"Tarhana Çorbası","nameAlt":"Fermented grain and yogurt soup","aliases":[],"course":"starter","type":"soup","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"winter","ingredients":"50g tarhana powder · 20g butter · 5g paprika · 2g salt · 400ml water","method":"Whisk the tarhana powder into a splash of cold water until you have a smooth slurry — powder dumped straight into hot liquid guarantees lumps. Melt the butter in a pot over medium heat, stir in the paprika and let it sizzle for just a few seconds until the butter turns brick-red and fragrant. Pour in the rest of the water, then whisk in the tarhana slurry. Bring to a gentle boil, then simmer for 15 minutes, whisking now and then, as the fermented grains wake up and thicken the pot into velvet. Taste before you add the salt — tarhana carries its own tangy saltiness from the ferment. Serve steaming, with bread for dunking and a wedge of lemon if you like it brighter.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":220,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 25g · fat 10g","storage":"Keep refrigerated for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Tarhana powder is nearly impossible to find in SA — so make it the village way: knead 100g flour, 100g plain yogurt and 50g finely grated onion, tomato and red pepper into a stiff dough, cover, and let it ferment at room temperature for 3–5 days, kneading daily, until pleasantly sour. Crumble it, dry it completely in the sun or a 50°C oven, and rub it to a powder. It keeps for a year — instant soup your past self made.","pairsWith":"bread · lemon","trivia":"Tarhana may be the world's oldest instant soup — Anatolian families ferment and dry a summer batch to carry the whole household through winter.","howThisFeels":"Sour-warm and quietly restoring — a living winter tang in a bowl, dinner from a jar you filled months ago.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-kumpir","name":"Kumpir","nameAlt":"Loaded baked potato","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"street-food","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"street-food","ingredients":"1 potato · 20g butter · 30g cheese · 30g corn · 30g sausage · 10g pickles","method":"Bake the potato at 200°C for about 45 minutes until soft inside. Cut open and mash the interior with butter and cheese. Mix until creamy. Add corn, sausage slices, and chopped pickles. Stir gently to combine. Return briefly to oven for 5 minutes. Serve hot.","cookTime":"50 min","kcal":650,"nutrition":"protein 18g · carbs 70g · fat 30g","storage":"Best eaten fresh.","chefNotes":"Use a large potato for better filling ratio.","pairsWith":"ayran · salad","trivia":"Kumpir is popular in Istanbul street markets.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-gozleme","name":"Gözleme","nameAlt":"Stuffed flatbread","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"flatbread","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"street-food","ingredients":"120g flour · 60ml water · 2g salt · 80g spinach · 50g cheese · 10ml olive oil","method":"Mix flour, water, and salt into a dough and knead until smooth. Let rest for 15 minutes. Roll into thin circles. Place spinach and cheese filling on one half. Fold over and seal edges. Cook on a hot pan with olive oil for about 4 minutes per side until golden. Remove and slice. Serve warm.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 15g · carbs 45g · fat 18g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Roll dough thin for a crisp finish.","pairsWith":"tea · yogurt","trivia":"Gözleme is traditionally cooked on a griddle called a sac.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-sutlac","name":"Sütlaç","nameAlt":"Rice pudding","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"pudding","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"50g rice · 400ml milk · 40g sugar · 5g vanilla · 2g cinnamon","method":"Cook rice in water until soft. Add milk and bring to a simmer over medium heat. Stir frequently to prevent sticking. Add sugar and vanilla and cook for 15 minutes. Pour into bowls. Sprinkle cinnamon on top. Bake at 180°C for 10 minutes if a browned top is desired. Cool slightly before serving.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 50g · fat 6g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Stir constantly to avoid burning the milk.","pairsWith":"tea · coffee","trivia":"Sütlaç is often served chilled or lightly baked.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-ayran","name":"Ayran","nameAlt":"Yogurt drink","aliases":[],"course":"drink","type":"beverage","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g yogurt · 100ml water · 2g salt","method":"Place yogurt in a bowl. Add water and salt. Whisk thoroughly until smooth and slightly frothy. Chill briefly if desired. Serve cold in a glass. Stir before serving. Adjust salt to taste.","cookTime":"5 min","kcal":120,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 8g · fat 6g","storage":"Keep refrigerated for up to 1 day.","chefNotes":"Use cold water for best refreshment.","pairsWith":"kebab · pide","trivia":"Ayran is a staple drink across Turkey.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-kunefe","name":"Künefe","nameAlt":"Cheese pastry with syrup","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"pastry","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"100g shredded phyllo · 80g unsalted mozzarella · 40g butter · 50g sugar · 50ml water","method":"Boil the sugar and water for 5 minutes until lightly syrupy, then set it aside to cool — künefe law says hot pastry, room-temperature syrup, never both hot. Toss the shredded phyllo strands in the melted butter until every thread glistens — dry strands scorch instead of crisping. Press half firmly and evenly into a pan; a compact, flat base browns evenly. Lay the mozzarella in the centre, leaving a clear border so the cheese cannot leak and burn. Press the remaining strands on top and cook over medium heat for about 10 minutes until the underside is deep gold — lift an edge to check. Now the flip: invert it onto a plate, slide it back, and give the second side 8 minutes. Pour the cooled syrup over the hot pastry — it will hiss and soak in. Serve that instant: the first cut must stretch.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 15g · carbs 65g · fat 30g","storage":"Best eaten fresh.","chefNotes":"Shredded phyllo is sold as kataifi at Greek and Mediterranean delis. Salted cheese ruins künefe — if salted mozzarella is all you can find, soak it in cold water for an hour first. And eat it straight away: künefe waits for no one.","pairsWith":"tea · pistachios","trivia":"In Hatay, künefe masters are judged on the stretch — a proper slice pulls the cheese in long ropes from pan to fork.","howThisFeels":"Crisp buttery threads over molten stretching cheese and cool syrup — a dessert with a deadline, eaten the minute it lands.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-pastirma","name":"Pastırma","nameAlt":"Cured spiced beef","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"cured-meat","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"snack","ingredients":"100g beef · 10g salt · 10g ground fenugreek · 10g garlic · 5g paprika · 5g cumin","method":"Rub the beef all over with the salt, set it in a dish with a weight on top, and cure it in the fridge for 3 days, pouring off the liquid daily — exactly as a biltong maker presses moisture out of the meat. Rinse the cured beef and pat it completely dry. Now the step where it parts ways with biltong: mix the fenugreek, crushed garlic, paprika and cumin with just enough water to make a thick, clinging paste — the çemen — and coat the meat completely, sealing every surface. Hang it in a cool, airy spot, or set it uncovered on a rack in the fridge, and let it dry for about a week until firm, its mustard-gold jacket set. Slice paper-thin across the grain and serve at room temperature, where the fenugreek perfume opens up.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":250,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 2g · fat 12g","storage":"Keep refrigerated for up to 1 week.","chefNotes":"Any biltong maker will feel at home in the first half of this recipe — the çemen jacket is what makes it pastırma. Ground fenugreek (methi) is at every Indian spice shop. Fry a few thin slices with eggs for the classic Turkish breakfast.","pairsWith":"bread · cheese","trivia":"Pastırma is biltong's Ottoman cousin — cavalry riders are said to have cured beef pressed under their saddles, and the word most likely gave 'pastrami' its name.","howThisFeels":"Deep garlicky air-dried beef in a golden fenugreek jacket — biltong's exotic cousin, sliced thin enough to read through.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-zeytinyagli-fasulye","name":"Zeytinyağlı Fasulye","nameAlt":"Green beans in olive oil","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"vegetable","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"summer","ingredients":"150g green beans · 50g tomato · 30g onion · 20ml olive oil · 2g salt","method":"Heat olive oil in a pan over medium heat. Add sliced onion and cook until soft. Add green beans and stir for 5 minutes. Add chopped tomato and salt. Pour in a small amount of water. Cover and simmer for 25 minutes until beans are tender. Let cool slightly. Serve warm or at room temperature.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":220,"nutrition":"protein 4g · carbs 20g · fat 14g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Cook slowly for best flavor.","pairsWith":"rice · yogurt","trivia":"Olive oil dishes are often served cold in Turkey.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-borek","name":"Börek","nameAlt":"Savory filled pastry","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"pastry","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g phyllo dough · 80g cheese · 30g spinach · 20ml milk · 1 egg","method":"Layer phyllo sheets brushing with milk and egg mixture. Add cheese and spinach filling between layers. Continue layering until used. Cut into portions. Bake at 180°C for 30 minutes until golden. Let cool slightly. Serve warm.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 18g · carbs 45g · fat 25g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Do not overfill to prevent sogginess.","pairsWith":"tea · salad","trivia":"Börek has many regional variations across Turkey.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-hamsi-tava","name":"Hamsi Tava","nameAlt":"Fried anchovies","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"seafood","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"winter","ingredients":"150g anchovies · 30g flour · 20ml sunflower oil · 2g salt","method":"Clean and rinse anchovies thoroughly. Pat dry and season with salt. Coat lightly in flour. Heat oil in a pan over medium heat. Fry anchovies for about 4 minutes per side until golden and crisp. Remove and drain on paper. Serve immediately with lemon if desired.","cookTime":"10 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 10g · fat 22g","storage":"Best eaten fresh.","chefNotes":"Use fresh anchovies for best taste.","pairsWith":"lemon · salad","trivia":"Hamsi is especially popular along the Black Sea coast.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-revani","name":"Revani","nameAlt":"Semolina sponge cake with syrup","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"cake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"80g semolina · 50g sugar · 2 eggs · 50g yogurt · 20g butter · 50ml water","method":"Beat eggs and sugar until light. Add yogurt, melted butter, and semolina. Mix until smooth. Pour into a greased pan. Bake at 180°C for 30 minutes until golden. Boil sugar and water for syrup. Pour syrup over hot cake. Let absorb and cool. Slice and serve.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 10g · carbs 55g · fat 18g","storage":"Keep covered at room temperature for 2 days.","chefNotes":"Pour syrup evenly for consistent sweetness.","pairsWith":"tea · coffee","trivia":"Revani is popular across the former Ottoman regions.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-sac-kavurma","name":"Sac Kavurma","nameAlt":"Pan-fried diced lamb","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stir-fry","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g stewing lamb (shoulder or neck) · 50g onion · 30g green pepper · 20ml sunflower oil · 2g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"Heat sunflower oil in a wide pan over high heat. Add diced lamb and cook for about 8 minutes until browned. Stir occasionally to avoid burning. Add sliced onion and green pepper. Cook on medium heat for 6 minutes until softened. Season with salt and black pepper. Continue cooking until meat is tender. Serve hot immediately.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 8g · fat 40g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Cook on high heat first to seal in juices.","pairsWith":"flatbread · ayran","trivia":"Traditionally cooked on a large metal pan called a sac.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-tantuni","name":"Tantuni","nameAlt":"Spiced beef wrap","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"wrap","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"street-food","ingredients":"150g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 50g flatbread · 30g tomato · 20g onion · 10ml sunflower oil · 2g paprika · 2g salt","method":"Cook finely chopped beef in sunflower oil over medium heat for about 8 minutes. Add paprika and salt and stir well. Warm the flatbread briefly. Place beef onto bread and add chopped tomato and onion. Roll tightly into a wrap. Heat briefly on the pan for 2 minutes to crisp. Serve hot.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 40g · fat 22g","storage":"Best eaten fresh.","chefNotes":"Use very finely chopped meat for authentic texture.","pairsWith":"ayran · pickles","trivia":"Tantuni originates from the city of Mersin.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-icli-kofte","name":"İçli Köfte","nameAlt":"Stuffed bulgur croquettes","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"croquette","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"special","ingredients":"100g bulgur · 100g beef mince · 30g onion · 10g walnuts · 5g cumin · 2g salt · 20ml sunflower oil","method":"İçli köfte is the stuffed torpedo — a kneaded bulgur shell around a spiced walnut-mince heart, and in Turkey the shaping is a badge of honour passed down between generations. Soak the bulgur for 10 minutes, then knead it with a portion of the raw mince into a smooth, tight dough — the kneading builds the bind that keeps the shells from bursting in the oil. For the filling, cook the remaining mince with the onion, walnuts, cumin and salt until rich and crumbly, then let it cool. With damp hands, hollow a ball of bulgur dough over your thumb into a thin-walled shell, spoon in the filling, and pinch it closed into the classic pointed torpedo — thin walls, well sealed, is the whole craft. Fry in medium-hot oil for about 6 minutes until deep golden and crisp. Drain and serve hot with yogurt.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":550,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 45g · fat 30g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Cool the filling fully before stuffing — warm filling softens the shell and it may split in the oil.","pairsWith":"yogurt · salad","trivia":"İçli köfte is similar to kibbeh found in Middle Eastern cuisine.","howThisFeels":"Crisp outside, spiced and secret inside — the stuffed torpedo that proves your hands have learnt something.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-alinazik","name":"Ali Nazik","nameAlt":"Eggplant puree with minced meat","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"meat-vegetable","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"restaurant","ingredients":"1 eggplant · 100g beef mince · 50g yogurt · 10g garlic · 10ml olive oil · 2g salt","method":"The soul of Ali Nazik is smoke. Roast the eggplant directly over flame — braai coals or a gas burner are perfect — turning until the skin blisters black all over and the inside collapses, about 15 minutes; the charring skin perfumes the flesh within. (No flame? A 200°C oven works, but you'll miss some smoke.) Scoop the smoky flesh and mash it with the yogurt, garlic and a pinch of the salt into a warm purée. Brown the beef mince in the olive oil with the rest of the salt until its edges crisp. Spread the eggplant purée over a warm plate and spoon the sizzling mince on top, so the hot meat meets the cool smoke below. Serve immediately with flatbread for scooping.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 12g · fat 30g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Flame-roasting over braai coals is the most natural SA move in all of Turkish cooking — do the eggplants while the fire dies down after the meat.","pairsWith":"flatbread · salad","trivia":"Ali Nazik originates from Gaziantep.","howThisFeels":"Smoky, garlicky and quietly luxurious — blistered eggplant doing what braai flames were made for.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-tavuk-sis","name":"Tavuk Şiş","nameAlt":"Grilled chicken skewers","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"kebab","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"barbecue","ingredients":"200g chicken thighs · 30g yogurt · 10ml olive oil · 5g paprika · 2g salt","method":"Cut the chicken thighs into even cubes and mix with the yogurt, half the olive oil, most of the paprika and the salt. The yogurt isn't just flavour — its gentle lactic acid tenderises the meat the way harsh marinades can't, which is why Turkish chicken skewers stay juicy where others dry out. Marinate at least 20 minutes; a few hours is even better. Thread onto skewers, leaving a little space between cubes, and grill over medium coals for about 12 minutes, turning, until cooked through with golden char. In the last minute, whisk the remaining oil with the last pinch of paprika and brush it over the skewers — that paprika-oil baste gives the glowing kebab-shop finish. Rest briefly and serve.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":400,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 5g · fat 28g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Thighs beat breast here every time — cheaper at any SA butcher and far harder to overcook.","pairsWith":"rice · salad · ayran","trivia":"Şiş refers to the skewers used for grilling.","howThisFeels":"Juicy, golden and reliably crowd-pleasing — the chicken skewer with science on its side.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-pilav","name":"Pilav","nameAlt":"Butter rice","aliases":["Pilaf"],"course":"side","type":"rice","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g rice · 15g orzo or broken vermicelli (şehriye) · 20g butter · 5g salt · 300ml water","method":"Rinse the rice until the water runs clear — surface starch is the enemy of separate grains. Melt the butter in a pot over medium heat, add the şehriye first and toast it, stirring constantly, until it turns a deep hazelnut brown — this toasting is the smell of every Turkish kitchen at noon, and it gives the pilav its nutty backbone. Add the rice and stir for 2 minutes until every grain is coated and glossy. Pour in the water, add the salt, and bring to a boil. Clap on the lid, drop to the lowest heat and cook for 15 minutes without peeking. Then the sacred rest: off the heat, lay a clean cloth or paper towel under the lid and leave it for 10 minutes — the cloth catches the steam that would otherwise drip back and gum the rice. Fluff with a fork, never a spoon, which crushes the grains.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":320,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 55g · fat 8g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"The cloth under the lid is the old trick every Turkish grandmother swears by. Broken-up vermicelli from any SA supermarket stands in perfectly for şehriye.","pairsWith":"kebab · stew","trivia":"Turks judge a cook by their pilav — every grain must stand apart, and in most homes the toasted şehriye is simply non-negotiable.","howThisFeels":"Butter-toasted golden noodles threaded through fluffy separate grains — the humble side dish that quietly upstages the main.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-kisir","name":"Kısır","nameAlt":"Bulgur salad with herbs","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"salad","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g fine bulgur · 30g tomato paste · 2g paprika · 20ml olive oil · 20g parsley · 10g lemon juice · 2g salt","method":"Kısır is tabbouleh's bulgur-forward Turkish cousin — here the grain is the star and the herbs are the supporting cast. Soak the fine bulgur in just enough warm water for 10 minutes until tender but still nutty. Work in the tomato paste and paprika until every grain blushes red — that paste-stained depth is what separates kısır from every other grain salad. Stir in the olive oil, chopped parsley, lemon juice and salt, mixing thoroughly with your hands the way it's done in Turkey. Then the patience step: let it rest an hour if you can — kısır genuinely improves as it sits, which makes it the perfect salad to build before the fire is even lit. Serve at room temperature, in lettuce leaves if you like.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":280,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 40g · fat 10g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Real Turkish kısır uses biber salçası, red pepper paste — tomato paste plus a paprika pinch is the SA-shelf answer.","pairsWith":"lettuce · ayran","trivia":"Kısır is often served as part of a mezze spread.","howThisFeels":"Fresh, nutty and better-in-an-hour — the make-ahead salad that earns its place beside the braai.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-tursu","name":"Turşu","nameAlt":"Pickled vegetables","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"pickle","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g mixed vegetables (cucumber, cabbage, carrot, green chilli) · 10g garlic · 15g coarse non-iodised salt · 400ml water · 5 dried chickpeas · 1 bay leaf","method":"Cut the vegetables into chunky pieces — big pieces stay crunchy through the ferment. Pack them tightly into a spotlessly clean jar with the garlic, chickpeas and bay leaf; the chickpeas are the old Turkish trick, giving the good bacteria a head start. Dissolve the salt in the water — non-iodised salt and unchlorinated water matter, because both iodine and chlorine can stunt the very microbes doing the work; boil tap water and cool it if unsure. Pour the brine over to cover the vegetables completely, and weigh them down with a small saucer or a folded cabbage leaf — anything poking above the brine will mould. Close the lid loosely, or open it once a day to burp: the fizz is carbon dioxide, the sign of a live lacto-ferment. Keep at room temperature out of direct sun for 5–7 days — a warm Pretoria kitchen ferments faster. Taste from day 4: when the vegetables are sour, faintly fizzy and still crunchy, move the jar to the fridge to slow the ferment right down.","cookTime":"15 min (+5–7 days ferment)","kcal":60,"nutrition":"protein 2g · carbs 10g · fat 0g","storage":"Once sour enough, keep refrigerated — it holds for 2 months and only improves.","chefNotes":"This is a live lacto-ferment, not a vinegar pickle — the sourness is grown, not poured, and every jar comes with its own probiotics. Cloudy brine is normal and healthy. Don't throw the brine out: Turks drink turşu suyu by the glass, and it is glorious.","pairsWith":"kebab · sandwiches","trivia":"Turkey has whole shops — turşucu — that sell nothing but pickles, ladling the sour brine into glasses as a drink alongside.","howThisFeels":"Alive in the best way — crunchy, sour, faintly fizzy vegetables that taste of patience and a well-kept jar.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-mucver","name":"Mücver","nameAlt":"Zucchini fritters","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"fritter","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"summer","ingredients":"150g zucchini · 1 egg · 50g flour · 10g dill · 20ml sunflower oil · 2g salt","method":"Grate zucchini and squeeze out excess moisture. Mix with egg, flour, dill, and salt. Heat oil in a pan over medium heat. Spoon mixture into the pan and flatten slightly. Fry for about 4 minutes per side until golden. Remove and drain on paper. Serve warm.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":280,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 30g · fat 14g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Remove as much moisture as possible for crisp fritters.","pairsWith":"yogurt · salad","trivia":"Mücver is commonly served as a meze or side dish.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-sekerpare","name":"Şekerpare","nameAlt":"Semolina cookies in syrup","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"cookie","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"80g flour · 40g semolina · 50g butter · 40g sugar · 1 egg · 50ml water","method":"Mix flour, semolina, butter, sugar, and egg into a dough. Shape into small oval cookies. Place on a tray and bake at 180°C for 25 minutes until lightly golden. Boil sugar and water into syrup. Pour syrup over hot cookies. Let absorb and cool. Serve at room temperature.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 7g · carbs 55g · fat 18g","storage":"Keep covered at room temperature for 3 days.","chefNotes":"Pour syrup while cookies are hot for best absorption.","pairsWith":"tea · coffee","trivia":"Şekerpare means 'piece of sugar' in Turkish.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-khachapuri","name":"ხაჭაპური","nameAlt":"Cheese-Filled Bread","aliases":["Khachapuri"],"course":"main","type":"bread","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g flour · 120ml milk · 1 egg · 100g sulguni cheese · 20g butter · 5g salt","method":"Warm the milk until just blood-warm — it wakes the dough without toughening it — then mix with the flour, egg and salt into a soft, slightly sticky dough. Knead for 8 minutes until smooth and elastic; a soft dough is what gives khachapuri its cloud-like crumb. Rest it under a cloth for 20 minutes so the gluten relaxes and rolling becomes easy. Grate the cheese coarsely — you want pockets that melt into pools, not a paste. Roll the dough into a round and pile ALL the cheese in the centre — be brave, the cheese-to-dough ratio is the whole point — then gather the edges over the top like a purse and pinch closed. Turn seam-side down and gently flatten with your palms into a disc, working from the centre out so the cheese spreads evenly to the edges. Bake at 220°C for about 15 minutes until deep golden with a few blistered spots. Brush the hot crust generously with butter so it softens and shines, and serve warm, cut in wedges, while the cheese still pulls.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 18g · carbs 55g · fat 22g","storage":"Store refrigerated in an airtight container for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Sulguni is the classic — a 50/50 mix of mozzarella and feta gets you remarkably close.","pairsWith":"yogurt · tomato salad · black tea","trivia":"Georgia tracks its cost of living with the 'khachapuri index' — the price of baking one at home.","howThisFeels":"Molten cheese in a golden crust — the bread a whole country measures itself by.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-khinkali","name":"ხინკალი","nameAlt":"Juicy Meat Dumplings","aliases":["Khinkali"],"course":"main","type":"dumpling","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"200g flour · 120ml water · 150g ground beef · 50g onion · 50ml cold water (for the filling) · 5g black pepper · 5g salt","method":"Mix the flour and water with a pinch of salt into a firm dough and knead well for 10 minutes — khinkali dough must be strong, because each dumpling will hold a spoonful of hot broth. Rest it for 30 minutes. For the filling, chop the onion very finely and mix with the beef, salt and a generous grind of black pepper. Now the secret: work the cold water into the meat a little at a time until the mixture is loose and almost sloppy — that water becomes the famous broth inside as the dumplings cook. Roll the dough thin and cut into circles. Place filling in the centre of each and pleat the edges up and around — Georgian cooks count their pleats with pride — twisting the top into a firm knot. Boil in well-salted water until they float, then 3 to 4 minutes more, about 10 minutes in all. Serve hot, dusted with more black pepper. Eat them the Georgian way: hold the topknot, bite a small hole, drink the broth first, then eat the rest — and leave the doughy knots on your plate as your scorecard.","cookTime":"50 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 45g · fat 18g","storage":"Refrigerate cooked dumplings for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Loose, wet filling = juicy khinkali. If the mixture holds a stiff shape, work in more cold water.","pairsWith":"beer · black pepper · fresh herbs","trivia":"The doughy topknot is never eaten — it's the handle, and the pile left on your plate counts your conquests.","howThisFeels":"Hold the topknot, bite, drink the broth — dinner with rules worth obeying.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-lobio","name":"ლობიო","nameAlt":"Bean Stew","aliases":["Lobio"],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g red kidney beans · 50g onion · 10g garlic · 5g coriander · 10ml sunflower oil · 5g salt","method":"Soak beans overnight and drain. Boil in fresh water on medium heat until tender, about 60 minutes. Chop onion and garlic finely. Fry onion in oil until soft, about 5 minutes. Add garlic and coriander, cooking briefly. Mix in beans and simmer for 10 minutes. Mash slightly for texture and serve warm.","cookTime":"75 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 15g · carbs 50g · fat 8g","storage":"Store in the fridge for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Add a splash of vinegar for brightness.","pairsWith":"cornbread · pickles · red wine","trivia":"Lobio varies widely across Georgian regions.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-badrijani-nigvzit","name":"ბადრიჯანი ნიგვზით","nameAlt":"Eggplant with Walnut Paste","aliases":["Badrijani Nigvzit"],"course":"starter","type":"vegetable","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"200g eggplant · 80g walnuts · 10g garlic · 5g coriander · 10ml sunflower oil · 5g salt","method":"Slice eggplant lengthwise and sprinkle with salt. Let sit for 15 minutes, then pat dry. Fry slices in oil on medium heat until golden, about 4 minutes per side. Grind walnuts with garlic and coriander into a paste. Spread walnut mixture on eggplant slices. Roll each slice and arrange on a plate. Chill slightly before serving.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 10g · carbs 20g · fat 32g","storage":"Keep refrigerated for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use fresh walnuts for best flavor.","pairsWith":"pomegranate seeds · bread · white wine","trivia":"Walnuts are central to many Georgian dishes.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-chakhokhbili","name":"ჩახოხბილი","nameAlt":"Chicken Tomato Stew","aliases":["Chakhokhbili"],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g chicken · 150g tomato · 50g onion · 10g garlic · 5g coriander · 5g salt","method":"Cut chicken into pieces and brown in a pan on medium heat, about 8 minutes. Chop onions and add to the pan. Cook until softened, about 5 minutes. Add chopped tomatoes and garlic. Simmer on low heat for 25 minutes until tender. Stir in coriander and season. Serve hot.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":430,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 12g · fat 30g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use ripe tomatoes for better flavor.","pairsWith":"bread · rice · red wine","trivia":"Originally made with pheasant, now chicken is common.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-pkhali","name":"ფხალი","nameAlt":"Vegetable Walnut Spread","aliases":["Pkhali"],"course":"starter","type":"spread","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"150g spinach · 80g walnuts · 10g garlic · 5g coriander · 5ml vinegar · 5g salt","method":"Boil spinach in water on medium heat for 3 minutes. Drain and squeeze out excess water. Grind walnuts with garlic and coriander. Mix spinach with walnut paste. Add vinegar and salt, mixing well. Shape into small balls or serve as a spread. Chill slightly before serving.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 9g · carbs 10g · fat 25g","storage":"Keep refrigerated for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Try with beetroot or eggplant variations.","pairsWith":"bread · wine · herbs","trivia":"Pkhali is served in colorful assortments.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-mtsvadi","name":"მწვადი","nameAlt":"Grilled Meat Skewers","aliases":["Mtsvadi"],"course":"main","type":"grill","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"200g pork neck (or leg) · 50g onion · 5g black pepper · 5g salt · 10ml sunflower oil","method":"Cut pork into chunks and season with salt and pepper. Slice onion and mix with meat. Let marinate for 30 minutes. Thread onto skewers. Grill over medium heat until browned, about 12 minutes. Turn occasionally for even cooking. Serve hot.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":550,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 5g · fat 45g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use charcoal for authentic smoky flavor.","pairsWith":"bread · onions · red wine","trivia":"Mtsvadi is a staple at Georgian feasts.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-satsivi","name":"საცივი","nameAlt":"Chicken in Walnut Sauce","aliases":["Satsivi"],"course":"main","type":"sauce","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"Christmas","ingredients":"200g chicken · 100g walnuts · 10g garlic · 5g coriander · 5g salt","method":"Place the chicken in a pot, cover with water, add a good pinch of salt and simmer gently — never a hard boil, which toughens the meat — for about 25 minutes until just cooked. Lift it out and keep every drop of that broth. Grind the walnuts with the garlic, coriander and a pinch of salt into the finest paste you can manage — the smoother the paste, the silkier the sauce, so grind twice if you must. Loosen the paste gradually with ladles of the warm broth, stirring until the sauce is the thickness of pouring cream. Cut the chicken into serving pieces and fold them in. Warm everything together very gently for 10 minutes — the sauce must never boil, or the walnut oil splits. Taste and correct the salt. Now the hardest step: let it cool completely. Satsivi means 'served cold', and overnight in the fridge the walnuts thicken the sauce into velvet — which is exactly why Georgians make it the day before the New Year feast.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 10g · fat 40g","storage":"Keep refrigerated up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Grind the walnuts twice for silk — and never let the finished sauce boil.","pairsWith":"bread · wine · herbs","trivia":"Satsivi means 'cold' — it is deliberately made a day ahead for Georgia's New Year table.","howThisFeels":"Cold chicken in walnut silk — New Year's patience, rewarded.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-acha-puri","name":"აჭარული ხაჭაპური","nameRoman":"Adjaruli Khachapuri","nameAlt":"Boat-Shaped Cheese Bread with Egg","aliases":["Acha Puri","Adjaruli Khachapuri"],"course":"main","type":"bread","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g flour · 120ml milk · 1 egg · 120g cheese · 20g butter · 5g salt","method":"Warm the milk gently, then mix with the flour and salt into a soft dough and knead until smooth, about 8 minutes. Rest it under a cloth for 20 minutes. Roll into an oval, then roll the two long sides inward and pinch the ends into points — you are building a boat, and in Adjara the boat is the Black Sea. Fill the hollow generously with grated cheese and bake at 220°C for about 15 minutes until the crust is golden and the cheese bubbles. Pull it out, make a small well in the molten cheese, and crack the egg into the centre — the yolk is the sun setting over the sea. Return to the oven for just 3 minutes; the white should barely set while the yolk stays gloriously runny. Top with the butter and serve immediately. The ritual happens at the table: tear off a pointed end of the boat and stir the egg, butter and cheese into one molten golden pool, then dip your way inward, bite by bite.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 55g · fat 30g","storage":"Best eaten fresh but can be refrigerated for 1 day.","chefNotes":"Pull it from the oven while the yolk is still liquid — it finishes setting in the hot cheese.","pairsWith":"tea · salad · yogurt","trivia":"The Adjaruli's shape honours the Black Sea coast: the bread is the boat, the egg the setting sun.","howThisFeels":"A bread boat with a sun-yolk heart — stirred at the table, eaten like a legend.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-tkemali","name":"ტყემალი","nameAlt":"Sour Plum Sauce","aliases":["Tkemali"],"course":"side","type":"sauce","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g sour plums · 10g garlic · 5g coriander · 5g dill · 5g salt","method":"Boil plums in water on medium heat until soft, about 10 minutes. Drain and mash to remove pits. Add crushed garlic, coriander, and dill. Simmer for 10 minutes until thickened. Season with salt. Cool before serving.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":120,"nutrition":"protein 2g · carbs 25g · fat 1g","storage":"Store refrigerated up to 1 week.","chefNotes":"Adjust sourness with a pinch of sugar if needed.","pairsWith":"grilled meat · bread · potatoes","trivia":"Tkemali is a staple Georgian condiment.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-elarji","name":"ელარჯი","nameAlt":"Cornmeal and Cheese Mash","aliases":["Elarji"],"course":"side","type":"porridge","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"150g cornmeal · 100g cheese · 300ml water · 5g salt","method":"Boil water with salt on medium heat. Add cornmeal slowly, stirring constantly. Cook until thick, about 10 minutes. Add cheese and stir until melted and stretchy. Continue stirring until smooth. Serve hot.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":400,"nutrition":"protein 12g · carbs 50g · fat 15g","storage":"Best eaten fresh; refrigerate up to 1 day.","chefNotes":"Stir constantly to prevent lumps.","pairsWith":"stew · herbs · wine","trivia":"Elarji is especially popular in western Georgia.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-churchkhela","name":"ჩურჩხელა","nameAlt":"Walnut Grape Candy","aliases":["Churchkhela"],"course":"dessert","type":"candy","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"150g walnuts · 300ml grape juice · 50g flour · 10g sugar","method":"Thread the walnut halves onto strong cotton string, leaving a loop at the top for hanging — this is harvest-season work, traditionally done when the grapes come in. Heat the grape juice with the sugar over medium heat. Mix the flour with a little cold juice into a smooth slurry, then whisk it into the pot and cook, stirring constantly, for about 10 minutes until it thickens into a glossy, custard-thick coating called tatara — it should coat a spoon and hold. Dip each walnut string into the tatara, lift, and let the excess run off, then hang to set for a few hours. Dip again for a thicker coat — two or three dips build the proper 'candle'. Then patience: hang the strings somewhere airy and dry for several days until the coating firms to a chewy, leathery skin. Slice into coins or bite straight off the string.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 45g · fat 18g","storage":"Store in a cool dry place for up to 2 weeks.","chefNotes":"The coating must be custard-thick before dipping — too thin and it slides straight off.","pairsWith":"tea · coffee · wine","trivia":"Georgian warriors carried churchkhela to battle — it keeps for months and feeds like a meal.","howThisFeels":"Walnuts dressed in grape leather — the sweet that marched with warriors.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-chakapuli","name":"ჩაქაფული","nameAlt":"Herbed Lamb and Tarragon Stew","aliases":["Chakapuli"],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"200g stewing lamb (shoulder or neck) · 50g onion · 30g spring onions · 20g tarragon · 100g green plums · 100ml dry white wine · 10g garlic · 5g salt","method":"Layer the lamb chunks in a pot with the chopped onion, garlic and spring onions — no browning, no oil: chakapuli is a green, fresh-tasting stew, and searing would muddy it. Add the green plums whole and half the tarragon, season with salt, and pour in the white wine with a splash of water. Bring just to a simmer, cover, and cook on low for 60 minutes until the lamb is tender and the plums have collapsed into a bright, sour sauce — the sourness is the dish; don't fight it. Add the remaining tarragon and cook 10 minutes more, so the stew finishes with two layers of the herb: one deep and mellow, one fresh and loud. Taste — it should be sharp, herbal and alive — and correct the salt. Serve hot with bread for the sauce. In Georgia this pot means spring has arrived: it appears when the plums are still green and hard, and it is the taste of Easter in the wine country.","cookTime":"75 min","kcal":460,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 10g · fat 35g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"No green plums? Use the firmest, sourest plums you can find and sharpen with lemon juice.","pairsWith":"bread · white wine · herbs","trivia":"Chakapuli is Georgia's Easter stew, made when the vineyard plums are still green and sour.","howThisFeels":"Lamb, tarragon and green plums — spring shouting from the pot.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-odjakhuri","name":"ოჯახური","nameAlt":"Fried Pork & Potatoes","aliases":["Odjakhuri","Ojakhuri"],"course":"main","type":"fry","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g pork shoulder · 200g potatoes · 50g onion · 10ml sunflower oil · 5g salt · 5g black pepper","method":"Cut pork and potatoes into chunks. Fry potatoes in oil on medium heat until golden, about 10 minutes. Remove and set aside. Fry pork in the same pan until browned, about 8 minutes. Add sliced onion and cook until soft. Return potatoes to the pan and mix well. Season and cook together for 5 minutes. Serve hot.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 40g · fat 38g","storage":"Store refrigerated up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Cook in a clay pan for traditional presentation.","pairsWith":"pickles · bread · beer","trivia":"Ojakhuri means 'family-style' in Georgian.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-ajapsandali","name":"აჯაფსანდალი","nameAlt":"Georgian Vegetable Stew","aliases":["Ajapsandali"],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g eggplant · 100g tomato · 50g bell pepper · 50g onion · 10g garlic · 10ml sunflower oil · 5g salt","method":"Chop all vegetables into chunks. Fry eggplant in oil on medium heat until softened, about 6 minutes. Remove and set aside. In the same pan, cook onion and bell pepper until soft. Add tomatoes and garlic and simmer for 10 minutes. Return eggplant and mix well. Cook together for another 10 minutes. Season and serve warm.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":260,"nutrition":"protein 5g · carbs 30g · fat 14g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Let it rest before serving for deeper flavor.","pairsWith":"bread · herbs · red wine","trivia":"Ajapsandali is similar to ratatouille.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-gomi","name":"ღომი","nameAlt":"Cornmeal Porridge","aliases":["Gomi"],"course":"side","type":"porridge","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g cornmeal · 400ml water · 5g salt","method":"Bring water and salt to a boil on medium heat. Add cornmeal gradually while stirring constantly. Cook until thick and smooth, about 15 minutes. Continue stirring to avoid lumps. Remove from heat and serve hot.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 60g · fat 2g","storage":"Best fresh; refrigerate for 1 day.","chefNotes":"Serve with cheese for richer taste.","pairsWith":"cheese · stew · herbs","trivia":"Gomi is a staple in western Georgia.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-kubdari","name":"კუბდარი","nameAlt":"Spiced Meat Bread","aliases":["Kubdari"],"course":"main","type":"bread","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"200g flour · 120ml water · 150g beef rump · 50g onion · 5g black pepper · 5g salt","method":"Mix flour, water, and salt into dough and knead well. Let rest for 20 minutes. Chop beef and onion finely and season. Roll dough into a round and place filling inside. Seal edges and flatten gently. Bake at 200°C for 20 minutes until golden. Serve hot.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 22g · carbs 50g · fat 20g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use fatty beef for juicier filling.","pairsWith":"yogurt · tea · herbs","trivia":"Kubdari comes from the Svaneti region.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-nadugi","name":"ნადუღი","nameAlt":"Fresh Whey Cheese Spread","aliases":["Nadugi","Nadughi"],"course":"starter","type":"spread","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g whey cheese · 10g mint · 5g salt","method":"Place whey cheese in a bowl. Finely chop mint and add to the cheese. Mix until smooth and creamy. Season with salt. Serve chilled or at room temperature.","cookTime":"10 min","kcal":180,"nutrition":"protein 12g · carbs 5g · fat 12g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Wrap in thin cheese slices for traditional serving.","pairsWith":"bread · herbs · wine","trivia":"Nadugi is often served in rolled sulguni slices.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-shkmeruli","name":"შქმერული","nameAlt":"Garlic Chicken in Milk Sauce","aliases":["Shkmeruli"],"course":"main","type":"roast","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"200g chicken · 150ml milk · 15g garlic · 20g butter · 5g salt","method":"Season the chicken pieces well with salt and roast at 200°C for about 25 minutes until the skin is deeply golden and crisp — that crispness matters, because it is about to meet a sauce and needs character to survive it. Meanwhile melt the butter in a wide pan over medium heat, add the crushed garlic — all of it, this dish does not apologise — and cook for barely a minute until fragrant but not coloured; browned garlic turns bitter. Pour in the milk and simmer gently for 5 minutes so the garlic infuses every drop. Nestle the roasted chicken into the garlic milk and simmer together for 10 minutes, spooning the sauce over the skin as it reduces slightly and takes up the roasting juices. Taste the sauce and correct the salt. Serve straight from the pan, blazing hot, with bread — the sauce is not a garnish, it is the point, and every drop gets mopped.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 8g · fat 40g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use plenty of garlic — shkmeruli is named for boldness, not balance.","pairsWith":"bread · potatoes · white wine","trivia":"Named for the mountain village of Shkmeri in Racha — and lately famous far beyond it.","howThisFeels":"Crisp chicken drowned in garlic milk — Racha's gift to the fearless.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-chvishtari","name":"ჭვიშტარი","nameAlt":"Cornbread with Cheese","aliases":["Chvishtari"],"course":"side","type":"bread","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g cornmeal · 100g cheese · 100ml water · 5g salt","method":"Mix cornmeal, water, and salt into a thick batter. Add grated cheese and mix well. Shape into small patties. Fry in a pan on medium heat until golden, about 4 minutes per side. Serve warm.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":380,"nutrition":"protein 12g · carbs 45g · fat 18g","storage":"Store refrigerated for 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use sulguni cheese for best results.","pairsWith":"stew · yogurt · herbs","trivia":"Chvishtari is a western Georgian favorite.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-kalmakhi","name":"კალმახი","nameAlt":"Grilled Trout","aliases":["Kalmakhi"],"course":"main","type":"grill","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g trout · 10ml sunflower oil · 5g salt · 5g black pepper · 10g lemon","method":"Clean and pat dry the trout. Rub with oil, salt, and pepper. Grill on medium heat until cooked through, about 10 minutes per side. Squeeze lemon juice over before serving. Serve hot.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":350,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 2g · fat 25g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Do not overcook to keep fish moist.","pairsWith":"herbs · potatoes · white wine","trivia":"Trout is common in Georgian mountain rivers.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-bazhe","name":"ბაჟე","nameAlt":"Walnut Garlic Sauce","aliases":["Bazhe"],"course":"side","type":"sauce","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"100g walnuts · 10g garlic · 5g coriander · 100ml water · 5g salt","method":"Grind walnuts into a fine paste. Crush garlic and mix into walnuts. Add coriander and salt. Gradually add water while stirring to create a smooth sauce. Serve at room temperature.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 10g · carbs 12g · fat 38g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Adjust thickness with water as needed.","pairsWith":"chicken · vegetables · bread","trivia":"Bazhe is often served with poultry dishes.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-sulguni","name":"სულგუნი","nameAlt":"Georgian Brined Cheese","aliases":["Sulguni"],"course":"side","type":"cheese","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200ml milk · 5g salt · 5ml vinegar","method":"Heat milk on medium until warm but not boiling. Add vinegar and stir until curds form. Strain curds and press gently. Shape into a round and salt lightly. Let rest for 30 minutes before serving.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":260,"nutrition":"protein 16g · carbs 5g · fat 20g","storage":"Keep refrigerated up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use full-fat milk for better texture.","pairsWith":"bread · wine · herbs","trivia":"Sulguni is known for its stretchy texture.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-mchadi","name":"მჭადი","nameAlt":"Georgian Cornbread","aliases":["Mchadi"],"course":"side","type":"bread","diet":"vegan","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"150g cornmeal · 120ml water · 5g salt · 10ml sunflower oil","method":"Mix cornmeal, water, and salt into a firm dough. Shape into small flat rounds. Heat oil in a pan on medium heat. Fry rounds until golden, about 4 minutes per side. Serve warm.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":320,"nutrition":"protein 6g · carbs 55g · fat 10g","storage":"Store in an airtight container for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Serve hot for best texture.","pairsWith":"beans · cheese · herbs","trivia":"Mchadi is a staple bread in Georgia.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"greece-paidakia","name":"Παϊδάκια","nameAlt":"Charred Lamb Chops","aliases":["Paidakia"],"course":"main","type":"grill","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Greece","occasion":"feast","ingredients":"300g lamb rib chops · 15ml olive oil · 5g dried oregano · 20ml lemon juice · 4g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"Paidakia are about the lamb and the fire, not a marinade, so keep it honest: bring the chops to room temperature, rub them with olive oil and a little oregano, and salt them only just before they hit the heat — salting early draws moisture to the surface, and you want it dry for a proper char. Get the grill or a heavy pan searing hot; thin rib chops cook fast, and the aim is a deep, smoky crust while the inside stays pink and juicy, about 2–3 minutes a side and no more. Don't crowd them and don't keep flipping — let each side sit still and colour. The finish is the Greek signature: off the heat, hit them hard with lemon juice, a shower of dried oregano and a scatter of coarse salt, then rest them a few minutes so the juices settle back through the meat. Eat them with your fingers, gnawing the bones, the way they are meant to be eaten.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min · Grill 6 min","kcal":620,"nutrition":"Protein 46g, Carbs 2g, Fat 48g, Sodium ~620mg","storage":"Best straight off the grill. Leftovers keep 2 days; the meat is lovely cold, picked off the bone into a salad. Freezes raw, not cooked.","chefNotes":"Salt at the last second, grill screaming-hot and fast, and finish with lemon-oregano-salt off the heat. Thin chops, big char, pink centre.","pairsWith":"Tzatziki · Greek Salad · pita · Skordalia","trivia":"Paidakia are the king of the Greek 'psistaria' (grill house), ordered by weight — you ask for a kilo of chops, never a 'portion' — and a table gnawing the bones clean is taken as the sign of a good meal, not bad manners.","howThisFeels":"Charred, smoky little rib chops, lemony and oregano-dusted, torn hot off the bone with your fingers — the taste of a Greek grill house.","servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic","icon":"🏆","default":true},{"name":"Yoghurt-Marinated","icon":"🌿","howThisFeels":"A more tender, tangy chop — a few hours in a garlicky yoghurt marinade before the fire softens the lamb and helps it char.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"60g Greek yoghurt"},{"item":"6g garlic"}],"swapStep":[{"from":"bring the chops to room temperature, rub them with olive oil and a little oregano, and salt them only just before they hit the heat — salting early draws moisture to the surface, and you want it dry for a proper char.","to":"marinate the chops 2–4 hours in the Greek yoghurt mashed with the garlic and oregano — the yoghurt’s mild acidity tenderises the lamb and its milk proteins help it char — then wipe off the excess, bring them to room temperature and salt them just before they hit the heat."}]},"trivia":"A yoghurt marinade is the cook’s trick for tougher or leaner chops — the lactic acid gently loosens the muscle where a straight-to-the-fire paidaki relies on tender, fatty rib chops alone."}]},
{"id":"greece-kontosouvli","name":"Κοντοσούβλι","nameAlt":"Spit-roasted pork chunks","aliases":["Kontosouvli"],"course":"main","type":"roast","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Greece","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"350g pork neck (or leg) · 15ml olive oil · 5g garlic · 5g oregano · 3g salt · 2g pepper · 20ml lemon juice","method":"Cut pork into large chunks and marinate with olive oil, garlic, oregano, salt, pepper and lemon juice. Thread onto skewers or a spit. Roast slowly over heat, turning regularly, until tender and charred. Slice and serve hot.","cookTime":"90-120 min","kcal":700,"nutrition":"protein 45g · carbs 3g · fat 55g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Slow roasting ensures tender meat.","pairsWith":"flatbread · mustard · salad","trivia":"Popular at Greek celebrations and festivals.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"greece-arni-sto-fourno","name":"Αρνί στο φούρνο","nameAlt":"Oven-roasted lamb","aliases":["Arni sto Fourno"],"course":"main","type":"roast","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Greece","occasion":"easter","ingredients":"300g lamb leg or shoulder · 15ml olive oil · 5g garlic · 5g oregano · 3g salt · 2g pepper · 50g potato","method":"Season lamb with olive oil, garlic, oregano, salt and pepper. Place in a roasting tray with potatoes. Roast in a hot oven until tender and golden, basting occasionally. Rest briefly before serving.","cookTime":"90-120 min","kcal":650,"nutrition":"protein 42g · carbs 20g · fat 45g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Add lemon juice before roasting for brightness.","pairsWith":"roasted potatoes · salad · bread","trivia":"A centerpiece dish at Easter feasts.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"greece-bifteki","name":"Μπιφτέκι","nameAlt":"Stuffed Greek burgers","aliases":["Bifteki"],"course":"main","type":"grill","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Greece","occasion":"casual","ingredients":"250g minced beef · 30g feta · 10ml olive oil · 5g onion · 3g salt · 2g pepper · 5g parsley","method":"Mix minced beef with onion, parsley, salt and pepper. Form into patties stuffed with feta. Grill or pan-cook until browned and cooked through. Serve hot.","cookTime":"20-25 min","kcal":540,"nutrition":"protein 40g · carbs 5g · fat 40g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Keep patties thick for juiciness.","pairsWith":"pita · fries · salad","trivia":"Often served in tavernas as a hearty main.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"greece-keftedes","name":"Κεφτέδες","nameAlt":"Fried Greek meatballs","aliases":["Keftedes"],"course":"main","type":"fry","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Greece","occasion":"casual","ingredients":"250g minced beef · 20g breadcrumb · 1 egg · 5g garlic · 5g parsley · 3g salt · 2g pepper · 20ml oil","method":"Combine minced beef with breadcrumbs, egg, garlic, parsley, salt and pepper. Form into small balls. Fry in hot oil until golden and cooked through. Drain and serve.","cookTime":"20-25 min","kcal":560,"nutrition":"protein 38g · carbs 12g · fat 42g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Chill mixture before shaping for easier handling.","pairsWith":"tzatziki · bread · salad","trivia":"A popular meze that also serves as a main.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"greece-kotopoulo-lemonato","name":"Κοτόπουλο λεμονάτο","nameAlt":"Lemon-Roasted Chicken & Potatoes","aliases":["Kotopoulo Lemonato"],"course":"main","type":"roast","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Greece","occasion":"family","ingredients":"300g chicken pieces (thighs or drumsticks) · 100g potato · 30ml lemon juice · 20ml olive oil · 6g garlic · 5g dried oregano · 100ml chicken broth · 4g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"Cut the potatoes into thick wedges so they roast without collapsing, and pack them around the chicken in a single layer in a roasting tin — snug so they baste each other, but not piled up, or nothing browns. Whisk the lemon juice, olive oil, crushed garlic, oregano, salt and pepper, pour it over and turn everything to coat, then set the chicken pieces skin-side up so the skin can crisp. Pour the broth into the base of the tin, never over the chicken — this keeps the potatoes moist and the meat basted from below while the tops roast dry and golden. Roast at 190°C for 45–55 minutes, basting once or twice, until the chicken skin is deep gold and the potatoes have drunk up the lemony stock and caught at the edges. The lemon should be bright and forward, not shy — this is 'lemonato', and the sharp, garlicky pan juices are the whole point, so put out plenty of bread to mop them.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Roast 45–55 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"Protein 42g, Carbs 20g, Fat 30g, Sodium ~640mg","storage":"Fridge up to 3 days; reheats well, the potatoes soaking up even more of the lemon. Freezes up to 2 months.","chefNotes":"Broth in the base of the tin (not over the chicken) is the trick — crisp golden tops, potatoes soaked in lemony stock underneath.","pairsWith":"Greek Salad · Horta · crusty bread · Tzatziki","trivia":"'Lemonato' simply means 'done with lemon' — the everyday yellow twin of 'kokkinisto', the red tomato version; Greek home cooks split half their repertoire this way, the same cut cooked lemon-yellow or tomato-red by the day's mood.","howThisFeels":"Golden lemon-roasted chicken over potatoes that have drunk the sharp, garlicky pan juices — a Sunday oven tray you eat straight from the dish.","servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Classic","icon":"🏆","default":true},{"name":"With Feta & Olives","icon":"🫒","howThisFeels":"A Mediterranean-taverna finish — salty feta and kalamata olives scattered over for the last stretch until the feta blushes gold.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"50g feta cheese"},{"item":"30g olives (kalamata)"}],"swapStep":[{"from":"Roast at 190°C for 45–55 minutes, basting once or twice, until the chicken skin is deep gold and the potatoes have drunk up the lemony stock and caught at the edges.","to":"Roast at 190°C for about 40 minutes, then scatter the crumbled feta and the olives over and roast a final 10–15 minutes, until the chicken skin is deep gold, the feta blushing and the potatoes have drunk up the lemony stock."}]},"chefNotes":"Feta and olives are salty — cut back the added salt in this version.","trivia":"Adding feta and olives turns the plain lemonato into the modern Greek-taverna traybake — the feta softens into the lemony juices without fully melting."}]},
{"id":"greece-pastitsada","name":"Παστιτσάδα","nameAlt":"Beef stew with pasta","aliases":["Pastitsada"],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Greece","occasion":"family","ingredients":"250g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 80g pasta · 100g tomato · 10ml olive oil · 5g onion · 3g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Brown beef in olive oil, then add onion and cook until soft. Stir in tomatoes and seasoning and simmer until tender. Cook pasta separately and combine with the sauce. Serve hot.","cookTime":"60-75 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 40g · carbs 50g · fat 25g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Add spices like cinnamon for authenticity.","pairsWith":"pasta · cheese · bread","trivia":"A signature dish from Corfu.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"greece-htapodi-sti-schara","name":"Χταπόδι στη σχάρα","nameAlt":"Charred Grilled Octopus","aliases":["Htapodi sti Schara","Chtapodi"],"course":"main","type":"grill","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Greece","occasion":"summer","ingredients":"250g octopus · 20ml extra virgin olive oil · 15ml red wine vinegar · 5g dried oregano · 20ml lemon juice · 3g salt · 2g black pepper","method":"The whole battle with octopus is tenderness, and it's won at the simmer, not the grill: put the octopus in a dry pot — no water, it releases plenty of its own — with a splash of vinegar, cover, and simmer very gently for 45–60 minutes until a knife slides easily into the thickest part of a tentacle. That is the only doneness cue that matters: overcook it and it's rubber, undercook it and it's rubber. Drain, pat it dry, and rub with a little oil. Now grill over the fiercest heat you have for just 2–3 minutes a side, only to char the edges and crisp the suckers — the flesh is already cooked, so this is about smoke and colour, not cooking through. Cut into pieces and dress hot with olive oil, red wine vinegar, oregano, lemon and a little salt. Serve warm, with bread and a glass of ouzo.","cookTime":"Simmer ~1 hr · Grill 6 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"Protein 30g, Carbs 4g, Fat 18g, Sodium ~600mg","storage":"Fridge up to 2 days; excellent cold, dressed as a vinegar salad (see the Xidato version). Freezes raw up to 3 months.","chefNotes":"Simmer until a knife slides in easily, then char fast on high heat. The grill is for smoke, not cooking — it is already done.","pairsWith":"Fava · Skordalia · crusty bread · ouzo","trivia":"You'll still see Greek fishermen beating a fresh octopus against the harbour rocks — dozens of thwacks — to break down its muscle before it ever meets a pot; the modern kitchen shortcut is to freeze and thaw it, which ruptures the same fibres and tenderises it just as well.","howThisFeels":"Charred, smoky tentacle with crisp-blistered suckers, glossy with oil and sharp with vinegar — a harbourside taverna at sunset.","servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Grilled (sti Schara)","icon":"🏆","default":true},{"name":"Xidato (Vinegar-Marinated)","icon":"🍷","howThisFeels":"The cold meze version — the charred octopus steeped in red wine vinegar and oregano until sharp and cool, keeping for days.","delta":{"swapStep":[{"from":"Cut into pieces and dress hot with olive oil, red wine vinegar, oregano, lemon and a little salt. Serve warm, with bread and a glass of ouzo.","to":"For htapodi xidato, cut the grilled octopus into bite-size pieces and marinate them in plenty of red wine vinegar, olive oil and oregano, then chill a few hours or overnight — the vinegar keeps it for days and turns it into a sharp, cool meze. Serve cold with bread and a glass of ouzo."}]},"trivia":"Htapodi xidato — octopus \"in vinegar\" — is how tavernas keep it through summer: the acidity preserves the cooked octopus for days, so a big catch becomes a standing cold meze."}]},
{"id":"portugal-cozido-a-portuguesa","name":"Cozido à Portuguesa","nameAlt":"Portuguese mixed meat boil","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"family","ingredients":"150g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 120g pork shoulder · 80g chicken · 60g chouriço (Portuguese cured sausage) · 40g cabbage · 40g carrot · 40g potato · 5g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Place meats in a large pot with water and bring to a boil. Skim and simmer gently until tender. Add sausages and vegetables and cook until soft. Season with salt and pepper and serve meats and vegetables together with broth.","cookTime":"120-150 min","kcal":720,"nutrition":"protein 55g · carbs 35g · fat 40g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use a mix of cured and fresh meats for depth.","pairsWith":"rice · mustard · red wine","trivia":"Considered Portugal’s ultimate comfort feast.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-espetada-madeirense","name":"Espetada Madeirense","nameAlt":"Madeira beef skewers","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"grill","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"250g beef cubes · 5g garlic · 2g salt · 2g pepper · 10ml olive oil · 1 bay leaf","method":"Season beef with garlic, salt, pepper and oil. Thread onto skewers with bay leaf. Grill over high heat turning until charred outside and juicy inside. Rest briefly and serve.","cookTime":"15-20 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 50g · carbs 2g · fat 44g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use large skewers for authentic presentation.","pairsWith":"bolo do caco · salad · wine","trivia":"Traditionally grilled on laurel wood skewers.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-alheira-de-mirandela","name":"Alheira de Mirandela","nameAlt":"Smoked poultry sausage","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"sausage","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"casual","ingredients":"200g alheira sausage · 10ml oil · 1 egg · 100g fries","method":"Prick sausage and fry gently in oil until browned. Fry egg separately. Serve sausage with fries and egg on top.","cookTime":"15-20 min","kcal":700,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 45g · fat 45g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Cook gently to avoid bursting the casing.","pairsWith":"fries · salad · beer","trivia":"Created by Jews to resemble pork sausages historically.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-bitoque","name":"Bitoque","nameAlt":"Steak with egg and fries","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"steak","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"casual","ingredients":"200g beef steak · 1 egg · 120g fries · 5g garlic · 10ml oil · 2g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Season steak and fry in hot oil with garlic until desired doneness. Fry egg separately. Serve steak topped with egg alongside fries.","cookTime":"15-20 min","kcal":800,"nutrition":"protein 55g · carbs 50g · fat 45g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use a hot pan for a good sear.","pairsWith":"fries · rice · salad","trivia":"A staple dish in Portuguese cafés.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-iscas-com-elas","name":"Iscas com Elas","nameAlt":"Liver with potatoes","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"pan-fry","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"200g pork liver · 100g potatoes · 5g garlic · 10ml vinegar · 10ml oil · 2g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Slice liver and marinate with garlic and vinegar. Fry in oil quickly until just cooked. Serve with boiled or fried potatoes.","cookTime":"20-25 min","kcal":550,"nutrition":"protein 40g · carbs 30g · fat 28g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Do not overcook liver to keep it tender.","pairsWith":"potatoes · pickles · wine","trivia":"Popular Lisbon tavern dish.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-cabrito-assado","name":"Cabrito Assado","nameAlt":"Roast kid goat","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"roast","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"250g goat meat · 5g garlic · 5g paprika · 10ml wine · 10ml oil · 2g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Marinate goat with garlic, paprika, wine and oil. Roast in oven until tender and browned. Rest and serve with pan juices.","cookTime":"90-120 min","kcal":650,"nutrition":"protein 50g · carbs 5g · fat 45g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Marinate overnight for best flavour.","pairsWith":"potatoes · greens · red wine","trivia":"A classic Easter dish in Portugal.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-bacalhau-a-bras","name":"Bacalhau à Brás","nameAlt":"Shredded cod with eggs","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"saute","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"casual","ingredients":"150g salted snoek (or salted cod) · 1 egg · 80g fries · 5g onion · 5g garlic · 10ml oil · 2g salt","method":"Soak and shred cod. Sauté onion and garlic in oil, add cod and fries. Stir in beaten egg until creamy and just set. Serve immediately.","cookTime":"20-25 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 35g · carbs 30g · fat 28g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Keep eggs soft and creamy, not dry.","pairsWith":"olives · salad · white wine","trivia":"Named after a Lisbon tavern owner.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-rojoes-a-minhota","name":"Rojões à Minhota","nameAlt":"Fried pork chunks","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"fry","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"250g pork shoulder · 5g garlic · 5g paprika · 10ml wine · 10ml oil · 2g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Marinate pork with garlic, paprika and wine. Fry in oil until browned and tender. Season and serve hot.","cookTime":"35-40 min","kcal":700,"nutrition":"protein 45g · carbs 5g · fat 55g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Let pork caramelise for deeper flavour.","pairsWith":"potatoes · pickles · bread","trivia":"Originates from the Minho region.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-bife-a-portuguesa","name":"Bife à Portuguesa","nameAlt":"Steak in Garlic Wine Sauce","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"steak","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"casual","ingredients":"200g beef steak · 10ml wine · 5g garlic · 10ml oil · 2g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Season steak and fry in oil with garlic. Deglaze pan with wine and spoon sauce over steak. Serve hot.","cookTime":"15-20 min","kcal":650,"nutrition":"protein 50g · carbs 3g · fat 48g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use a splash of wine for authentic flavour.","pairsWith":"fries · rice · salad","trivia":"Simple but deeply traditional steak dish.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"portugal-ensopado-de-borrego","name":"Ensopado de Borrego","nameAlt":"Lamb bread stew","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"southern-europe","country":"Portugal","occasion":"family","ingredients":"250g stewing lamb (shoulder or neck) · 40g bread · 5g garlic · 5g onion · 10ml wine · 10ml oil · 2g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Brown lamb in oil with garlic and onion. Add wine and simmer until tender. Pour stew over slices of bread and serve.","cookTime":"90-120 min","kcal":680,"nutrition":"protein 45g · carbs 30g · fat 40g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use crusty bread to soak up the broth.","pairsWith":"bread · greens · red wine","trivia":"A beloved Alentejo regional dish.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-urfa-kebabi","name":"Urfa Kebabı","nameAlt":"Mild spiced lamb kebab","aliases":["Urfa kebab"],"course":"main","type":"grill","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"dinner","ingredients":"250g lamb mince · 20g onion · 5g garlic · 3g smoked paprika · 2g chili flakes · 3g salt · 10ml oil","method":"Mix the lamb with the grated onion, garlic, smoked paprika, a small pinch of chilli flakes and the salt — Urfa's famous isot pepper is sun-dried and then smoked, so smoked paprika captures its soul better than raw heat ever could. Knead until sticky so the meat grips the skewer, keeping your hands damp so it doesn't grip you instead. Shape onto skewers and grill over medium coals, turning often, until cooked through with light char, about 12 minutes. Rest briefly before serving. This is Adana's gentler sibling — same skewer, rival city: where Adana leads with fire, Urfa leads with smoke, and the two cities have argued about which is right for generations.","cookTime":"12-15 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 38g · carbs 6g · fat 38g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"No isot in SA? Smoked paprika plus a small pinch of chilli flakes is the honest match.","pairsWith":"lavash · grilled peppers · onion salad","trivia":"Named after the city of Şanlıurfa, home of isot — a pepper sun-dried by day and sweated under cover by night until it turns dark, smoky and almost raisin-sweet.","howThisFeels":"Smoky, mellow and quietly confident — the kebab that wins the argument without raising its voice.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-sis-kebap","name":"Şiş Kebap","nameAlt":"Grilled lamb skewers","aliases":["Sis kebab"],"course":"main","type":"grill","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"bbq","ingredients":"250g lamb cubes · 10ml oil · 5g paprika · 3g salt · 20g onion · 50g tomato","method":"Grate the onion and squeeze it — only the JUICE goes into the marinade, because onion flesh burns bitter over coals while the juice tenderises quietly. Toss the lamb cubes with the onion juice, oil, paprika and salt and let them stand at least 20 minutes. Thread onto skewers with the tomato, leaving a small gap between the cubes — crowded meat steams, spaced meat roasts. Grill over high heat, turning, until browned outside and just cooked to your liking inside. Finish with a final pinch of salt off the heat and serve immediately.","cookTime":"10-12 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 40g · carbs 5g · fat 34g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Ask the butcher for lamb leg or shoulder cubes — a little fat on them means juicier skewers.","pairsWith":"rice pilaf · flatbread · yogurt","trivia":"Şiş means skewer in Turkish.","howThisFeels":"Clean, fire-kissed and generous — cubes of lamb tasting of the coals with nothing in the way.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-doner-kebap","name":"Döner Kebap","nameAlt":"Rotisserie sliced meat","aliases":["Doner kebab"],"course":"main","type":"roast","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"street-food","ingredients":"250g lamb or beef · 50g yogurt · 5g garlic · 5g paprika · 3g salt · 10ml oil","method":"Real döner turns on a vertical spit for hours — no home kitchen has one, so here is the honest home version, and it's proudly good. Mix the yogurt, garlic, paprika, salt and oil and marinate the thinly sliced meat for at least an hour. Layer the slices tightly into a loaf tin, pressing each layer down firmly — that compression is what lets you carve it like the real thing. Roast at 180°C for about an hour until cooked through and browned on top. Then the step no good kebab shop skips: rest it, a full 15 minutes, so the layers set. Slice thinly across the loaf into ribbons, crisp them briefly in a hot dry pan, and pile into bread or over rice.","cookTime":"60-75 min","kcal":550,"nutrition":"protein 42g · carbs 10g · fat 38g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Slice thinly against the grain for tenderness — and don't skip the final crisping in the dry pan; that's where the kebab-shop edges come from.","pairsWith":"pita · salad · yogurt sauce","trivia":"Inspired many global street food variations.","howThisFeels":"Savoury, layered and street-corner familiar — the honest home answer to the turning spit.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-beyti-kebap","name":"Beyti Kebap","nameAlt":"Wrapped minced lamb kebab","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"grill","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"restaurant","ingredients":"250g lamb mince · 1 lavash · 20g yogurt · 20g tomato sauce · 5g garlic · 3g salt","method":"Season the lamb mince with the garlic and salt, shape it into long logs and grill until cooked through and lightly charred. Warm the lavash so it rolls without cracking, wrap it snugly around the meat, then slice the roll into pinwheels — that spiral cross-section is beyti's signature, and the reason it's plated with a knife and fork rather than eaten from the hand. Stand the pinwheels on a plate, stripe them with the tomato sauce warmed with a little butter, and spoon the yogurt alongside so its coolness meets the hot tomato. Serve warm.","cookTime":"20-25 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 35g · carbs 35g · fat 38g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"No lavash in SA? Soft tortilla wraps roll and slice just as neatly.","pairsWith":"yogurt · parsley · grilled tomato","trivia":"Named after chef Beyti Güler.","howThisFeels":"Elegant, saucy and a little theatrical — grilled lamb dressed up in its Sunday spiral.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-hunkar-begendi","name":"Hünkar Beğendi","nameAlt":"Lamb on eggplant purée","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"dinner","ingredients":"200g lamb leg or shoulder · 150g eggplant · 30g butter · 20g flour · 200ml milk · 30g mature cheddar · 5g garlic · 3g salt","method":"'Sultan's delight' is two dishes on one plate: tender lamb above, and beneath it the beğendi — a smoked-eggplant béchamel, which is exactly what separates it from Ali Nazik's yogurt base. Brown the lamb pieces, then cook them low and slow with the garlic, half the salt and a splash of water until spoon-tender. For the beğendi, char the eggplant until the skin blackens and the flesh collapses, then scoop and mash it. Melt the butter, stir in the flour and cook for a minute, then whisk in the milk to a smooth white sauce. Fold in the mashed eggplant, the grated cheese and the rest of the salt, and stir until silky and faintly smoky. Pool the beğendi on the plate and sit the lamb on top, palace-style.","cookTime":"40-50 min","kcal":620,"nutrition":"protein 32g · carbs 20g · fat 44g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Kaşar is the traditional cheese in the purée — mature cheddar is the honest SA stand-in.","pairsWith":"rice · flatbread","trivia":"Means 'the Sultan liked it'.","howThisFeels":"Regal, smoky and spoon-soft — lamb resting on a cloud that once pleased a sultan.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-kuzu-tandir","name":"Kuzu Tandır","nameAlt":"Slow roasted lamb","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"roast","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"feast","ingredients":"300g lamb shoulder · 10ml oil · 5g salt · 3g pepper · 50ml water","method":"Season lamb and place in a roasting dish with water. Cover and cook slowly until very tender. Uncover and roast to brown the surface before serving.","cookTime":"120-150 min","kcal":700,"nutrition":"protein 45g · carbs 2g · fat 58g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Low and slow cooking yields best texture.","pairsWith":"pilaf · salad","trivia":"Traditionally cooked in underground ovens.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-karniyarik","name":"Karnıyarık","nameAlt":"Stuffed eggplant with mince","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"bake","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"dinner","ingredients":"1 eggplant · 150g beef mince · 50g tomato · 20g onion · 5g garlic · 10ml oil · 3g salt","method":"Karnıyarık is İmam Bayıldı's meaty twin — the same split brinjal, but served HOT and filled with spiced mince. Halve the brinjals and fry them first until soft and golden; this step is not optional, because unfried brinjal drinks the pan dry in the oven and never turns silky. Meanwhile brown the mince, add the onion and garlic and cook until sweet, then stir in the chopped tomato with the salt and simmer into a rich filling. Split each fried brinjal down the middle — the 'split belly' of the name — and pile the filling in generously. Bake until everything is fully tender and bubbling. Serve hot, which is exactly what separates it from its meatless, room-temperature cousin.","cookTime":"35-40 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 18g · fat 32g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Salt the cut brinjal first and pat dry — it seasons the flesh and helps it fry golden instead of soggy.","pairsWith":"rice · yogurt","trivia":"Name means 'split belly'.","howThisFeels":"Hot, hearty and split open with pride — the brinjal dish that eats like a proper supper.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-testi-kebabi","name":"Testi Kebabı","nameAlt":"Clay pot meat stew","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"special","ingredients":"250g lamb leg (or shoulder) · 100g tomato · 50g green pepper · 20g onion · 5g garlic · 10ml oil · 3g salt","method":"Combine all ingredients in a sealed clay pot. Cook slowly until tender. Break open pot at table and serve the stew.","cookTime":"90-120 min","kcal":550,"nutrition":"protein 36g · carbs 12g · fat 38g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Slow cooking deepens flavour.","pairsWith":"bread · salad","trivia":"Pot is traditionally cracked open before serving.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-kuzu-pirzola","name":"Kuzu Pirzola","nameAlt":"Grilled lamb chops","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"grill","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"bbq","ingredients":"250g lamb chops · 10ml oil · 3g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Season lamb chops with oil, salt and pepper. Grill over high heat until browned and cooked to preference. Rest briefly and serve.","cookTime":"8-10 min","kcal":650,"nutrition":"protein 42g · carbs 0g · fat 54g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Let meat rest before serving.","pairsWith":"salad · grilled vegetables","trivia":"A staple of Turkish grill houses.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"turkey-balik-ekmek","name":"Balık Ekmek","nameAlt":"Fish sandwich street food","aliases":["Levrek sandwich"],"course":"main","type":"grill","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Turkey","occasion":"street-food","ingredients":"200g hake fillet (or fresh snoek) · 1 bread roll · 20g onion · 20g lettuce · 10ml oil · 3g salt · black pepper","method":"Pat the fish completely dry — wet fish steams, dry fish crisps — and season both sides with the salt and a good grind of black pepper. Slice the onion thinly and rub it with a pinch of salt until it softens and wilts, the way the Istanbul carts do. Heat the oil in a pan until properly hot and fry the fish for 3–4 minutes a side, leaving it alone so it builds a golden crust, until it flakes at a nudge. Split the roll and press it cut-side down into the same pan for a minute — toasting it in the fish's own juices is half the flavour. Build it fast: lettuce on the base, the hot fish, the wilted onions on top, and a big squeeze of lemon. Eat it standing up, from the hand, preferably somewhere with a breeze.","cookTime":"10-12 min","kcal":430,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 28g · fat 18g","storage":"Best eaten fresh; refrigerate fish only up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Istanbul uses mackerel straight off the boats; here hake gives the same clean white flake, and fresh snoek — flaked off the bone — turns it properly Cape. A pinch of sumac rubbed into the onions is the authentic touch if your spice rack runs to it.","pairsWith":"lemon · pickles","trivia":"Balık ekmek is sold from rocking boats along Istanbul's Eminönü waterfront and eaten standing up facing the Bosphorus — usually with a glass of sour pickle juice alongside.","howThisFeels":"Hot flaking fish, sharp wilted onion and pan-toasted bread in the hand — Istanbul's waterfront lunch, perfectly at home with a Cape sea breeze.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-cordon-bleu","name":"Cordon Bleu","nameAlt":"Stuffed veal schnitzel","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"fried","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"dinner","ingredients":"200g veal escalope · 40g ham · 40g cheese · 1 egg · 40g flour · 60g breadcrumbs · 50ml oil · 3g salt","method":"Butterfly the veal and place ham and cheese inside, then fold and secure. Dredge in flour, dip in beaten egg and coat in breadcrumbs. Heat oil in a pan and fry until golden and cooked through. Drain briefly and serve hot.","cookTime":"20-25 min","kcal":650,"nutrition":"protein 45g · carbs 30g · fat 38g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use Gruyère for authentic flavour.","pairsWith":"fries · lemon wedge · salad","trivia":"Despite its French name, it is a Swiss restaurant classic.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-filets-de-perche","name":"Filets de Perche","nameAlt":"Lake perch fillets","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"pan-fried","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"lunch","ingredients":"200g perch fillets · 30g butter · 5g parsley · 10ml lemon juice · 3g salt","method":"Season the fish lightly. Melt butter in a pan and cook fillets gently until golden and just cooked. Finish with lemon juice and chopped parsley, then serve immediately.","cookTime":"10-12 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 38g · carbs 2g · fat 28g","storage":"Best eaten fresh; refrigerate up to 1 day.","chefNotes":"Do not overcook to keep the fish delicate.","pairsWith":"fries · tartar sauce · white wine","trivia":"A specialty around Lake Geneva.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-saucisson-vaudois","name":"Saucisson Vaudois","nameAlt":"Vaud smoked sausage","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"boiled","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"winter","ingredients":"250g saucisson vaudois · 500ml water · 2g salt","method":"Place the sausage in simmering water and cook gently without boiling hard. Remove once heated through and serve hot, often sliced.","cookTime":"35-40 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 25g · carbs 2g · fat 55g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Avoid boiling to prevent splitting the casing.","pairsWith":"potatoes · mustard · leeks","trivia":"Protected regional sausage from Vaud.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-papet-vaudois","name":"Papet Vaudois","nameAlt":"Leek potato sausage stew","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"winter","ingredients":"200g leeks · 200g potatoes · 200g saucisson vaudois · 20g butter · 3g salt","method":"Slice leeks and potatoes and cook slowly in butter until soft. Add water and simmer into a thick stew. Cook the sausage separately and serve on top of the leek mixture.","cookTime":"40-45 min","kcal":680,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 45g · fat 40g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Cook leeks slowly for sweetness.","pairsWith":"mustard · crusty bread · wine","trivia":"Considered the national dish of Vaud canton.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-st-galler-bratwurst","name":"St. Galler Bratwurst with Onion Sauce","nameAlt":"Veal sausage with onions","aliases":["St Galler Bratwurst"],"course":"main","type":"grilled","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"street-food","ingredients":"250g bratwurst · 100g onions · 10ml oil · 3g salt","method":"Grill the sausage until browned and cooked through. Slice onions and sauté in oil until caramelised. Serve the sausage with the onion sauce on top.","cookTime":"20-25 min","kcal":700,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 12g · fat 58g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Traditionally served without mustard.","pairsWith":"bread roll · beer · mustard","trivia":"A pride of St. Gallen with protected status.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-fondue-chinoise","name":"Fondue Chinoise","nameAlt":"Meat broth fondue","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"hotpot","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"200g beef slices · 500ml broth · 20g sauces · 50g bread","method":"Heat broth in a fondue pot. Dip thin slices of meat into the hot liquid and cook briefly. Serve with sauces and bread on the side.","cookTime":"25-30 min","kcal":550,"nutrition":"protein 45g · carbs 20g · fat 30g","storage":"Consume immediately; leftovers up to 1 day.","chefNotes":"Slice meat very thin for quick cooking.","pairsWith":"sauces · bread · pickles","trivia":"Popular festive dish, especially at Christmas.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-landjager","name":"Landjäger","nameAlt":"Dried sausage sticks","aliases":["Landjaeger","Schublig"],"course":"main","type":"cured","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"snack","ingredients":"200g landjäger sausage · 50g bread · 10g mustard","method":"Slice or serve the cured sausage whole. Pair with bread and mustard for a simple hearty meal.","cookTime":"5 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 18g · fat 45g","storage":"Store in a cool place for several days.","chefNotes":"Best enjoyed slightly dried for texture.","pairsWith":"bread · cheese · pickles","trivia":"Popular hiking food due to long shelf life.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-hornli-mit-ghackets","name":"Hörnli mit Ghackets","nameAlt":"Macaroni with minced beef","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"pasta","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"family","ingredients":"100g macaroni · 150g minced beef · 50g onions · 10ml oil · 3g salt","method":"Cook macaroni until tender. In a pan sauté onions in oil, add minced beef and cook until browned. Combine with pasta and season, then serve hot.","cookTime":"25-30 min","kcal":720,"nutrition":"protein 40g · carbs 60g · fat 35g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Add a little stock for extra moisture.","pairsWith":"apple sauce · salad · cheese","trivia":"Often served with apple sauce for contrast.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-forelle","name":"Forelle","nameAlt":"Pan-fried trout","aliases":["Truite"],"course":"main","type":"pan-fried","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"dinner","ingredients":"250g trout · 30g butter · 10ml lemon juice · 3g salt","method":"Season the trout and cook in butter over medium heat until crisp skin forms. Turn and cook through, then finish with lemon juice before serving.","cookTime":"15-18 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 42g · carbs 2g · fat 34g","storage":"Refrigerate up to 1 day.","chefNotes":"Cook skin-side first for crispiness.","pairsWith":"potatoes · spinach · white wine","trivia":"Common in Alpine lake regions.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"switzerland-saltimbocca-alla-ticinese","name":"Saltimbocca alla Ticinese","nameAlt":"Veal with ham and sage","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"pan-fried","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Switzerland","occasion":"dinner","ingredients":"200g veal · 40g ham · 5g sage · 20g butter · 3g salt","method":"Top veal with ham and sage and secure. Cook in butter over medium heat until golden and just cooked. Serve with pan juices spooned over.","cookTime":"15-20 min","kcal":580,"nutrition":"protein 45g · carbs 2g · fat 42g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use thin veal slices for tenderness.","pairsWith":"risotto · vegetables · wine","trivia":"Ticino version influenced by Italian cuisine.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-zrazy","name":"Zrazy","nameAlt":"Beef roulades with filling","aliases":["Zrazy wołowe"],"course":"main","type":"braise","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"family","ingredients":"250g beef rump · 30g bacon · 20g onion · 10g pickles · 5g mustard · 5g flour · 5ml oil · 3g salt","method":"Flatten the beef and spread with mustard. Place bacon, onion and pickles on top, roll tightly and secure. Brown in oil, sprinkle with flour, add water and simmer covered until tender. Slice and serve with the sauce.","cookTime":"90-110 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 42g · carbs 12g · fat 34g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Simmer slowly for maximum tenderness.","pairsWith":"mashed potatoes · beet salad · dill pickles","trivia":"A classic noble dish once served in Polish manors.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-golonka","name":"Golonka","nameAlt":"Braised pork knuckle","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"braise","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"feast","ingredients":"400g pork knuckle · 50g onion · 5g garlic · 5g marjoram · 3g salt · 500ml water","method":"Place pork knuckle in a pot with onion, garlic and spices. Cover with water and simmer until very tender. Optionally roast briefly to crisp the skin. Serve hot.","cookTime":"150-180 min","kcal":780,"nutrition":"protein 55g · carbs 5g · fat 60g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Finish in the oven for crispy skin.","pairsWith":"sauerkraut · mustard · rye bread","trivia":"Popular beer-hall dish across Poland.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-kaczka-pieczona","name":"Kaczka pieczona","nameAlt":"Roast duck with apples","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"roast","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"holiday","ingredients":"300g duck · 100g apple · 5g marjoram · 3g salt · 5ml oil","method":"Season the duck with salt and marjoram. Stuff with apples and place in a roasting pan. Roast until skin is crisp and meat tender, basting occasionally. Rest before serving.","cookTime":"90-110 min","kcal":650,"nutrition":"protein 38g · carbs 10g · fat 50g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Score the skin to help render fat.","pairsWith":"red cabbage · potatoes · cranberry sauce","trivia":"Often served during festive family meals.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-schab-pieczony","name":"Schab pieczony","nameAlt":"Roast pork loin","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"roast","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"family","ingredients":"250g pork loin · 5g garlic · 5g marjoram · 3g salt · 5ml oil","method":"Rub pork with garlic, marjoram, salt and oil. Place in a roasting dish and cook until tender and lightly browned. Rest before slicing and serving.","cookTime":"70-90 min","kcal":540,"nutrition":"protein 45g · carbs 2g · fat 38g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Rest the meat to keep it juicy.","pairsWith":"potatoes · horseradish · pickled cucumbers","trivia":"A staple of Polish Sunday dinners.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-kotlet-mielony","name":"Kotlet mielony","nameAlt":"Polish fried meatballs","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"fry","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"family","ingredients":"200g ground pork · 30g bread · 50ml milk · 20g onion · 1 egg · 3g salt · 10ml oil","method":"Soak bread in milk, then mix with pork, onion, egg and salt. Shape into patties and fry in oil until browned and cooked through. Serve hot.","cookTime":"25-30 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 32g · carbs 18g · fat 44g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use slightly stale bread for better texture.","pairsWith":"mashed potatoes · beet salad · cabbage","trivia":"Often compared to a Polish-style hamburger patty.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-pierogi-z-miesem","name":"Pierogi z mięsem","nameAlt":"Meat-filled dumplings","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"boil","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"family","ingredients":"150g flour · 80g cooked meat · 30g onion · 1 egg · 50ml water · 3g salt","method":"Make a dough with flour, egg, water and salt. Roll thin and cut circles. Fill with minced meat and onion, seal and boil until they float. Serve warm.","cookTime":"40-50 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 55g · fat 20g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Pan-fry after boiling for extra texture.","pairsWith":"fried onions · sour cream · pickles","trivia":"A central dish in Polish cuisine.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-gulasz","name":"Gulasz","nameAlt":"Polish beef stew","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"family","ingredients":"250g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 50g onion · 50g green pepper · 5g paprika · 5ml oil · 3g salt · 200ml stock","method":"Brown beef in oil, add onion and pepper, then paprika. Pour in stock and simmer until tender and thickened. Serve hot.","cookTime":"80-100 min","kcal":560,"nutrition":"protein 40g · carbs 15g · fat 36g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Cook low and slow for best flavour.","pairsWith":"bread · potatoes · pickles","trivia":"Adapted from Hungarian goulash.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-bitki-wolowe","name":"Bitki wołowe","nameAlt":"Braised beef slices","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"braise","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"family","ingredients":"250g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 20g onion · 5g flour · 5ml oil · 3g salt · 200ml stock","method":"Brown beef slices in oil, add onion and sprinkle with flour. Pour in stock and simmer gently until meat is tender and sauce thickens. Serve hot.","cookTime":"70-90 min","kcal":530,"nutrition":"protein 42g · carbs 10g · fat 34g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use a tougher cut for richer flavour.","pairsWith":"buckwheat · cabbage · pickles","trivia":"A traditional home-style comfort dish.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-watrobka","name":"Wątróbka","nameAlt":"Pan-fried liver and onions","aliases":["Watrobka"],"course":"main","type":"fry","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"200g liver · 50g onion · 10ml oil · 3g salt · 5g flour","method":"Coat liver lightly in flour. Fry onions in oil, add liver and cook quickly until just done. Season at the end and serve immediately.","cookTime":"15-20 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 35g · carbs 10g · fat 30g","storage":"Best eaten fresh; refrigerate up to 1 day.","chefNotes":"Salt after frying to keep liver tender.","pairsWith":"bread · potatoes · pickles","trivia":"A popular affordable classic in Poland.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"poland-kaszanka","name":"Kaszanka","nameAlt":"Blood sausage with groats","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"grill","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Poland","occasion":"street-food","ingredients":"250g blood sausage · 50g onion · 5ml oil · 3g salt","method":"Slice sausage and fry or grill with onions in oil until heated through and slightly crisp. Serve hot.","cookTime":"15-20 min","kcal":620,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 20g · fat 48g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Grill for a smoky flavour.","pairsWith":"mustard · bread · sauerkraut","trivia":"Often enjoyed at outdoor markets and grills.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-paprikas-csirke","name":"Paprikás Csirke","nameAlt":"Paprika chicken stew","aliases":["Chicken Paprikash"],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"family","ingredients":"250g chicken · 50g onion · 10g paprika · 50ml sour cream · 10g flour · 10ml oil · 3g salt","method":"Heat the oil in a wide pan over medium heat and cook the chopped onion gently until soft and golden, about 6 minutes. Pull the pan off the heat and stir in the paprika so it blooms in the warm oil without burning. Return to the heat, add the chicken pieces and the salt, and turn them through the red base until well coated. Pour in a little water — just enough to keep things saucy, never swimming — then cover and simmer gently for about 30 minutes until the chicken is tender. Now the silk: whisk the flour into the sour cream until smooth, stir in a spoonful of the hot cooking liquid to warm it, then stir the mixture back into the pan. Simmer very gently for 5 minutes until the sauce turns pale coral and coats the back of a spoon — never let it boil hard, or the cream will split. Taste and correct the seasoning. Serve over nokedli dumplings — in Hungary that pairing is not a suggestion, it is the law.","cookTime":"45-55 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 42g · carbs 10g · fat 34g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Temper the sour cream with a spoonful of hot liquid before it goes in — dumped into a boiling pot it curdles; eased in, it turns to silk.","pairsWith":"nokedli · cucumber salad · pickles","trivia":"Paprikás is the creamy corner of Hungary's sacred triangle — gulyás the soup, pörkölt the stew, paprikás the one finished with sour-cream silk.","howThisFeels":"Pale-coral sour-cream silk over tender chicken — Sunday in a Hungarian kitchen.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-rantott-hus","name":"Rántott Hús","nameAlt":"Breaded fried cutlet","aliases":["Hungarian schnitzel"],"course":"main","type":"fried","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"family","ingredients":"200g pork loin (or leg) · 1 egg · 30g flour · 40g breadcrumbs · 100ml oil · 3g salt","method":"Pound pork thin and season with salt. Coat in flour, dip in beaten egg, then coat in breadcrumbs. Fry in hot oil until golden and crisp on both sides. Drain and serve hot.","cookTime":"20-25 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 38g · carbs 28g · fat 38g","storage":"Best eaten fresh but can be refrigerated for 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use fine breadcrumbs for a crisp coating.","pairsWith":"mashed potatoes · lemon wedges · salad","trivia":"A staple Sunday lunch dish in Hungary.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-csulok-pekne-modra","name":"Csülök Pékné Módra","nameAlt":"Roast pork knuckle potatoes","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"roast","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"feast","ingredients":"400g pork knuckle · 200g potatoes · 50g onion · 5g garlic · 10ml oil · 4g salt","method":"Season pork knuckle and roast until starting to crisp. Add sliced potatoes, onion, and garlic with oil. Continue roasting until everything is golden and tender.","cookTime":"90-120 min","kcal":750,"nutrition":"protein 45g · carbs 35g · fat 48g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Roast slowly for tender meat and crispy skin.","pairsWith":"pickled cabbage · mustard · bread","trivia":"A classic pub-style Hungarian dish.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-brassoi-apropecsenye","name":"Brassói Aprópecsenye","nameAlt":"Garlic pork with potatoes","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"saute","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"casual","ingredients":"250g pork shoulder · 200g potatoes · 5g garlic · 10ml oil · 3g salt","method":"Fry diced pork in oil until browned. Add garlic and cook briefly. Fry potatoes separately until crisp, then combine with pork and season. Serve hot.","cookTime":"30-40 min","kcal":680,"nutrition":"protein 40g · carbs 45g · fat 36g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Cook potatoes separately for crisp texture.","pairsWith":"pickles · salad · bread","trivia":"Named after the city of Brașov, though its origins are debated.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-ciganypecsenye","name":"Cigánypecsenye","nameAlt":"Garlic pork with bacon","aliases":["Gypsy-style pork"],"course":"main","type":"grill","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"casual","ingredients":"250g pork neck (or leg) · 30g bacon · 5g garlic · 10ml oil · 3g salt","method":"Season pork and grill or pan-fry until cooked through. Fry bacon until crisp and place on top. Rub with garlic and serve hot.","cookTime":"25-30 min","kcal":700,"nutrition":"protein 42g · carbs 2g · fat 58g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Serve immediately for best texture.","pairsWith":"bread · pickles · mustard","trivia":"Traditionally cooked over open flame.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-vadas-marha","name":"Vadas Marha","nameAlt":"Beef in vegetable sauce","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"braise","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"family","ingredients":"250g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 100g carrot · 50g onion · 30g celery · 50ml sour cream · 10g mustard · 10ml oil · 4g salt","method":"Brown beef in oil, then add vegetables and cook briefly. Add water and simmer until tender. Blend sauce smooth, stir in mustard and sour cream, then return beef and heat through.","cookTime":"90-110 min","kcal":560,"nutrition":"protein 45g · carbs 20g · fat 32g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Blend sauce finely for smooth texture.","pairsWith":"dumplings · bread · pickles","trivia":"Originally inspired by game dishes in aristocratic cuisine.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-toltott-paprika","name":"Töltött Paprika","nameAlt":"Stuffed peppers with mince","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"family","ingredients":"2 peppers · 200g minced pork · 50g rice · 50g tomato sauce · 30g onion · 5g paprika · 3g salt","method":"Mix minced pork with rice, onion, paprika, and salt. Stuff into peppers and place in a pot with tomato sauce. Simmer gently until peppers are tender and filling is cooked through.","cookTime":"50-60 min","kcal":500,"nutrition":"protein 32g · carbs 35g · fat 24g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Use slightly undercooked rice for best texture.","pairsWith":"bread · sour cream · salad","trivia":"A beloved home-style dish across Hungary.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"hungary-disznotoros","name":"Disznótoros","nameAlt":"Hungarian pork feast platter","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"mixed","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Hungary","occasion":"festival","ingredients":"200g sausage · 200g roast pork · 100g blood sausage · 100g sauerkraut · 10ml oil · 3g salt","method":"Cook sausages and roast pork until browned and cooked through. Heat blood sausage gently. Serve all meats together with warmed sauerkraut.","cookTime":"40-50 min","kcal":900,"nutrition":"protein 50g · carbs 12g · fat 72g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Serve hot for best flavour balance.","pairsWith":"bread · mustard · pickles","trivia":"Traditionally prepared during winter pig slaughter feasts.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-backhendl","name":"Backhendl","nameAlt":"Viennese fried chicken","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"fry","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"family","ingredients":"250g chicken pieces · 40g flour · 1 egg · 60g breadcrumbs · 3g salt · 1g pepper · 200ml oil","method":"Season the chicken with salt and pepper. Dredge in flour, dip in beaten egg, then coat with breadcrumbs. Heat oil in a deep pan and fry the chicken until golden brown and cooked through, about 12 minutes. Drain on paper and serve hot.","cookTime":"15-20 min","kcal":620,"nutrition":"protein 38g · carbs 35g · fat 38g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use a mix of dark and white meat for better flavour.","pairsWith":"potato salad · lemon wedges · parsley","trivia":"A classic Viennese dish often served in traditional wine taverns.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-zwiebelrostbraten","name":"Zwiebelrostbraten","nameAlt":"Beef with crispy onions","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"pan-fry","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"dinner","ingredients":"200g beef steak · 80g onions · 20g flour · 10g butter · 5ml oil · 3g salt · 1g pepper","method":"Season the beef and sear in hot oil until browned, then remove. Slice onions, dust lightly with flour and fry in butter until crispy. Return beef to pan briefly with juices. Top with fried onions and serve.","cookTime":"20-25 min","kcal":540,"nutrition":"protein 42g · carbs 18g · fat 34g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Use high-quality beef like sirloin for best results.","pairsWith":"fried potatoes · green beans · gravy","trivia":"A staple of Viennese beef cuisine with deep roots in imperial kitchens.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-selchfleisch","name":"Selchfleisch","nameAlt":"Smoked pork with sides","aliases":["Geselchtes"],"course":"main","type":"boil","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"250g smoked pork · 200ml water · 2g salt · 1g pepper · 100g sauerkraut · 150g potatoes","method":"Place smoked pork in a pot with water and simmer gently until heated through and tender. Boil potatoes separately until soft. Warm sauerkraut. Slice the pork and serve with potatoes and sauerkraut.","cookTime":"40-50 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 40g · carbs 35g · fat 32g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Do not over-salt as the pork is already cured.","pairsWith":"mustard · sauerkraut · boiled potatoes","trivia":"Smoking meat was historically essential for preservation in Alpine regions.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-bauernschmaus","name":"Bauernschmaus","nameAlt":"Mixed roast meat platter","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"roast","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"feast","ingredients":"150g pork belly · 100g sausages · 150g smoked pork · 200g sauerkraut · 150g dumplings · 3g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Season pork belly and roast until crispy and tender. Grill sausages until browned. Warm smoked pork and sauerkraut. Serve all meats together with dumplings and sauerkraut.","cookTime":"60-75 min","kcal":850,"nutrition":"protein 45g · carbs 40g · fat 58g","storage":"Refrigerate leftovers for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Serve family-style for a traditional experience.","pairsWith":"beer · mustard · horseradish","trivia":"The name means 'farmer’s feast' reflecting hearty rural cooking.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-stelze","name":"Stelze","nameAlt":"Roast pork knuckle","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"roast","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"beerhall","ingredients":"400g pork knuckle · 5g salt · 3g garlic · 2g caraway seeds · 10ml oil · 200ml beer","method":"Score the pork skin and rub with salt, garlic and caraway. Roast in oven, basting with beer, until skin is crispy and meat tender. Rest briefly and carve.","cookTime":"90-120 min","kcal":920,"nutrition":"protein 55g · carbs 5g · fat 78g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Dry the skin well before roasting for extra crispiness.","pairsWith":"mustard · sauerkraut · bread","trivia":"A favorite in Austrian beer halls, especially in Vienna.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"austria-faschierter-braten","name":"Faschierter Braten","nameAlt":"Austrian meatloaf","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"bake","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Austria","occasion":"family","ingredients":"250g ground pork and beef · 40g breadcrumbs · 1 egg · 30g onion · 5g garlic · 5g mustard · 3g salt · 1g pepper","method":"Mix meat with breadcrumbs, egg, finely chopped onion, garlic, mustard, salt and pepper. Shape into a loaf and place in a baking dish. Bake until cooked through and browned. Rest before slicing and serving.","cookTime":"45-55 min","kcal":650,"nutrition":"protein 42g · carbs 20g · fat 45g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Add a splash of milk for a softer texture.","pairsWith":"mashed potatoes · gravy · green beans","trivia":"A comforting home-style dish found across Austria.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-kharcho","name":"ხარჩო","nameAlt":"spicy beef walnut stew","aliases":["Kharcho"],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"family","ingredients":"250g stewing beef (chuck or shin) · 20g walnuts · 30g rice · 50g onion · 10g garlic · 30g tomato puree · 5g chili paste · 5g khmeli suneli · 5g fresh coriander · 3g salt · 10ml oil · 300ml water","method":"Heat the oil in a heavy pot and sauté the chopped onion over medium heat until soft and golden, about 8 minutes — that sweetness balances the sour and heat coming later. Add the beef and brown it properly on all sides; those dark edges are the backbone of the stew. Stir in the garlic, tomato purée, chilli paste and khmeli suneli and cook for a minute so the spices bloom in the hot oil, then pour in the water, season with salt, and simmer gently, covered, for 60 to 80 minutes until the beef gives no resistance. Stir in the rice and cook for 15 minutes — kharcho lives between soup and stew, and the rice is what makes it a meal. Grind the walnuts into a paste and stir them in, cooking a further 10 minutes; the walnuts thicken the pot the Georgian way — no flour needed — and add a deep, buttery body. Taste and correct the salt and heat. Shower with fresh coriander and serve hot with bread.","cookTime":"90-110 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 38g · carbs 12g · fat 36g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"No khmeli suneli? Blend ground coriander and fenugreek with dried basil and a pinch of marjoram.","pairsWith":"rice · bread · fresh herbs","trivia":"Kharcho is traditionally thickened with ground walnuts instead of flour.","howThisFeels":"Beef, walnuts and slow fire — Georgia's deepest answer to winter.","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-chicken-tabaka","name":"ტაბაკა","nameAlt":"flattened fried chicken","aliases":["Chicken Tabaka","Tabaka"],"course":"main","type":"fried","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"casual","ingredients":"1 small chicken · 5g garlic · 3g salt · 2g black pepper · 20ml oil · 10g butter","method":"Split and flatten the chicken by pressing it firmly. Rub with salt, pepper and crushed garlic. Heat oil and butter in a heavy pan and place the chicken skin-side down. Weigh it down and fry until golden and crisp, then flip and cook until fully done and juicy inside. Serve immediately.","cookTime":"30-40 min","kcal":600,"nutrition":"protein 45g · carbs 2g · fat 46g","storage":"Best eaten fresh but can be refrigerated for 2 days.","chefNotes":"Pressing the chicken ensures even crisping.","pairsWith":"garlic sauce · pickles · flatbread","trivia":"Named after the traditional heavy pan used to press the chicken.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-chanakhi","name":"ჩანახი","nameAlt":"lamb vegetable clay stew","aliases":["Chanakhi"],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"family","ingredients":"250g stewing lamb (shoulder or neck) · 80g potato · 60g eggplant · 50g tomato · 40g onion · 5g garlic · 5g herbs · 3g salt · 200ml water","method":"Layer lamb pieces, chopped vegetables and garlic in a clay pot. Season with salt and herbs, then add water. Cover and bake slowly until the lamb is tender and vegetables are soft, allowing the flavors to meld together. Serve directly from the pot.","cookTime":"100-120 min","kcal":540,"nutrition":"protein 34g · carbs 28g · fat 34g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 3 days.","chefNotes":"Cook slowly for maximum tenderness.","pairsWith":"bread · fresh herbs · pickled vegetables","trivia":"Traditionally cooked in individual clay pots in the oven.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"georgia-kuchmachi","name":"კუჭმაჩი","nameAlt":"spiced organ meat dish","aliases":["Kuchmachi"],"course":"main","type":"saute","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"eastern-europe","country":"Georgia","occasion":"feast","ingredients":"150g chicken liver · 100g chicken heart · 50g onion · 10g garlic · 20g walnuts · 5g pomegranate seeds · 5g spices · 3g salt · 15ml oil","method":"Heat oil and sauté chopped onion until soft. Add liver and heart pieces and cook until browned and just cooked through. Stir in garlic, spices and ground walnuts, cooking briefly to combine. Finish with pomegranate seeds and serve hot.","cookTime":"20-25 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 36g · carbs 10g · fat 32g","storage":"Refrigerate for up to 2 days.","chefNotes":"Avoid overcooking to keep organs tender.","pairsWith":"flatbread · herbs · red wine","trivia":"Often garnished with pomegranate for a sweet-tart contrast.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"germany-bavarian-cheese-spread","name":"Bavarian cheese spread","nameAlt":"Obatzda","aliases":[],"course":"starter","type":"spread","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Germany","occasion":"beerhall","ingredients":"100g ripe camembert · 30g butter (softened) · 20g cream cheese · 30g red onion (finely chopped) · 5g sweet paprika · 2g caraway seeds · pinch salt","method":"Mash the ripe camembert with a fork. Beat in the soft butter and cream cheese until smooth and creamy. Stir in the paprika, caraway and a pinch of salt, then fold through the finely chopped onion. Chill for 30 minutes to firm up. Serve with fresh pretzels and radishes.","cookTime":"10 min","kcal":340,"nutrition":"protein 12g · carbs 4g · fat 30g","storage":"Keeps covered in the fridge for 3 days.","chefNotes":"The riper the camembert, the better the flavour.","pairsWith":"soft pretzel · radishes · rye bread","trivia":"A classic of Bavarian beer gardens.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"germany-beef-rouladen","name":"Beef rouladen","nameAlt":"Stuffed beef rolls","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"braise","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Germany","occasion":"Sunday lunch","ingredients":"180g thin beef slices · 30g bacon · 40g onion · 20g pickle (sliced) · 10g mustard · 60ml red wine · 120ml beef stock · 10g butter · 5g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Spread the beef slices with mustard and season. Lay bacon, onion and pickle along each, then roll up tightly and fix with a toothpick. Brown the rolls in butter on all sides. Pour in the red wine and stock, cover, and braise gently for about 1.5 hours until very tender. Lift out the rolls and reduce the sauce, then serve it poured over.","cookTime":"1 hr 45 min","kcal":430,"nutrition":"protein 34g · carbs 6g · fat 26g","storage":"Keeps in the fridge for 2 days; reheats well.","chefNotes":"Remove the toothpicks before serving.","pairsWith":"red cabbage · dumplings · mashed potato","trivia":"A beloved German Sunday roast dish.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"germany-black-forest-cake","name":"Black Forest cake","nameAlt":"Schwarzwälder Kirschtorte","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"cake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Germany","occasion":"celebration","ingredients":"60g flour · 20g cocoa · 60g sugar · 2 eggs · 120g cherries · 15ml kirsch · 120ml cream · 20g dark chocolate","method":"Whisk the eggs and sugar until pale and thick. Gently fold in the flour and cocoa, then bake at 180°C for about 25 minutes until springy. Cool, then split into layers and brush with kirsch. Whip the cream and layer it with the cherries between the sponge. Cover with more cream and finish with dark chocolate shavings and a few cherries on top.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":380,"nutrition":"protein 7g · carbs 42g · fat 20g","storage":"Keep chilled and eat within 2 days.","chefNotes":"Leave out the kirsch for a child-friendly version.","pairsWith":"coffee · whipped cream","trivia":"Named after the cherry brandy of the Black Forest region.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"germany-braised-red-cabbage","name":"Braised red cabbage","nameAlt":"Rotkohl","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"braise","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Germany","occasion":"festive","ingredients":"200g red cabbage (shredded) · 60g apple (grated) · 40g onion · 15g butter · 30ml red wine · 10g sugar · 10ml vinegar · 1 bay leaf · 2 cloves · pinch salt","method":"Soften the onion in butter. Add the shredded cabbage and grated apple and stir well. Pour in the red wine, vinegar and sugar, then tuck in the bay leaf and cloves. Cover and braise gently for about 45 minutes, stirring now and then, until soft, sweet and glossy. Season with salt before serving.","cookTime":"50 min","kcal":130,"nutrition":"protein 2g · carbs 18g · fat 5g","storage":"Keeps 3 days; even better the next day.","chefNotes":"A splash more vinegar keeps the colour bright.","pairsWith":"roast pork · dumplings · sausages","trivia":"A staple of German Christmas dinners.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"germany-currywurst","name":"Currywurst","nameAlt":"Curried sausage","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"street-food","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Germany","occasion":"street food","ingredients":"150g pork sausage (bratwurst) · 60g tomato ketchup · 5g curry powder · 3g paprika · 5ml worcestershire sauce · 120g chips (to serve)","method":"Fry or grill the sausage until browned, then slice into chunks. Warm the ketchup with the paprika and worcestershire sauce in a small pan. Spoon the sauce over the sausage and dust generously with curry powder. Serve hot with a pile of chips.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":480,"nutrition":"protein 16g · carbs 40g · fat 28g","storage":"Best fresh; not suited to storing.","chefNotes":"Pile the curry powder on at the end, not in the sauce.","pairsWith":"chips · bread roll","trivia":"An icon of Berlin street food.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"germany-green-herb-sauce","name":"Green herb sauce","nameAlt":"Grüne Soße","aliases":[],"course":"starter","type":"sauce","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Germany","occasion":"spring","ingredients":"40g mixed fresh herbs (parsley, chives, chervil, cress) · 60g sour cream · 60g quark · 5g mustard · 5ml lemon juice · 1 egg (hard-boiled) · pinch salt","method":"Finely chop the fresh herbs. Stir them into the sour cream and quark with the mustard, lemon juice and a pinch of salt. Chop the hard-boiled egg and fold it through, or grate it over the top. Chill briefly and serve with boiled potatoes.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":180,"nutrition":"protein 9g · carbs 6g · fat 13g","storage":"Best eaten the same day.","chefNotes":"Use as many soft green herbs as you can find.","pairsWith":"boiled potatoes · hard-boiled eggs","trivia":"A speciality of Frankfurt, traditional at Easter.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"germany-lentil-soup","name":"Lentil soup","nameAlt":"Linsensuppe","aliases":[],"course":"starter","type":"soup","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Germany","occasion":"winter","ingredients":"80g green lentils · 40g carrot · 30g celery · 40g onion · 30g bacon · 10g butter · 1 garlic clove · 5ml vinegar · 2g marjoram · 5g parsley · pinch salt","method":"Fry the bacon and onion in butter until soft. Add the diced carrot, celery and garlic and stir for a minute. Add the lentils and enough water or stock to cover well. Simmer for about 35 minutes until the lentils are soft. Finish with a splash of vinegar, the marjoram and chopped parsley.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":260,"nutrition":"protein 14g · carbs 30g · fat 9g","storage":"Keeps 3 days; thickens as it stands.","chefNotes":"A little vinegar at the end lifts the whole pot.","pairsWith":"crusty bread · sausages","trivia":"Often served with a frankfurter on top.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"germany-meatballs-in-caper-sauce","name":"Meatballs in caper sauce","nameAlt":"Königsberger Klopse","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"meatballs","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Germany","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"150g ground meat (beef and pork) · 20g breadcrumbs · 1 egg · 40g onion · 60ml white wine · 60ml cream · 15g capers · 5ml lemon juice · 5g anchovy paste · pinch salt","method":"Mix the ground meat with breadcrumbs, egg, grated onion, anchovy paste and salt. Shape into small balls and poach gently in simmering stock until cooked through, then lift out. Make a white sauce with the cooking stock, wine and cream, then stir in the capers and lemon juice. Return the meatballs to warm through and serve.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":410,"nutrition":"protein 26g · carbs 9g · fat 28g","storage":"Keeps 2 days in the fridge.","chefNotes":"The capers and lemon are what make this dish.","pairsWith":"boiled potatoes · rice · beetroot","trivia":"Named after the old Prussian city of Königsberg.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"germany-potato-soup","name":"Potato soup","nameAlt":"Kartoffelsuppe","aliases":[],"course":"starter","type":"soup","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Germany","occasion":"comfort food","ingredients":"200g potatoes · 30g carrot · 30g leek · 20g celery · 30g bacon · 30ml cream · 10g butter · 1 garlic clove · 2g marjoram · 5g parsley · pinch salt","method":"Fry the bacon until crisp, then soften the leek, carrot, celery and garlic in butter. Add the diced potato and enough stock to cover. Simmer for about 25 minutes until soft, then mash part of it to thicken. Stir in the cream and marjoram, and scatter with parsley to serve.","cookTime":"40 min","kcal":240,"nutrition":"protein 7g · carbs 28g · fat 12g","storage":"Keeps 3 days; reheats well.","chefNotes":"Mash only half for a soup with body and bite.","pairsWith":"crusty bread · sausages","trivia":"A homely staple across all of Germany.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"germany-roast-pork-knuckle","name":"Roast pork knuckle","nameAlt":"Schweinshaxe","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"roast","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Germany","occasion":"beerhall","ingredients":"350g pork knuckle · 120ml beer · 40g onion · 2 garlic cloves · 5g caraway seeds · 5g salt · 2g pepper","method":"Score the skin and rub all over with salt, pepper and caraway. Sit the knuckle on the onion and garlic and pour the beer into the tin. Roast slowly at 160°C for about 2 hours, basting now and then. Turn the oven up to 220°C for the last 20 minutes to crisp the crackling. Rest before serving.","cookTime":"2 hr 30 min","kcal":560,"nutrition":"protein 42g · carbs 4g · fat 42g","storage":"Keeps 2 days; crackling softens once stored.","chefNotes":"Dry the skin well before roasting for the best crackling.","pairsWith":"sauerkraut · dumplings · mustard","trivia":"A Bavarian beer-hall favourite.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"germany-sauerbraten","name":"Sauerbraten","nameAlt":"German pot roast","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"braise","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Germany","occasion":"Sunday lunch","ingredients":"180g beef (rump or chuck) · 80ml red wine vinegar · 80ml water · 40g onion · 30g carrot · 1 bay leaf · 3 juniper berries · 10g butter · 10g flour · 15g raisins · pinch salt","method":"Marinate the beef in the vinegar, water, onion, carrot, bay and juniper for a day or two in the fridge, turning now and then. Pat dry and brown well in butter, then pour the strained marinade back in. Braise gently for about 2 hours until very tender. Make a gravy from the liquid with a little flour, stir in the raisins, and serve sliced.","cookTime":"2 hr 30 min","kcal":400,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 12g · fat 24g","storage":"Keeps 3 days; flavour deepens.","chefNotes":"A few crushed gingersnaps in the gravy is the traditional touch.","pairsWith":"red cabbage · dumplings · potato","trivia":"Germany's national dish, marinated for days.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"germany-spatzle","name":"Spätzle","nameAlt":"Soft egg noodles","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"noodles","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"western-europe","country":"Germany","occasion":"everyday","ingredients":"100g flour · 1 egg · 40ml milk · 10g butter · pinch salt · 30g cheese (optional, for Käsespätzle)","method":"Beat the flour, egg, milk and salt into a thick, sticky batter and let it rest a few minutes. Press or scrape the batter into a pot of boiling salted water in small pieces. Lift them out as they float to the top. Toss in butter, or layer with grated cheese and fried onions to make Käsespätzle.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 11g · carbs 42g · fat 9g","storage":"Keeps 2 days; fry in butter to reheat.","chefNotes":"A colander with big holes works as a spätzle press.","pairsWith":"goulash · roast meat · gravy","trivia":"A speciality of southern Germany and Swabia.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"northern-ireland-apple-and-blackberry-tart","name":"Apple and blackberry tart","nameAlt":"Apple and blackberry tart","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"tart","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Northern Ireland","occasion":"autumn","ingredients":"60g flour · 30g butter · 80g apple (peeled, sliced) · 40g blackberries · 20g sugar · 5ml milk (to glaze)","method":"Rub the butter into the flour and add a little cold water to make a soft pastry. Roll out and line a small tin, keeping some back for the top. Fill with the sliced apple, blackberries and sugar. Cover with pastry, brush with milk and bake at 180°C for about 35 minutes until golden.","cookTime":"50 min","kcal":300,"nutrition":"protein 4g · carbs 44g · fat 12g","storage":"Keeps 2 days; warm gently to serve.","chefNotes":"A handful of blackberries adds colour and tartness.","pairsWith":"custard · cream · ice cream","trivia":"A favourite when the autumn brambles are ripe.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"northern-ireland-bacon-and-cabbage","name":"Bacon and cabbage","nameAlt":"Bacon and cabbage","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"boiled","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Northern Ireland","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"180g bacon (collar or back) · 150g cabbage (shredded) · 80g potato · 1 bay leaf · 10g butter · 2g pepper","method":"Cover the bacon with water, add the bay leaf and simmer gently for about an hour until tender. Add the potatoes to cook through. Lift out the bacon, then cook the shredded cabbage briefly in some of the bacon water until just soft. Drain, toss with butter and pepper, and serve with the sliced bacon.","cookTime":"1 hr 10 min","kcal":380,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 16g · fat 24g","storage":"Keeps 2 days in the fridge.","chefNotes":"Use a little of the bacon water to cook the cabbage for flavour.","pairsWith":"parsley sauce · boiled potatoes · mustard","trivia":"A homely staple across the island of Ireland.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"northern-ireland-battered-pork-patty","name":"Battered pork patty","nameAlt":"Pastie","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"fried","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Northern Ireland","occasion":"street food","ingredients":"120g minced pork · 40g potato (mashed) · 30g onion · 2g mixed herbs · 50g flour (batter) · 60ml milk (batter) · oil for frying · pinch salt","method":"Mix the minced pork with the mashed potato, finely chopped onion, herbs and salt. Shape into a flat oval patty. Whisk the flour and milk into a smooth batter. Dip the patty in the batter to coat, then deep-fry until golden and cooked through. Drain and serve hot, often with chips.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 20g · carbs 38g · fat 32g","storage":"Best fresh from the pan.","chefNotes":"Keep the patty thin so the middle cooks through.","pairsWith":"chips · curry sauce","trivia":"A chip-shop classic known in Ulster as the pastie supper.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"northern-ireland-chicken-and-ham-pie","name":"Chicken and ham pie","nameAlt":"Chicken and ham pie","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"pie","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Northern Ireland","occasion":"family","ingredients":"100g chicken · 60g ham · 40g leek · 30ml cream · 10g flour · 10g butter · 80g shortcrust pastry · 1 egg (to glaze) · pinch salt","method":"Soften the leek in butter, then stir in the flour and a little stock and cream to make a thick sauce. Fold in the cooked chicken and ham and season. Spoon into a dish and top with the pastry. Brush with beaten egg and bake at 200°C for about 30 minutes until golden.","cookTime":"45 min","kcal":460,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 30g · fat 26g","storage":"Keeps 2 days; reheats well.","chefNotes":"Use up leftover roast chicken here.","pairsWith":"mashed potato · peas · gravy","trivia":"A comforting way to stretch a Sunday roast.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"northern-ireland-irish-stew","name":"Irish stew","nameAlt":"Irish stew","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"stew","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Northern Ireland","occasion":"winter","ingredients":"180g lamb (neck or shoulder, bone-in) · 120g potato · 60g onion · 40g carrot · 1 bay leaf · 200ml stock · 5g parsley · pinch salt · 2g pepper","method":"Layer the lamb, onion and carrot in a heavy pot and season. Add the bay leaf and pour in the stock. Lay the potatoes on top so they steam and thicken the stew. Cover and simmer very gently for about 2 hours until the lamb is tender and falling from the bone. Scatter with parsley to serve.","cookTime":"2 hr 15 min","kcal":420,"nutrition":"protein 28g · carbs 24g · fat 22g","storage":"Keeps 3 days; even better next day.","chefNotes":"Bone-in lamb gives the richest flavour.","pairsWith":"soda bread · butter","trivia":"One of Ireland's most cherished dishes.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"northern-ireland-leek-and-oat-soup","name":"Leek and oat soup","nameAlt":"Brotchán Roy","aliases":[],"course":"starter","type":"soup","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Northern Ireland","occasion":"comfort food","ingredients":"80g leek · 20g pinhead oats · 200ml milk · 100ml stock · 10g butter · 5g parsley · pinch salt","method":"Soften the sliced leek gently in butter without colouring. Stir in the oats, then pour in the stock and milk. Simmer for about 20 minutes until the oats are soft and the soup has thickened. Season and finish with chopped parsley.","cookTime":"30 min","kcal":200,"nutrition":"protein 8g · carbs 24g · fat 9g","storage":"Keeps 2 days; loosen with milk to reheat.","chefNotes":"Pinhead oats give a lovely nutty body.","pairsWith":"wheaten bread · butter","trivia":"An old Irish soup, once a monastery dish.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"northern-ireland-marshmallow-tray-bake","name":"Marshmallow tray bake","nameAlt":"Fifteens","aliases":[],"course":"dessert","type":"traybake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Northern Ireland","occasion":"treat","ingredients":"60g digestive biscuits (crushed) · 30g marshmallows · 30g glacé cherries · 60ml condensed milk · 30g desiccated coconut","method":"Mix the crushed biscuits, marshmallows and chopped cherries in a bowl. Stir through the condensed milk until everything clumps together. Roll into a log on a bed of desiccated coconut and coat all over. Chill until firm, then slice into rounds.","cookTime":"15 min","kcal":280,"nutrition":"protein 4g · carbs 40g · fat 12g","storage":"Keeps a week in the fridge.","chefNotes":"No baking needed; just chill and slice.","pairsWith":"tea · coffee","trivia":"Named for the fifteen of each main ingredient in the classic recipe.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"northern-ireland-mashed-potato-with-spring-onion","name":"Mashed potato with spring onion","nameAlt":"Champ","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"mash","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Northern Ireland","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"200g potato · 30g spring onion · 40ml milk · 20g butter · pinch salt","method":"Boil the potatoes until soft and mash them well. Warm the milk gently with the chopped spring onion. Beat the milk and most of the butter into the mash until light and fluffy, and season. Pile into a bowl, make a well in the centre and drop the last of the butter in to melt.","cookTime":"25 min","kcal":230,"nutrition":"protein 5g · carbs 32g · fat 10g","storage":"Keeps 2 days; reheat with a splash of milk.","chefNotes":"Warm the milk so the mash stays fluffy.","pairsWith":"sausages · bacon · stew","trivia":"A much-loved Ulster comfort dish.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"northern-ireland-potato-bread-farls","name":"Potato bread farls","nameAlt":"Fadge","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"griddle bread","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Northern Ireland","occasion":"breakfast","ingredients":"150g potato (mashed) · 40g flour · 10g butter · pinch salt","method":"Mix the warm mashed potato with the flour, melted butter and salt to make a soft dough. Roll out on a floured surface and cut into four triangular farls. Cook on a dry hot griddle for a few minutes each side until browned and cooked through.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":190,"nutrition":"protein 4g · carbs 30g · fat 6g","storage":"Keeps 2 days; fry to refresh.","chefNotes":"Best fried in bacon fat as part of an Ulster fry.","pairsWith":"bacon · egg · sausage","trivia":"A cornerstone of the traditional Ulster fry.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"northern-ireland-potato-pancake","name":"Potato pancake","nameAlt":"Boxty","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"pancake","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Northern Ireland","occasion":"traditional","ingredients":"80g potato (grated, raw) · 60g potato (mashed) · 30g flour · 30ml milk · pinch bicarbonate of soda · pinch salt","method":"Squeeze the raw grated potato dry, then mix with the mashed potato, flour, milk, soda and salt into a thick batter. Drop spoonfuls onto a buttered hot pan and flatten slightly. Fry for a few minutes each side until golden and cooked through.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":210,"nutrition":"protein 5g · carbs 34g · fat 6g","storage":"Keeps 2 days; fry to reheat.","chefNotes":"Squeezing the grated potato dry keeps them from going soggy.","pairsWith":"butter · bacon · apple sauce","trivia":"Boxty bridges grated and mashed potato in one cake.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"northern-ireland-soda-bread-farls","name":"Soda bread farls","nameAlt":"Soda farls","aliases":[],"course":"side","type":"griddle bread","diet":"vegetarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Northern Ireland","occasion":"breakfast","ingredients":"100g flour · 80ml buttermilk · 2g bicarbonate of soda · pinch salt","method":"Mix the flour, bicarbonate of soda and salt, then stir in the buttermilk to make a soft, slightly sticky dough. Shape into a round and flatten, then cut into four farls. Cook on a dry hot griddle for about 6 minutes each side until risen and browned.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":170,"nutrition":"protein 5g · carbs 34g · fat 1g","storage":"Best fresh; toast or fry the next day.","chefNotes":"Buttermilk and soda make it rise without yeast.","pairsWith":"butter · jam · fried egg","trivia":"A griddle bread eaten warm across Ulster.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"northern-ireland-steak-and-guinness-pie","name":"Steak and Guinness pie","nameAlt":"Steak and Guinness pie","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"pie","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Northern Ireland","occasion":"pub","ingredients":"180g stewing beef · 40g onion · 30g mushroom · 120ml Guinness · 100ml stock · 10g flour · 10g butter · 80g puff pastry · 1 egg (to glaze) · pinch salt","method":"Toss the beef in seasoned flour and brown it in butter, then soften the onion and mushroom. Pour in the Guinness and stock and simmer gently for about 1.5 hours until the beef is tender and the gravy is rich. Spoon into a dish, top with puff pastry, brush with beaten egg and bake at 200°C for about 25 minutes until golden.","cookTime":"2 hr 15 min","kcal":520,"nutrition":"protein 30g · carbs 30g · fat 30g","storage":"Keeps 2 days; reheats well.","chefNotes":"Let the filling cool a little before topping with pastry.","pairsWith":"mashed potato · peas · gravy","trivia":"The dark stout gives the gravy its deep flavour.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"northern-ireland-ulster-fry","name":"Ulster fry","nameAlt":"Ulster fry","aliases":[],"course":"main","type":"fry-up","diet":"omnivore","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Northern Ireland","occasion":"breakfast","ingredients":"1 egg · 60g bacon · 50g sausage · 1 potato farl · 1 soda farl · 40g tomato · 10g butter","method":"Fry the bacon and sausage until cooked, then add the halved tomato. Fry the soda and potato farls in the pan fat until golden on both sides. Finally fry the egg to your liking. Pile everything onto a warm plate and serve together.","cookTime":"20 min","kcal":620,"nutrition":"protein 26g · carbs 38g · fat 40g","storage":"Best fresh; not for storing.","chefNotes":"The farls fried in bacon fat are what set it apart from a full English.","pairsWith":"toast · brown sauce · tea","trivia":"The breads make the Ulster fry distinct from other fry-ups.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""},
{"id":"northern-ireland-wheaten-bread-with-smoked-salmon","name":"Wheaten bread with smoked salmon","nameAlt":"Wheaten bread with smoked salmon","aliases":[],"course":"starter","type":"open sandwich","diet":"pescatarian","cuisine":"northern-europe","country":"Northern Ireland","occasion":"social","ingredients":"60g wholemeal flour (wheaten) · 50ml buttermilk · 2g bicarbonate of soda · 40g smoked salmon · 10g butter · 5ml lemon juice · pinch salt","method":"Mix the wholemeal flour, soda and salt, then stir in the buttermilk to a soft dough. Shape and bake at 200°C for about 25 minutes until it sounds hollow, then cool. Slice and butter the wheaten bread, lay over the smoked salmon and finish with a squeeze of lemon.","cookTime":"35 min","kcal":260,"nutrition":"protein 12g · carbs 28g · fat 11g","storage":"Bread keeps 3 days; top just before serving.","chefNotes":"Wheaten bread is the nutty Ulster cousin of soda bread.","pairsWith":"cream cheese · lemon · black pepper","trivia":"Wheaten bread is the wholemeal soda bread of Ulster.","howThisFeels":"","servings":1,"sharedWith":""}
